window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("serving-inference", {"topics":[{"key":"serving-inference/model-serving","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **A checkpoint answers nothing.** Someone must put weights on a device, tokenize, run the forward pass,\n  sample, detokenize and stream back, for hundreds of users or agents at once, without one 20k-token\n  document starving everyone else. That set of responsibilities is \"serving\".\n- **An LLM request is a loop, not a pass.** Classic ML serving (one request, one forward pass, scale\n  horizontally) works because a classifier's cost is fixed. An LLM runs one forward pass per generated\n  token, and a one-word tool call and a 2,000-word report arrive looking identical.\n- **The loop is stateful.** Each step needs the KV cache of every earlier token, so the state lives on the\n  GPU for the life of the request and grows every step. Memory, not compute, usually caps how many\n  requests (and so how many concurrent agents) a card can hold.\n- **GPUs are efficient only when busy with large matmuls.** A single decode request reads all weights per\n  token: for an 8B model in fp16 that is ~16 GB per token, so ~3 TB/s of bandwidth caps it near 200\n  tokens/s no matter how many FLOPs the GPU has, with the arithmetic units almost idle. Put 32 requests in\n  the same step and the weights are still read once, so the same ~16 GB yields 32 tokens.\n- **Batching loops is a scheduling problem.** Variable-length, variable-lifetime loops are something the\n  one-request-one-pass model never had to solve. The whole module works through these three facts; this\n  lesson is the map.","how":"### The layers of a serving stack\nFrom the outside in, as your agent's request travels:\n\n| # | Layer | What it owns |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **API layer** | HTTP server with a chat/completions protocol (most self-hosted engines expose an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, which is what agent frameworks expect): auth, rate limits, validation, SSE streaming, cancellation on client disconnect |\n| 2 | **Router** | Picks a replica. Round-robin works until you care about prefix-cache locality (an agent's growing transcript) or model-per-tenant isolation; then routing becomes content-aware |\n| 3 | **Engine** | Owns the GPU(s): vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, llama.cpp and others. Loads weights (optionally quantized, optionally sharded with tensor parallelism), owns the KV-cache pool, runs the scheduler |\n| 4 | **Scheduler** | Each iteration decides which waiting requests get prefill and which running ones get a decode step, under a token budget and free KV memory. The heart of the system: continuous batching and paged memory live here |\n| 5 | **Model executor and kernels** | The forward pass: attention kernels reading the paged cache, fused matmuls, CUDA graphs to cut launch overhead, on-device sampling |\n\n### The request lifecycle (one agent step)\n1. **Arrive and queue.** The request (system prompt, tool schemas, transcript so far) is tokenized and\n   waits in a queue.\n2. **Prefill.** When admitted, the prompt runs in one compute-heavy pass that populates the KV cache. The\n   first token comes out of it; that time is the **time to first token (TTFT)**. For an agent with a long\n   transcript, prefill is often most of the step.\n3. **Decode.** The request joins the running batch and gets one step per iteration alongside every other\n   running request. The spacing between its tokens is the **time per output token (TPOT)**.\n4. **Leave.** It exits on an end-of-sequence token, on hitting `max_tokens`, or on client cancel, and its\n   cache blocks are freed for the next request. Your agent then runs the tool and sends the next step.\n- **Why this keeps the GPU full:** requests join and leave at iteration granularity rather than waiting\n  for a whole batch to finish. That is continuous batching, and it is why hundreds of agents interleave\n  on one replica.\n\n### Why memory arithmetic comes first\n- **Weights are fixed; the cache is variable.** An 8B-parameter model in fp16 is about 16 GB of weights.\n- **Per-token cache is model-specific:** around 128 KB for a GQA model with 8 KV heads and 32 layers.\n- **The pool is what is left.** The engine reserves a fraction of VRAM (vLLM's default is 90%) and gives\n  what remains after weights to the cache pool.\n- **The real concurrency limit** is free cache tokens divided by typical sequence length. That is why \"how\n  many users (or agents) can one H100 serve\" has no answer until you know sequence lengths. Agents are the\n  hard case because their transcripts grow every step. The KV-cache memory estimation lesson does this\n  arithmetic in full.\n\n### Where the trade-offs live\nEvery serving knob trades latency against throughput:\n\n| Knob | Gain | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bigger batch | More tokens/s for the whole GPU | Longer TPOT per user, because each decode step reads more cache |\n| Larger `max_model_len` | Admits longer documents and agent transcripts | Fewer requests fit |\n| Quantized weights or cache | Frees memory and bandwidth | A quality cost you must measure on your agent evals |\n| Speculative decoding | Lower per-token latency | Extra compute |\n\nThe rest of this module makes each of those trades deliberate.\n\n### Startup, cold start and scaling\n- **What startup does:** load tens of gigabytes of weights (local disk, network storage or a model hub),\n  profile a worst-case forward pass to size the cache pool, and usually capture CUDA graphs.\n- **How long:** from tens of seconds to several minutes depending on model size and storage. So LLM\n  replicas cannot be autoscaled like stateless web pods, because a spike is over before a new replica is\n  warm.\n- **The usual answers:** a warm floor of replicas sized for normal peak; weights pre-staged on local NVMe\n  or baked into the image; scaling on queue depth or cache utilization (which lead) rather than CPU or\n  request count (which lag or mislead).\n\n### Managed API versus self-hosting\n- **A provider API is serving outsourced.** The same machinery runs on their side; pricing, rate limits\n  and prompt-caching rules are its visible surface, and they shape agent design just as engine flags do.\n- **Self-hosting buys control** (data residency, custom or fine-tuned agent models, predictable cost at\n  high utilization) at the price of owning everything above.\n- **The mechanics are identical,** so understanding them makes you better at both. The decision itself is\n  covered in the infrastructure and economics module.","example":"### Worked scenario: how many users does one GPU hold?\nAn 8B GQA model on one 80 GB GPU, with the flags from the stack example below. Illustrative arithmetic,\nignoring activation and CUDA-graph memory, which take a few more GB in practice.\n\n| Step | Value |\n|---|---|\n| VRAM the engine may use (`--gpu-memory-utilization 0.9`) | 0.9 × 80 GB = 72 GB |\n| Minus fp16 weights | 72 − 16 = 56 GB for the cache pool |\n| Cache per token (2 × 32 layers × 8 KV heads × 128 dim × 2 bytes) | 131,072 bytes = 128 KB |\n| Pool in tokens | 56 GB ÷ 128 KB ≈ 427,000 tokens |\n| Every request at the 8,192-token cap | ≈ 52 concurrent |\n| Typical request of 2,000 tokens | ≈ 213 fit, but `--max-num-seqs 128` caps it at 128 |\n| Agent steps averaging a 20,000-token transcript (needs a larger `max_model_len`) | ≈ 21 concurrent steps |\n\n- **Read it as:** the binding limit moves between the sequence cap and the memory pool depending on the\n  length mix, so you cannot quote a user count without the length distribution.\n- **For agents:** late steps of a long run are the expensive ones. The same GPU that holds 128 short chats\n  holds about 21 agent steps at 20k tokens, so size from transcript length at step *n*, not step 1.\n\n```python\npool_tokens = (0.9 * 80e9 - 16e9) / (2 * 32 * 8 * 128 * 2)\nfor avg_len in (8192, 2000):\n    print(avg_len, min(int(pool_tokens // avg_len), 128))   # 52, then 128 (seq cap binds)\n```\n\n### A minimal stack: OpenAI-compatible engine plus a streaming client\nThe server flags encode the memory and concurrency decisions above.\n\n```python\n# Terminal (one GPU, a GQA model that fits in fp16):\n#   vllm serve meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct \\\n#       --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.9 \\\n#       --max-num-seqs 128 --enable-prefix-caching\n# The startup log prints how many KV-cache blocks were allocated; that number\n# times the block size is the pool of context tokens the scheduler can hand out.\n\nimport time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")\n\nt0 = time.perf_counter()\nfirst = None\nn = 0\nstream = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a terse assistant.\"},\n              {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain continuous batching in 3 sentences.\"}],\n    max_tokens=200,\n    stream=True,                                   # SSE: one chunk per sampled token\n)\nfor chunk in stream:\n    if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n        if first is None:\n            first = time.perf_counter() - t0      # time to first token\n        n += 1\ntotal = time.perf_counter() - t0\nprint(f\"TTFT={first*1000:.0f} ms, chunks={n}, \"\n      f\"TPOT≈{(total-first)/max(n-1,1)*1000:.1f} ms/chunk\")\n# Run two copies of this script at once: TTFT barely moves, TPOT rises slightly,\n# and the server's tokens/sec roughly doubles — the signature of a batched decode loop.\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: timing every step of a tool-calling loop\nPoint the same client at the engine from an agent loop and log per-step latency and token counts.\n- **Use a tool-trained model.** Llama 3.1 Instruct was trained for tool calls; the original Llama 3 was not.\n- **Enable tool parsing on the server.** In vLLM that is `--enable-auto-tool-choice` plus a\n  `--tool-call-parser` matching the model's format (check the vLLM tool-calling docs for the name).\n\n```python\nimport time, json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")\nMODEL = \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\"   # serve this one for the agent run\ntools = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"search_docs\", \"description\": \"Search internal docs\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"q\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"q\"]}}}]\n\ndef search_docs(q):                       # stand-in tool\n    return f\"3 results for {q!r}\"\n\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a support agent. Use tools.\"},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"How do I rotate an API key?\"}]\nfor step in range(8):                     # hard cap on agent steps\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages, tools=tools,\n                                       max_tokens=300)   # per-step cap, not per task\n    dt = (time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000\n    msg = r.choices[0].message\n    print(f\"step {step}: {dt:.0f} ms, prompt={r.usage.prompt_tokens}, out={r.usage.completion_tokens}\")\n    messages.append(msg.model_dump(exclude_none=True))   # append-only transcript\n    if not msg.tool_calls:\n        break\n    for call in msg.tool_calls:\n        args = json.loads(call.function.arguments)\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id, \"content\": search_docs(**args)})\n# Watch prompt_tokens climb each step: that growth is what fills the KV pool.\n```","patterns":["**Instrument before tuning.** On day one, scrape the engine's Prometheus `/metrics` endpoint (vLLM exposes request queue depth, running requests, KV-cache usage, preemptions, TTFT and TPOT histograms) and build a dashboard of TTFT, TPOT, tokens/s per GPU and cache utilization. Alert on p99 TTFT, not averages, and add end-to-end agent task latency next to them.","**Cap per-step lengths at the gateway.** Enforce a maximum prompt size and a default `max_tokens` per route (and per agent step) before requests reach the engine, so one runaway generation cannot hold cache blocks for minutes. Pair it with a maximum step count per agent run. Monitor requests rejected or truncated per route.","**Session-affine routing for agent runs.** Route by a session, conversation or agent run id (consistent hashing) so step *n* lands on the replica that prefilled steps 1 to *n−1*. Monitor fleet-wide prefix-cache hit rate against a single replica's.","**Warm floor plus queue-based autoscaling.** Keep enough warm replicas for normal peak, pre-stage weights on local NVMe or in the image, and scale on queue depth or KV-cache utilization. Monitor time from scale-up decision to first served token.","**Propagate cancellation through the agent.** Stream with SSE and make sure the proxy and API layer abort the engine request when the client goes away, and that an aborted agent run cancels its in-flight model call. Monitor generated tokens versus tokens delivered to clients or consumed by the agent.","**Separate interactive agents from batch agents.** Offline eval runs and backfills go to their own deployment or a lower-priority queue, so a 1,000-task eval sweep cannot push live users' TTFT over budget."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** One agent task is several to dozens of sequential model calls, each\n  blocked on the previous one. Task latency is the sum of every step's queueing, prefill and decode, so serving behaviour\n  multiplies across the loop instead of showing up once.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The base URL your agent framework points at: a provider API, or a\n  self-hosted engine (vLLM, SGLang, TGI) behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, plus the gateway, router\n  and autoscaler in front of it.\n- **What you control.** Per-step `max_tokens`, prompt size, streaming and cancellation, session-affine\n  routing by agent run id, and, when self-hosting, the engine's memory and concurrency flags.\n- **What you can skip.** Kernel internals and CUDA graphs. Know the five layers, the request lifecycle\n  and the memory arithmetic; that is enough to debug a slow agent fleet.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Choosing hosted versus self-hosted for an agent product.** The five-layer view and memory arithmetic\n  decide whether a provider API or your own vLLM or SGLang deployment serves the agent's model.\n- **Debugging slow agent tasks.** Timing queueing, prefill and decode per step shows whether a 40-second\n  task is a serving problem, a prompt-size problem or a tool problem.\n- **Capacity planning an agent fleet.** Turning concurrent runs and transcript length into GPUs and\n  replicas before launch, instead of discovering the limit in production.\n- **Routing and autoscaling.** Session-affine routing by run id and queue-based autoscaling keep multi-step\n  agents on warm replicas.","mini_project":"**Self-host a model behind an agent and time every step (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** see where an agent task's latency goes when you own the serving stack.\n- **Steps:** start an OpenAI-compatible engine (for example vLLM) with a small instruct model; point a\n  minimal tool-calling agent with two fake tools at it; log per step the time to first token, decode time,\n  input and output tokens; then run 1, 8 and 32 agents concurrently.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-step table at each concurrency level showing which phase grows, and a short\n  note on which engine setting or prompt change you would try first."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Serve an LLM like any model: stateless replicas behind a round-robin balancer.\"** Each request is a stateful loop pinning a growing KV cache on one GPU, replicas take tens of seconds to minutes to warm, and round-robin scatters an agent's transcript across caches. Scale on queue depth and cache use, and route by agent run id.","**\"How many users a GPU holds is a property of the GPU.\"** It is free cache tokens divided by sequence length. The same 80 GB card and 8B model hold 128 chats at 2,000 tokens (capped by `--max-num-seqs`), about 52 requests at 8,192 and about 21 agent steps at 20,000.","**\"Record tokens/s on the dashboard means users are happy.\"** Throughput rises with batch size while every user's TPOT rises too, so a fleet can set records while streams feel slow. Watch p99 TTFT and TPOT next to tokens/s.","**\"An agent task's latency is one model call's latency.\"** It is the sum of every step's queueing, prefill and decode, and later steps carry longer transcripts, so a small per-call regression is paid dozens of times per task.","**\"With a managed API, serving mechanics are not my problem.\"** The same machinery runs on the provider's side. Rate limits, prompt-caching rules and token pricing are its visible surface, and they shape prompt layout and step count just as engine flags do."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Agent loop\"] -->|\"POST chat/completions\"| API[\"API layer: auth, limits, SSE\"]\n    API --> R[\"Router picks replica\"]\n    R --> Q[\"Engine queue\"]\n    Q --> S[\"Scheduler: token budget, free KV\"]\n    S -->|\"admit\"| PF[\"Prefill pass\"]\n    PF -->|\"first token = TTFT\"| D[\"Decode step in batch\"]\n    D -->|\"next token = TPOT\"| D\n    D --> KV[(\"Paged KV cache pool\")]\n    D -->|\"EOS, max_tokens, cancel\"| F[\"Free cache blocks\"]\n    F --> S\n    D -.->|\"stream chunks or tool call\"| API","caption":"Follow one agent step from the API to the GPU and back; the scheduler loop is where TTFT and TPOT are\nmade, and the agent repeats this trip once per step."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't you serve an LLM like a classic stateless prediction service?","a":"- **Cost is not fixed per request.** An LLM request is one forward pass per output token, and the length\n  is unknown at arrival, so per-request cost varies by orders of magnitude.\n- **It holds state.** The KV cache sits on the GPU for the request's whole life and grows every step, so\n  memory, not CPU, decides how many requests a replica holds.\n- **It must be batched.** Single-request decode leaves the GPU's arithmetic idle, so the engine batches\n  many live loops per step, which needs an iteration-level scheduler."},{"q":"Your agent takes 40 seconds per task on a self-hosted model. How do you find where the time goes?","a":"- **Break it down per step:** log steps per task, prompt and completion tokens, TTFT and total time for\n  every model call, plus tool time. Agent latency is a sum, so find the largest terms.\n- **Long prompts, short outputs** mean prefill dominates: check prefix-cache hit rate and session-affine\n  routing so each step only prefills the new tool result.\n- **High queue time** means admission: check KV-cache utilization and preemptions, then cap\n  concurrency, add replicas or move batch evals elsewhere."},{"q":"Users say the chatbot \"types slowly\" at peak but dashboards show record tokens/s. What is going on?","a":"- **That is the throughput-latency trade.** Larger batches raise aggregate tokens/s, but each decode step\n  reads more KV cache, so every user's TPOT grows.\n- **Confirm with metrics:** running requests per step and TPOT p95 rising together while TTFT is flat.\n- **Fix by product goal:** lower the maximum concurrent sequences per replica and add replicas, or split\n  interactive and batch traffic onto separate deployments with different limits."},{"q":"When would you not self-host your agent's model, even with a capable platform team?","a":"- **Bursty or small traffic.** Idle GPUs cost the same as busy ones, so low or spiky utilization makes a\n  managed API cheaper per useful token.\n- **Cold starts you cannot hide.** Replicas take tens of seconds to minutes to warm, so spiky demand needs\n  a paid warm floor anyway.\n- **Self-host when** data residency, custom or fine-tuned agent models, or high steady utilization make\n  control and unit cost win."},{"q":"How would you capacity-plan one 80 GB GPU for an 8B model?","a":"- **Start from the pool:** 90% of 80 GB minus 16 GB of fp16 weights leaves about 56 GB for cache.\n- **Per-token cost:** about 128 KB for a 32-layer GQA model with 8 KV heads, so roughly 427,000 cached\n  tokens.\n- **Divide by the real length mix,** not the cap: ~52 requests at 8k tokens each, far more at 2k, then\n  check which limit (memory or the sequence cap) binds and leave headroom for activations."},{"q":"Why do LLM replicas autoscale badly, and what do you do about it for an agent fleet?","a":"- **Startup is slow:** loading weights, profiling the cache pool and capturing CUDA graphs take tens of\n  seconds to minutes.\n- **CPU and request count mislead:** they lag the real pressure, which shows first as queue depth and\n  KV-cache utilization.\n- **So** keep a warm floor for normal peak, pre-stage weights on local NVMe or in the image, and scale on\n  leading signals; agent runs in flight also make draining slow, so scale down gently."}],"tradeoffs":["**Bigger batches vs per-token latency.** Batching more requests per step raises GPU throughput but lengthens each request's per-token latency. Flips toward smaller batches when the product is interactive and TPOT is the user-visible metric; an agent pays it on every step.","**Long `max_model_len` vs concurrency.** Long limits serve document workloads and long agent transcripts but shrink concurrency, because every admitted request may claim that much cache. Flips when p99 prompt length is far below the cap and admission uses actual lengths.","**Self-hosting vs managed API.** Self-hosting gives data control and a lower cost per token at high, steady utilization. A managed API wins when traffic is bursty or small, because idle GPUs cost the same as busy ones.","**Streaming vs simplicity.** Streaming tokens improves perceived latency and lets clients cancel early, but complicates proxies, retries and observability. It stops being worth it for short structured outputs consumed by machines, such as an agent's intermediate tool calls."],"pitfalls":["**Capacity-planning from weight size alone.** Symptom: the engine admits far fewer concurrent requests than expected, or preempts them mid-generation under load. Fix: add the cache to the plan, sized at a late agent step.","**Measuring latency with a single request and shipping.** Symptom: TPOT doubles the first time real concurrency arrives, because decode steps now read many caches per iteration. Fix: load-test at the expected concurrency and length mix before quoting latency.","**A generic HTTP load balancer in front of prefix-cached replicas.** Symptom: fleet-wide prefix hit rate far below what one replica achieves, because turn *n* of a conversation lands on a GPU that never prefilled turns 1 to *n−1*. Fix: route by session or conversation id.","**Treating `max_tokens` as cosmetic.** Symptom: a few runaway requests hold cache blocks for minutes and starve the queue, visible as TTFT spikes unrelated to request rate. Fix: set a per-route default and cap.","**Ignoring client disconnects.** Symptom: GPU time spent generating tokens nobody reads, showing up as throughput that does not translate into completed requests. Fix: abort the engine request on disconnect.","**The wrong chat template.** Serving an instruct model with a template other than the one it was trained with (often a hand-built prompt string) raises no error. Symptom: answers that ramble, ignore the system prompt, fail to stop, or emit malformed tool calls, because the model never sees the role markers it expects. Fix: send messages to the chat endpoint so the engine applies the model's own template.","**Autoscaling on CPU utilization or request count.** Symptom: new replicas become ready minutes after the queue already blew through the TTFT budget, then idle after the spike. Fix: scale on queue depth or KV-cache utilization, above a warm floor."],"when":"You meet serving the moment a model leaves a notebook, and every agent you ship sits on top of it. Use\nthis lesson as the map.\n- **Find the layer:** when a question is about *why* a number looks the way it does, place it in API,\n  router, scheduler, memory or kernels, then go deep: prefill vs decode for the latency split, KV-cache\n  memory estimation for capacity, continuous batching and paging for scheduling, engines for which\n  software to run.\n- **Measure from day one:** TTFT, TPOT, tokens per second per GPU and cache utilization, because every\n  later decision is made against those four. For agents, add steps per task and latency per step.\n- **Avoid when:** you call a managed API at low volume; then the provider owns these layers and you\n  control only prompt size, `max_tokens`, streaming and retries.\n- **Interviews:** explain the request lifecycle end to end and name which resource (memory, bandwidth,\n  compute) binds at each phase.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Stands up an engine with an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, sets memory and concurrency flags from measured sequence lengths, and instruments TTFT and TPOT before tuning anything.","architect":"Owns the managed-versus-self-hosted decision, the GPU tier and replica count, and the routing design that keeps tenant isolation and cache locality from fighting each other.","fde":"Deploys the stack inside a customer's VPC, explains why their latency differs from the demo, and translates their document sizes and user counts into a concrete GPU footprint."},"check":{"q":"A customer runs an 8B model on one 80 GB GPU and reports that p50 latency is fine but p99 TTFT\nspikes to several seconds every few minutes with no change in request rate. Walk through where in the\nserving stack you would look, and why.","a":"TTFT is dominated by queueing plus prefill, so periodic spikes with flat request rate point at\nadmission, not load. First suspect is a few very long prompts: a 20k-token prefill is compute-heavy and,\nwithout chunked prefill, blocks a whole iteration so every waiting request's TTFT jumps. Second is memory:\nif long-running generations fill the KV-cache pool, new requests wait for blocks to free, and the engine\nmay preempt running sequences, which shows as both TTFT and TPOT spikes. Check the engine's metrics for\nqueue depth, cache utilization and preemption count at the spike times. Fixes, in order: cap `max_tokens`\nand prompt length per endpoint, enable chunked prefill so long prompts are interleaved with decode steps,\nand if cache utilization is pinned at 100%, either lower concurrency, quantize the cache, or add a replica."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is serving an LLM fundamentally different from serving a classification model?","options":["LLM weights are larger, so they must be loaded lazily from disk per request","Each LLM request is a stateful loop of unknown length whose working set grows while it runs","LLM requests cannot be batched, so each one needs a dedicated GPU","Classifiers return probabilities while LLMs return text, which changes the serialization layer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A classifier costs one fixed forward pass; an LLM request runs one forward pass per generated token, holds a growing KV cache on the GPU throughout, and finishes at an unpredictable time. Batching is not only possible but essential, which is the opposite of the third option."},{"q":"Which single quantity most directly determines how many concurrent requests one GPU can hold?","options":["Peak FLOPS of the GPU divided by model parameter count","Number of CPU cores available to the tokenizer","Free VRAM after weights, divided by per-request KV-cache size at typical sequence length","Network bandwidth between the API server and the engine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concurrency is a memory question: each running request pins its KV cache for its whole lifetime. FLOPS affects how fast steps run, not how many requests fit, and the tokenizer and network are rarely the binding resource."},{"q":"A team doubles concurrent users on the same GPU and sees per-token latency rise while total tokens/sec also rises. What is happening?","options":["The GPU is thermally throttling under the extra load","The tokenizer has become the bottleneck","Decode is bandwidth-bound; larger batches amortize weight reads but each step now reads more KV cache","Prefix caching is evicting entries faster than it can fill them"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Batching lets one weight read serve many requests, so aggregate throughput climbs. But each decode step also reads every running request's cache, so step time grows and every user's TPOT lengthens. That is the expected shape of the throughput-versus-latency curve, not a fault."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/inference-optimization","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Dao et al., 2022 — FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness","Leviathan et al., 2023 — Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding"],"why":"- **GPUs have a fixed compute-to-bandwidth ratio.** An H100 does on the order of a thousand teraFLOPs of\n  dense fp16 math but moves only a few terabytes per second from HBM. It needs roughly a few hundred\n  FLOPs per byte loaded to keep its arithmetic busy.\n- **Decode at batch 1 is nowhere near that.** A matmul over *B* tokens does about 2·B FLOPs per weight\n  element while reading it once, so intensity is about *B* FLOPs per byte, and single-request decode has\n  *B* = 1.\n- **So the tensor cores idle.** An 8B model at batch 1 does ~16 GFLOPs in a ~5 ms step: a few TFLOP/s\n  against a peak near a thousand, a utilization well under one percent. That is the default state of a\n  naive LLM server.\n- **\"Buy a bigger GPU\" fails.** More FLOPs with similar bandwidth makes decode no faster.\n- **\"Run more requests at once\" is correct but hits memory.** Each concurrent request pins a KV cache, and\n  the pool fills long before the batch is large enough to be compute-bound.\n- **Every technique gets around one of those two walls:** move fewer bytes per token, do less work per\n  request, or fit more requests into the same bytes. Matching lever to wall is the discipline, which is\n  why measurement precedes tuning.","how":"### Start from the roofline\nFor a decode step at batch size *B* with weights of *W* bytes and a total KV cache of *C* bytes across the\nbatch:\n\n```\ntime_per_step ≈ max( (W + C) / bandwidth ,  2·B·params / FLOPs )\n```\n\n- **Left term, the bandwidth floor,** dominates until *B* reaches the GPU's ridge point (a few hundred\n  tokens per step on current hardware).\n- **Prefill** processes the whole prompt as one batch of *B* = prompt length tokens, so it sits on the\n  right-hand, compute-bound side.\n- **Each lever below shrinks one term.**\n\n### Levers that move fewer bytes (help decode)\n| Lever | What it shrinks | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Weight quantization** (int8, fp8, 4-bit formats such as AWQ or GPTQ) | Halves or quarters *W*, so the batch-1 floor drops proportionally; frees VRAM for cache | Evaluate per model and task, because the loss is not uniform |\n| **KV-cache quantization** (fp8 or int8 cache) | Shrinks *C* | Matters once cache reads rival weight reads; long-context, high-concurrency workloads benefit most |\n| **Architecture choices** (grouped-query attention, multi-head latent attention) | Cache bytes per token, by sharing or compressing K/V | You pick these by picking the model, not by tuning |\n\n### Levers that do less work per request\n- **Prefix caching.** Skip prefill for a prompt prefix already in cache. Cuts TTFT and compute for shared\n  system prompts, few-shot blocks, tool schemas, multi-turn chat and every step of an append-only agent\n  transcript.\n- **Prompt and output discipline.** Shorter prompts cut prefill compute linearly; a sensible `max_tokens`\n  and stop sequences stop decode from generating tokens nobody uses. For agents, trimming verbose tool\n  results before appending them shrinks every later step.\n- **Fewer steps.** Each agent step is a full request. Parallel tool calls in one step, or a plan that\n  needs fewer round trips, removes whole prefill and decode passes.\n- **Constrained decoding.** Masking logits with a grammar or JSON schema removes retries for invalid\n  output (such as malformed tool-call arguments), a request-level optimization. Computing the mask is CPU\n  work per step, so a slow grammar engine can itself bottleneck at high batch.\n\n### Speculative decoding\n1. **Draft.** A small draft model (or a lightweight head) proposes *k* tokens.\n2. **Verify.** The large model checks all of them in one forward pass. That costs barely more than one\n   decode step, because the step was bandwidth-bound anyway, so accepted drafts come almost free.\n3. **Correct.** Rejection sampling corrects every draft to the target model's distribution, so the\n   output distribution is unchanged.\n\n```\nexpected tokens per target pass = (1 − α^(k+1)) / (1 − α)      # α = per-token acceptance\nα = 0.8, k = 4  ->  about 3.4 tokens per pass instead of 1, minus the draft's own cost\n```\n\n- **When it helps:** small batch and an accurate draft. Acceptance falls on creative or unusual text, and\n  tends to be high on predictable text such as code and structured tool-call JSON.\n- **When it hurts:** at large batch the GPU is already busy, so verification competes with real work.\n\n### Levers that fit more work into the same bytes\n- **Continuous batching.** Admit and retire requests every iteration so the running batch stays full.\n  Each weight read then serves many tokens, pushing *B* toward the ridge point.\n- **Paged KV cache.** Eliminates fragmentation so the pool holds more sequences, which is what makes large\n  batches reachable.\n- **Chunked prefill.** Split long prompts into chunks interleaved with decode steps, so prefill's compute\n  and decode's bandwidth demand overlap rather than queue.\n- **Disaggregated prefill and decode.** Run the phases on separate GPU pools and ship the KV cache between\n  them, so long prefills stop stalling decode and each pool is sized for its own bound. Pays at large\n  scale with mixed traffic; below that, chunked prefill gets most of the benefit.\n- **Tensor parallelism.** Shard weights so each GPU reads a fraction of *W* per step. It adds an\n  all-reduce per layer, so it pays when a model does not fit or per-GPU bandwidth is the limit.\n\n### Levers at the kernel level\n- **Fused attention kernels** (FlashAttention and successors) avoid materializing the full attention\n  matrix, cutting prefill memory traffic and enabling long contexts.\n- **Fused elementwise ops and CUDA graphs** remove per-kernel launch overhead, which at batch 1 can be a\n  surprisingly large fraction of a step.\n- **Compiled or engine-specific runtimes** (TensorRT-LLM style) specialize kernels to the model shape.\n\n### Levers outside the engine\n- **Route easy requests to a smaller model, cache whole responses, distill a task-specific small model, or\n  precompute embeddings.**\n- **For agents:** use a small model for routing, intent classification and summarizing tool output, and\n  keep the large model for planning; cache deterministic tool results so repeated lookups skip a step.\n- **These often beat any engine knob,** because they cut the number of tokens that touch an expensive GPU\n  in the first place.\n\n### The order of operations\n1. **Measure** TTFT, TPOT, tokens/sec per GPU, batch size per step and KV-cache utilization.\n2. **Classify and pick:**\n\n| Signal | Regime | Levers |\n|---|---|---|\n| Small batch, pool far from full | Latency-bound per request | Quantize weights, add speculative decoding, trim prompts |\n| Pool full | Memory-bound | Quantize cache, enable prefix caching, choose a GQA model, add GPUs |\n| Prefill dominates, TTFT is the complaint (typical of agents) | Compute-bound prefill | Chunked prefill, prefix caching, shorter prompts |\n\n3. **Change one knob, re-measure.** Changing a knob without knowing the regime is how teams \"optimize\"\n   into a slower system.","example":"### A roofline calculator\nThe hardware numbers are rounded, publicly quoted figures, meant to show the shape of the result, not to\npredict a benchmark.\n\n```python\ndef decode_step_ms(params_b, batch, ctx_tokens, kv_bytes_per_token,\n                   weight_bytes=2, bw_tb_s=3.0, tflops=900):\n    \"\"\"Lower bound on one decode iteration: bandwidth floor vs compute floor.\"\"\"\n    W = params_b * 1e9 * weight_bytes                # bytes of weights read per step\n    C = batch * ctx_tokens * kv_bytes_per_token      # bytes of KV cache read per step\n    t_bw = (W + C) / (bw_tb_s * 1e12)\n    t_fl = 2 * batch * params_b * 1e9 / (tflops * 1e12)\n    bound = \"bandwidth\" if t_bw >= t_fl else \"compute\"\n    return 1000 * max(t_bw, t_fl), bound\n\nKV8B = 2 * 32 * 8 * 128 * 2        # 8B GQA model, fp16 cache: ~128 KB per token\nfor batch in (1, 8, 64, 256):\n    ms, b = decode_step_ms(8, batch, ctx_tokens=2048, kv_bytes_per_token=KV8B)\n    print(f\"fp16 batch={batch:4d}: {ms:6.2f} ms/step, {batch/ms*1000:8.0f} tok/s  ({b}-bound)\")\n\nms4, _ = decode_step_ms(8, 1, 2048, KV8B, weight_bytes=0.5)   # 4-bit weights, fp16 cache\nprint(f\"4-bit batch=1: {ms4:.2f} ms/step  -> weight bytes were the floor, so ~3-4x faster\")\n# Illustrative output shape: ~5.4 ms at batch 1 (~180 tok/s); by batch 256 the step is\n# ~5x longer while aggregate tok/s is ~50x higher, and the growth is all KV-cache traffic.\n# Note the roofline is not a capacity check: 256 x 2048 tokens of cache is 64 GiB, which would\n# not fit beside 16 GB of weights on an 80 GB card; the memory-estimation lesson covers that.\n```\n\n- **At batch 1** the step time is almost entirely weight traffic, so quantizing weights is the lever.\n- **At batch 256 with 2k contexts** cache reads already exceed weight reads, so cache size (GQA, cache\n  quantization) is the lever. The compute term only takes over at larger batches with short contexts.\n- **With `ctx_tokens=32000`** cache reads overtake weight reads at modest batch sizes (about batch 4 here),\n  which is when KV quantization and GQA start to pay.\n\n### What speculative decoding buys at different acceptance rates\nThe formula from above, swept over α. Real speedup is lower, because the draft model costs time too.\n\n```python\ndef tokens_per_pass(alpha, k):\n    return (1 - alpha ** (k + 1)) / (1 - alpha)\n\nfor alpha in (0.5, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9):\n    print(alpha, [round(tokens_per_pass(alpha, k), 2) for k in (2, 4, 8)])\n# 0.5 -> [1.75, 1.94, 2.0]   drafting more than a few tokens is wasted\n# 0.8 -> [2.44, 3.36, 4.33]  the lesson's ~3.4 at k = 4\n# 0.9 -> [2.71, 4.1, 6.13]   predictable text (code, templates) rewards longer drafts\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: where a 10-step task spends its GPU time\nA tool-calling agent on the 8B model above: a 6k-token prefix (system prompt plus tool schemas), ~1k\ntokens appended per step (tool call plus result), ~150 output tokens per step. Rates are illustrative\nassumptions (10,000 prefill tokens/s, the ~5.4 ms batch-1 decode floor), not a benchmark.\n\n| Quantity over 10 steps | No prefix reuse | Append-only transcript plus prefix caching |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prefill tokens | 6k + 7k + … + 15k = 105k | 6k + 9 × 1k = 15k |\n| Prefill time | ~10.5 s | ~1.5 s |\n| Decode tokens and time | 1,500 tokens, ~8.1 s | 1,500 tokens, ~8.1 s |\n| Prefill share of GPU time | ~56% | ~16% |\n\n```python\nprefix, per_step, out, steps = 6000, 1000, 150, 10\nno_reuse = sum(prefix + per_step * i for i in range(steps))      # 105,000\nreuse = prefix + per_step * (steps - 1)                          # 15,000\ndecode_s = steps * out * 5.4e-3                                  # ~8.1 s\nfor name, toks in ((\"no reuse\", no_reuse), (\"prefix cache\", reuse)):\n    pre_s = toks / 10_000\n    print(name, toks, f\"{pre_s:.1f}s prefill\", f\"{pre_s / (pre_s + decode_s):.0%} of GPU time\")\n```\n\n- **Read it as:** for agents the first lever is a stable, append-only prompt, not a decode trick. Once\n  prefill is small, decode dominates and weight quantization or speculative decoding (high acceptance on\n  tool-call JSON) becomes the next lever.\n\n### Worked triage: three slow deployments\n| Deployment | Measured | Regime | First lever |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Internal copilot, 70B on 2 GPUs | Batch 3, pool 30% used, slow streaming | Bandwidth-bound decode | fp8 or 4-bit weights with an eval, then speculative decoding |\n| Support chat at peak | Pool pinned near 100%, preemptions climbing | Memory-capacity bound | fp8 KV cache, prefix caching for the shared system prompt |\n| Contract review, 30k-token prompts | TTFT is the complaint, decode fine | Compute-bound prefill | Chunked prefill, prefix caching, trimming boilerplate from prompts |\n| Research agent, 25 steps per task | Per-step TTFT grows each step, low cache hit rate | Compute-bound prefill, re-done every step | Stable prefix, append-only transcript, session-affine routing, trim tool output |","patterns":["**Load test with the production length mix.** Replay real prompt/output length distributions (for example 6k in / 800 out, not 500 in / 100 out) at target concurrency with a serving benchmark such as vLLM's, before and after each flag. For agents, replay recorded multi-step traces so later, longer steps are represented. Monitor TTFT p95, TPOT p95 and tokens/s per GPU together.","**One flag per change, with an agent eval gate.** Treat quantization (`--quantization` with an AWQ, GPTQ or fp8 checkpoint in vLLM) and `--kv-cache-dtype fp8` as releases: run the task eval set, especially its hard tail and tool-call validity rate, and roll back on regression. Monitor eval score alongside latency.","**Make the prompt cacheable before tuning the engine.** Put system prompt and tool schemas first and byte-identical, append tool results instead of rewriting history, and trim tool output. Monitor cached-token share per agent step.","**Chunked prefill for mixed traffic.** Enable it (`--enable-chunked-prefill` in vLLM, on by default in recent versions) and tune `--max-num-batched-tokens` to bound how much prefill rides in each step. Monitor TPOT p99 during long-prompt bursts.","**Speculative decoding only on low-batch routes.** Enable it for latency-sensitive, low-concurrency deployments and keep it off on high-utilization batch pools. Monitor acceptance rate and throughput; turn it off when acceptance drops or throughput falls.","**Cheap path first.** Route easy intents and easy agent steps (routing, summarizing tool output) to a smaller model and cache whole responses for repeated queries, gated by an eval proving the cheaper path meets the bar. Monitor share of traffic on the cheap path and its quality score."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent multiplies every per-call cost by its step count. A 10-step\n  task with long transcripts and 150-token tool calls can spend over half its GPU time re-prefilling the\n  same history (worked example below), so prefix caching and prompt discipline come before decode tricks.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider prompt caching and smaller-model tiers for hosted APIs;\n  vLLM flags (`--enable-chunked-prefill`, `--kv-cache-dtype fp8`, `--quantization`) or their SGLang\n  equivalents, plus speculative decoding, for self-hosted models; structured-output or tool-call\n  constraints in both.\n- **What you control.** Fewer steps per task, shorter and stable prompts, per-step `max_tokens`, routing\n  easy steps (classify, summarize a tool result) to a small model, and one-flag-at-a-time changes gated\n  by your agent eval.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing kernels or tuning CUDA graphs yourself. Know the roofline and the regime\n  table; that tells you which lever to ask for.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Cutting agent task latency.** Locating whether a multi-step task is prefill-heavy or decode-heavy\n  picks the lever: prefix caching and shorter prompts first, decode tricks second.\n- **Lowering cost per agent task.** Routing easy steps such as classifying or summarizing a tool result to\n  a smaller model, and capping per-step `max_tokens`, cuts GPU time without touching the hard steps.\n- **Fitting more agent runs per GPU.** Weight and cache quantization raise concurrency once an agent eval\n  confirms quality holds.\n- **Triage of a slow self-hosted deployment.** The roofline and regime table tell you which flag to try\n  before buying hardware.","mini_project":"**One-lever-at-a-time optimization of an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure what each inference lever does to a real agent task's time and quality.\n- **Steps:** record a baseline for a 10-step tool-calling agent on a small eval set (task time, tokens per\n  step, pass rate); then change one thing at a time, such as stable prefix plus prefix caching, a lower\n  per-step `max_tokens`, or a quantized model; rerun the same set after each change.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of lever, task-time change and pass-rate change, and a recommendation of\n  which levers to keep."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A GPU with more FLOPs makes decode faster.\"** Batch-1 decode re-reads every weight per token and sits on the bandwidth floor with tensor cores well under 1% busy, so more FLOPs at similar bandwidth buys almost nothing. Fewer bytes (quantized weights or cache) or more requests per weight read do.","**\"Quantization helps because low-precision math is cheaper.\"** In decode the math was idle anyway. 4-bit weights help because each step reads a quarter of the bytes, which is why the batch-1 step gets roughly 3-4x faster and why the gain shrinks once cache reads or compute dominate the step.","**\"Speculative decoding trades quality for speed.\"** Rejection sampling keeps the target model's output distribution unchanged. Its real cost is draft time and verification compute, so it pays at small batch with high acceptance (about 3.4 tokens per pass at α = 0.8, k = 4) and hurts on a busy GPU.","**\"For an agent, decode is where the GPU time goes.\"** Without prefix reuse, a 10-step task re-prefills 105k tokens, about 56% of its GPU time. A stable, append-only prompt cuts that to 15k tokens before any decode trick is worth trying.","**\"Turn on every optimization flag and the gains add up.\"** Each lever shrinks one term of the roofline. Applied in the wrong regime it does nothing or makes things slower, so classify the regime, change one knob, and re-run the load test and agent eval."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Measure TTFT, TPOT, batch, KV use\"] --> Q{\"Which number misses target?\"}\n    Q -->|\"TTFT, prefill heavy\"| P[\"Compute-bound prefill\"]\n    Q -->|\"TPOT, small batch\"| L[\"Bandwidth-bound decode\"]\n    Q -->|\"pool full, preemptions\"| C[\"Memory-capacity bound\"]\n    P --> P1[\"Chunked prefill, prefix cache, shorter prompts\"]\n    L --> L1[\"Weight quant, speculative decoding, CUDA graphs\"]\n    C --> C1[\"KV quant, GQA model, paging, more GPUs\"]\n    P1 --> R[\"Re-run load test plus agent eval\"]\n    L1 --> R\n    C1 --> R\n    R --> M","caption":"Measure agent steps first, then let the regime pick the lever; each branch attacks a different term of\nthe roofline, and every change goes back through the agent eval."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the roofline model and why does it matter for LLM inference?","a":"- **Definition:** step time is the larger of bytes moved divided by bandwidth and FLOPs divided by peak\n  compute. Arithmetic intensity (FLOPs per byte) decides which term wins.\n- **Why it matters:** decode at small batch has intensity near *B* FLOPs per byte, far below the few\n  hundred an H100 needs, so it is bandwidth-bound and only byte-cutting levers help it. Prefill is\n  compute-bound and needs different levers."},{"q":"A faster, more expensive GPU gave you almost no decode speedup. Explain and propose what to do instead.","a":"- **Diagnosis:** if its bandwidth is similar, the bandwidth floor of a decode step barely moves, because\n  each step still streams all weights and cache.\n- **Instead:** quantize weights to cut *W*, raise effective batch with continuous batching, add speculative\n  decoding at low batch, or pick a GPU on memory bandwidth rather than peak FLOPS."},{"q":"When would you not use speculative decoding?","a":"- **High-utilization batches:** the GPU is already compute-busy, so verification steals compute and\n  throughput falls.\n- **Low acceptance:** creative or unusual text lowers α; at α = 0.5 even long drafts yield about 2 tokens\n  per pass, which the draft's cost can erase.\n- **Use it** for small-batch, latency-sensitive routes with predictable output."},{"q":"Your agent fleet's pool is at 100% and preemptions are climbing. Which levers and in what order?","a":"- **Confirm the regime:** pool full means memory-capacity bound, not bandwidth or compute. Long-running\n  agents with growing transcripts are the usual cause.\n- **Cheap first:** prefix caching for shared prompts, tighter `max_tokens`, fp8 KV cache after an eval.\n- **Then structural:** a GQA model, more GPUs or replicas. Weight quantization helps only by freeing VRAM\n  for cache."},{"q":"Estimate the decode latency floor of an 8B fp16 model on a ~3 TB/s GPU.","a":"- **Bytes per step:** ~16 GB of weights plus the batch's cache.\n- **Floor:** 16 GB ÷ 3 TB/s ≈ 5.3 ms, so ~5.4 ms with a 2k-token cache at batch 1, about 180 tokens/s.\n- **4-bit weights** cut that to about 1.4 ms, roughly 3-4x faster, because weight bytes were the floor."},{"q":"A coding agent averages 12 steps and 30 seconds per task on a self-hosted model. Where do you start?","a":"- **Profile the steps, not one call:** prompt tokens, cached tokens, output tokens and time per step. Long\n  prompts with short tool-call outputs mean prefill is the bulk of GPU time.\n- **Cheapest levers first:** a stable, append-only prefix with prefix caching and session-affine routing,\n  trimmed tool output, fewer steps via parallel tool calls.\n- **Then the engine:** chunked prefill, weight quantization or speculative decoding, each gated on the\n  agent eval's task success rate."}],"tradeoffs":["**Weight quantization vs accuracy.** Cuts bytes per step and VRAM but costs some accuracy. Flips against you when the task is sensitive (math, code, long reasoning) and no evaluation has been run on it.","**Speculative decoding vs throughput.** Lowers per-token latency at small batch by spending extra compute. At high utilization the GPU has no spare compute and it can reduce throughput.","**Larger batches vs TPOT.** They raise throughput per GPU but lengthen every user's TPOT. The right point depends on whether you sell tokens per dollar or seconds per answer.","**Tensor parallelism vs communication.** Spreads bandwidth across GPUs but adds inter-GPU communication every layer. It stops helping once the model fits comfortably and the interconnect is slow.","**Smaller models and response caches vs proof.** They can beat every engine knob, but only if an evaluation proves the cheaper path meets the quality bar; in an agent, a weaker step can cost more steps later, so measure task success, not per-call accuracy.","**Shorter context vs agent memory.** Trimming tool output and compacting history cuts prefill on every later step, but drops facts the agent may need. Flips when tasks depend on details from early steps."],"pitfalls":["**Tuning for batch throughput, shipping to an interactive product.** Symptom: good tokens/sec dashboards alongside user complaints about slow typing. Fix: set a TPOT p95 target and tune batch limits against it.","**Speculative decoding under heavy load.** Symptom: throughput drops while per-request latency barely improves, because verification steals compute from the batch. Fix: keep it to low-batch routes.","**Quantizing to 4-bit without a task-specific eval.** Symptom: a regression that only appears on the hard tail of the evaluation set, weeks after deployment. Fix: gate every quantized checkpoint on the task eval.","**Chasing kernel gains while prompts carry thousands of tokens of boilerplate.** Symptom: prefill dominates TTFT no matter what engine flags change. Fix: trim the prompt or cache its shared prefix first.","**Optimizing per-call latency while the agent takes too many steps.** Symptom: each call got faster but task latency and cost barely moved. Fix: measure steps per task and cut round trips (parallel tool calls, better plans) before tuning the engine.","**Reading vendor peak FLOPS as a throughput promise.** Symptom: utilization in the single digits and a purchase order for faster GPUs that do not help decode. Fix: compare GPUs on memory bandwidth for decode.","**Load-testing with the wrong length mix** (say, 500 in / 100 out when real traffic is 6k in / 800 out). Symptom: a lever that won in the benchmark does nothing, or harm, in production, because the benchmark sat in a different regime. Fix: replay length distributions sampled from production logs."],"when":"- **Use when a measured number misses a target:** TTFT above budget, TPOT making text feel slow, agent\n  task latency above budget, or cost per million tokens above plan.\n- **Classify the regime first** (bandwidth-bound decode, compute-bound prefill, or memory-capacity-bound\n  concurrency) and pick the lever that attacks it.\n- **Avoid** optimizing a workload you have not profiled, and stacking levers blindly: quantization plus\n  speculative decoding plus aggressive batching interact.\n- **Day to day:** change one flag, re-run the load test, compare TTFT/TPOT/throughput and a quality eval.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you make this faster\" starts with \"which bound are we hitting\", not with a\n  technique.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the load test, reads batch size and cache utilization from engine metrics, and changes one flag at a time with a quality eval in the loop.","architect":"Sets the latency and cost targets that define which regime matters, chooses GQA models and GPU tiers accordingly, and decides where routing to smaller models is acceptable.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's slow deployment by classifying the bottleneck from their metrics, then proposes the cheapest lever rather than the most impressive one."},"check":{"q":"A team serves a 70B model across two GPUs with tensor parallelism. Batch size per step averages 3,\nthe KV-cache pool is 30% used, and users complain about slow token streaming. Which optimizations would\nyou try first, which would you avoid, and why?","a":"Batch 3 with a nearly empty cache pool means they are not memory-capacity-bound; they are\nbandwidth-bound per step, reading 140 GB of fp16 weights for three tokens. The first lever is weight\nquantization (fp8 or 4-bit with an eval), which cuts bytes per step directly; the second is speculative\ndecoding, which is ideal at low batch because the verification step is nearly free. Checking that CUDA\ngraphs and a fused attention kernel are enabled is cheap. Avoid adding GPUs for capacity or quantizing the\nKV cache: the pool is not the constraint and cache traffic is small at batch 3. Also avoid raising batch\nlimits; there is no demand to fill them. If traffic later grows and the pool fills, the answer changes."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does weight quantization speed up single-request decode even though the GPU was not compute-limited?","options":["Smaller numbers are faster to multiply on tensor cores","Decode at batch 1 is bound by bytes read per step, and quantization halves or quarters those bytes","Quantized models have fewer layers to run","It reduces the number of tokens that must be generated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At batch 1 the step time is the time to stream the weights through memory. Fewer bytes per weight means less traffic and a lower floor. Multiply speed is not the limit, and quantization changes neither depth nor output length."},{"q":"When does speculative decoding stop paying off?","options":["When the draft model is smaller than the target model","When prompts are longer than 1,000 tokens","When the batch is large enough that the GPU is already compute-busy, so verification has no slack to use","When the KV cache is quantized"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Speculation exploits idle compute during bandwidth-bound decode. Once batching has made the step compute-bound, verifying drafted tokens competes with real work, and throughput falls. The draft being smaller is the normal setup, not a failure condition."},{"q":"Which measurement tells you whether to prioritize KV-cache reductions over weight reductions?","options":["Average prompt length in characters","Whether the cache pool is near full and cache bytes read per step rival weight bytes","The GPU's peak FLOPS rating","Tokenizer vocabulary size"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cache levers (quantized cache, GQA models, prefix caching) matter when concurrency and context make the cache the dominant memory traffic or the thing that caps concurrency. If the pool is mostly empty and batches are small, weight bytes dominate and weight quantization is the lever."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Agrawal et al., 2023 — SARATHI: Efficient LLM Inference by Piggybacking Decodes with Chunked Prefills","Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized LLM Serving"],"why":"- **The split falls out of autoregression, not design.** The prompt is known in full, so all its tokens go\n  through the model together as one big matmul per layer. Generated tokens are not known in advance, so\n  each depends on the previous one and is produced sequentially. Same weights, radically different shapes\n  of work.\n- **GPU efficiency depends on that shape.** A matmul over 2,000 prompt tokens reads each weight once and\n  uses it 2,000 times, saturating the arithmetic units. A decode step over one token uses each weight once,\n  so the GPU waits on memory.\n- **So prefill is cheap per token.** Prefill of a 4,000-token prompt takes about as long as a dozen or so\n  decode steps, not 4,000 of them (illustrative: ~70 ms of compute-bound prefill against ~5.5 ms per\n  bandwidth-bound step). A faster GPU helps prefill a lot and decode barely at all.\n- **\"Cost is proportional to tokens\" is wrong.** It predicts a long prompt with a short answer costs the\n  same as a short prompt with a long answer; the second occupies the GPU far longer. It also predicts\n  batching helps uniformly, when batching rescues decode and does almost nothing for prefill.\n- **Every serving metric belongs to one phase.** TTFT, TPOT and throughput are each a statement about\n  prefill or decode, and you cannot tune what you have not separated. An agent's per-step latency is\n  mostly TTFT, because its outputs are short.","how":"### The two phases side by side\n| | Prefill | Decode |\n|---|---|---|\n| Input per request | *P* prompt embeddings, `[P, d_model]` | one new token, `[1, d_model]` |\n| Work per layer | dense matmuls over all *P* rows, causal attention among *P* positions | same matmuls over one row, one query against every cached K and V |\n| Cache effect | writes K and V for every position | appends one K and one V |\n| Arithmetic intensity | roughly *P* FLOPs per weight byte | a handful of FLOPs per byte |\n| Bound | compute (for prompts over a few hundred tokens) | memory bandwidth |\n| Sets | TTFT; the last position's logits give the first token | TPOT |\n| Batching helps? | barely | it is the throughput lever |\n\n### Prefill\n- **Cost** scales with *P* for the matmuls and with *P²* for attention, mitigated by fused attention\n  kernels that avoid materializing the full matrix.\n- **Compute-bound,** so TTFT grows roughly linearly with prompt length and shrinks with more FLOPs.\n- **In an agent loop** the prompt at step *n* is everything from steps 1 to *n−1*, so without prefix\n  reuse each step's TTFT is larger than the last, even when the new tool result is tiny.\n\n### Decode\n- **Per step** the GPU reads all weights plus the request's entire KV cache:\n\n```\nstep_time ≈ (weight_bytes + cache_bytes) / bandwidth\n```\n\n- **TPOT is almost independent of prompt length,** except through the cache-read term.\n- **Batching *B* requests** reads the weights once for *B* tokens, which is why continuous batching is the\n  throughput lever for decode and irrelevant for prefill.\n- **The amortization is partial.** Every request's KV cache is its own and is read separately, so the cache\n  term grows linearly with *B* and context length. At large batches of long contexts it, not the weights,\n  sets step time, which is why decode at scale is limited by cache bytes (GQA, cache quantization) as much\n  as by weight bytes.\n\n### Why they interfere\n- **Same GPU, same iteration.** Admit a 10k-token prompt into a batch of 50 decoding requests and that\n  iteration takes as long as the prefill. All 50 users see a pause: a TPOT spike caused by someone else's\n  TTFT.\n- **The reverse also hurts.** Prioritizing decode makes new arrivals wait, and TTFT climbs.\n- **So the two phases trade against each other inside every scheduling decision.**\n\n### Techniques that manage the collision\n| Technique | How | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Chunked prefill** | Split long prompts into fixed-size chunks (a token budget per iteration) run alongside the decode batch. Decode tokens ride along at near-zero marginal cost because the iteration was compute-bound anyway, and no single iteration becomes a stall | Slightly longer TTFT for the long prompt |\n| **Prefill vs decode priority** | Scheduler policy: prefill-first minimizes TTFT, decode-first smooths TPOT | Depends on the product |\n| **Disaggregated serving** | Prefill on one GPU pool, decode on another, KV cache transferred over the interconnect. Each pool is tuned for its phase (prefill wants FLOPs, decode wants bandwidth and memory) and one phase cannot spike the other | The transfer and a more complex system; pays at scale |\n| **Prefix caching** | Reuse an already-computed KV prefix so part of prefill is skipped | The biggest TTFT lever when prompts share a long system prompt, and for every step of an append-only agent transcript |\n| **Speculative decoding** | Turns several decode steps into one verification pass | Uses the slack compute bandwidth-bound decode leaves idle |\n\n### What the metrics mean\n- **TTFT** ≈ queue wait + prefill time (+ a cache transfer in disaggregated setups).\n- **TPOT** ≈ decode step time, which rises with batch size and with the total cache the batch reads.\n- **Throughput** in tokens/sec is dominated by decode batching. Prefill throughput is a separate number,\n  and it is what a long-document, short-answer workload (and most agent traffic) actually consumes.\n- **Agent step latency** ≈ TTFT + output tokens × TPOT + tool time. With 50 to 200 output tokens per tool\n  call, the TTFT term is often the one to attack.","example":"### A phase cost calculator\nRounded, illustrative figures for one 80 GB GPU serving an 8B GQA model in fp16 (weights ~16 GB, cache\n~128 KB per token) at ~3 TB/s and ~900 TFLOPs. Pure Python; it reproduces the scenario below.\n\n```python\nBW, FLOPS = 3e12, 900e12            # illustrative: ~3 TB/s, ~900 TFLOPs\nW, KV = 16e9, 2 * 32 * 8 * 128 * 2  # 8B fp16 weights, bytes of KV per token\n\ndef prefill_s(prompt):              # compute-bound: 2 FLOPs per param per token\n    return 2 * 8e9 * prompt / FLOPS\n\ndef decode_step_s(ctx_tokens_in_batch):  # bandwidth-bound: weights once + every cache\n    return (W + ctx_tokens_in_batch * KV) / BW\n\nfor name, p, o in [(\"A\", 4000, 50), (\"B\", 200, 1000)]:\n    dec = sum(decode_step_s(p + i) for i in range(o))\n    print(f\"{name}: prefill {prefill_s(p)*1e3:5.1f} ms, decode {dec:5.2f} s, \"\n          f\"{dec/o*1e3:.1f} ms per output token\")\nprint(f\"32 B-like requests near the end: {decode_step_s(32 * 1200)*1e3:.1f} ms/step\")\n# A: prefill  71.1 ms, decode  0.28 s, 5.5 ms per output token\n# B: prefill   3.6 ms, decode  5.36 s, 5.4 ms per output token\n# 32 B-like requests near the end: 7.0 ms/step\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a long prompt and a long answer\nRequest A: 4,000-token prompt, 50-token answer. Request B: 200-token prompt, 1,000-token answer.\n\n| | Request A | Request B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prefill work | 2 × 8e9 × 4,000 = 64 TFLOPs; ~70 ms at ~900 TFLOPs | 20× less work: a few milliseconds |\n| TTFT on an idle GPU | ~100 ms (plus attention and overhead) | dominated by queueing |\n| Bytes per decode step | ~16 GB weights + ~0.5 GB cache (4,050 tokens × 128 KB) | ~16 GB + ~0.15 GB |\n| Decode step | ~5.5 ms | ~5.4 ms |\n| Decode total | 50 tokens ≈ ~280 ms | 1,000 tokens ≈ 5.4 s |\n| Total GPU time | 0.1 + 0.28 ≈ 0.4 s | 0.005 + 5.4 ≈ 5.4 s |\n\n- **B has one-twentieth the prompt tokens but occupies the GPU 13× longer,** nearly all of it streaming the\n  same 16 GB of weights over and over.\n- **Batch 32 B-like requests** and the step rises only to ~7 ms (weights read once, cache reads grow), so\n  aggregate throughput goes up ~25× while each user's TPOT rises from 5.4 to 7 ms.\n- **Admit one A-like prompt without chunked prefill:** that iteration takes ~100 ms instead of 7 ms, and all\n  32 users see a visible hitch.\n- **With chunked prefill at a 512-token budget,** A's prefill spreads over 8 iterations of ~15 ms each. A's\n  TTFT becomes ~120 ms instead of ~100 ms, and nobody else stalls.\n\n### Agent scenario: one late step of a tool-calling agent\nSame illustrative 8B setup. At step 10 the agent's transcript is 20,000 tokens and it emits a 100-token\ntool call. Only the last ~1,000 tokens (the newest tool result) are new.\n\n| | No prefix reuse | Prefix cached, 1,000 new tokens |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prefill | 2 × 8e9 × 20,000 ≈ 320 TFLOPs, ~356 ms | ~18 ms |\n| Decode step (16 GB + 20,000 × 128 KB ≈ 2.6 GB of cache) | ~6.2 ms | ~6.2 ms |\n| Decode total, 100 tokens | ~0.62 s | ~0.62 s |\n| Prefill share of the step | ~36% | ~3% |\n\n```python\nstep = dict(prompt=20_000, new=1_000, out=100)\npre_full = prefill_s(step[\"prompt\"])                 # ~0.356 s\npre_cached = prefill_s(step[\"new\"])                  # ~0.018 s\ndec = sum(decode_step_s(step[\"prompt\"] + i) for i in range(step[\"out\"]))   # ~0.62 s\nprint(f\"TTFT {pre_full*1e3:.0f} ms -> {pre_cached*1e3:.0f} ms, decode {dec:.2f} s\")\n```\n\n- **Read it as:** a single step looks decode-heavy, but prefill grows every step while decode stays flat.\n  Without reuse, a 12-step run prefills ~171,000 tokens (~3 s) for a 6k prefix plus 1.5k per step; with\n  reuse it prefills roughly the transcript once.\n- **Over a run, a stable prefix beats shorter tool calls.** Halving output tokens saves ~0.3 s on every\n  step; a stable prefix saves ~0.34 s on this step and more on every later one, because the transcript\n  it avoids re-reading keeps growing.","patterns":["**Treat agent steps as prefill-heavy traffic.** Keep the prefix stable and append-only, trim tool results before appending, and route by agent run id so the cached prefix is on the same replica. Monitor cached versus uncached input tokens per step; uncached should stay near the newest step's size.","**Chunked prefill with a deliberate budget.** For mixed workloads enable chunked prefill (`--enable-chunked-prefill` in vLLM, on by default in recent versions) and set the per-iteration token budget with `--max-num-batched-tokens`: smaller favours TPOT, larger favours TTFT and prefill throughput. Monitor TPOT p99 when long prompts arrive.","**Separate histograms per phase.** Export TTFT and per-token latency as separate histograms (engines such as vLLM publish both on `/metrics`) and set an SLO on each. Monitor them against prompt-length and output-length percentiles, not a blended \"latency\".","**Split pools by workload shape.** Put long-document, short-answer traffic and chat on separate deployments with different scheduler settings, routed by endpoint. Monitor each pool's utilization; move to full prefill/decode disaggregation (supported in frameworks such as SGLang and NVIDIA Dynamo) only when interference is measurable at scale.","**Meter input and output tokens separately.** Bill and budget per phase, with output priced well above input, as public API price lists do. Agents consume far more input than output tokens, so input volume often dominates their bill despite the lower unit price. Monitor GPU-seconds per input token and per output token per tenant.","**Reproduce stutter on purpose.** In load tests, inject a long prompt into a loaded replica and confirm TPOT of the other streams stays within budget. Monitor the per-iteration step time during the injection."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents are prefill-heavy: a step is usually a long prompt (system\n  prompt, tool schemas, transcript) and a short output (a tool call). Prefill grows every step unless the\n  prefix is reused, while decode per step stays roughly flat.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider usage fields that report input, cached-input and output\n  tokens separately and price them differently; TTFT and TPOT histograms and `--enable-chunked-prefill`\n  on self-hosted engines such as vLLM.\n- **What you control.** Prompt length per step (trim tool output, compact history), prefix stability so\n  caching skips prefill, per-step `max_tokens`, and whether intermediate steps stream at all.\n- **What you can skip.** Disaggregated serving internals and KV transfer protocols unless you run a large\n  self-hosted fleet. The intensity argument and the two cost formulas are enough.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Explaining agent step latency.** Long prompts and short tool-call outputs make prefill the main cost of\n  each step, so TTFT, not TPOT, is what grows across a run.\n- **Reading provider bills.** Input, cached-input and output tokens are priced differently because the two\n  phases cost differently; agent cost models need all three per step.\n- **Fixing stream stalls on shared replicas.** Chunked prefill keeps one agent's long prompt from freezing\n  every other user's stream.\n- **Deciding what to stream.** Intermediate tool-call steps rarely need streaming; final answers do.","mini_project":"**Per-step phase profile of an agent run (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show how prefill and decode time evolve across the steps of one agent task.\n- **Steps:** run a 15-step tool-calling agent; per step, log input tokens, cached input tokens, output\n  tokens, TTFT and total generation time; repeat with tool results trimmed to a fixed size.\n- **Done when:** you have a chart of TTFT and decode time per step for both runs, and a sentence explaining\n  which phase grew and why trimming changed it."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"GPU cost is proportional to total tokens.\"** An input token and an output token cost very different GPU time. A 200-token prompt with a 1,000-token answer occupies the GPU about 13x longer than a 4,000-token prompt with a 50-token answer, because every output token re-streams the weights.","**\"Prefill is cheap, so long agent prompts do not matter.\"** Per token it is cheap, but an agent re-sends its growing transcript every step. At 20,000 tokens an uncached step prefills for ~356 ms against ~18 ms with the prefix cached, and that cost grows every step while decode stays flat.","**\"A faster GPU speeds up both phases.\"** Prefill is compute-bound and gains from more FLOPs; decode is bandwidth-bound and barely moves unless memory bandwidth grows too.","**\"Batching helps prefill and decode alike.\"** A prefill is already a large matmul over *P* rows, so batching adds little. Decode reads the weights once for all *B* requests, which is where batching earns its ~25x throughput gain at batch 32.","**\"Chunked prefill makes prefill faster.\"** It makes the long prompt's own TTFT slightly worse (~120 ms instead of ~100 ms in the example). What it buys is that no iteration stalls the other users' decode."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Request arrives\"] --> Q[\"Queue wait\"]\n    Q --> P[\"Prefill: all P tokens, compute-bound\"]\n    P --> KV[(\"KV cache written\")]\n    P -->|\"first token, ends TTFT\"| D[\"Decode step: 1 token, bandwidth-bound\"]\n    KV --> D\n    D -->|\"append k, v\"| KV\n    D -->|\"not done, each gap is TPOT\"| D\n    D -->|\"EOS or max_tokens\"| E[\"Tool call done, free cache\"]\n    L[\"Long prompt from another agent\"] -.->|\"unchunked, stalls the step\"| D\n    L -.->|\"chunked, rides along\"| C[\"Chunk plus decode batch\"]","caption":"One agent step's path: prefill fills the cache once, then decode loops until the tool call is complete,\nand an unchunked long prompt from another agent can stall that loop."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is prefill compute-bound and decode bandwidth-bound if they run the same weights?","a":"- **Arithmetic intensity differs.** Prefill multiplies each weight by all *P* prompt rows, roughly *P* FLOPs\n  per weight byte, enough to saturate tensor cores.\n- **Decode has one row per request.** Each step reads all weights and the cache for a handful of FLOPs per\n  byte, so memory bandwidth sets the step time."},{"q":"Every few minutes all chat users see their stream freeze for a fraction of a second. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Correlate:** check whether freezes line up with long-prompt arrivals rather than request rate, using\n  per-iteration step time and the prompt-length log.\n- **Mechanism:** an unchunked long prefill makes one iteration as long as the prefill, so every decoding\n  user's TPOT spikes.\n- **Fix:** enable chunked prefill with a token budget, or route long-document traffic to its own pool."},{"q":"Your agent's per-step latency grows from 0.7 s at step 1 to 2 s at step 15, though tool results are small. Why?","a":"- **Prefill is growing.** Each step's prompt contains the whole transcript, so without prefix reuse TTFT\n  rises roughly linearly with step number while decode time per step stays flat.\n- **Check** cached versus uncached input tokens per step and whether steps land on the same replica.\n- **Fix:** stable byte-identical prefix, append-only history, session-affine routing, trimmed tool output,\n  and compaction at a boundary when the transcript gets long."},{"q":"When would you not disaggregate prefill and decode?","a":"- **Below the scale where interference is measurable.** Chunked prefill gets most of the benefit on one\n  pool without KV transfer.\n- **When the interconnect is slow or the system must stay simple,** because each request's cache must move\n  between pools and both pools need capacity planning.\n- **Do it** at large scale with mixed traffic, when each pool can be sized for its own bound."},{"q":"Roughly how much more GPU time does an output token cost than an input token?","a":"- **Input:** ~70 ms of prefill for 4,000 tokens is ≈ 0.02 ms per token on the illustrative 8B setup.\n- **Output:** a ~7 ms decode step shared by 32 users is ≈ 0.2 ms per token, about an order of magnitude\n  more.\n- **So** meter and price them separately; blended per-token pricing misprices both workloads."},{"q":"A team doubles GPU FLOPs for its agent platform. Which workloads benefit and which do not?","a":"- **Benefit:** long-prompt, short-output workloads (summarization, classification over documents, most\n  tool-calling agent steps), because prefill and TTFT are compute-bound.\n- **Barely benefit:** chat with long answers, because decode tracks memory bandwidth, which did not change\n  much. Measure TTFT and per-user tokens/sec separately to show it."}],"tradeoffs":["**Chunked prefill: smooth TPOT vs long-prompt TTFT.** It smooths TPOT for everyone and raises utilization, but lengthens TTFT for long prompts. Flips when the workload is mostly long prompts with short answers and TTFT is the metric sold.","**Disaggregation: isolation vs complexity.** Separate phases let each pool be sized for its bottleneck, but add KV transfer and operational complexity. It pays only at a scale where interference is measurable.","**Prefill-first vs decode-first.** Prefill-first minimizes TTFT at the cost of decode stalls; decode-first does the opposite. Choose by whether users wait for the first token or read the stream.","**More FLOPs vs more bandwidth.** Faster-FLOPs hardware shortens prefill substantially but barely moves decode. Worth it for document-heavy, short-output workloads, not for chat with long answers."],"pitfalls":["**Benchmarking \"latency\" as a single number.** Symptom: fine average latency, but users complain about the pause before text appears or about stuttering mid-stream. Fix: report TTFT and TPOT separately.","**Assuming cost is proportional to total tokens.** Symptom: a pricing or capacity model wildly wrong for long-output workloads, because output tokens cost far more GPU time than input tokens. Fix: model input and output tokens with separate unit costs.","**Leaving chunked prefill off on a mixed workload.** Symptom: periodic TPOT spikes across all users that coincide with the arrival of long prompts, not with request rate. Fix: enable chunked prefill with a per-iteration token budget.","**Expecting a GPU upgrade to speed up chat decode.** Symptom: TTFT improves while tokens/sec per user is unchanged, because bandwidth did not change much. Fix: compare GPUs on memory bandwidth for decode.","**Reporting prefill and decode throughput as one \"tokens/sec\".** Symptom: capacity estimates off by an order of magnitude when the input/output mix shifts. Fix: track input and output tokens/sec separately.","**Sizing an agent fleet from chat benchmarks.** Symptom: chat-shaped load tests (short prompt, long answer) look fine, then agents with 20k-token transcripts and 100-token outputs saturate prefill and TTFT climbs every step. Fix: load-test with replayed agent traces."],"when":"This split is the first thing to apply when a latency or cost number looks wrong.\n- **Attribute the metric:** TTFT problems lead to prefill (prompt length, queueing, prefix caching,\n  chunking); TPOT problems lead to decode (batch size, cache size, bandwidth, quantization).\n- **Size hardware:** summarizing long documents wants FLOPs and prefill throughput; chat with long answers\n  wants bandwidth and memory.\n- **Price and budget:** count input and output tokens separately, because they cost different resources.\n- **Agents:** when per-step latency grows over a run, look at prefill (prompt growth, cache misses) before\n  decode.\n- **Interviews:** explaining why decode is bandwidth-bound and prefill compute-bound, with the\n  arithmetic-intensity reasoning, is the baseline for any serving question.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Enables chunked prefill and sets the per-iteration token budget, reads TTFT and TPOT as separate histograms, and attributes spikes to the phase that caused them.","architect":"Chooses GPU types and whether to disaggregate the phases based on the workload's input/output mix, and sets latency targets per phase rather than one blended number.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their long-document pipeline and their chat assistant need different tuning, and reproduces their stutter by injecting a long prompt into a loaded replica."},"check":{"q":"A document-summarization service has 15k-token prompts and 300-token outputs. A chat assistant has\n300-token prompts and 1,500-token outputs. Both run on identical GPUs. Which hardware upgrade would help\neach, which scheduling settings differ, and how should the two be billed relative to each other?","a":"Summarization is prefill-dominated: 15k tokens of compute-bound matmul per request (for an 8B\nmodel, 2 × 8e9 × 15,000 ≈ 240 TFLOPs, a few hundred milliseconds on one GPU), so TTFT is the pain and more\nFLOPs per GPU would help directly; prefix caching helps only if documents share prefixes, which they\nusually do not. Chat is decode-dominated: 1,500 bandwidth-bound steps per request, so more\nbandwidth and more memory for larger batches help, and FLOPs do not. Scheduling: summarization benefits\nfrom chunked prefill so that its long prompts do not stall other users, and from a higher per-iteration\ntoken budget; chat wants a large `max_num_seqs`, decode-friendly scheduling and possibly speculative\ndecoding. Billing: compare cost per token, not per request. With the illustrative figures from\nthe example, a prefill token costs ~70 ms / 4,000 ≈ 0.02 ms of GPU time, while a decode token costs a share\nof a ~7 ms step, ≈ 0.2 ms even when 32 users share each step: roughly an order of magnitude more. So\ninput and output tokens must be metered and priced separately, with output well above input, which is\nthe shape of public API price lists. A blended per-token price would undercharge the chat product and\novercharge the summarizer."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the decode phase memory-bandwidth-bound while prefill is compute-bound?","options":["Decode uses a different, slower set of kernels than prefill","Decode reads all weights and the cache to produce one token per request, while prefill reuses each weight across every prompt token","Prefill does not need to write to the KV cache","Decode runs on the CPU in most engines"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Arithmetic intensity is FLOPs per byte moved. Prefill performs thousands of operations per weight byte; decode performs one or a few, so its time is set by how fast memory can be streamed. Same kernels, same device, different shape of work."},{"q":"What is the primary purpose of chunked prefill?","options":["To reduce the total compute needed for long prompts","To allow prompts longer than the context window","To interleave pieces of a long prompt with decode steps so one prefill does not stall every running request","To compress the KV cache written during prefill"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chunking does not reduce work; it spreads it across iterations so decode batches keep flowing. The prefill's own TTFT gets slightly longer, in exchange for smooth TPOT for everyone else."},{"q":"A team upgrades to a GPU with twice the FLOPs and similar memory bandwidth. Which metric improves most?","options":["Time per output token for a single chat request","Time to first token on long prompts","Maximum number of concurrent requests","KV-cache memory per token"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefill is compute-bound, so doubling FLOPs roughly halves its time and TTFT drops. Decode is bandwidth-bound and gains little; concurrency is set by memory capacity; cache size per token is a model property."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **The cache is the invisible cost.** A team that budgets 16 GB for an 8B fp16 model and calls the\n  remaining 64 GB \"headroom\" has not budgeted at all, because every running request pins a cache that\n  grows with its context, and the engine refuses or preempts requests once that pool is exhausted.\n- **The failure is silent until load arrives.** Nothing crashes at low traffic. Under real concurrency the\n  server OOMs, stalls admissions, or evicts half-finished generations and recomputes them, which shows up\n  as latency collapse.\n- **\"Try it and see\" fails.** The answer depends on what you do not observe until production: the\n  distribution of prompt lengths, output lengths and concurrency, and how they move together. Synthetic\n  500-token load tests say nothing about the day a customer pastes a 40-page contract, or the day an\n  agent run takes 30 steps instead of 8.\n- **\"Pick the biggest GPU\" fails** on cost, and often still does not fit: a 70B model's cache at 128k\n  context on a modest batch exceeds any single card.\n- **What the estimate buys:** reason forward from a config file and a traffic model to a GPU count before\n  buying anything, and backward from an OOM to the parameter that caused it.","how":"### Bytes per token\nEach layer stores one key vector and one value vector per KV head per token:\n\n```\nbytes_per_token = 2 × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element\n```\n\n| Term | Meaning | Config field |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2 | K and V | — |\n| `n_layers` | transformer layers | `num_hidden_layers` |\n| `n_kv_heads` | key/value heads, **not** attention heads: equal under multi-head attention, fewer under GQA (8 is common), 1 under multi-query attention | `num_key_value_heads` |\n| `head_dim` | usually `hidden_size / n_attention_heads` | `hidden_size`, `num_attention_heads` |\n| `bytes_per_element` | 2 for fp16/bf16, 1 for fp8 or int8 cache | engine setting |\n\n### Two architectures that break the formula\n- **Multi-head latent attention** (DeepSeek-V2/V3 and derivatives) replaces per-head K and V with one\n  compressed latent vector per layer per token plus a small decoupled RoPE key:\n\n```\nbytes_per_token = 1 × layers × (latent_dim + rope_dim) × bytes\nDeepSeek-V3: 61 × (512 + 64) × 2 ≈ 70 KB in bf16   (an equivalent MHA layout: several MB)\n```\n\n  Read the architecture before applying the standard formula.\n- **Sliding-window attention.** A layer attending only to the last *W* positions needs at most *W* tokens of\n  cache however long the sequence grows. Several recent model families interleave windowed and\n  full-attention layers, so the estimate becomes a sum: full layers scale with sequence length, windowed\n  layers cap at *W*, and average bytes per token falls past the window.\n- **Caveat:** whether an engine actually frees out-of-window blocks depends on engine and version, so\n  confirm with its startup log.\n\n### From per-token to per-GPU\n```\ncache_per_request = bytes_per_token × (prompt_tokens + max_output_tokens)\npool_bytes        = gpu_memory × utilization_fraction − weight_bytes − activation_workspace\nmax_concurrency   ≈ pool_bytes / cache_per_request\n```\n\nFour refinements matter in practice, plus one constraint on the fourth:\n\n1. **Weights.** Parameters × bytes per parameter: 8B × 2 = 16 GB in fp16, 8 GB at int8, ~4.5 GB at 4-bit\n   (plus a little for scales). Quantizing weights frees cache space, often its biggest payoff.\n2. **Activation and workspace.** Engines profile a worst-case forward pass at startup (the largest prefill\n   batch they allow) and reserve that memory before sizing the pool. CUDA graphs, sampling buffers and\n   allocator overhead take a few GB more. vLLM's knob is `gpu_memory_utilization` (default 0.9), and it\n   prints the cache blocks it allocated; that number times block size is the real pool in tokens.\n3. **Sequence length is a distribution.** Use p95 or p99 of (prompt + output) to decide what fits without\n   preemption, and the mean for steady-state concurrency. Paged allocation means a request only consumes\n   what it has generated so far, so admission can be optimistic about the mean while max context bounds\n   the worst case.\n4. **Tensor parallelism splits the cache, up to a point.** At degree *T* each GPU holds the K/V of\n   `n_kv_heads / T` heads, so per-GPU cache per token divides by *T* along with weights, and the pool is the\n   sum over GPUs. Once *T* exceeds `n_kv_heads` (say *T* = 16 with 8 KV heads) heads are replicated and\n   per-GPU cache stops shrinking.\n5. **TP degree must divide the head counts,** which is why deployments use TP 2, 4 or 8, not 3 or 6.\n\n### Turning an agent run into a sequence length\nAn agent's \"sequence\" is its transcript at the current step, so estimate it from the loop's shape:\n\n```\ntranscript_tokens(step n) = prefix_tokens + (n − 1) × tokens_appended_per_step + output_tokens\n```\n\n- **Prefix** is the system prompt plus every tool schema; dozens of tools can add thousands of tokens.\n- **Appended per step** is the tool call plus its result; one verbose tool result can dominate it.\n- **Use the step-count distribution:** the p50 step count gives steady-state demand, the p95 or the step\n  cap gives the tail you must fit or preempt. Averaged over a run, each agent holds roughly the cache of\n  its midpoint step.\n- **Idle time between steps matters.** While a tool runs the request has finished, so its blocks return\n  to the pool. With prefix caching they stay as evictable cached blocks: they still count as free, so\n  other requests can reclaim them, and a busy pool or a slow tool can evict the prefix before the next\n  step arrives.\n\n### Running the numbers for common shapes\nIllustrative, computed from public config values:\n\n| Shape (fp16) | Bytes per token | 8k context | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 32 layers, 32 KV heads, head_dim 128 (Llama-2-7B-class MHA) | 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 = 524,288 B ≈ 512 KB | ≈ 4 GB per request | |\n| 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128 (Llama-3-8B-class GQA) | 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 B = 128 KB | ≈ 1 GB per request | Same size class, 4× less cache: this is why GQA exists |\n| 80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128 (70B-class GQA) | 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 B = 320 KB | ≈ 2.5 GB per request | 128k context ≈ 40 GB for one request |\n\n### Reading it backwards\n1. **Engine reports N cache blocks of 16 tokens:** the pool holds 16N tokens.\n2. **Divide by p50 sequence length** for expected concurrency, and by `max_model_len` for guaranteed\n   concurrency.\n3. **A large gap between the two** means the scheduler will run many requests at once and preempt when the\n   tail arrives. That is a policy choice, not an error, but make it consciously.","example":"### A capacity helper for any Hugging Face config\nAnswers \"how many fit\" before touching a GPU.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\ndef kv_bytes_per_token(cfg, cache_bytes=2):\n    n_heads = cfg[\"num_attention_heads\"]\n    n_kv = cfg.get(\"num_key_value_heads\", n_heads)        # GQA/MQA; defaults to MHA\n    head_dim = cfg.get(\"head_dim\") or cfg[\"hidden_size\"] // n_heads\n    return 2 * cfg[\"num_hidden_layers\"] * n_kv * head_dim * cache_bytes\n\ndef capacity(cfg, params_b, gpu_gb=80, util=0.90, weight_bytes=2, workspace_gb=4,\n             p50_seq=3000, max_seq=8192, cache_bytes=2):\n    per_tok = kv_bytes_per_token(cfg, cache_bytes)\n    weights = params_b * 1e9 * weight_bytes\n    pool = gpu_gb * 1e9 * util - weights - workspace_gb * 1e9\n    return {\n        \"kv_kb_per_token\": per_tok / 1024,\n        \"pool_gb\": pool / 1e9,\n        \"pool_tokens\": int(pool // per_tok),\n        \"concurrency_at_p50\": int(pool // (per_tok * p50_seq)),\n        \"concurrency_at_max\": int(pool // (per_tok * max_seq)),\n    }\n\nllama3_8b_like = {\"num_hidden_layers\": 32, \"num_attention_heads\": 32,\n                  \"num_key_value_heads\": 8, \"hidden_size\": 4096}\nprint(json.dumps(capacity(llama3_8b_like, params_b=8), indent=1))\n# -> ~128 KB/token, pool ≈ 52 GB ≈ 400k tokens, ~132 requests at a 3k p50, ~48 at an 8k max.\nprint(capacity(llama3_8b_like, 8, cache_bytes=1)[\"concurrency_at_p50\"])   # fp8 cache: ~2x\nprint(capacity(llama3_8b_like, 8, weight_bytes=0.5)[\"pool_gb\"])           # 4-bit weights: +12 GB pool\n```\n\n- **The last two lines are the two cheapest levers** when the answer is \"not enough\": quantize the cache to\n  double token capacity (264 requests at the p50), or quantize the weights to enlarge the pool (64 GB).\n- **Compare the printed `pool_tokens`** with the block count the engine logs at startup. If they disagree\n  by more than ~10%, the workspace estimate is wrong for that engine and model; trust the log.\n\n### Agent scenario: how many research agents fit on that GPU?\nSame 8B GQA model and ~52 GB pool (~396,700 tokens). Each agent has a 6k-token prefix and appends ~1.5k\ntokens per step; runs take 10 steps at p50 and 25 at p95.\n\n| Quantity | Transcript tokens | Agents in flight (fp16 cache) | With fp8 cache |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Midpoint of a p50 run (averaged over its life) | (6,000 + 19,500) / 2 = 12,750 | ~31 | ~62 |\n| End of a p50 run, step 10 | 6,000 + 9 × 1,500 = 19,500 | ~20 | ~40 |\n| End of a p95 run, step 25 | 6,000 + 24 × 1,500 = 42,000 | ~9 | ~18 |\n\n```python\ndef transcript(step, prefix=6000, per_step=1500):   # current step's output (~100s of tokens) omitted\n    return prefix + (step - 1) * per_step\n\nfor label, seq in ((\"mid p50\", (6000 + transcript(10)) // 2), (\"p50 end\", transcript(10)),\n                   (\"p95 end\", transcript(25))):\n    print(label, seq, capacity(llama3_8b_like, 8, p50_seq=seq)[\"concurrency_at_p50\"],\n          capacity(llama3_8b_like, 8, p50_seq=seq, cache_bytes=1)[\"concurrency_at_p50\"])\n# mid p50 12750 31 62 / p50 end 19500 20 40 / p95 end 42000 9 18\n```\n\n- **Read it as:** the same GPU that holds ~132 chat requests at a 3k p50 holds about 20 agents near the\n  end of a normal run, and `max_model_len` must be at least 42,000 (and within the model's window) for the\n  tail to run at all.\n- **Levers specific to agents:** trim tool results (cuts the 1.5k), lazy-load tool schemas (cuts the\n  prefix), compact history at a step threshold, and cap steps per run.\n\n### Worked scenario: how far tensor parallelism shrinks a 70B cache\nThe 70B-class GQA shape above (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, fp16), per-GPU cache per token as TP\ngrows:\n\n| TP degree *T* | KV heads per GPU | Per-GPU bytes per token | 12k-token session per GPU |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 8 | 320 KB | ~3.9 GB |\n| 2 | 4 | 160 KB | ~2 GB |\n| 4 | 2 | 80 KB | ~1 GB |\n| 8 | 1 | 40 KB | ~0.5 GB |\n| 16 | 1 (replicated) | 40 KB | ~0.5 GB, no further saving |\n\n- **Read it as:** past *T* = 8 you add GPUs for weights and bandwidth, not for cache per token.\n\n### Worked scenario: a model with interleaved sliding-window layers\nA hypothetical shape for illustration: 32 layers, of which 8 are full attention and 24 use a 4,096-token\nwindow, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, fp16 (2 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 4 KB per layer per token, 128 KB across all 32\nlayers).\n\n| Sequence length | Cache per request | Average bytes per token |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4,096 | 0.5 GiB | 128 KB (same as all-full) |\n| 32,768 | ~1.4 GiB | 44 KB |\n| 131,072 | ~4.4 GiB | 35 KB |\n\n- **The naive formula** would say 16 GiB at 131,072 tokens, about 3.7× too high, but only if the engine\n  really frees out-of-window blocks.","patterns":["**Keep a bytes-per-token table for every served model.** Compute it from `config.json` in CI and store it next to the deployment config, so capacity questions take seconds. Recompute on every model change.","**Gate deploys on the engine's own number.** After startup, parse the logged cache size (blocks, or tokens and maximum concurrency at `max_model_len` in recent vLLM versions) and fail the rollout if it is more than ~10% below plan. Monitor it per model and GPU type.","**Set flags from the distribution.** Derive `max_model_len` from the p99 of prompt + output and `max_num_seqs` from pool tokens divided by the mean, then load-test the tail. Monitor KV-cache utilization and preemption counts per replica.","**Quantize the cache before adding GPUs.** Try `--kv-cache-dtype fp8` (vLLM) with a quality eval; it roughly doubles token capacity. Monitor eval scores on long-range recall tasks specifically.","**Size agent fleets from late-step transcripts.** Log prompt tokens per agent step, take the p50 and p95 at the final step, and size `max_num_seqs` and replica count from those, not from step 1. Monitor prompt tokens by step number and preemptions per replica.","**Cap and compact agent context by policy.** Set a maximum step count and a compaction threshold (say, summarize history when the transcript passes a fixed fraction of `max_model_len`) so the tail is bounded by design. Monitor how often runs hit the cap or compact.","**Review context-limit requests against the model.** Treat \"raise the context window\" as a capacity change: rerun the estimate at the new p99 and the new guaranteed concurrency before approving. Monitor context-length p95 per route after the change."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent transcripts grow every step, so a fleet's cache demand rises over\n  each run. Capacity sized from step 1 (or from chat traffic) collapses into preemption storms once runs\n  reach step 15 or 20.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `max_model_len`, `max_num_seqs`, `gpu_memory_utilization` and\n  `--kv-cache-dtype fp8` on a self-hosted engine such as vLLM; the model's `config.json` fields; the\n  engine's startup log of cache blocks. With a hosted API the provider does this math for you.\n- **What you control.** Prefix size (tool schemas count), tokens appended per step, maximum steps per run,\n  when you compact history, and how many agent runs you allow in flight per replica.\n- **What you can skip.** Exact allocator and CUDA-graph overheads; trust the startup log for those. Learn\n  the formula, the pool arithmetic and how to turn a step count into a transcript length.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Sizing replicas for an agent fleet.** Bytes per token times the transcript length at the last step\n  gives the cache each run needs, and therefore how many runs a GPU holds.\n- **Choosing a model for long-running agents.** Two models of similar quality can differ several-fold in\n  KV heads, and so in concurrent runs per GPU.\n- **Setting step and compaction limits.** Maximum steps per run and when to compact history follow from\n  the cache budget per run.\n- **Explaining preemption storms late in agent runs.** Capacity sized from step 1 fails once transcripts\n  reach step 15 or 20.","mini_project":"**Agent capacity calculator (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** predict how many concurrent agent runs one replica holds, then check it against the engine.\n- **Steps:** write a script that reads a model's `config.json`, computes bytes per token, and turns prefix\n  size, tokens per step and maximum steps into a final transcript length; compute runs per GPU for two\n  models and an fp16 versus fp8 cache; then start vLLM on a GPU and compare with the KV-cache size in\n  its startup log.\n- **Done when:** your estimate and the engine's log agree within your stated workspace margin, and you have\n  a table of runs per GPU for each model and cache precision."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Weights are the memory budget; the rest of the card is headroom.\"** The rest is the cache pool, and every running request pins a slice of it that grows with its context. On an 80 GB card an 8B fp16 model leaves ~52 GB after workspace, about 400k tokens, not unlimited concurrency.","**\"More attention heads means a bigger cache.\"** Only KV heads count. Two 7-8B-class models with 32 attention heads differ 4x in cache (512 KB vs 128 KB per token) because one has 32 KV heads and the other 8.","**\"Every request reserves `max_model_len` of cache.\"** With paged allocation a request holds only what it has generated so far. `max_model_len` bounds the guaranteed concurrency; the p50 length sets the expected one, and the gap between them is where preemption lives.","**\"Size an agent fleet from chat traffic or step 1.\"** Transcripts grow every step. The GPU that holds ~132 requests at a 3k p50 holds about 20 agents at the end of a 10-step run and about 9 at a 25-step p95 run.","**\"Adding tensor parallelism always shrinks per-GPU cache.\"** It divides KV heads across GPUs only until *T* reaches `n_kv_heads`. For the 70B-class shape with 8 KV heads, per-GPU cache bottoms out at 40 KB per token at TP 8; TP 16 replicates heads and saves nothing more.","**\"One formula fits every model.\"** MLA models store one compressed latent per layer (~70 KB per token for DeepSeek-V3), and sliding-window layers cap at *W* tokens. Read the architecture and trust the engine's startup log."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    CFG[\"Model config.json\"] --> BPT[\"Bytes per token: 2 x L x KV heads x dim x bytes\"]\n    GPU[\"GPU memory x utilization\"] --> POOL[\"Cache pool bytes\"]\n    W[\"Weights: params x bytes\"] -->|\"subtract\"| POOL\n    WS[\"Activation workspace, CUDA graphs\"] -->|\"subtract\"| POOL\n    BPT --> TOK[\"Pool in tokens\"]\n    POOL --> TOK\n    RUN[\"Agent prefix plus steps x tokens per step\"] -->|\"p50 and p95 transcript\"| TOK\n    TOK -->|\"divide by p50 length\"| EXP[\"Expected concurrency\"]\n    TOK -->|\"divide by max_model_len\"| GUA[\"Guaranteed concurrency\"]\n    TOK -.->|\"compare\"| LOG[\"Engine startup log block count\"]","caption":"Work from the config and the agent loop's shape down to a concurrency number, then check it against what\nthe engine actually allocated."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through estimating how many concurrent requests an 8B GQA model serves on one 80 GB GPU.","a":"- **Bytes per token:** 2 × 32 layers × 8 KV heads × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KB.\n- **Pool:** 0.9 × 80 GB minus 16 GB of weights minus ~4 GB of workspace ≈ 52 GB, about 400k tokens.\n- **Concurrency:** ~132 at a 3k p50, ~48 guaranteed at an 8k max; then confirm against the engine's\n  startup log."},{"q":"Why does the KV-head count matter more than the attention-head count?","a":"- **Only KV heads are cached.** Under GQA several query heads share one K/V head, so a 32-query-head model\n  with 8 KV heads stores a quarter of the cache of its MHA twin.\n- **Getting it wrong** makes estimates four or eight times too pessimistic and leads to buying GPUs you do\n  not need."},{"q":"Your agent platform shows preemption storms at only 60% average cache utilization. What is going on?","a":"- **Averages hide the tail.** Admission was sized on mean length, and bursts of long-context requests\n  (several agent runs reaching late steps together) arrive together and exhaust the pool.\n- **Check** p95/p99 of prompt + output and the preemption counter over time, not the mean.\n- **Fix:** cap context per route, lower the concurrency cap, quantize the cache, or split long-context\n  traffic onto its own replicas."},{"q":"How many concurrent agent runs can one replica hold, and how do you estimate it?","a":"- **Model the transcript:** prefix (system prompt plus tool schemas) plus steps × tokens appended per\n  step, at the p50 and p95 step counts. With a 6k prefix and 1.5k per step, a 10-step run ends near 19.5k\n  tokens.\n- **Divide the pool:** ~396,700 tokens on the 8B example gives ~20 runs at their final step and ~9 at a\n  25-step tail; an fp8 cache roughly doubles both.\n- **Then bound the tail** with a step cap and history compaction rather than buying for the worst run."},{"q":"When would you not trust the standard formula, and when does adding tensor parallelism stop helping?","a":"- **Latent attention** caches one compressed vector per layer (DeepSeek-V3 is ~70 KB per token in bf16), so\n  the MHA formula is far too high.\n- **Sliding-window layers** cap at *W* tokens, so the cost per token falls with length, but only if the\n  engine frees out-of-window blocks. In both cases read the architecture and the startup log.\n- **TP stops shrinking the cache when *T* exceeds the KV-head count.** With 8 KV heads, TP 16 replicates\n  heads, so per-GPU cache per token stays at the TP 8 value; *T* must divide the head counts, so 3 or 6\n  GPUs are not valid degrees; real steps are 2, 4, 8."}],"tradeoffs":["**Size to the max vs size to the median.** Sizing to the maximum context guarantees no preemption but leaves most of the pool idle on typical traffic; sizing to the median packs more users but accepts preemption on the tail.","**8-bit cache vs recall.** It doubles token capacity at a small, task-dependent quality cost. The trade flips against it for workloads where exact long-range recall is the product.","**GQA model vs model choice.** A GQA model shrinks the cache several-fold and raises concurrency, but constrains model choice; a stronger MHA model may still win when quality per request matters more than requests per GPU.","**Rich agent context vs agents per GPU.** Keeping every tool result verbatim helps the agent recall details but grows each transcript; compaction and trimming fit two or three times more runs. Flips when task success depends on details from early steps.","**`gpu_memory_utilization` toward 1.0 vs headroom.** It enlarges the pool but removes headroom for activation spikes and other processes. Dangerous when the GPU is shared or prompts are bursty."],"pitfalls":["**Using the attention-head count instead of the KV-head count.** Symptom: a capacity estimate four or eight times too pessimistic for GQA models, and a purchase of GPUs you did not need. Fix: read `num_key_value_heads` from the config.","**Forgetting that output tokens also need cache.** Symptom: OOM or preemption on long generations even though every prompt fit comfortably in the estimate. Fix: size on prompt plus `max_tokens`.","**Trusting the formula over the engine's startup log.** Symptom: a pool 10–20% smaller than planned, because activation workspace and allocator overhead were not budgeted. Fix: plan from the logged block count.","**Estimating with mean sequence length and promising that concurrency.** Symptom: latency collapse the first time a batch of long-context requests arrives together. Fix: promise concurrency at p95/p99 length.","**Sizing an agent fleet from its first step.** Symptom: fine at launch, then preemptions and TTFT spikes as runs reach later steps, worst when many runs are long at once. Fix: size on the transcript at the p95 step, and cap steps or compact history.","**The standard formula on latent or sliding-window attention.** Symptom: an estimate off by a large factor in either direction, because the cache layout is different. Fix: read the architecture and use its own cache formula."],"when":"- **Do the arithmetic at four moments:** choosing a model and GPU before any code exists; setting\n  `max_model_len`, `max_num_seqs` and utilization flags; an OOM or preemption storm in production; and a\n  request for a longer context window or a bigger model on the same hardware.\n- **For agents, also redo it** when you add tools (bigger prefix), raise the step cap, or change how much\n  tool output is kept.\n- **Keep the per-token number** for every model you serve in your head or a table; it answers most capacity\n  questions in seconds.\n- **Measure** actual cache utilization and preemption counts in production to validate the estimate\n  against real traffic.\n- **Interviews:** deriving bytes per token from a config, then a concurrency number with weights\n  subtracted, is the canonical serving question; the follow-up is always \"what would you change if it does\n  not fit\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes the pool before deployment, sets context and concurrency flags from it, and checks the engine's logged block count against the estimate on every model change.","architect":"Owns the capacity model that links context-window policy, model choice (GQA or not), GPU tier and replica count, and reviews any request to raise the context limit against it.","fde":"Turns a customer's \"we need 200 concurrent users with 50-page documents\" into a per-token number, a GPU count and a cost, and shows the trade when it does not fit their budget."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a 70B-class GQA model (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128) in fp16 to serve 40\nconcurrent sessions that average 12k tokens of context, on 80 GB GPUs. How many GPUs, and what would you\npropose if they only have two?","a":"Cache per token is 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 B ≈ 320 KB. Per session at 12k tokens: ~3.9 GB.\nForty sessions: ~157 GB of cache. Weights in fp16 are ~140 GB, which already needs at least two 80 GB GPUs\nwith tensor parallelism, and at 90% utilization those two offer ~144 GB, so after weights and workspace\nthere is almost nothing left for cache: the model loads but serves one or two sessions. Four GPUs (TP = 4)\ngive ~288 GB usable, roughly 130–140 GB after weights and per-GPU workspace, about 34 sessions at the\naverage and no tail headroom, so slightly short. Six GPUs is not a valid TP degree for 8 KV heads, so the\nnext real step is eight: either one TP = 8 replica with ~400 GB of cache, or two TP = 4 replicas of ~34\nsessions each, which also buys redundancy. With only two GPUs: quantize the weights to 8-bit (~70 GB) and\nthe cache to 8-bit (halving per-session cost to ~2 GB); with ~65 GB left after weights and workspace that\nfits roughly 30–33 sessions at the average. Then cap context per session at 16k to bound the tail, enable\npaging and prefix caching, and run a quality eval on the quantized configuration before promising\nanything. A strong answer notes that decode at 40 sessions\nwill have higher TPOT because every step reads all 40 caches."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which model-config fields do you need to compute KV-cache bytes per token?","options":["Vocabulary size, hidden size and number of layers","Number of layers, number of KV heads, head dimension and the cache element size","Number of parameters and the context length","Number of attention heads, MLP width and dropout rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cache stores one K and one V vector per layer per KV head per token, so those dimensions plus the bytes per element are exactly what is needed. Vocabulary, parameter count and MLP width do not appear in the cache at all."},{"q":"Two 8B models differ only in that one has 32 KV heads and the other 8. What is the effect on serving capacity?","options":["No difference; cache size depends on parameter count","The 8-KV-head model needs 4× more cache because each head is larger","The 8-KV-head model needs about one quarter of the cache per token, so roughly 4× more sequences fit","The 32-KV-head model is faster to decode because attention is more parallel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cache bytes scale linearly with the number of KV heads. Grouped-query attention shares each K/V head across several query heads precisely to cut this number, which is why GQA models serve far more concurrent requests per GPU."},{"q":"Your estimate says 60 requests fit, but the engine's startup log allocates blocks for only 45. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The engine is using a larger block size than you assumed","Activation workspace, CUDA graphs and allocator overhead reduce the pool below \"VRAM minus weights\"","The engine stores keys and values in separate pools","Your formula double-counted the factor of 2 for K and V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Engines profile a worst-case forward pass and reserve memory for activations, graphs and sampling before sizing the cache pool. Block size does not change total pool bytes, and the K/V factor is correct. Trust the log and tighten the workspace term in the estimate."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Zheng et al., 2023 — SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs","Xiao et al., 2023 — Efficient Streaming Language Models with Attention Sinks"],"why":"- **Final length is unknown.** A request's cache grows one token per decode step until the model emits a\n  stop token, so the allocator cannot know how much to give it.\n- **Contiguous max-length reservation wastes memory internally.** A request allowed 8k tokens that stops at\n  600 pins 8k tokens' worth for its whole life, so the pool is mostly reserved-but-empty and concurrency\n  is a fraction of what the hardware could hold.\n- **It also fragments externally.** Requests finish at different times, leaving holes each too small for\n  the next arrival even though the free total is large.\n- **\"Grow in place\" fails too.** The neighbour's region is right behind you, so growing means copying the\n  whole cache to a bigger hole, which is expensive and still fragments. Attention kernels read K and V as\n  one contiguous tensor, which is why everyone accepted the waste for years.\n- **Paging breaks the contiguity assumption.** If the kernel follows a table from logical positions to\n  physical blocks, memory goes out in small fixed units on demand, waste is bounded by one block per\n  sequence, any free block fits any request, and two sequences can share physical blocks (the basis of\n  prefix sharing). Eviction exists because the pool is usable but not infinite; the policy decides whether\n  overload shows up as graceful queueing or latency collapse.","how":"### Blocks and block tables\n1. **Carve the pool** into blocks of a fixed number of tokens (vLLM's default is 16). Each block holds K and\n   V for every layer and KV head for those positions.\n2. **Give each running sequence a block table:** logical block *i* maps to some physical block number.\n3. **Grow on demand:** when the current last block is full, pop a free block from the free list and append\n   it to the table.\n4. **Attend through the table:** the kernel receives the table and, for each query, gathers keys and values\n   block by block. This is PagedAttention; the dedicated lesson covers the kernel.\n\nConsequences that follow directly:\n- **Waste is bounded.** Only the last block of each sequence can be partially filled, so internal waste is\n  under one block per sequence rather than up to a full context length.\n- **Any free block serves any request.** External fragmentation disappears because allocation is in\n  uniform units.\n- **Admission is optimistic.** The scheduler admits on the blocks the prompt needs plus a small reserve, not\n  the maximum output. Far more requests run at once, with the risk that they collectively outgrow the pool.\n\n### Sharing and copy-on-write\n- **Reference-counted blocks.** Because mapping is indirect, several sequences can map logical blocks to the\n  same physical block.\n- **Parallel sampling and beam search** (n candidates from one prompt) share the prompt's blocks. When a\n  sequence must write into a shared block, the engine copies it first.\n- **Prefix caching generalizes this across requests** by keeping blocks alive after their owner finishes,\n  keyed by a hash of their content.\n\n### Running out: sequence-level preemption\n- **Why whole sequences:** a sequence needs all of its blocks to take even one step, so freeing half of one\n  helps nobody.\n\n| Mechanism | How | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Recomputation** | Free the victim's blocks, requeue it, and on readmission re-run prefill over its prompt plus the tokens generated so far | No host memory or transfer machinery; re-prefill is compute-bound and batches well, which is why it is vLLM's default | Wastes prefill compute |\n| **Swapping** | Copy the victim's blocks to pinned CPU memory and back on resumption | Saves compute | Limited by PCIe bandwidth and host memory; many small block copies are less efficient than one large transfer |\n\n- **Which is cheaper is arithmetic, not doctrine.** Recompute costs roughly `2 × params` FLOPs per token\n  plus an attention term growing with the square of sequence length. Swap costs `bytes_per_token` over\n  PCIe, twice.\n- **Swap gains** as sequences get long (the quadratic attention term) and as cache per token gets small\n  relative to the model (GQA, fp8 cache). **Recompute gains** when the cache is fat (MHA) or the host link\n  is slow or shared. Measure resume latency for both on your hardware before choosing.\n- **Victim selection** is typically most recently admitted first, so older requests, which have already\n  consumed the most resources, finish. Preemption is all-or-nothing per sequence.\n- **User-visible symptom:** a pause in the token stream and a jump in TTFT-like latency for the preempted\n  request.\n\n### Running out: evicting cached prefixes\n- **Unreferenced blocks go first.** Blocks kept for prefix reuse have a reference count of zero and are\n  reclaimable, usually in LRU order, before anything is preempted. So a prefix cache degrades gracefully:\n  hit rate falls, nobody is preempted.\n- **A tier below VRAM.** Some deployments demote evicted prefix blocks to host memory or local disk instead\n  of discarding them, turning a miss into a transfer rather than a full re-prefill. It pays when prefixes\n  are long and reused across minutes.\n- **SGLang's radix tree.** It organizes cached prefixes as a radix tree and evicts leaves\n  least-recently-used, which keeps shared ancestors (the system prompt) resident longest.\n- **What this means for an agent between steps.** When a step finishes, its blocks drop to zero\n  references and become cached prefix. The longer the tool runs and the busier the pool, the likelier the\n  transcript's tail (its newest, least-shared blocks) is evicted before the next step arrives. The shared\n  system prompt and tool schemas usually survive.\n\n### Eviction inside a sequence\n- **Exact for sliding-window models.** They attend only to the last *W* positions, so older blocks can be\n  released as soon as the window moves past them.\n- **Approximate for full-attention models.** Research methods drop tokens judged unimportant (low\n  accumulated attention, or everything except the first few \"sink\" tokens plus a recent window) to bound\n  memory for very long streams.\n- **They change the output,** sometimes imperceptibly and sometimes badly on recall-heavy tasks, so evaluate\n  them like any lossy optimization. Production engines treat them as opt-in or do not ship them at all.\n\n### What to watch\n- **Signals:** free and used blocks, the preemption count and the prefix-cache hit rate.\n- **Preemptions above zero under normal load** mean admission is too optimistic for the sequence-length\n  distribution.\n- **Falling prefix hit rate with rising cache usage** means the pool is too small for both duties.\n- **For agents,** compare cached-token share on steps that followed a slow tool with steps that followed a\n  fast one; a gap means transcripts are being evicted during tool waits.","example":"### A toy block allocator\nA block table per sequence and LRU reclaim of unreferenced cached blocks. It is not an attention kernel;\nit shows the bookkeeping that makes paging and eviction work.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nBLOCK = 16                      # tokens per block\n\nclass Pool:\n    def __init__(self, n_blocks):\n        self.free = list(range(n_blocks))\n        self.refs = {}                 # physical block -> reference count\n        self.cached = OrderedDict()    # content_hash -> block, LRU order (ref 0)\n\n    def alloc(self):\n        if not self.free and self.cached:            # evict an unreferenced cached block\n            _, blk = self.cached.popitem(last=False)\n            self.free.append(blk)\n        if not self.free:\n            raise MemoryError(\"pool exhausted: preempt a sequence\")\n        blk = self.free.pop(); self.refs[blk] = 1\n        return blk\n\n    def release(self, blk, content_hash=None):\n        self.refs[blk] -= 1\n        if self.refs[blk] == 0:\n            if content_hash: self.cached[content_hash] = blk   # keep for prefix reuse\n            else: self.free.append(blk)\n\nclass Seq:\n    def __init__(self, pool): self.pool, self.table, self.n = pool, [], 0\n    def append_token(self):\n        if self.n % BLOCK == 0: self.table.append(self.pool.alloc())\n        self.n += 1\n\npool = Pool(n_blocks=4)\na, b = Seq(pool), Seq(pool)\nfor _ in range(40): a.append_token()           # a uses 3 blocks (last one 8/16 full)\nfor _ in range(16): b.append_token()           # b uses the 4th block; pool is now full\ntry:\n    b.append_token()                           # needs a 5th block -> no free, no cached\nexcept MemoryError as e:\n    print(e)                                   # scheduler would now preempt b (recompute or swap)\n```\n\n- **Capacity:** 4 blocks of 16 tokens hold 64 tokens; internal waste is the 8 empty slots in `a`'s last\n  block.\n- **With reuse:** if `a` had finished and released its blocks with a content hash, `b`'s next allocation\n  would have evicted the least-recently-used of them instead of raising.\n\n### Worked scenario: contiguous versus paged at real scale\n`max_model_len` 8,192, an 8B GQA model at 128 KB per token, and a request that stops after 600 tokens.\n\n| | Contiguous | Paged (16-token blocks) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reserved for the request | 8,192 slots (1 GB) | 38 blocks = 608 slots |\n| Actually used | 600 (75 MB) | 600 |\n| Wasted | over 90% of the reservation, for the request's whole life | 8 slots |\n| Resident requests on a ~50 GB pool | about 50 | several hundred, if typical requests look like this one |\n\n- **This is why paging is what made large continuous batches possible at all.**\n\n### Swap or recompute? Do the arithmetic\nIllustrative lower bounds: recompute counts only the matmul term at ~900 TFLOPs (attention adds a\nquadratic term on top), and swap assumes ~25 GB/s of effective host-link bandwidth each way.\n\n```python\nFLOPS, PCIE = 900e12, 25e9              # illustrative hardware figures\n\ndef recompute_ms(params_b, tokens):     # matmul term only; attention grows quadratically on top\n    return 2 * params_b * 1e9 * tokens / FLOPS * 1e3\n\ndef swap_ms(kv_bytes_per_token, tokens):  # out to host and back in\n    return 2 * kv_bytes_per_token * tokens / PCIE * 1e3\n\nGQA, MHA = 128 * 1024, 512 * 1024       # 8B-class GQA vs 7B-class MHA, fp16 cache\nfor name, kv, params in [(\"GQA 8B\", GQA, 8), (\"MHA 7B\", MHA, 7)]:\n    for t in (4_000, 32_000):\n        print(f\"{name} {t:>6} tokens: recompute >= {recompute_ms(params, t):4.0f} ms, \"\n              f\"swap ~ {swap_ms(kv, t):4.0f} ms\")\n# GQA 8B   4000 tokens: recompute >=   71 ms, swap ~   42 ms\n# GQA 8B  32000 tokens: recompute >=  569 ms, swap ~  336 ms\n# MHA 7B   4000 tokens: recompute >=   62 ms, swap ~  168 ms\n# MHA 7B  32000 tokens: recompute >=  498 ms, swap ~ 1342 ms\n```\n\n- **Read it as:** the thin GQA cache favours swap, and more so at long lengths once attention's quadratic\n  term is added; the fat MHA cache favours recompute. Recompute also competes with live decode for\n  compute, which this sketch ignores.\n\n### Agent scenario: the transcript evicted during a slow tool call\nA research agent on the 8B GQA model has a 20,000-token transcript and calls a web-scrape tool that takes\n30 s. The pool is busy, so its unreferenced blocks are evicted LRU before the next step. Same illustrative\nfigures as above.\n\n| Next step finds | What runs | Extra latency on that step |\n|---|---|---|\n| Whole transcript still cached | prefill only the ~1,000-token tool result | ~18 ms |\n| System prompt and tools cached (6,000), rest evicted | re-prefill ~15,000 tokens | ~267 ms |\n| Everything evicted | re-prefill 21,000 tokens | ~373 ms |\n| Evicted to a host-memory tier | copy ~2.6 GB back over the host link, then prefill ~1,000 | ~105 ms + ~18 ms |\n\n```python\nKV = 128 * 1024\nctx, new = 20_000, 1_000\nprint(\"hit:\", round(recompute_ms(8, new)), \"ms\")\nprint(\"tail evicted:\", round(recompute_ms(8, ctx - 6_000 + new)), \"ms\")\nprint(\"all evicted:\", round(recompute_ms(8, ctx + new)), \"ms\")\nprint(\"host tier:\", round(swap_ms(KV, ctx) / 2), \"ms in +\", round(recompute_ms(8, new)), \"ms\")\n```\n\n- **Read it as:** one miss costs a few hundred milliseconds per step on this model (more on bigger models\n  or longer transcripts) and burns prefill compute other agents needed. Across a 20-step run with many\n  slow tools, misses can dominate task latency.","patterns":["**Keep agent transcripts resident across tool waits.** Route every step of a run to the same replica, leave headroom in the pool so idle prefixes survive, and add a host-memory tier when tool waits are long. Monitor prefix hit rate on the step after each tool call, split by tool latency.","**Alert on preemptions, not memory percent.** Page on the engine's preemption counter (in vLLM, `vllm:num_preemptions_total` on `/metrics`) and on free blocks, and tune `max_num_seqs`, the per-iteration token budget and `max_model_len` until preemptions are near zero at target peak.","**Bound the worst case with context policy.** Set `max_model_len` near the real p99 of prompt + output per deployment, and route rare very long requests to a separate replica class. Monitor preemptions per replica class after the split.","**Choose the preemption mode by measurement.** Recompute is the default; consider swap only where your engine version still supports it, pinned host memory is plentiful and sequences are long. Benchmark the resume latency of a preempted 4k and 32k request both ways on the target host.","**Add a host-memory tier for long reused prefixes.** When long documents or agent histories are reused across minutes, offload evicted prefix blocks to CPU memory (for example SGLang's hierarchical cache, or LMCache with vLLM). Monitor prefix hit rate and TTFT on hits served from the lower tier.","**Treat block size as a tuned constant.** Keep the default (16 in vLLM) unless a measurement says otherwise, and re-measure prefix hit rate and kernel throughput whenever you change it."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** While your agent waits on a tool, its transcript's blocks are\n  unreferenced cached prefix, eligible for LRU eviction and closer to the front of the queue the longer\n  the tool runs. Under load, a slow tool means the next step re-prefills much of the transcript; an\n  oversubscribed pool also preempts steps mid-generation.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Self-hosted engines: vLLM's preemption counter and block metrics,\n  SGLang's radix cache, host-memory prefix tiers (SGLang's hierarchical cache, LMCache with vLLM). Hosted\n  APIs hide it, but cached-token fields in usage reveal misses.\n- **What you control.** Admission limits (`max_num_seqs`, `max_model_len`), whether long-lived agent\n  prefixes get a host-memory tier, routing steps back to the same replica, and keeping in-sequence token\n  eviction off unless an agent eval approves it.\n- **What you can skip.** The PagedAttention kernel itself and block-table data structures beyond the\n  toy below.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents with slow tools.** While a tool runs, the transcript's blocks are evictable; headroom, a\n  host-memory tier and same-replica routing decide whether the next step re-prefills.\n- **Long-context agents on a shared replica.** Admission limits and preemption counters explain mid-answer\n  pauses that show no errors.\n- **Many runs sharing one system prompt and toolset.** Shared blocks with reference counts hold one copy of\n  the common prefix for every run.\n- **Evaluating KV compression for agents.** Token-dropping eviction changes what the model attends to, so\n  it ships only after an agent eval approves it.","mini_project":"**Watch a transcript get evicted during a tool wait (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** reproduce the slow-tool eviction effect and show one fix.\n- **Steps:** serve a small model with vLLM and prefix caching on, shrinking the pool (a low\n  `--gpu-memory-utilization`) so it fills quickly; run one agent whose tool sleeps 30 s while a load\n  generator sends unrelated long prompts; record cached tokens (usage details or the engine's prefix-cache\n  metrics) and TTFT on the step after the tool; repeat with lower background load or more pool headroom.\n- **Done when:** you have both runs' post-tool TTFT and cached-token counts, plus the preemption and\n  prefix-hit metrics from `/metrics` that explain the difference."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Paging means spilling the cache to CPU memory, like OS swap.\"** Paging is indirection inside VRAM: a block table maps logical positions to fixed-size physical blocks. Copying blocks to host memory is a separate mechanism (swap preemption, or a host tier for evicted prefixes), used only when VRAM runs out.","**\"Paged KV memory eliminates waste.\"** It bounds it: only each sequence's last block can be partly empty, so waste is under one block per sequence (8 slots for a 600-token request in 16-token blocks) instead of most of an 8,192-token reservation.","**\"When the pool fills, the engine trims a little from every request.\"** A sequence needs all its blocks to take even one step, so engines first reclaim unreferenced cached blocks and then preempt whole sequences, newest first.","**\"Recompute is the wasteful mode, so swap is always better.\"** It is arithmetic. A thin GQA cache swaps faster (~42 ms vs ≥71 ms at 4,000 tokens on the 8B model), but a fat MHA cache recomputes faster (~62 ms vs ~168 ms), and a slow or shared host link tilts it further.","**\"My agent's cache stays reserved while its tool runs.\"** Between steps no request references the transcript, so its blocks are LRU-evictable cached prefix. After a slow tool on a busy pool the next step can re-prefill ~15,000-21,000 tokens, a few hundred milliseconds on an 8B model.","**\"Dropping old tokens is a safe way to fit long contexts.\"** It is exact only for sliding-window layers. On full-attention models it changes outputs, sometimes badly on recall-heavy tasks, so gate it on an agent eval."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    N[\"Sequence needs a new block\"] --> F{\"Free block available?\"}\n    F -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Append to block table\"]\n    F -->|\"no\"| C{\"Unreferenced cached prefix block?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Evict LRU prefix block, e.g. idle agent\"]\n    E --> A\n    C -->|\"no\"| P[\"Preempt newest sequence\"]\n    P --> M{\"Preemption mode\"}\n    M -->|\"recompute\"| R[\"Free blocks, requeue, re-prefill later\"]\n    M -->|\"swap\"| S[\"Copy blocks to CPU, restore later\"]\n    R --> A\n    S --> A","caption":"What the engine does when a running sequence needs a new block: free list first, then unreferenced cached\nprefixes (including transcripts of agents waiting on tools), and only then preemption."},"interview":[{"q":"What problem does paging solve that contiguous KV allocation cannot?","a":"- **Unknown length:** contiguous allocation must reserve the maximum context up front, so a request that\n  stops at 600 of 8,192 tokens wastes over 90% of its slot.\n- **Fragmentation:** finished requests leave holes too small to reuse. Fixed-size blocks behind a block\n  table bound waste to under one block per sequence and let any free block serve any request.\n- **Bonus:** indirection enables shared, copy-on-write blocks for parallel sampling and prefix caching."},{"q":"A user reports their stream pausing for two seconds mid-answer, with no errors. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Suspect preemption:** check the preemption counter and free-block count at that timestamp.\n- **Confirm the mechanism:** the request was the most recently admitted when the pool ran dry, so it was\n  evicted and had to re-prefill its prompt plus generated tokens.\n- **Fix admission:** lower `max_num_seqs` or the token budget, cap `max_model_len` to real p99, or quantize\n  the cache; queueing at admission is cheaper than preemption mid-stream."},{"q":"When would you choose swap over recompute for preemption?","a":"- **Long sequences with a small cache per token** (GQA, fp8): re-prefill grows faster than linearly while\n  transfer grows linearly.\n- **Ample pinned host memory and a fast, unshared PCIe link.**\n- **Otherwise recompute,** especially for fat MHA caches or short sequences, and measure resume latency\n  both ways before deciding."},{"q":"Why is evicting cached prefix blocks safe but dropping tokens inside a sequence is not?","a":"- **Prefix blocks with zero references** are optimization state; losing one costs a re-prefill on the next\n  matching request but changes no output.\n- **Dropping tokens** (attention sinks plus a window, low-attention pruning) changes what the model attends\n  to, so outputs change, and recall-heavy tasks can break while memory charts look great."},{"q":"What does a falling prefix-cache hit rate alongside rising cache usage tell you?","a":"- **The pool is too small for both duties.** Live sequences are crowding out cached prefixes, which LRU\n  evicts first.\n- **Next stage is preemption** once no unreferenced blocks remain.\n- **Remedy:** more memory (replica or larger GPU), a host-memory tier for prefixes, or less optimistic\n  admission; flags alone will not restore the hit rate."}],"tradeoffs":["**Smaller vs larger blocks.** Smaller blocks cut internal waste and make prefix matching finer-grained, but enlarge block tables and reduce kernel efficiency. The balance flips toward larger blocks on long contexts with few sequences.","**Optimistic admission vs preemption risk.** It packs many more sequences per GPU but makes preemption possible. It flips to harmful when lengths are long-tailed and preemption storms recompute more than they save.","**Recompute vs swap.** Recomputation is simple and batches well but wastes prefill compute. Swapping saves compute at the cost of PCIe traffic and host memory, and wins as sequences grow long, because re-prefill cost grows faster than linearly with length while transfer cost grows linearly.","**Resident prefixes vs live sequences.** Keeping finished prefixes raises hit rate but competes with live sequences for blocks. Under sustained load LRU eviction turns prefix caching into a weak benefit rather than a guarantee.","**In-sequence token eviction vs fidelity.** It bounds memory for unbounded streams but changes outputs. Acceptable for chatty, low-recall workloads, not for retrieval or long-document QA."],"pitfalls":["**Reading \"memory utilization 95%\" as healthy while the preemption counter climbs.** Symptom: periodic multi-second stalls on individual streams with no error logged. Fix: alert on the preemption counter.","**Setting `max_model_len` far above real prompt lengths and assuming it is free.** Symptom: a preemption storm the first time a handful of requests actually grow into that headroom, because optimistic admission let them in on their prompt size and the pool was sized for the typical case. Fix: set it near the real p99.","**Swap-based preemption on hosts with little pinned memory or slow PCIe.** Symptom: preempted requests take longer to resume than recomputation would have. Fix: benchmark resume latency both ways first.","**Changing block size on a tuned deployment without re-measuring.** Symptom: a surprise drop in prefix hit rate or kernel throughput that nobody attributes to the flag. Fix: re-run the load test on any change.","**An approximate token-eviction method for long conversations.** Symptom: the model forgets instructions from early in the chat while memory charts look great. Fix: gate it on a long-range recall eval."],"when":"Paging is not optional in a modern engine; what you decide is the policy around it.\n- **Overload signal:** use the preemption counter, and tune admission (`max_num_seqs`, per-iteration token\n  budget, `max_model_len`) until it is near zero at target load.\n- **Preemption mode:** choose recomputation unless sequences are so long that re-prefill is the dominant\n  cost.\n- **Prefix caching:** keep it on when prompts share prefixes and watch hit rate under load. If it\n  collapses, the pool is too small for both duties and you need memory, not flags.\n- **Avoid by default:** in-sequence token eviction is a research option to evaluate, not a default.\n- **Interviews:** explain why preemption is per-sequence, how blocks bound waste, and what LRU prefix\n  eviction buys.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes block size, admission limits and the preemption mode, and alerts on preemption count and free-block count rather than on raw memory utilization.","architect":"Decides whether the pool is shared between live sequences and cached prefixes or split by replica class, and sets the context-length policy that bounds worst-case reservations.","fde":"Reads a customer's engine metrics to explain mid-stream pauses as preemption, then proposes the admission or hardware change that removes them without breaking their latency promise."},"check":{"q":"A deployment runs at 92% KV-cache utilization with 3–5 preemptions per minute and a prefix-cache hit\nrate that has fallen from 60% to 15% since traffic grew. Explain what is happening inside the pool and\nwhat you would change, in order.","a":"The pool is oversubscribed. Live sequences have grown until there are almost no free blocks, so the\nengine first evicted unreferenced cached prefixes (hit rate collapsed) and, once those were gone, began\npreempting the most recently admitted sequences, which then recompute their prefill and consume the\ncompute that was supposed to go to decode. First, make admission less optimistic: lower `max_num_seqs` or\nthe per-iteration token budget so that live sequences fit without eviction; queueing at admission is\ncheaper than preemption mid-stream. Second, reduce per-sequence cost: cap `max_model_len` to the real\np99, and quantize the cache to fp8 if an eval allows. Third, if hit rate matters to TTFT, add memory\n(another replica or a larger GPU) so that cached prefixes survive; prefix caching is only a benefit when\nthere is slack. Measure preemptions per minute after each change and stop when it is near zero at peak."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does paging bound internal fragmentation to less than one block per sequence?","options":["Because blocks are allocated in sorted order of sequence length","Because a sequence only ever has one partially filled block, its most recent one; all earlier blocks are full","Because the engine compacts the cache after every decode step","Because block size is chosen to divide the maximum context length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tokens are appended sequentially, so every block except the last is completely used. Contiguous reservation, by contrast, wastes the whole unused remainder of the maximum context for each request."},{"q":"When the pool is exhausted, why do engines preempt entire sequences rather than freeing some blocks from several?","options":["Because block tables cannot be modified while a sequence is running","Because partial frees would corrupt the attention mask","Because a sequence needs all of its blocks to take even one decode step, so partially freeing it makes it useless","Because recomputation is only possible from the beginning of a sequence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Attention for the next token reads every cached position. Removing some blocks from a live sequence means it cannot proceed at all, so the only useful units of eviction are whole sequences or blocks nobody references."},{"q":"Which eviction is lossless for a full-attention model?","options":["Dropping tokens with the lowest accumulated attention scores","Keeping only the first few tokens and a recent window","Reclaiming cached prefix blocks that no running sequence references","Discarding every other block once context exceeds a threshold"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unreferenced prefix blocks are pure optimization state; losing them costs a recompute on the next matching request but changes no output. Token-dropping methods alter what the model attends to and are approximations that must be evaluated."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/prefix-caching","sources":["Zheng et al., 2023 — SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **Agent prompts are mostly repetition.** A system prompt is the same thousands of tokens on every call; a\n  few-shot block is the same examples; turn *n* of a chat begins with turns 1 to *n−1*; an agent loop\n  re-sends the whole trajectory every tool step.\n- **Repeating it is pure waste.** Prefill is compute-bound and linear in prompt length, so a 3,000-token\n  boilerplate prefix costs 3,000 tokens of matmul on every request, producing exactly the same K and V\n  vectors every time.\n- **The naive fixes fall short.** \"Shorten the prompt\" has a floor, because instructions, tool schemas and\n  examples are there for quality. \"Cache the response\" only works for identical full prompts, which almost\n  never recur beyond FAQs. What recurs is the *beginning*.\n- **Causal attention makes the beginning exploitable.** Position *i* attends only to positions ≤ *i*, so a\n  prefix's cache is a pure function of the prefix tokens and the weights. One request's first 3,000 tokens\n  give bit-for-bit what the next request with the same 3,000 tokens would compute.\n- **Paging made it cheap.** The work left is keeping blocks, finding them fast and reclaiming them under\n  pressure. Blocks are already the allocation unit, so sharing is a reference count, not a copy.","how":"### Content-addressed blocks\n- **A prefix is a sequence of full blocks.** The engine hashes each block's content, typically the hash of\n  the previous block combined with this block's token ids.\n- **So a key encodes everything before the block,** not just its own 16 tokens. That matters because the\n  same 16 tokens at a different position, or after a different prefix, have different K and V (position\n  and preceding context both feed in).\n- **Agent consequence:** an edit at step 3 of a transcript invalidates every block after it, including all\n  later steps, even if they are unchanged.\n\n### Lookup on arrival\n1. **Walk the prompt** block by block at admission, computing hashes and looking each up.\n2. **Take the longest chain of hits from the start** as the cached prefix.\n3. **Attach those physical blocks** to the new request's block table and increment their reference counts.\n4. **Prefill only the remaining tokens.** In some engines a partial last block is always recomputed,\n   because only full blocks are cached.\n- **TTFT drops** in proportion to the fraction of the prompt that hit. For an agent step that is usually\n  everything except the latest tool call and result.\n\n### Two payoffs: compute and memory\n| Payoff | Arithmetic (8B model, illustrative peak rates) |\n|---|---|\n| **Prefill compute** | A 3,000-token prefix is 2 × 8e9 × 3,000 ≈ 48 TFLOPs, on the order of 50 ms on one GPU, against a millisecond or two for a 50-token question. With a hit, TTFT is dominated by the question and the queue |\n| **Capacity** | Requests sharing a prefix point at the same physical blocks. 100 concurrent requests with that prefix (at 128 KB per token, ~390 MB each) hold one copy instead of 100: roughly 39 GB returned to live sequences |\n\n- **On long shared prefixes the capacity win is often worth more than the TTFT gain,** because it raises\n  the batch size. Many concurrent agent runs sharing one tool-schema block is exactly this case.\n\n### Lifetime and eviction\n- **Zero-reference blocks stay cached.** When a request finishes, its blocks are not returned to the free\n  list; they stay in the cache table, reclaimable in LRU order when live sequences need memory.\n- **Tool waits are the danger window.** Between steps, an agent's transcript has no running request, so its\n  blocks are zero-reference and evictable while the tool runs.\n- **Load decides residency.** Under light load a system prompt stays resident indefinitely; under heavy\n  load it is evicted and recomputed. The hit-rate metric tells you which regime you are in.\n- **Two data structures, same behaviour.** SGLang uses a radix tree, so requests sharing a long ancestor\n  and diverging late reuse the ancestor and evict leaves first. vLLM's hash table reaches the same\n  behaviour with per-block LRU.\n\n### What counts as \"the same prefix\"\n- **Token identity, not semantic similarity.** A single different token invalidates every block from that\n  point on.\n- **Classic mistakes:** a timestamp, request id, user name, step counter or randomly ordered retrieval\n  results or tool list near the top of the prompt.\n- **Layout rule for an agent prompt:**\n  1. Stable content first: system prompt, tool definitions, few-shot examples.\n  2. Slowly changing content next: conversation history, the append-only trajectory, documents.\n  3. The volatile part last: the current question, current time, latest tool result.\n- **Chat templates must be stable too.** A template that emits the date, or formats the system prompt\n  differently when tools are present, silently breaks sharing.\n- **The key covers everything that changes K and V.** A different LoRA adapter or different image inputs\n  produce different caches, so engines mix those identities into the block hash, and two tenants on\n  different adapters never share.\n\n### Multi-replica serving\n- **A cache lives on one GPU.** Behind a plain load balancer, an agent's next step lands on a replica that\n  has never seen its trajectory.\n- **Options:** sticky routing by tenant, conversation or agent run id, consistent hashing on a prefix hash\n  computed at the router, or a router that queries replicas' cache state.\n- **Without one,** fleet hit rate is far below a single replica's, and the problem is routing, not the\n  engine.\n\n### Managed APIs\n- **Same mechanism, different controls.** Some providers cache automatically above a minimum prompt\n  length; others need explicit cache breakpoints in the request.\n- **Pricing shape:** most price cached input tokens at a fraction of normal input, and some (Anthropic's\n  explicit breakpoints, for example) also charge a premium to write an entry. Lifetime is short (minutes) unless refreshed, so a slow tool call can outlive it.\n- **Look the numbers up, do not memorize them,** because rules differ per provider and change over time.\n  The discipline is identical: stable prefix first, measure hit rate, keep volatile content out of the top.\n\n### Sampling independence and correctness\n- **The cache stores K and V, not outputs,** so it is independent of temperature, seed and sampling\n  settings; reuse never changes the sampled distribution.\n- **One numerical subtlety:** with batched kernels, a prefix cached in one batch shape may differ in the\n  last bits from a fresh prefill in another, so cached and uncached runs are not always bit-identical even\n  at temperature 0. Agent evals should not demand bit-identical trajectories.","example":"### Agent scenario: prefill tokens for a 10-step run, simulated\nPure Python, no GPU. A 6,000-token prefix (system prompt plus tool schemas), 1,500 tokens appended per step\n(tool call plus result). The only difference between runs is one step id placed before the system prompt.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\n\nBLOCK = 16\n\ndef block_hashes(ids):\n    out, prev = [], b\"\"\n    for i in range(0, len(ids) - len(ids) % BLOCK, BLOCK):\n        prev = hashlib.sha256(prev + str(ids[i:i + BLOCK]).encode()).digest()\n        out.append(prev)\n    return out\n\ndef run_agent(header, steps=10, prefix=6000, per_step=1500):\n    \"\"\"Total prefill tokens for one agent run; header(step) is what precedes the system prompt.\"\"\"\n    cache, transcript, prefilled = set(), [], 0\n    for s in range(steps):\n        prompt = header(s) + list(range(prefix)) + transcript\n        hit = 0\n        for h in block_hashes(prompt):\n            if h not in cache:\n                break                                   # first miss ends reuse\n            hit += BLOCK\n        prefilled += len(prompt) - hit\n        cache.update(block_hashes(prompt))\n        transcript += [100_000 + s * per_step + j for j in range(per_step)]  # tool call + result\n    return prefilled\n\nprint(\"stable prefix    :\", run_agent(lambda s: []))              # 19548\nprint(\"step id at top   :\", run_agent(lambda s: [900_000 + s]))   # 127510\n```\n\n| Run | Prefill tokens over 10 steps | Per step after step 1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stable prefix, append-only | 19,548 | ~1.5k (the newest tool result) |\n| Step id before the system prompt | 127,510 | the whole prompt, growing by 1.5k each step |\n\n- **About 6.5x more prefill** from one token, because chained hashes make every later block miss.\n\n### Measure prompt layout in vLLM's offline engine\nThe two prompt styles contain identical content; only the position of the volatile part changes.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\n\nllm = LLM(\"meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct\", enable_prefix_caching=True,\n          max_model_len=8192, gpu_memory_utilization=0.9)\nsp = SamplingParams(temperature=0, max_tokens=32)\n\nsystem = \"You are a contracts assistant.\\n\" + (\"Policy clause.\\n\" * 400)   # ~1.2k+ tokens, stable\nquestions = [f\"Question {i}: does clause {i} allow early termination?\" for i in range(20)]\n\ndef run(prompts):\n    llm.generate(prompts[:1], sp)                      # warm: first call pays full prefill\n    t = time.perf_counter(); llm.generate(prompts[1:], sp)\n    return (time.perf_counter() - t) / (len(prompts) - 1)\n\ngood = [f\"{system}\\n{q}\" for q in questions]                               # stable prefix first\nbad  = [f\"Request-id: {i}\\n{system}\\n{q}\" for i, q in enumerate(questions)]  # volatile token first\n\nprint(f\"stable-first : {run(good)*1000:.0f} ms/request\")\nprint(f\"volatile-first: {run(bad)*1000:.0f} ms/request\")\n# Expect the stable-first layout to be markedly faster per request: the >1k-token\n# policy block hits the cache on every call. The volatile-first layout misses on\n# every block because the first few tokens differ, so every request re-prefills the whole block.\n# llm.llm_engine metrics (or the server's /metrics endpoint) expose the prefix hit rate.\n```\n\n- **Confirm with the counters, not only the timer.** On a server, the prefix-cache query and hit counters\n  on `/metrics` give the hit rate directly; timings on a small offline run are noisy.\n\n### Why one early token kills every later hit: a block-hash simulator\nEach block's key chains the previous key, exactly as described above.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\n\nBLOCK = 16\n\ndef block_hashes(token_ids):\n    \"\"\"Chained hash per full block: each key covers the whole prefix up to that block.\"\"\"\n    hashes, prev = [], b\"\"\n    for i in range(0, len(token_ids) - len(token_ids) % BLOCK, BLOCK):\n        h = hashlib.sha256(prev + str(token_ids[i:i + BLOCK]).encode()).digest()\n        hashes.append(h); prev = h\n    return hashes\n\ndef cached_tokens(cache, token_ids):\n    hit = 0\n    for h in block_hashes(token_ids):\n        if h not in cache: break          # longest chain of hits from the start\n        hit += BLOCK\n    cache.update(block_hashes(token_ids))\n    return hit\n\nsystem = list(range(1000, 4000))          # 3,000 stable \"system prompt\" tokens\ncache = set()\nfor turn, q in enumerate([[1, 2, 3] * 10, [4, 5, 6] * 10]):\n    print(\"stable-first  turn\", turn, cached_tokens(cache, system + q))   # 0, then 2992\ncache = set()\nfor req_id in (7, 8):\n    print(\"id-first   request\", req_id, cached_tokens(cache, [req_id] + system))  # 0, 0\n```\n\n- **2992, not 3,000:** the block holding the system prompt's last 8 tokens also holds question tokens, so\n  it differs and is recomputed.\n- **The id-first layout never hits,** because the first block differs and every chained key after it does\n  too.\n\n### Worked scenario: an agent loop on a four-replica fleet\nA 4k-token system prompt plus a growing trajectory, 10 tool steps per task, illustrative.\n\n| Setup | What prefills each step | Fleet effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| Timestamp at the top, round-robin routing | the whole prompt, every step | TTFT grows with trajectory length; hit rate near zero |\n| Timestamp moved to the end, still round-robin | the shared system prompt hits once each replica is warm, but the trajectory since that replica last saw the task re-prefills on most steps (the previous step's replica comes back only about 1 time in 4) | fleet hit rate well below what one replica serving the whole task would reach |\n| Stable prefix and routing by conversation id | only the newest tool result | step latency stops growing, except the decode-side cache-read term |","patterns":["**Lint agent prompt templates for cache stability.** In CI, render each template twice with different volatile inputs (time, user, request id, step number) and assert that the token ids of the stable section are identical. Also serialize tool lists in a fixed order. Monitor prefix hit rate per route after every template change.","**Route every step of a run to the same replica.** Use consistent hashing on conversation, tenant or agent run id at the gateway, or a cache-aware router (SGLang's router offers one), so repeat traffic hits a warm replica. Monitor fleet hit rate against single-replica hit rate.","**Track hit rate and TTFT as a pair, with preemptions.** Enable caching (`--enable-prefix-caching` in vLLM, on by default in recent versions) and chart prefix hit rate, TTFT p50/p95 and the preemption counter together; a falling hit rate with rising preemptions means the pool is too small.","**Log cached tokens per agent step from managed-API responses.** Place stable content first and add breakpoints where the provider needs them (for example `cache_control` blocks on Anthropic's Messages API). Log the cached token counts the response reports (`usage.cache_read_input_tokens` on Anthropic, `usage.prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens` on OpenAI) per step, and alert when a step after the first reads near zero.","**Compact history at a boundary, not every step.** Summarizing or trimming earlier turns invalidates every block after the edit. Do it once when context nears the limit, accept one miss, then append again.","**Isolate untrusted tenants.** Where cross-tenant timing probes matter, give tenants separate replicas or a per-tenant salt in the block hash (vLLM accepts a per-request `cache_salt`). Monitor per-tenant hit rate to confirm sharing only happens within a tenant."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Step *n* of an agent loop begins with steps 1 to *n−1*. With a stable,\n  append-only transcript each step prefills only the newest tool result; without it, every step\n  re-prefills the whole trajectory and step latency and cost climb with step count.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Hosted APIs: automatic caching or explicit breakpoints such as\n  `cache_control`, and cached-token fields in `usage`. Self-hosted: `--enable-prefix-caching` in vLLM,\n  RadixAttention in SGLang, and the router that decides which replica sees step *n*.\n- **What you control.** Prompt layout (system prompt and tool definitions first, byte-identical), whether\n  history is append-only or rewritten, where timestamps and run ids go, deterministic ordering of retrieved\n  chunks and tool lists, and routing every step of a run to the same replica.\n- **What you can skip.** The hash-table and radix-tree internals. Know that block keys are chained, that\n  the first changed token ends reuse, and how to read hit-rate counters.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Long-running coding and research agents.** An append-only transcript with a stable prefix keeps step\n  20's prefill close to step 2's, so latency and cost stop growing with step count.\n- **Many agents sharing one toolset.** Concurrent runs that share the same system prompt and tool schemas\n  point at one physical copy of those blocks, which raises how many runs a replica holds.\n- **Multi-turn support assistants on hosted APIs.** Cache breakpoints after the system prompt and history\n  turn most input tokens into discounted cached reads.\n- **Multi-replica agent platforms.** Routing by run id is what turns a single-replica hit rate into a\n  fleet-wide one.","mini_project":"**Prefix-stability audit for an agent loop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove which parts of your agent's prompt break caching, and fix them.\n- **Steps:** build a 10-step agent with two fake tools; log input and cached-input tokens per step from the\n  provider usage fields (or vLLM's `/metrics` when self-hosting); run it once as written, once with a\n  timestamp injected before the system prompt, and once rewriting the oldest turn every step; then add a\n  CI test that renders the template twice with different volatile inputs and compares stable token ids.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-step table for all three runs, the baseline's cached tokens climb on every\n  step after the first, and the CI test fails on the timestamp variant."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Prefix caching is a semantic cache that reuses answers to similar prompts.\"** It reuses K and V for a byte-identical token prefix and still runs the model on the rest. Similar wording, a reordered tool list or a different chat template is a miss.","**\"Serving from the cache changes what the model samples.\"** The cache holds K and V, which causal attention makes a pure function of the prefix and the weights, so reuse is exact and independent of temperature or seed. Batched kernels can still differ in the last bits, so do not demand bit-identical trajectories.","**\"One volatile token costs only that token.\"** Block keys chain the previous key, so the first changed token invalidates every later block. A step id placed before the system prompt turns 19,548 prefill tokens into 127,510 over a 10-step run, about 6.5x.","**\"Enabling `--enable-prefix-caching` is enough on a fleet.\"** The cache lives on one GPU. Behind round-robin routing an agent's next step usually lands on a replica that never saw its trajectory, so route by run or conversation id.","**\"Once cached, my agent's prefix stays cached.\"** Between steps its blocks have zero references and are LRU-evictable under load, and hosted caches expire after minutes, so a slow tool call can outlive the entry.","**\"The payoff is only lower TTFT.\"** Requests sharing a prefix share physical blocks. 100 concurrent requests on a 3,000-token prefix hold one copy instead of 100, roughly 39 GB returned to live sequences, which raises the batch size."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Agent step arrives\"] --> H[\"Hash next full block, chained to previous\"]\n    H --> L{\"Hash in cache table?\"}\n    L -->|\"hit\"| A[\"Attach block, ref count plus 1\"]\n    A --> H\n    L -->|\"miss or no full block left\"| P[\"Prefill only remaining tokens\"]\n    P --> D[\"Decode the tool call\"]\n    D --> F[\"Step finishes, ref count minus 1\"]\n    F --> C[(\"Zero-ref blocks stay cached\")]\n    C -->|\"memory pressure during tool wait\"| E[\"LRU eviction\"]\n    C -.->|\"next step of the run\"| L","caption":"On arrival the engine walks the prompt's chained block hashes; the first miss ends reuse, and only the\nremaining tokens (for an agent step, the newest tool result) are prefilled."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is prefix caching exact rather than an approximation?","a":"- **Causality:** K and V at position *i* depend only on tokens ≤ *i* and the weights, so a byte-identical\n  prefix yields bit-for-bit the same cache (up to batch-shape floating-point differences).\n- **It stores K and V, not outputs,** so sampling settings are untouched and reuse never changes the\n  distribution the model samples from."},{"q":"How does an engine find a cached prefix, and why are block hashes chained?","a":"- **Lookup:** hash each full block in order and take the longest run of hits from the start; attach those\n  blocks and prefill the rest.\n- **Chaining:** each key combines the previous block's hash with this block's tokens, because the same 16\n  tokens after a different prefix have different K and V. One early difference therefore misses every\n  later block."},{"q":"Your agent fleet's hit rate is 70% on one replica but 20% fleet-wide. What do you change?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** routing, not the engine. Round-robin sends each agent step to a replica that likely never\n  prefilled that run's trajectory.\n- **Fix:** consistent hashing on conversation, tenant or run id, or a cache-aware router; then watch for\n  hot replicas, since locality concentrates load."},{"q":"An agent's cached input tokens fall to near zero on every step after a prompt refactor. How do you find the cause?","a":"- **Diff two rendered prompts token by token** from consecutive steps of one run and find the first\n  differing position; everything after it misses.\n- **Usual culprits:** a timestamp or step counter moved into the system prompt, tool schemas serialized in\n  a different order, or history rewritten each step.\n- **Prevent it** with a CI lint that renders templates with different volatile inputs and asserts the\n  stable section's token ids match."},{"q":"When would you turn prefix caching off or isolate it?","a":"- **Mutually untrusted tenants:** hit latency leaks whether someone else sent a prefix, so isolate by\n  replica or salt the hash per tenant.\n- **Unique-prompt bulk jobs:** little to gain while cached blocks compete with live sequences.\n- **A saturated pool:** evictions make hits rare, and the fix is memory or admission limits."},{"q":"Estimate what a 3,000-token shared system prompt saves on an 8B model at 100 concurrent requests.","a":"- **Compute:** about 48 TFLOPs per request, roughly 50 ms of prefill at illustrative peak rates, skipped on\n  every hit.\n- **Memory:** ~390 MB of cache per request at 128 KB per token; one shared copy instead of 100 returns about\n  39 GB to live sequences, which raises batch size."}],"tradeoffs":["**Skipped prefill vs held VRAM.** It cuts TTFT and GPU time for shared prefixes and agent trajectories, but holds cached blocks live sequences could use. The gain reverses when the pool is tight and cached blocks keep getting evicted, for example during long tool waits.","**Layout discipline vs prompt freedom.** Stable-first, volatile-last improves hit rate but constrains prompt design (no \"current time\" in the system prompt). It becomes irrelevant when prompts share nothing beyond a short system line.","**Append-only history vs context size.** Never editing history maximizes hits but lets the transcript grow; compaction saves tokens and memory at the cost of one full re-prefill per compaction.","**Cache-aware routing vs load balance.** It raises fleet-wide hit rate but concentrates a tenant's or a run's traffic on one replica. Worth it when prefixes are long and tenants are many.","**Managed-API write premium vs read discount.** Where the provider charges for writes, they cost more than plain input and reads cost less. It pays once a prefix is reused a few times within the cache lifetime, as in any multi-step agent run, and loses money on one-off prompts."],"pitfalls":["**A timestamp, request id or user name at the top of the prompt.** Symptom: hit rate near zero despite a long identical system prompt, and agent step latency that grows every step. Fix: move volatile fields to the end of the prompt.","**Retrieval results or tool lists before the question in a non-deterministic order.** Symptom: hits only on the system prompt, never on the document block, with TTFT still scaling with retrieved length. Fix: sort retrieved chunks deterministically (for example by document id) and serialize tools in fixed order.","**Rewriting earlier turns on every step.** Symptom: cached tokens drop to the system-prompt length on every step even though the prefix looks stable. Fix: keep the trajectory append-only and compact only at a boundary.","**Round-robin load balancing across replicas.** Symptom: single-replica hit rate far higher than the fleet's, and TTFT that depends on which replica the request landed on. Fix: route by conversation or tenant id.","**Measuring hit rate on a benchmark that replays one prompt.** Symptom: 95% hits on the dashboard and 20% in production, because real conversations diverge after the system prompt. Fix: replay sampled production traffic or recorded agent trajectories.","**Assuming the cache stores responses.** Symptom: confusion when the same prompt yields different text at non-zero temperature, which is expected because only K and V are reused. Fix: use a response cache for repeated full prompts.","**One prefix cache across mutually untrusted tenants.** Invisible in metrics, but a hit is measurably faster than a miss, so a tenant can probe whether someone recently sent a given prefix (a timing side channel). Isolate tenants by replica or mix a per-tenant salt into the block hash, which some engines support, at the cost of cross-tenant hits."],"when":"- **Use when** prompts share a long common beginning: product system prompts, few-shot blocks, tool\n  schemas, multi-turn chat, and agent loops that re-send the trajectory each step. That is the majority of\n  production workloads and almost every agent.\n- **Measure** prefix hit rate (or cached tokens per agent step) and TTFT before and after, and check the\n  preemption counter to confirm cached blocks are not starving live sequences.\n- **Expect little** for single-shot workloads with unique prompts, such as bulk document summarization.\n- **On managed APIs,** put the stable part first and add explicit cache breakpoints where the provider\n  requires them.\n- **Interviews:** explain why causal attention makes reuse exact, why one changed token invalidates\n  everything after it, and how routing affects fleet hit rate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Restructures prompts so stable content comes first, enables the flag or cache breakpoints, and tracks hit rate and TTFT as a pair in the serving dashboard.","architect":"Decides on cache-aware routing versus simple load balancing and how much of the pool cached prefixes may hold, trading hit rate against live concurrency.","fde":"Audits a customer's prompt templates for volatile tokens near the top and shows the TTFT and cost difference with a before-and-after measurement on their traffic."},"check":{"q":"A customer's agent loop sends a 4k-token system prompt plus the growing trajectory on every tool\nstep, and complains that each step's latency grows and that prefix caching \"does not work\" on their\nfour-replica deployment. What would you check, and what would you change?","a":"First confirm the prefix really is identical across steps: look for a timestamp, step counter or\nfreshly serialized tool list near the top, and check that the chat template emits the system prompt\nidentically whether or not tools are present. Second, check routing: four replicas behind round-robin\nmeans each step lands on a replica that likely has not seen the prefix, so the fleet hit rate is poor\neven if the engine's is fine; route by conversation id or prefix hash so a trajectory sticks to one\nreplica. Third, verify the trajectory is append-only: if the agent rewrites or summarizes earlier steps,\nevery block after the edit is invalidated, which is correct behaviour and must be weighed against context\nsavings. Finally check the preemption and eviction metrics; if the pool is saturated the cached blocks are\nbeing evicted between steps, and the fix is memory or admission limits, not the cache flag. With those in\nplace, each step should prefill only the newest tool result, and step latency should stop growing with\ntrajectory length except for the decode-side cache-read term."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the KV cache of a prompt prefix be reused exactly by a different request?","options":["Because the engine stores the model's output for that prefix","Because with causal attention, K and V at each position depend only on the tokens before it, so an identical prefix yields identical cache","Because the attention weights are averaged across requests","Because prefix tokens are not attended to by later tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Causality makes the cache for positions 1..n a pure function of tokens 1..n. Nothing that follows can change it, so the vectors computed once are correct for any request beginning with those tokens. Outputs are not stored; later tokens do attend to the prefix."},{"q":"A system prompt is 3,000 tokens and byte-identical across requests, but the measured hit rate is near zero. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The system prompt is too long to cache","Temperature is set above zero, which disables caching","A volatile token such as a timestamp or request id appears before the system prompt in the rendered prompt","The model uses grouped-query attention, which is incompatible with prefix caching"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Block hashes include everything before the block, so any difference at the start changes every hash. Length is not a problem, sampling settings do not affect K and V, and GQA simply makes the cache smaller."},{"q":"What is the main reason prefix caching under-delivers on a multi-replica deployment?","options":["Each replica has a smaller KV pool, so eviction is more frequent","Without cache-aware routing, repeat requests land on replicas that have not computed the prefix","Hashes are computed differently on each replica","Replicas cannot share GPU memory over the network"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cache is local to a GPU. If a load balancer spreads a tenant's requests across replicas, most of them are cold on arrival. Sticky or hash-based routing fixes it; the engine was never the problem."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/continuous-batching","sources":["Yu et al., 2022 — Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Agrawal et al., 2023 — SARATHI: Efficient LLM Inference by Piggybacking Decodes with Chunked Prefills"],"why":"- **Decode needs many tokens per weight read.** It is memory-bandwidth-bound, one token per full read of the\n  weights per request, so the only way to use the hardware is to batch requests so one step advances all\n  of them.\n- **Static request-level batching breaks on generation.** Collect *B* requests, run until all finish, then\n  collect the next *B*: fine for fixed-cost models. But one request stops after 20 tokens and another runs\n  to 2,000, and the early finisher's slot idles, padded with nothing useful, until the longest ends.\n- **Agent steps make the skew worse.** A tool call is tens of tokens; a final answer or code file is\n  thousands. A static batch of agent steps would wait on the one run writing its report.\n- **The cost is twofold.** Utilization collapses toward that of the single longest sequence, and arrivals\n  wait for the remaining life of the current batch, because no new request can start until it drains.\n- **Smaller or time-boxed batches trade one waste for another.** Small batches lose the bandwidth\n  amortization that was the point; time-boxing kills long generations.\n- **The fix: stop treating the batch as a unit of work.** Every step is just \"take one token from each of\n  these sequences\", so membership can change between steps. A finished sequence frees its slot and KV\n  memory immediately; a waiting request is prefilled and joins next step. The original paper called it\n  iteration-level scheduling.\n- **Paging made it scale.** Orca, which introduced it, still reserved each sequence's maximum length\n  contiguously, so only a small batch fit. Paged KV memory (vLLM) let the batch grow to sizes the hardware\n  could use, because joining and leaving need no contiguous allocation or copying, and no slot is reserved\n  for tokens that may never be generated.","how":"### The scheduler loop\nEach iteration the engine does roughly this:\n\n1. **Retire.** Remove sequences that emitted a stop token, reached `max_tokens`, or were cancelled; free\n   their KV blocks. An agent step ending in a tool call retires here and frees its slot during the tool wait.\n2. **Admit.** Pull waiting requests, oldest first (or by priority), while two budgets hold:\n   - running sequences stay under `max_num_seqs`;\n   - tokens processed this iteration stay under `max_num_batched_tokens`. A newcomer's prompt counts its\n     full length (minus any prefix-cache hit), because its prefill runs this step; a running request\n     counts one token.\n3. **Check memory.** Ensure every running sequence can get at least one more block; if not, preempt the most\n   recently admitted ones (recompute or swap) until the rest fit.\n4. **Run one forward pass** over the union: prefill tokens for newcomers and one decode token per running\n   sequence, packed into one batch with per-sequence block tables.\n5. **Sample and stream** one token per sequence to its client, then loop.\n\n### Why one forward pass can mix prefill and decode\n- **Weights do not care which sequence a token belongs to.** Only attention needs the boundaries, and with\n  block tables each query reads its own sequence's cache.\n- **Example:** 3 newcomers of 500 tokens plus 60 decoding sequences is a single batch of 1,560 tokens, close\n  to compute-bound and therefore efficient.\n- **Decode tokens ride along almost free,** because the weights were going to be read anyway.\n\n### The interference problem and chunked prefill\n- **Mixing has a cost.** A 10k-token newcomer makes the iteration as long as its prefill, and all 60\n  decoding users see a pause. In an agent fleet that newcomer is often a late step with an uncached\n  transcript.\n- **Early implementations picked a policy:** prefill-first (admit eagerly, accept TPOT spikes, minimize\n  TTFT) or decode-first (defer admissions until decode is quiet, accept TTFT growth).\n- **Chunked prefill removes the dilemma.** Cap prefill tokens per iteration and split a long prompt across\n  steps, each also carrying every decode token. TPOT stays smooth, the long prompt's TTFT grows a little,\n  and every step is filled up to the token budget.\n\n### Throughput versus latency inside the loop\n- **Step time grows slowly with batch size** (weights read once; cache reads and compute grow with tokens),\n  while tokens per step grow linearly. Throughput rises steeply until the step becomes compute- or\n  cache-bound, and every user's TPOT rises along the way.\n- **Two dials:** `max_num_seqs` and the token budget. Raise them to sell throughput, lower them to sell\n  latency. For agents, every point of TPOT is paid on every step of every run.\n- **Little's law sizes the batch you need** in front of the loop:\n\n```\nrunning sequences ≈ arrival rate × time each spends generating\n5 requests/s × (300 output tokens × ~20 ms TPOT = 6 s) ≈ 30 sequences in flight\n```\n\n- **So a `max_num_seqs` of 16 guarantees a growing queue** however fast each step is.\n- **For an agent fleet, count generating steps, not runs.** A run waiting on a tool holds no slot, so in\n  flight ≈ active runs × (decode time per step ÷ total time per step).\n- **If arrivals exceed what the loop retires,** the queue grows without bound and TTFT explodes: the signal\n  for backpressure or more replicas.\n\n### What it does not fix\n- **Single-request decode** stays at the bandwidth floor; nothing makes a batch of one efficient. A lone\n  agent run on an idle replica is no faster than the floor.\n- **Memory becomes the binding constraint,** because the batch is limited by how many caches fit, not a\n  fixed batch size. That is why this lesson and the KV-cache lessons are inseparable.","example":"### Static versus continuous batching, simulated\nOne workload under both schedulers, an illustrative step-time model (a fixed weight-read time plus a small per-token cost), and\ndeliberately skewed output lengths, as real traffic is.\n\n```python\nimport random\nrandom.seed(0)\nN, MAX_SEQS = 200, 32\nlengths = [random.choice([20, 50, 100, 400, 1500]) for _ in range(N)]   # output tokens per request\n\ndef step_ms(n_tokens):               # illustrative: 5 ms weight read + 0.02 ms per token\n    return 5.0 + 0.02 * n_tokens\n\ndef static_batching():\n    t = 0.0\n    for i in range(0, N, MAX_SEQS):\n        batch = lengths[i:i + MAX_SEQS]\n        for step in range(max(batch)):                       # batch lives until its longest finishes\n            active = sum(1 for L in batch if L > step)       # idle slots are wasted\n            t += step_ms(active)\n    return t\n\ndef continuous_batching():\n    t, waiting, running = 0.0, list(lengths), []\n    while waiting or running:\n        running = [L for L in running if L > 0]              # retire finished sequences\n        while waiting and len(running) < MAX_SEQS:           # admit newcomers into freed slots\n            running.append(waiting.pop(0))\n        t += step_ms(len(running))\n        running = [L - 1 for L in running]\n    return t\n\ntotal_tokens = sum(lengths)\nfor name, fn in ((\"static\", static_batching), (\"continuous\", continuous_batching)):\n    ms = fn()\n    print(f\"{name:11s}: {ms/1000:6.1f} s total, {total_tokens/ms*1000:8.0f} tok/s\")\n# With this seed: static ~54 s (~1.6k tok/s), continuous ~20 s (~4.5k tok/s), close to 3x,\n# because static batches spend most steps at low occupancy waiting for the 1500-token\n# outliers. Make the length skew harsher and the gap widens; make lengths uniform and it vanishes.\n```\n\n- **Why it is clean:** it ignores prefill and memory, isolating the one effect continuous batching is\n  responsible for.\n- **Extend it:** add a random long prefill to `step_ms` for newcomers and you reproduce the TPOT spike that\n  chunked prefill exists to smooth.\n\n### Agent scenario: how many slots does a 100-run agent fleet need?\nEach step decodes a 150-token tool call at ~25 ms TPOT, then waits on a tool. Illustrative numbers.\n\n```python\ndef agent_fleet(active_runs, out_tokens, tpot_ms, tool_wait_s, new_prompt_tokens):\n    gen_s = out_tokens * tpot_ms / 1000                 # time one step spends decoding\n    step_s = gen_s + tool_wait_s                        # one loop iteration of one agent run\n    in_flight = active_runs * gen_s / step_s            # Little's law: only generating steps hold a slot\n    steps_per_s = active_runs / step_s\n    return round(in_flight), round(steps_per_s, 1), round(steps_per_s * new_prompt_tokens)\n\nprint(agent_fleet(100, 150, 25, 2.0, 1_500))    # (65, 17.4, 26087)  prefix hits: newest tool result only\nprint(agent_fleet(100, 150, 25, 2.0, 20_000))   # (65, 17.4, 347826) misses: whole transcript every step\nprint(agent_fleet(100, 150, 25, 0.2, 1_500))    # (95, 25.3, 37975)  fast tools: runs come back sooner\n```\n\n| Case | Sequences in flight | Steps admitted per s | Prefill tokens per s |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 s tools, prefix cache hits | 65 | 17.4 | ~26k |\n| 2 s tools, cache misses | 65 | 17.4 | ~348k |\n| 0.2 s tools, prefix cache hits | 95 | 25.3 | ~38k |\n\n- **Read it as:** a `max_num_seqs` of 32 would queue these steps; and cache misses multiply prefill work\n  per iteration about 13x, which is what turns admissions into TPOT stalls without chunked prefill.\n- **Faster tools raise demand,** because runs spend less time off the GPU.\n\n### Sizing `max_num_seqs` with Little's law\nThe section's numbers as a calculator: what the loop must hold, and how fast a cap lets it retire work.\n\n```python\ndef in_flight(arrivals_per_s, out_tokens, tpot_ms):\n    return arrivals_per_s * out_tokens * tpot_ms / 1000      # Little's law: L = lambda x W\n\ndef max_retire_rate(max_num_seqs, out_tokens, tpot_ms):\n    return max_num_seqs / (out_tokens * tpot_ms / 1000)      # requests/s the cap can finish\n\nprint(in_flight(5, 300, 20))                 # 30.0 sequences needed on average\nfor cap in (16, 32, 48):\n    print(cap, round(max_retire_rate(cap, 300, 20), 2))\n# 16 -> 2.67 req/s: below 5 arrivals/s, queue grows ~2.3 req/s (~140 per minute)\n# 32 -> 5.33 req/s: barely above, so queueing delay is large at any burst\n# 48 -> 8.0 req/s:  headroom, if TPOT stays near 20 ms at that batch (measure it)\n```\n\n| Day vs night (illustrative) | Arrivals | TPOT | Needed in flight |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Night | 5 req/s | ~20 ms | 30 |\n| Day, rate doubles | 10 req/s | ~25 ms (bigger batches, more cache reads) | 75 |\n\n- **Read it as:** doubling traffic more than doubles the needed batch, because TPOT rises with the batch.\n  The cap you can afford is set by the TPOT budget; beyond it, add replicas.","patterns":["**Pick dials from a load-test knee on agent traces.** Sweep `--max-num-seqs` and `--max-num-batched-tokens` (vLLM) while replaying recorded agent runs (real prompt lengths, output lengths and tool waits), record TTFT, TPOT and tokens/s together, and choose the highest concurrency whose TPOT p95 is inside budget. Re-run on every model or hardware change.","**Chunked prefill on mixed workloads.** Keep `--enable-chunked-prefill` on (the default in recent vLLM) unless prompts are uniformly short; agent steps rarely are. Monitor per-step time and TPOT p99 when long prompts arrive.","**Short steps retire fast.** Set a per-step `max_tokens` sized to the tool-call schema and stop at the end of the call, and cancel requests the agent abandons (a timed-out step, a losing parallel branch) so their slots and blocks free at once. Monitor output-length p99 per step type.","**Backpressure at the gateway.** Reject or shed load (HTTP 429 or 503 with `Retry-After`) when the engine's waiting-queue depth (vLLM's `vllm:num_requests_waiting`) passes a threshold derived from the TTFT budget. Agent clients should back off with jitter rather than retry instantly. Monitor rejection rate and TTFT p99.","**Scale on the queue, not CPU.** Autoscale replicas when waiting requests stay above zero for a sustained window or arrivals approach the retirement rate. Monitor queue depth trend and preemptions per replica.","**Priority for interactive agents over eval sweeps.** Where the engine supports a priority scheduling policy (vLLM has a priority mode alongside first-come-first-served), give interactive agent steps precedence over batch jobs and offline eval runs sharing the replica. Monitor TTFT per priority class."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent traffic is the skewed mix this was built for: a 30-token tool call\n  next to a 2,000-token final report, long prompts on every step, and runs that pause for tools. Each step\n  waits in the engine's queue, so scheduler dials multiply across a 10-step task.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Self-hosted engines (vLLM, SGLang, TGI) do it for you; you set\n  `--max-num-seqs`, `--max-num-batched-tokens` and `--enable-chunked-prefill` in vLLM, and read\n  `vllm:num_requests_waiting`. Hosted APIs do it behind rate limits and latency you observe.\n- **What you control.** Per-step `max_tokens` and stop conditions (short steps retire fast), cancelling\n  abandoned steps, prefix stability (less prefill per iteration), concurrency per agent run, and priority\n  between interactive agents and batch eval runs.\n- **What you can skip.** Kernel-level batching of attention. Know the scheduler loop, the two budgets,\n  chunked prefill and Little's law.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosted agent platforms.** Continuous batching is why one replica can serve dozens of agent runs\n  whose steps start and finish at different times.\n- **Mixed tool-call and long-answer traffic.** Short tool calls retire within a few hundred steps while a\n  long report keeps decoding, without either waiting for the other.\n- **Sharing GPUs between live agents and eval sweeps.** Priority scheduling and per-class caps keep batch\n  evaluation from inflating interactive step latency.\n- **Capacity planning for agent fleets.** Little's law on generating steps turns run counts and tool\n  latency into a `max_num_seqs` target and a replica count.","mini_project":"**Load-test an engine with agent-shaped traffic (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find the `max_num_seqs` knee for an agent workload and see chunked prefill's effect.\n- **Steps:** serve a small model with vLLM; write an async client that runs N fake agents, each looping\n  10 steps of a growing prompt, a short capped generation and a sleep standing in for a tool; sweep N and\n  `--max-num-seqs`, then repeat with chunked prefill off; record TTFT, TPOT and task time per run.\n- **Done when:** you have a chart of task time and TPOT p95 against concurrency, the knee is marked, and a\n  short note compares your measured in-flight count with the Little's-law estimate."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Continuous batching makes each request faster.\"** It raises aggregate throughput and cuts queue wait, since newcomers join the next step instead of waiting for a batch to drain. It does not speed up decode: a lone request still runs at the bandwidth floor, and in a busy batch each user's TPOT rises, because every step reads more cache.","**\"It is dynamic batching with a timeout, as in classic model servers.\"** Those still run a batch as one unit until its longest member finishes. Here membership changes every decode step: a finished sequence frees its slot and blocks at once and a waiting one joins the next step.","**\"Set `max_num_seqs` as high as memory allows.\"** Every extra sequence adds TPOT that an agent pays on every step of every run. Size the cap from Little's law and a TPOT budget, then add replicas.","**\"Each agent run holds a batch slot for its whole life.\"** Only generating steps do. 100 runs with 2 s tool waits and 150-token steps at ~25 ms TPOT need about 65 slots, and faster tools raise that to about 95.","**\"Twice the traffic needs twice the batch.\"** Bigger batches raise TPOT, which lengthens each sequence's time in flight. In the day-versus-night example below, doubling 5 req/s to 10 req/s moves the needed in-flight count from 30 to 75."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[(\"Waiting queue\")] --> AD[\"Admit under max_num_seqs and token budget\"]\n    RT[\"Retire: stop token, max_tokens, cancel\"] --> AD\n    AD --> MEM{\"Every sequence gets a block?\"}\n    MEM -->|\"no\"| PR[\"Preempt newest sequences\"]\n    PR --> Q\n    MEM -->|\"yes\"| FP[\"One forward pass: prefill chunks plus decode tokens\"]\n    FP --> SS[\"Sample and stream one token each\"]\n    SS --> RT\n    RT -.->|\"free KV blocks\"| MEM","caption":"One pass of the iteration-level scheduler; the loop runs once per decode step, so batch membership changes\nevery step and agent steps that end in a tool call leave immediately."},"interview":[{"q":"What is continuous batching and why do LLMs need it when classic ML servers did not?","a":"- **Definition:** scheduling at iteration granularity, so sequences join and leave the running batch every\n  decode step instead of the batch living until its longest member finishes.\n- **Why LLMs need it:** output lengths vary from tens to thousands of tokens, so static batches idle most\n  slots; classic models have fixed cost per request, so their batches finish together."},{"q":"When would you deliberately run smaller batches than the GPU could hold?","a":"- **Interactive products and agents with a TPOT budget:** every admitted sequence lengthens each step for\n  everyone, and an agent pays that on every step of its run.\n- **Long-tailed lengths:** optimistic admission near the memory limit invites preemption storms, as when\n  many agent runs reach late, long-transcript steps together.\n- **The cost** is lower tokens per GPU and more replicas, so do it per endpoint class, not fleet-wide."},{"q":"Your load test showed 3x throughput but production users say streaming stutters. Debug it.","a":"- **Check the length mix:** a uniform-length test hides both the gains and the interference of skewed real\n  traffic.\n- **Correlate stalls with long-prompt arrivals;** an unchunked prefill stretches one step for every decoding\n  user.\n- **Fix:** enable chunked prefill with a token budget, and cap `max_num_seqs` at the TPOT budget rather than\n  at peak throughput."},{"q":"You run 100 concurrent agents on one self-hosted replica. How do you size `max_num_seqs`?","a":"- **Count generating steps, not runs:** with 150-token tool calls at ~25 ms TPOT and 2 s tool waits, about\n  65 sequences are in flight on average.\n- **Check the TPOT knee there:** if TPOT p95 at ~65 sequences breaks the step budget, add a replica rather\n  than raising the cap.\n- **Recheck when tools speed up,** because runs return sooner and demand rises (about 95 with 0.2 s tools)."},{"q":"Interactive agents and a nightly eval sweep share a replica, and users complain during the sweep. What do you do?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** the sweep fills the waiting queue and the batch, so interactive steps wait longer for\n  admission and every step decodes at a higher TPOT.\n- **Fixes:** a priority scheduling policy where the engine offers one, a concurrency cap on the eval runner,\n  or a separate replica class for batch work.\n- **Measure** TTFT and TPOT per traffic class before and after."},{"q":"Arrivals are 5 requests/s, outputs average 300 tokens at ~20 ms TPOT. Is `max_num_seqs` 16 enough?","a":"- **Little's law:** 5 × 6 s ≈ 30 sequences must be in flight on average.\n- **A cap of 16** retires at most ~2.7 requests/s, so the queue grows by over 2 per second and TTFT climbs\n  without bound.\n- **Raise the cap** if TPOT at ~30-48 sequences stays in budget; otherwise add a replica and backpressure."}],"tradeoffs":["**Saturated GPU vs per-user TPOT.** It multiplies throughput, but every admitted request lengthens each step and every user's TPOT. The balance flips toward smaller `max_num_seqs` for interactive products, and agents pay that TPOT once per step.","**Eager vs deferred admission.** Eager admission minimizes TTFT but injects prefill into decode steps; deferring does the opposite. Chunked prefill blunts the trade at the cost of slightly longer TTFT on long prompts.","**Memory as the limit vs preemption risk.** Iteration-level scheduling makes memory, not batch size, the limit, which packs far more requests but introduces preemption as a failure mode when the sequence-length tail is long, as with late steps of long agent runs.","**Large vs small token budget.** A large per-iteration budget raises utilization on long-prompt workloads but makes each step heavier; on chat with short prompts a smaller budget gives smoother streaming."],"pitfalls":["**Benchmarking with uniform output lengths.** Symptom: little gain over static batching in the load test, then surprise when production's skewed lengths behave completely differently. Fix: replay output lengths sampled from production logs or recorded agent traces.","**Raising `max_num_seqs` until throughput peaks and shipping that.** Symptom: excellent tokens/sec with complaints that text streams slowly and agent tasks take longer, because TPOT was never on the dashboard. Fix: cap it where TPOT p95 meets the budget.","**Chunked prefill disabled on a mixed workload.** Symptom: periodic stalls across all streams that correlate with long-prompt arrivals rather than request rate. Fix: enable it with a token budget.","**An unbounded waiting queue.** Symptom: TTFT climbing linearly over minutes under steady overload, because the loop retires fewer sequences per second than arrive. Fix: backpressure at the gateway and scale on queue depth.","**Sizing the fleet by active agent runs.** Symptom: slots sit idle or the queue explodes when tool latency changes. Fix: size by generating steps (runs × decode share of each step) and re-check when tools get faster.","**Expecting better single-request latency.** Symptom: disappointment when a lightly loaded server's TPOT equals the bandwidth floor regardless of settings. Fix: use per-request levers such as weight quantization or speculative decoding."],"when":"Every production LLM engine does this; what you choose is the dials.\n- **Set the dials from a load test:** `max_num_seqs` and the token budget, recording TTFT, TPOT and\n  tokens/sec together, at the point where TPOT is still inside the product's (or agent step's) budget.\n- **Enable chunked prefill** unless the workload is purely short prompts.\n- **Watch overload signals:** queue depth and preemptions; add replicas or backpressure when the queue grows\n  faster than it drains.\n- **Building on a raw model library instead of an engine?** This is the first feature you are missing, and\n  the reason to switch before you serve any agent fleet.\n- **Interviews:** why static batching wastes slots, how iteration-level scheduling fixes it, why it needs\n  paged memory, and what chunked prefill adds.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes `max_num_seqs`, the per-iteration token budget and chunked prefill from load tests, and plots TPOT against concurrency to find the knee for the product's latency budget.","architect":"Sets the throughput-versus-latency policy per endpoint class, decides replica counts from the loop's retirement rate versus arrival rate, and defines the backpressure contract.","fde":"Demonstrates to a customer why their homegrown batching server caps out early, and reproduces their stutter by injecting long prompts into a loaded replica before proposing chunked prefill."},"check":{"q":"A team's engine shows 85% KV-cache utilization, average batch size 12, and users report that\nresponses stream fast at night and slowly during the day. Request rate doubles during the day. Explain\nwhat the scheduler is doing and how you would tune it without buying hardware.","a":"(Note the metric: KV-cache utilization, not GPU memory, which an engine pre-allocates and which\ntherefore always reads ~90%.) At night the running batch is small, so each decode step reads the\nweights plus a few caches and TPOT sits near the bandwidth floor. During the day the loop admits more sequences up to `max_num_seqs` or\nthe memory limit; average batch 12 is a daily average hiding daytime batches several times larger. Each\nstep now reads many caches and carries newcomers' prefill, so TPOT grows and streaming slows; that is the\nexpected throughput-for-latency trade, not a bug. First, put TPOT and batch size per step on the\ndashboard to confirm. Then enable chunked prefill so daytime admissions stop injecting stalls. Next,\ndecide the TPOT budget and cap `max_num_seqs` at the concurrency where the load test crosses it, accepting\nthat excess requests queue and TTFT rises; if the queue then grows without bound, the fleet is undersized\nand backpressure or a replica is the honest answer. Prefix caching for the shared system prompt reduces\nprefill tokens per iteration and helps both metrics. Only after those should quantization or hardware\nenter the conversation."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the core change continuous batching makes relative to static request-level batching?","options":["It pads shorter sequences so that all sequences in a batch finish together","It lets sequences join and leave the batch at every decode iteration rather than waiting for the whole batch to finish","It runs prefill and decode on separate GPUs","It increases the batch size until the GPU is compute-bound"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The unit of scheduling becomes one iteration. Finished sequences free their slots immediately and waiting requests fill them, so occupancy stays high regardless of length skew. Padding is what static batching does; disaggregation and batch sizing are separate techniques."},{"q":"Why does continuous batching gain so much from paged KV-cache memory?","options":["Paging compresses the cache so more sequences fit in a static batch","Without paging each sequence reserves its maximum length contiguously, so few fit and the batch stays small; blocks let sequences join, grow and leave without reservations or copies","Paging allows prefill to be skipped for new sequences","Paging makes decode compute-bound"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Iteration-level scheduling works without paging (Orca did it), but contiguous worst-case reservations cap the number of resident sequences far below what memory could hold, and growing in place forces copies. Block-based allocation removes both limits. Skipping prefill is prefix caching, a different feature."},{"q":"What problem does chunked prefill solve inside a continuous-batching scheduler?","options":["It reduces the total compute required to prefill a long prompt","It lets prompts exceed the model's context window","It prevents a long newcomer's prefill from making one iteration so slow that every decoding user sees a stall","It allows prefill to run on the CPU while decode runs on the GPU"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mixing a long prefill into one step stretches that step for everyone. Chunking spreads the prefill across several steps, each still carrying all decode tokens, so TPOT stays smooth. Total work and context limits are unchanged."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/pagedattention","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **Every token in flight costs memory.** About 0.5 MB per token for a Llama-2-7B-style model in fp16\n  (see the KV-cache lesson for the derivation).\n- **Output length is unknown at admission.** Kernels before PagedAttention expected each sequence's K and\n  V to be contiguous, so the only safe move was a contiguous slab for the maximum length. For a\n  2,048-token limit that is about 1 GB per request, whether it generates 20 tokens or 2,000.\n- **Reservation wastes memory three ways.** *Reserved* slots hold tokens not generated yet; *internal\n  fragmentation* is the part a request never reaches because it stops early; *external fragmentation* is\n  holes between slabs of different sizes, too small for the next arrival.\n- **Most memory was waste.** The vLLM paper measured that in prior systems only roughly 20–40% of\n  KV-cache memory actually held token state.\n- **The naive fixes fail.** Exact-length allocation is impossible (length unknown). Reallocate-and-copy\n  copies hundreds of megabytes every few tokens and still fragments. A lower length limit refuses\n  legitimate long requests. The answer was the OS move for RAM: drop physical contiguity and translate\n  addresses through a table.","how":"### Why agent traffic is the worst case for contiguous allocation\n- **Length variance is extreme.** One agent answers in 2 steps at 7.5k tokens; another loops 20 times to\n  34.5k tokens. A contiguous allocator must reserve the route's maximum for both.\n- **Growth happens between calls.** Each step appends a tool call and its result, then the engine\n  decodes again, so the cache grows in bursts the engine cannot predict at admission.\n- **Agents share a lot.** Every run starts with the same system prompt and tool schemas, often thousands\n  of tokens. Block sharing stores that prefix once instead of once per agent.\n\n### Blocks and block tables\n- **Physical blocks.** The cache is carved into blocks of a fixed number of tokens, B. For one layer a\n  block holds K and V for B tokens across all KV heads: `2 × n_kv_heads × head_dim × B ×\n  bytes_per_element`.\n- **Worked size.** Llama-2-7B, fp16, B = 16: 2 × 32 × 128 × 16 × 2 = 262,144 bytes, 256 KB per layer, or\n  8 MB per block summed over 32 layers. That is exactly 16 tokens × 0.5 MB.\n- **Logical blocks.** Each request owns a sequence of logical blocks (block 0 holds tokens 0–15, block 1\n  holds 16–31, and so on).\n- **Block table.** Maps each logical block to the physical block holding it. Logical blocks are\n  contiguous; physical blocks can be anywhere in the pool. This is a page table, and the lookup is the\n  address translation.\n\n### Allocation on demand\n1. **Admit** a request with only the blocks its prompt needs.\n2. **Grow** by one block when the last block fills during decode.\n3. **Waste** is only the unused tail of the final block, strictly less than B tokens per sequence.\n4. **No external fragmentation**, because every block has the same size, so any free block fits any\n   request. Fixed-size units are the one scheme where freed space is always reusable, which is why the\n   OS page allocator works the same way.\n\n### Background: the attention kernel\n- **Inputs.** A block-table tensor of shape [num_seqs, max_blocks_per_seq] plus each sequence's current\n  length.\n- **Per sequence.** Take the new token's query, walk that row of the table, load K and V from each\n  physical block, and accumulate attention block by block.\n- **Online softmax.** Softmax needs the maximum and sum over *all* scores, unknown until the last block.\n  The kernel keeps a running maximum *m*, a running denominator *l* and a running weighted sum of V, and\n  rescales by `exp(m_old − m_new)` whenever a later block raises the maximum (the FlashAttention trick).\n  The output is exact; only the layout changed.\n- **The indirection costs something.** Accesses are less coalesced than for one contiguous tensor, so the\n  kernel is somewhat slower. The design accepts that *because* decode throughput is limited by how many\n  sequences fit, not per-sequence kernel speed.\n- **The name outlived the kernel.** Current engines often run FlashAttention- or FlashInfer-family kernels\n  that accept a block table directly, so \"PagedAttention\" now names the memory-management scheme.\n\n### Sizing the pool\n1. Measure what the weights take.\n2. Run a dummy forward pass at the maximum batch to find peak activation memory.\n3. Give the rest of the configured budget (vLLM's `gpu_memory_utilization`, historically defaulting to\n   0.9) to the block pool.\n4. Capacity in tokens is `num_blocks × B`; the scheduler admits a request only when enough free blocks\n   exist for its prompt.\n\n### Sharing and copy-on-write\n- **Reference counts.** Requests reference blocks indirectly, so two can map the same physical block.\n- **Within a request.** Parallel sampling (`n > 1`) and beam search share the prompt's blocks instead of\n  copying them.\n- **Across requests (prefix caching).** Full blocks are hashed by their content and the prefix before\n  them, so a matching system prompt reuses resident blocks and skips that part of prefill.\n- **Copy-on-write.** Before writing into a shared block the engine copies it, the same rule as `fork()`.\n\n### Running out of blocks\n- **Preemption.** On-demand allocation can run the pool dry mid-generation. The scheduler then swaps a\n  sequence's blocks to CPU memory or frees them and recomputes the prefix later. Which an engine offers\n  varies by version: vLLM's V1 engine recomputes only.\n\n| Option | Cost grows with | Rough fit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Swap | cache bytes over PCIe | long sequences |\n| Recompute | prefill FLOPs to rebuild | short sequences |\n\n- **Neither wins universally.** The crossover depends on hardware and how busy the GPU already is.\n- **The signal.** Preemption counters rising in the metrics mean the pool is too small for the traffic.\n\n### Choosing the block size\n| Block size | Gains | Costs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Larger | fewer table entries, better coalescing | more waste in the last block, coarser prefix sharing (only complete blocks are shared) |\n| Smaller | less tail waste, finer sharing | more table entries, worse coalescing |\n\nSixteen tokens is the common compromise; deviate only after measuring with your own length distribution.","example":"### Capacity: contiguous vs paged on an 80 GB GPU\nA Llama-3-8B-class model (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, fp16).\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| KV per token | 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes = 128 KB |\n| One 16-token block, all layers | 2 MB |\n| Weights | ~16 GB |\n| Budget at 0.9 | 72 GB |\n| Activation peak (illustrative) | 2 GB |\n| Block pool | about 54 GB = 27,648 blocks ≈ 442,000 tokens |\n\nChats averaging 1,500 actual tokens under an 8,192-token limit:\n\n| Allocator | Memory per request | Requests that fit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contiguous | 8,192 × 128 KB = 1 GB, always | 54 |\n| Paged | ~1,500 tokens + a partial block ≈ 188 MB | around 290 (about 5× more) |\n\nThe paged kernel also never copies anything when a chat runs long. The script reproduces the arithmetic:\n\n```python\ndef kv_capacity(gpu_gb, weights_gb, util=0.9, act_gb=2.0,\n                layers=32, kv_heads=8, head_dim=128, dtype_bytes=2, block=16):\n    per_token = 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * dtype_bytes          # bytes\n    pool = (gpu_gb * util - weights_gb - act_gb) * 2**30\n    blocks = int(pool // (per_token * block))\n    return per_token, blocks, blocks * block\n\nper_tok, blocks, tokens = kv_capacity(80, 16)\nprint(f\"{per_tok/1024:.0f} KB/token, {blocks} blocks, {tokens} tokens of KV\")\nfor avg_len, max_len in [(1500, 8192), (6000, 8192)]:\n    contiguous = tokens // max_len                       # reserves max_len per request\n    paged = tokens // (avg_len + 15)                     # actual tokens + at most one partial block\n    print(f\"avg {avg_len}: contiguous fits {contiguous}, paged fits {paged}\")\n```\n\n### A toy block allocator with sharing and copy-on-write\nPure Python, runnable as is. It shows on-demand growth, `fork()`-style sharing for parallel sampling and\ncopy-on-write of a shared tail block.\n\n```python\nclass BlockPool:\n    def __init__(self, num_blocks, block=16):\n        self.block, self.free = block, list(range(num_blocks))\n        self.ref = [0] * num_blocks\n\n    def alloc(self):\n        if not self.free:\n            raise MemoryError(\"out of blocks: preempt a sequence\")\n        b = self.free.pop()\n        self.ref[b] = 1\n        return b\n\n    def release(self, b):\n        self.ref[b] -= 1\n        if self.ref[b] == 0:\n            self.free.append(b)\n\nclass Sequence:\n    def __init__(self, pool, n_prompt=0):\n        self.pool, self.len, self.table = pool, 0, []\n        for _ in range(n_prompt):\n            self.append()\n\n    def append(self):                              # store one new token's K and V\n        p = self.pool\n        if self.len % p.block == 0:                # last block full, or no block yet\n            self.table.append(p.alloc())\n        elif p.ref[self.table[-1]] > 1:            # copy-on-write a shared partial block\n            p.release(self.table[-1])\n            self.table[-1] = p.alloc()             # (a real engine also copies its K/V)\n        self.len += 1\n\n    def fork(self):                                # parallel sampling: share, do not copy\n        child = Sequence(self.pool)\n        child.len, child.table = self.len, list(self.table)\n        for b in self.table:\n            self.pool.ref[b] += 1\n        return child\n\npool = BlockPool(num_blocks=8)\na = Sequence(pool, n_prompt=40)                    # 40 tokens -> 3 blocks (16 + 16 + 8)\nb = a.fork()                                       # n=2 sampling shares all 3\nprint(a.table, b.table, \"free:\", len(pool.free))   # [7, 6, 5] [7, 6, 5] free: 5\na.append(); b.append()                             # both write into the shared tail block\nprint(a.table, b.table, \"free:\", len(pool.free))   # [7, 6, 4] [7, 6, 5] free: 4\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: how many research agents fit on one GPU\nSame 8B GQA model and pool as above (27,648 blocks ≈ 442,000 tokens).\n\nEach agent starts with a 6k-token prefix (system prompt plus tool schemas) and appends ~1.5k tokens per step after the first, up to 20 steps (34.5k\ntokens, about 4.2 GB of KV). Agents are spread evenly across steps 1 to 20, so the average context is\nabout 20,250 tokens.\n\n| Allocator | Memory charged per agent | Agents resident |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contiguous, `max_model_len` 32,768 | 32,768 tokens, always | 13 |\n| Paged | ~20,250 tokens + a partial block | about 21 |\n| Paged + prefix caching | 6k prefix stored once, ~14,250 unique tokens each | about 30 |\n\n- **The win is smaller than for chat.** Long agents use much of their limit, so paging alone gives\n  ~1.6×, not 5×. Prefix sharing adds most of the rest, because the 6k prefix is 375 shared blocks.\n- **The real limit is the tail.** If all 30 agents reach step 20 together they need ~860,000 tokens,\n  so the pool empties and the engine preempts. Cap steps or compact transcripts before that point.","patterns":["**Lay out agent prompts for block sharing.** Turn on prefix caching (`--enable-prefix-caching` in vLLM; the V1 engine enables it by default). Put the system prompt and tool schemas first and byte-identical across every run, with dates and user IDs after them. Monitor prefix-cache hit rate per agent type and time to first token.","**Compact long agent transcripts at a step boundary.** When a run nears its context cap, summarize old tool results into one block and continue, instead of letting long runs drain the pool and trigger preemptions for every other agent. Monitor context length per step and blocks in use.","**Profile, then set the memory fraction.** When deploying a new model or GPU, start vLLM at the default `gpu_memory_utilization` (0.9) and raise it only after re-profiling activation peaks with your real `max_model_len` and batch size. Monitor KV-cache usage and OOMs.","**Alert on preemptions, not OOMs.** Under lazy allocation a small pool shows up as preemptions and latency jitter. Scrape the engine's Prometheus metrics endpoint and alert when the preemption counter rises steadily while KV-cache usage sits near 100%.","**Cap context per route.** When long-document chats share a deployment with short Q&A, lower `max_model_len` (or route long requests to their own deployment) so a few long chats cannot drain the pool. Monitor block usage per route and p95 prompt length.","**Stretch the pool with an FP8 cache.** When block usage is the bottleneck, set the KV-cache dtype to fp8 (`--kv-cache-dtype fp8` in vLLM) and run your eval set before rollout. It roughly doubles tokens of capacity per GPU versus an fp16 cache."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** You never know how long an agent run will get: a 3-step lookup and a\n  20-step research loop hit the same server. Paged allocation charges each agent only for the tokens it\n  actually holds, so short runs do not pay for the longest one.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Inside vLLM, SGLang and TensorRT-LLM when you self-host the model\n  behind your agent framework. Its knobs are `gpu_memory_utilization`, `max_model_len`, prefix caching\n  and the KV-cache dtype; its symptoms are preemption counters and latency jitter.\n- **What you control.** Prompt layout (shared system prompt and tool schemas first, byte-identical, so\n  blocks are shared across agents), the context cap per route, and when to compact long transcripts.\n- **What you can skip.** The kernel internals and online-softmax math. Know blocks, block tables,\n  copy-on-write and preemption; that is enough to size and debug an agent deployment.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosted agent platforms.** Paged allocation lets one GPU hold dozens of agent runs of wildly\n  different lengths, because each run is charged only for the blocks it has filled.\n- **Shared system prompts and tool schemas.** Many agents with the same byte-identical prefix share its\n  blocks, so the per-agent memory cost is only the transcript after the shared part.\n- **Parallel sampling and best-of-n planners.** Copy-on-write lets several candidate plans branch from one\n  prompt without duplicating its KV cache.\n- **Capacity debugging.** Preemption counts and KV-usage metrics explain latency spikes when long research\n  agents and short lookups share a server.","mini_project":"**Watch blocks fill and share under an agent load (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** see how paged allocation and block sharing change how many agent runs fit on one GPU.\n- **Steps:** serve a small instruct model with vLLM; write a script that starts 20 fake agent loops, each\n  appending a tool result per step, all sharing one long system prompt; run it with prefix caching on and\n  off; scrape the engine's metrics endpoint for KV-cache usage and preemptions every second.\n- **Done when:** you have a chart of KV usage and preemptions over time for both runs, and a short note\n  explaining the gap using block counts (prefix tokens divided by the block size)."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"PagedAttention is a faster attention kernel.\"** The block-table indirection makes the kernel slightly slower. The 2–4× throughput gain comes from fitting more sequences into the batch, so a GPU running one long request sees no benefit.","**\"Paging means the KV cache lives in CPU memory, like OS swap.\"** Blocks are GPU memory; \"paging\" names the logical-to-physical translation. Data leaves the GPU only when the scheduler preempts a sequence, and vLLM's V1 engine recomputes instead of swapping.","**\"Paging removes all waste, so memory can no longer run out.\"** Each sequence still wastes up to B − 1 tokens in its last block, and on-demand growth can drain the pool mid-generation. Exhaustion shows up as preemptions and latency jitter, not as an OOM.","**\"Prefix caching shares any prompts that start with similar text.\"** Only complete blocks whose content and entire preceding prefix are identical are shared. A date on the first line of the system prompt changes every block after it, so the hit rate drops to near zero.","**\"Agents get the same 5× gain as chat.\"** The 5× comes from short chats under a long limit. Long agent runs use much of their limit, so paging alone gives about 1.6× in the worked scenario; prefix sharing supplies most of the rest."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request admitted with prompt\"] --> T[\"Block table: logical to physical\"]\n    T --> P[(\"Physical block pool\")]\n    T --> D[\"Decode: append K, V\"]\n    D --> F{\"Last block full?\"}\n    F -->|\"no\"| W[\"Write into tail block\"]\n    F -->|\"yes\"| A{\"Free block available?\"}\n    A -->|\"yes\"| N[\"Allocate one block\"]\n    N --> T\n    A -->|\"no\"| X[\"Preempt: swap or recompute\"]\n    X -.->|\"frees blocks\"| P\n    K[\"Attention kernel\"] -->|\"walks table row\"| P","caption":"Each agent step is a request whose logical blocks map through its block table to scattered physical\nblocks; a full last block triggers one more allocation, and an empty pool preempts some agent's step."},"interview":[{"q":"What problem does PagedAttention solve, and why is the analogy to virtual memory apt?","a":"- **Problem:** output length is unknown at admission, so contiguous KV buffers must reserve the maximum\n  length, wasting memory to reservation, internal and external fragmentation.\n- **Analogy:** like OS paging, logical blocks map through a per-request table to physical blocks anywhere\n  in the pool, so contiguity is no longer needed and any free block fits any request."},{"q":"How does paging make parallel sampling (n > 1) cheap, and what happens when two samples diverge?","a":"- **Shared blocks by reference count:** all n samples point their block tables at the same prompt blocks,\n  so the prompt's KV is stored once instead of n times.\n- **Copy-on-write at divergence:** when a sample must write into a block another sample still references\n  (the partially filled tail), the engine copies that block first and decrements the old one's count,\n  like `fork()` in an OS.\n- **Cost:** only the diverging tail blocks are duplicated, so memory grows with generated tokens per\n  sample, not with prompt length times n."},{"q":"When would PagedAttention give you little or no benefit?","a":"- **Small batches.** The win comes from fitting more sequences; one long request alone on a GPU only pays\n  the indirection cost of the slower kernel.\n- **Requests that always use their full length.** If actual length is close to the limit, contiguous\n  reservation wastes little, so paging recovers little."},{"q":"Estimate how many 1,500-token chats fit on an 80 GB GPU for an 8B GQA model.","a":"- **Per token:** 2 × 32 layers × 8 KV heads × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KB.\n- **Pool:** 80 × 0.9 − 16 GB weights − ~2 GB activations ≈ 54 GB, about 442,000 tokens.\n- **Answer:** around 290 chats paged versus 54 with contiguous 8,192-token reservations, roughly 5× more."},{"q":"Your self-hosted agents get slow only in the afternoon, with no OOMs. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect the long tail of runs.** As the day goes on, more agents are mid-run with long transcripts,\n  so the block pool empties and the engine preempts and recomputes steps.\n- **Confirm with metrics:** preemption counter rising while KV-cache usage sits near 100%, and context\n  length per step climbing.\n- **Fix:** compact transcripts at a step boundary, cap steps per run, keep the prefix shareable, or\n  admit fewer concurrent agents."},{"q":"How would you design agent prompts to get the most out of block sharing?","a":"- **Stable prefix first:** system prompt and tool schemas byte-identical and at the top, because only\n  whole blocks with identical content and prefix are shared.\n- **Variable content later:** dates, user IDs and retrieved documents after the shared prefix.\n- **Append only:** never rewrite earlier turns, since a change invalidates every block after it."}],"tradeoffs":["**Recovered memory vs kernel speed.** You recover most memory lost to reservation and fragmentation, but the kernel pays an indirection and coalescing cost per block. It is a net loss only when the batch is too small for extra sequences to matter, such as one long request alone on a GPU.","**More admissions vs mid-generation exhaustion.** On-demand allocation admits many more requests, but can run out of blocks mid-generation, so the system needs a preemption policy (swap or recompute) that contiguous reservation never needed.","**Free sharing vs memory-manager complexity.** Block sharing makes beam search, parallel sampling and prefix caching nearly free in memory, at the cost of reference counting and copy-on-write logic that is harder to reason about.","**Large vs small blocks.** Larger blocks give faster kernels and fewer table entries; smaller ones waste less in partial blocks and share prefixes at finer granularity. The best size flips as average request length shrinks.","**More concurrent agents vs tail latency.** Admitting more agents raises GPU throughput, but when many runs grow long at once the pool empties and preemption adds seconds to some agent's step. Admit fewer agents when a slow step breaks a user-facing deadline."],"pitfalls":["**Reading the gain as \"a faster attention kernel\".** The kernel is slightly slower; the paper's 2–4× throughput gain comes from batching more sequences. If your batch is already small, you will not see it.","**Ignoring preemption metrics.** A pool slightly too small shows up as a throughput cliff and jittery latency under load, not as an OOM, because sequences are silently swapped or recomputed.","**Expecting prefix caching to share partially identical prompts.** Only whole blocks with identical content and prefix are shared, so a timestamp in the first line of the system prompt defeats it entirely. The symptom is a prefix-cache hit rate near zero for prompts that look identical.","**Setting `gpu_memory_utilization` to 0.98 for more blocks.** It then OOMs when another process, a CUDA graph or a larger-than-profiled activation peak needs the headroom.","**Sizing agent capacity from the first step.** Load tests with fresh runs show plenty of free blocks; production agents at step 15 do not. Symptom: preemptions appear only hours into real traffic. Fix: load-test with replayed full-length agent traces."],"when":"You do not switch PagedAttention on: every mainstream open engine (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM and others)\nuses block-based KV management. The decision is how to configure and observe it.\n- **Self-hosting agents:** this is the lesson to know when your agent framework calls a vLLM or SGLang\n  endpoint; with a hosted API the provider manages blocks and you only see the prompt-caching effect.\n- **Sizing a deployment:** use the block arithmetic for tokens of capacity, expected tokens per request,\n  and therefore concurrency.\n- **In production:** watch block utilization and preemption counts. They tell you whether to lower\n  `max_model_len`, raise the memory fraction, add prefix caching, or add GPUs.\n- **Interviews:** explain the three kinds of waste, why fixed-size blocks remove two of them, and why the\n  win is measured in concurrent sequences rather than kernel speed.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes block pool size, `max_model_len` and prefix caching in the engine config, and reads preemption and cache-hit metrics to diagnose latency jitter under load.","architect":"Translates block capacity into a concurrency and context-length policy per GPU tier, and decides whether prompt design should favour shared prefixes to exploit block sharing.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their self-hosted engine serves 5× more chats than their old contiguous deployment, and why a dynamic header in their system prompt kills prefix reuse."},"check":{"q":"A team sees aggregate throughput collapse by half whenever traffic shifts from short Q&A to long\ndocument chats, with no OOM errors. Using PagedAttention concepts, what is happening and what would you\nchange?","a":"Long chats consume many more blocks per sequence, so the pool runs out mid-generation and the\nscheduler starts preempting sequences, swapping their blocks to CPU or dropping them and recomputing the\nprefix. Both cost time and the recomputation or swap traffic halves useful throughput while never raising\nan OOM, because allocation is lazy. I would confirm with the preemption and block-usage metrics, then\neither shrink demand per sequence (lower `max_model_len`, shorter retrieved context, fp8 KV cache),\nincrease supply (higher memory fraction after re-profiling activation peaks, a GQA model, more GPUs with\ntensor parallelism), or cap concurrency so admitted requests finish instead of thrashing. If the chats\nshare a long system prompt, enabling prefix caching recovers blocks too."},"quiz":[{"q":"Where does most of PagedAttention's throughput improvement come from?","options":["A faster attention kernel that reads memory in larger contiguous chunks","Fitting far more concurrent sequences in the same KV-cache memory because little is wasted","Skipping attention over padding tokens inside each block","Storing keys and values in 8-bit precision inside each block"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The paged kernel is actually slightly slower than a contiguous one because of the indirection. The gain comes from eliminating reservation and fragmentation waste, so many more sequences are decoded per step on a memory-bound workload."},{"q":"Why does fixed block size eliminate external fragmentation?","options":["Because blocks are allocated at startup and never freed","Because the block table compacts memory after each request finishes","Because any free block can satisfy any allocation, so freed space is always reusable","Because blocks are always allocated in contiguous runs per sequence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"External fragmentation arises when freed holes are the wrong size for the next request. With one block size there is no wrong size; the only waste left is the unused tail of a sequence's last block."},{"q":"Two requests share a 400-token system prompt but one adds a date on its first line. What happens to block sharing under prefix caching?","options":["Both still share all prompt blocks because the hash ignores digits","They share blocks after the differing line once the content realigns","They share nothing, because block hashes include the full prefix and diverge from the first block","They share blocks only if both requests arrive in the same batch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blocks are identified by their content and everything before them, so a difference in the first block changes every subsequent hash. Shared content must be a byte-identical prefix to be reused."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","sources":["Yu et al., 2022 — Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models","Agrawal et al., 2024 — Taming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **Decode is bandwidth-bound.** A Llama-3-8B-class model in fp16 has about 16 GB of weights. At roughly\n  3.35 TB/s (an H100 SXM's published figure), reading them once takes about 4.8 ms, capping one sequence\n  at roughly 200 tokens per second however fast the tensor cores are. The FLOPs for that one token, about\n  2 × 8 billion = 16 GFLOP, take a small fraction of that time: the GPU is mostly waiting on memory.\n- **Batching reuses each weight read.** With 64 sequences the weights are read once for all 64 tokens, so\n  the step takes about the same 4.8 ms plus extra KV-cache reads, and throughput rises toward 64×.\n- **Over-admission ends badly.** KV memory is finite and each sequence holds it for its whole lifetime,\n  so admitting everything leads to preemption, swap traffic and eventually OOM.\n- **Latency objectives.** Past the knee, time per output token (TPOT) climbs with every extra sequence and\n  time to first token (TTFT) climbs with queue depth. Throughput keeps rising while goodput, the fraction\n  of requests meeting their SLO, falls.\n- **Blast radius.** One tenant sending 10,000-token prompts can stall everyone's decode unless something\n  bounds the prefill tokens entering a step. Limits make an engine queue or reject predictably instead of\n  degrading for everyone.","how":"### Agent traffic is calls, not users\n- **One run, many calls.** A tool-calling loop makes one LLM call per step, so a 10-step run is 10\n  requests to the engine, each with a longer context than the last.\n- **Fan-out is instantaneous concurrency.** Parallel tool calls that each need a model call, or a\n  planner that launches 5 subagents, add 5 in-flight requests in the same second.\n- **Context grows inside a run.** Later steps carry the whole transcript, so the same request count\n  costs more KV reads at step 10 than at step 1. That is why limits belong in tokens.\n\n### Background: where batching stops being free\n- **Arithmetic intensity grows with batch.** Each 2-byte bf16 weight does 2 FLOPs (a multiply and an add)\n  per sequence, so a batch of B gives about B FLOPs per weight byte.\n- **The ridge point.** Peak FLOPs ÷ bandwidth is roughly 300 FLOP/byte for that GPU in bf16. Past it the\n  step is compute-bound and latency per token grows with the batch.\n- **Weight matmuls alone** would stay bandwidth-bound up to a batch in the low hundreds.\n- **The knee usually arrives earlier** because each sequence also brings its own KV cache to read, and\n  that traffic does not amortise across the batch (see the latency section below).\n\n### Static, dynamic and continuous batching\n| Mode | How it batches | Fits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Static | groups N requests, runs until the longest finishes, then admits more; GPU idles on finished slots | offline jobs |\n| Dynamic | waits up to a few ms to collect a batch, runs it as a unit (Triton's queue-delay setting is the familiar example) | single-pass models (embedders, classifiers), where every request is one forward pass |\n| Continuous (iteration-level, from Orca) | re-forms the batch every decode step: finished sequences leave, waiting ones join | generation |\n\n- **Dynamic batching still holds a generation batch** until its longest output finishes.\n- **Continuous batching makes batch size dynamic,** bounded by limits rather than a fixed number you\n  pick. The continuous-batching lesson covers the scheduler.\n\n### The three limits inside an engine\nEvery engine exposes some version of these (vLLM's names in parentheses):\n1. **Maximum sequences per step** (`max_num_seqs`). Bounds decode batch size, and so worst-case TPOT once\n   compute-bound, plus the per-step sampling buffers.\n2. **Maximum tokens per step** (`max_num_batched_tokens`). Bounds prompt and decode tokens together in one\n   step. With chunked prefill (Sarathi-Serve's idea) a long prompt is split into chunks that ride along\n   with decode tokens, so a 20k-token prompt cannot monopolise a step and stall every conversation.\n   Lower values give smoother TPOT; higher values give faster prefill.\n3. **KV-cache capacity** (the block pool). The hard ceiling on tokens in flight. The scheduler admits a\n   request only if its prompt fits, and preempts when generation outgrows the pool.\n\n- **Why the token budget can be modest.** Prefill is already batched across positions: a chunk of T\n  prompt tokens reuses each weight T times, so a few hundred to a few thousand tokens already saturate the\n  tensor cores. A larger chunk mainly lengthens the step decode tokens wait on.\n- **Fill order.** Each step takes decode tokens first, then admits waiting prompts up to the token budget,\n  *because* stalling in-flight sequences is visible to users while delaying a new one by a few steps\n  usually is not.\n\n### Where the latency actually goes\n```\nstep_time ≈ max(bytes_read / bandwidth, FLOPs / peak_FLOPs) + fixed overhead\nbytes_read = weights + KV cache of every active sequence\n```\n- **The forgotten term is the KV cache.** With 64 sequences at 6k tokens each on the model above (128 KB\n  of KV per token), the step reads 64 × 6,144 × 128 KB = 48 GiB (about 51.5 GB) of cache on top of 16 GB\n  of weights.\n- **Result:** about 20 ms per step, roughly 50 tokens per second per user instead of 200. Cache reads\n  outweigh the weights three to one and grow with every sequence added.\n- **The full-pool floor.** That configuration nearly fills an 80 GB card. With the pool full, each step\n  reads close to all of HBM, so the step-time floor is roughly HBM capacity ÷ bandwidth (about 24 ms here)\n  however bytes divide between weights and cache.\n- **So express limits in tokens.** Safe concurrency depends on context length, not just request count.\n\n### Little's law and the queue\n- **Concurrency = arrival rate × residence time.** 10 requests/s that each live 8 s need 80 in flight.\n- **Past the knee the queue never drains.** If the knee for your traffic shape is 48, the queue grows\n  without bound. No scheduler setting fixes that; only more replicas, a smaller model, shorter outputs or\n  load shedding do.\n- **This converts an engine benchmark into a replica count.**\n- **For agents, count LLM calls.** Arrival rate is runs per second × calls per run, and residence time is\n  one call's queue + prefill + decode, not the whole run.\n\n### Enforcing the limit at three layers\n| Layer | Mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| Engine | the parameters above plus a bounded waiting queue; when full, fail fast with 429/503 rather than accept a request that will time out anyway |\n| Gateway | per-tenant concurrency and token-rate caps so one client cannot consume the whole knee; priority or fair-share queuing where SLOs differ |\n| Client (agent runtime) | a semaphore sized to its share, a per-run cap on parallel calls and subagents, retries with jitter only on explicit retry-after signals, timeouts longer than expected queueing time so retries do not amplify load |\n\n### Measuring the knee\n1. **Sweep concurrency** with prompts and output lengths that match production.\n2. **Record** aggregate tokens/s, p50 and p95 TTFT and TPOT.\n3. **Plot** throughput against p95 latency: throughput rises steeply then flattens while p95 bends\n   upward. The bend is the knee.\n4. **Re-run** whenever the model, quantization, context distribution or GPU changes, because every one of\n   them moves it.","example":"### Step-time model: why context length moves the knee\nA roofline estimate of the formula above in pure Python. It ignores fixed overhead, so treat outputs as\nillustrative orders of magnitude, not benchmarks.\n\n```python\nBW, PEAK = 3.35e12, 989e12          # H100 SXM: bytes/s, dense bf16 FLOP/s (published figures)\nW_BYTES, PARAMS = 16e9, 8e9         # Llama-3-8B-class in bf16\nKV_PER_TOKEN = 128 * 1024           # 32 layers x 8 KV heads x 128 dim x 2 (K,V) x 2 bytes\n\ndef step_ms(batch, ctx):\n    bytes_read = W_BYTES + batch * ctx * KV_PER_TOKEN      # weights once + every sequence's cache\n    flops = 2 * PARAMS * batch                             # one token per sequence\n    return max(bytes_read / BW, flops / PEAK) * 1e3        # ignores fixed overhead\n\nfor batch, ctx in [(1, 1000), (64, 1000), (64, 6144), (256, 1000)]:\n    ms = step_ms(batch, ctx)\n    print(f\"batch={batch:3d} ctx={ctx:5d} step={ms:5.1f}ms \"\n          f\"per-user={1e3/ms:4.0f} tok/s total={batch*1e3/ms:6.0f} tok/s\")\n```\n\n| Batch | Context | Step | Per user | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1,000 | 4.8 ms | ~208 tok/s | ~208 tok/s |\n| 64 | 1,000 | 7.3 ms | ~137 tok/s | ~8,800 tok/s |\n| 64 | 6,144 | 20.2 ms | ~50 tok/s | ~3,200 tok/s |\n| 256 | 1,000 | 14.8 ms | ~68 tok/s | ~17,300 tok/s |\n\nSame batch of 64, six times the context: total throughput falls by almost two thirds, because the KV\nreads, not the weights, now set the step time.\n\n### Load sweep against an OpenAI-compatible endpoint\nThe client-side semaphore is the concurrency limit; the script reports throughput and tail latency per\nlevel so you can see where the curve flattens. Streaming separates TTFT and TPOT; each streamed chunk\ncounts as one token.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time, httpx\n\nURL, MODEL = \"http://localhost:8000/v1/completions\", \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\"\nPROMPT, MAX_TOK = \"Explain why decode is memory-bandwidth bound.\", 128\n\nasync def one(client, sem):\n    async with sem:                                        # the client-side concurrency limit\n        t0, first, n = time.perf_counter(), None, 0\n        body = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": PROMPT, \"max_tokens\": MAX_TOK, \"stream\": True}\n        async with client.stream(\"POST\", URL, json=body, timeout=300) as r:\n            async for line in r.aiter_lines():\n                if line.startswith(\"data:\") and \"[DONE]\" not in line:\n                    n += 1\n                    first = first or time.perf_counter() - t0\n        total = time.perf_counter() - t0\n        first = total if first is None else first           # no tokens streamed: count as all wait\n        return first, (total - first) / max(n - 1, 1), n   # TTFT, TPOT, tokens\n\nasync def sweep(level, requests=64):\n    sem, t0 = asyncio.Semaphore(level), time.perf_counter()\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:\n        res = await asyncio.gather(*(one(client, sem) for _ in range(requests)))\n    wall = time.perf_counter() - t0\n    ttft = sorted(r[0] for r in res); tpot = sorted(r[1] for r in res)\n    print(f\"conc={level:3d} tok/s={sum(r[2] for r in res)/wall:7.0f} \"\n          f\"p50 TTFT={ttft[len(ttft)//2]*1e3:6.0f}ms p95 TPOT={tpot[int(len(tpot)*.95)-1]*1e3:5.1f}ms\")\n\nfor level in (1, 4, 16, 64):\n    asyncio.run(sweep(level))\n# Expect tok/s to climb steeply then flatten while p95 TPOT bends upward: that bend is the knee.\n# Re-run with your real prompt and output length distribution before choosing limits.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: from benchmark to replica count\n| Step | Number |\n|---|---|\n| Target traffic | 10 requests/s |\n| Residence time per request (queue + prefill + decode) | 8 s |\n| Required concurrency (Little's law) | 10 × 8 = 80 in flight |\n| Measured knee per replica, production-shaped traffic | 48 |\n| Replicas | 80 ÷ 48 ≈ 1.7, so 2 at the knee, 3 with headroom for bursts and one replica down |\n\n### Agent runtime: a shared call budget plus a per-run fan-out cap\nA planner fans out to subagents. A global semaphore keeps total in-flight LLM calls at the engine's\nknee; a per-run semaphore stops one run's fan-out from taking the whole budget. The model call is faked\nwith a sleep so the script runs as is.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, random, time\n\nKNEE, MAX_FANOUT = 48, 4                # measured engine knee, per-run cap on parallel calls\nGLOBAL = None                           # created inside the event loop (Python 3.9 binds loops)\n\nasync def llm_call(prompt: str) -> str:\n    async with GLOBAL:                  # every call, from every run, goes through here\n        await asyncio.sleep(random.uniform(0.5, 1.5))   # stand-in for the HTTP call\n        return f\"answer to {prompt[:20]}\"\n\nasync def run_agent(task: str, subtasks: int = 10) -> list[str]:\n    plan = await llm_call(f\"plan: {task}\")\n    local = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_FANOUT)\n    async def sub(i: int) -> str:\n        async with local:\n            return await llm_call(f\"{plan} / step {i}\")\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(sub(i) for i in range(subtasks)))\n    return [await llm_call(f\"summarize {len(results)} results\")]\n\nasync def main(runs: int = 30):\n    global GLOBAL\n    GLOBAL, t0 = asyncio.Semaphore(KNEE), time.perf_counter()\n    await asyncio.gather(*(run_agent(f\"task {r}\") for r in range(runs)))\n    print(f\"{runs} runs, {runs * 12} calls, {time.perf_counter() - t0:.1f}s\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n# Without MAX_FANOUT the first runs to finish planning queue 10 calls each and fill all 48 slots, so\n# later runs wait for whole fan-outs; with it, no run holds more than 4 slots at once.\n```","patterns":["**Two-level limits in the agent runtime.** Wrap every LLM call in one shared semaphore sized to the engine knee (or your provider rate limit), and give each run its own cap on parallel tool calls and subagents. Monitor in-flight calls, queue wait inside the client, and steps per run.","**Limit in tokens, not just requests.** When contexts vary widely, set `max_num_seqs` near the measured knee and size `max_num_batched_tokens` so one long prompt cannot dominate a step. Monitor KV-cache usage, preemptions and p95 TPOT together.","**Keep chunked prefill on for chat.** When long prompts share a GPU with live conversations, use chunked prefill (default in recent vLLM, otherwise `--enable-chunked-prefill`) and tune the token budget down if TPOT spikes on prompt bursts. Monitor p95 TPOT during long-prompt arrivals.","**Bound the queue and shed early.** Return 429 or 503 with a `Retry-After` header once queue depth or expected wait exceeds the client timeout. Monitor queue depth, rejection rate and the fraction of requests that time out after admission.","**Per-tenant semaphores at the gateway.** When several teams share an engine, give each a concurrency and tokens-per-minute cap and lend idle share only if the gateway supports it. Monitor per-tenant in-flight count and rejections.","**Re-sweep on every model or traffic change.** Run a load sweep (the script above, or vLLM's serving benchmark) with production-shaped prompts before changing limits. Track the knee as a dashboard number per model and GPU type."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents are bursty, multiplying clients: one user task becomes 5 to 20\n  sequential LLM calls, and a planner that fans out to subagents turns one call into many at once.\n  Without limits, a few fan-outs push the engine past its knee and every agent's step gets slower.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The client-side semaphore around your LLM client (or your\n  framework's max-concurrency setting), the gateway's per-tenant caps, provider rate limits and 429s\n  for hosted models, and `max_num_seqs` and `max_num_batched_tokens` when you self-host with vLLM.\n- **What you control.** How many LLM calls one agent run may have in flight, how many runs share the\n  pool, timeouts and retry policy, and how much retrieved context each call carries.\n- **What you can skip.** Deriving the roofline yourself. Remember that long contexts move the knee down\n  and that Little's law turns call rate into replicas.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Planner-and-subagent fan-out.** A per-run cap on in-flight LLM calls stops one planner that spawns\n  many subagents from pushing the shared engine past its knee.\n- **Multi-tenant agent products.** Gateway caps per tenant keep one customer's long-context agents from\n  stalling everyone else's decode.\n- **Hosted-API agents.** A client-side semaphore sized below the provider rate limit turns 429 storms into\n  a short, predictable queue.\n- **Capacity planning.** Little's law turns expected agent runs per minute, calls per run and seconds per\n  call into a replica count.","mini_project":"**Find the knee for your agent's call pattern (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure where adding concurrent agent calls stops raising goodput on one endpoint.\n- **Steps:** pick a self-hosted or hosted model; replay a fixed set of agent-shaped calls (system prompt,\n  tool schemas, a few prior steps) at concurrency 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 and 64; record TTFT, TPOT, tokens\n  per second and the share meeting a latency SLO you choose; then wrap your agent client in an\n  `asyncio.Semaphore` set at the knee.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of concurrency versus throughput and goodput, a marked knee, and a run of\n  your agent with the semaphore showing stable step latency under a burst."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Batching always trades per-user latency for throughput.\"** Decode is bandwidth-bound, so up to the knee extra sequences reuse the same weight read: 64 short sequences take 7.3 ms per step versus 4.8 ms for one, about 1.5× the latency for about 42× the throughput. Latency climbs steeply only past the knee.","**\"The safe concurrency limit is a request count.\"** Each sequence adds its own KV reads, which do not amortise. The same 64 sequences at 6,144 tokens take about 20 ms per step, so the knee moves with context length and limits belong in tokens.","**\"Concurrency means users or agent runs.\"** The engine sees LLM calls. One run makes a call per step, and a planner fanning out to 5 subagents adds 5 in-flight calls at once, so count calls per second × residence time per call.","**\"A smarter scheduler setting can drain a growing queue.\"** If arrival rate × residence time exceeds the knee, Little's law says the queue grows without bound. Only more replicas, a smaller model, shorter outputs or load shedding fix it.","**\"Client retries make agents more robust under load.\"** Retrying on timeouts while the first request is still running doubles load exactly when the engine is saturated. Retry only on an explicit `Retry-After`, within one budget per run."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Waiting queue (bounded)\"] --> S[\"Scheduler step\"]\n    I[\"In-flight sequences\"] -->|\"decode tokens first\"| S\n    S --> B{\"Token budget left?\"}\n    B -->|\"yes\"| K{\"Prompt fits in KV pool?\"}\n    K -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Admit prompt chunk\"]\n    K -->|\"no\"| Q\n    B -->|\"no\"| R[\"Run step on GPU\"]\n    C --> R\n    R --> I\n    G[\"Gateway: per-tenant caps\"] --> Q\n    Q -.->|\"queue full\"| E[\"Fail fast: 429 or 503\"]","caption":"One scheduler step as agent calls see it: decode tokens go in first, waiting prompts (new agent steps)\nfill the remaining token budget in chunks, and a full queue rejects instead of accepting work that will\ntime out."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is batching nearly free for decode but not for prefill?","a":"- **Decode is bandwidth-bound:** one token per sequence means each weight read does ~2 FLOPs per sequence,\n  so adding sequences reuses the same read and costs little extra time.\n- **Prefill is already compute-bound:** a prompt of T tokens reuses each weight T times, so batching more\n  prompts adds proportional compute time instead of riding on idle tensor cores."},{"q":"Users report TPOT doubling after a RAG feature launched, with the same request rate. What happened?","a":"- **Context grew:** retrieved passages raise tokens per sequence, and every active sequence's KV cache is\n  read each step, so step time rises even at constant concurrency.\n- **Confirm** with KV-cache usage, prompt-length p95 and preemption counters.\n- **Fix:** lower `max_num_seqs` for that route, trim retrieved context, use an fp8 KV cache, or add\n  replicas."},{"q":"When would you choose dynamic batching over continuous batching?","a":"- **Single-pass models:** embedders, rerankers and classifiers do one forward pass per request, so\n  collecting a batch for a few milliseconds is simple and efficient.\n- **Not for generation:** a dynamic batch holds every slot until the longest output finishes, wasting the\n  GPU on finished sequences."},{"q":"Your agents make 30 LLM requests/s, each lives 4 s, and your knee is 40. How many replicas, and what else matters?","a":"- **Little's law:** 30 × 4 = 120 in flight, so 3 replicas at the knee, more with headroom.\n- **Residence time is not fixed:** past the knee queueing raises it, which raises required concurrency\n  again, so size with margin and bound the queue.\n- **Agent twist:** 30 requests/s may be only 3 runs/s at 10 calls each, and later calls carry longer\n  contexts, so measure the knee with replayed agent traces."},{"q":"A planner agent fans out to 8 subagents and the whole platform slows down. What do you change?","a":"- **Diagnose:** each fan-out adds 8 in-flight calls at once; a few concurrent planners push the engine\n  past its knee, so TPOT rises for every run.\n- **Fix in the runtime:** a per-run fan-out cap plus one shared semaphore sized to the knee, so runs\n  queue in the client instead of thrashing the engine.\n- **Then measure:** per-run wall-clock time against fleet p95 TPOT to pick the cap."},{"q":"Why should clients retry only on explicit retry-after signals?","a":"- **Blind retries amplify overload:** a client that times out while its request still runs adds a second\n  copy exactly when the engine is saturated.\n- **So** set client timeouts above expected queueing time, add jitter, and let the server say when to come\n  back."}],"tradeoffs":["**Higher sequence limit vs per-user TPOT.** It raises throughput and lowers cost per token, but once the step is compute-bound or KV-read-bound every extra sequence adds to every user's TPOT.","**Low tokens-per-step budget vs TTFT.** It keeps TPOT smooth during prompt bursts, but stretches TTFT for long prompts because their prefill is spread over more steps.","**Rejecting vs accepting everything.** Rejecting at the queue (429/503) protects latency for admitted requests but pushes retry logic and fairness onto clients; accepting everything is simpler and fails slowly for all.","**Agent fan-out vs fleet latency.** Parallel subagents finish one run sooner, but each fan-out adds in-flight calls that raise TPOT for every other run. Cap fan-out low when many runs share one engine; raise it when a single run's wall-clock time is what users see.","**Per-tenant caps vs utilisation.** Caps guarantee isolation, yet leave capacity idle when one tenant is quiet unless the gateway can lend unused share, which adds scheduling complexity."],"pitfalls":["**Limiting by request count only.** A handful of 100k-token requests exhausts the KV pool while the count looks healthy. Symptom: preemption spikes and latency jitter at low request rates. Fix: cap tokens per step and per tenant, and cap `max_model_len` per route.","**Benchmarking with short synthetic prompts.** The knee measured that way is too high for traffic with long retrieved context: throughput looks fine in the benchmark and TPOT doubles in production. Sweep with production-shaped prompt and output lengths.","**Client timeouts shorter than queueing time.** Clients retry while the original request is still running, so load doubles exactly when the system is saturated. Fix: timeouts above expected queueing time, and retry only on an explicit `Retry-After`.","**Retrying each agent step independently.** A loop that retries every failed call three times turns a saturated engine into a retry storm across dozens of runs. Symptom: 429s and timeouts spike together. Fix: one retry budget per run, honour `Retry-After`, and fail the run cleanly when it is spent.","**Disabling chunked prefill for peak prefill speed.** In-flight conversations then stall for hundreds of milliseconds each time a long prompt arrives. Fix: keep it on and tune the token budget instead.","**Setting the tokens-per-step budget very high.** Engines that profile the activation peak at startup (vLLM) respond by shrinking the KV pool, so capacity silently drops. Engines that do not profile OOM on a large prefill, a failure that only appears when a big prompt and a full decode batch coincide."],"when":"Every self-hosted deployment needs explicit limits; the question is their values.\n- **Start from VRAM** to get a token ceiling, run the sweep with production-shaped traffic to find the\n  latency knee, and set the sequence and token limits a little below it.\n- **Use Little's law** to convert the target request rate into replicas; for agents, the rate is runs\n  per second times LLM calls per run.\n- **Cap agent fan-out** whenever a planner can launch parallel tool calls or subagents, on self-hosted\n  and hosted models alike.\n- **Apply per-tenant caps** whenever more than one team or customer shares an engine, and always bound the\n  queue.\n- **Hosted APIs too:** the same thinking applies to provider rate limits; a client-side semaphore and\n  token budget avoid 429 storms.\n- **Interviews:** derive why batching is nearly free for decode, where it stops being free, and what you\n  would measure to find out.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets engine limits, writes the load-sweep harness, sizes client semaphores, and adds queue-depth and preemption metrics so the knee is visible on a dashboard.","architect":"Owns the SLO-to-capacity model: goodput targets, per-tenant isolation policy, replica count from Little's law, and when to split prefill and decode onto separate pools.","fde":"Reproduces the customer's traffic shape in a sweep, explains why their long-document workload needs lower concurrency than the vendor benchmark suggested, and tunes limits on site."},"check":{"q":"A single-GPU deployment reports 3,000 tokens/s at concurrency 32 and 3,400 tokens/s at\nconcurrency 96, so the team wants to raise the limit to 96. What would you check before agreeing, and\nwhat do those numbers suggest?","a":"Throughput rose only 13% for a 3× increase in concurrency, so the engine is already past the\nknee: it is compute-bound or, more likely with long contexts, bound by KV-cache reads. Per-user rate fell from\nabout 94 tokens/s (3,000 ÷ 32) to about 35 (3,400 ÷ 96), so each user's TPOT is roughly 2.5–3× worse at\n96 for almost no extra throughput, so goodput against the\nlatency SLO probably fell. I would check p95 TPOT and TTFT at both levels, preemption and queue-depth\nmetrics, and KV block utilization, and I would re-run the sweep with production prompt and output length\ndistributions. If more throughput is needed, the answer is a second replica, a quantized or smaller\nmodel, or shorter contexts, not a higher limit on the same GPU."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does adding sequences to a decode batch raise throughput almost linearly at first?","options":["Because the GPU runs each sequence on a separate streaming multiprocessor in parallel","Because the weights are read from memory once per step and reused for every sequence in the batch","Because attention cost is shared across sequences that have similar prompts","Because the sampler can pick tokens for all sequences in a single kernel launch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Decode is bound by streaming the weights through memory bandwidth. One read serves all sequences in the step, so until the step becomes compute-bound or KV reads dominate, more sequences cost little extra time."},{"q":"What does a tokens-per-step budget with chunked prefill primarily protect?","options":["Peak memory used by the model weights","Time to first token for the longest prompts in the queue","Time per output token for conversations already in flight when long prompts arrive","The accuracy of generated tokens under high load"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without the budget, a long prompt's prefill occupies entire steps and stalls every active decode. Chunking spreads it over steps so TPOT stays smooth, at the cost of a slower TTFT for that long prompt."},{"q":"You need 20 requests/s and each request lives about 6 s in the system. The measured knee for one replica is 40 concurrent requests. How many replicas do you need, roughly?","options":["One, because 40 concurrent requests exceeds 20 requests per second","Two, because 20 requests per second is half the knee","Three, because Little's law gives 120 concurrent requests and each replica handles 40","Six, because 120 concurrent requests must be halved for headroom"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concurrency = arrival rate × residence time = 20 × 6 = 120 in flight. At a knee of 40 per replica that is three replicas before headroom. Confusing requests per second with concurrent requests is the classic error."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","sources":["Shoeybi et al., 2019 — Megatron-LM: Training Multi-Billion Parameter Language Models Using Model Parallelism","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **The model does not fit.** A 70B-parameter model in bf16 is about 140 GB of weights. No single 80 GB\n  GPU can hold it, let alone leave room for a KV cache.\n- **Pipeline parallelism fits but does not speed up a token.** GPU 0 runs layers 1–40, GPU 1 runs 41–80.\n  A request still passes through them one after another, so per-token latency does not improve, and\n  keeping both busy needs several requests in flight at different stages.\n- **Offloading is too slow.** Keeping part of the weights in CPU memory and streaming them in means PCIe,\n  roughly 50–100× slower than HBM, so decode slows to seconds per token.\n- **Quantization alone is not always enough.** It halves or quarters bytes but costs quality. A 4-bit 70B\n  model still needs about 40 GB: it fits an 80 GB card, not a 24 GB one, and leaves little cache room on a\n  48 GB one.\n- **TP pools bandwidth.** It splits *inside* each layer, so every GPU works on the same token at once on\n  its slice. Bandwidth-bound decode gets faster roughly in proportion to N while exchanges stay small.\n  That is why TP is the default within a node and why the interconnect sets its limits.","how":"### What an agent run feels from TP\n- **Sequential steps compound TPOT.** A 15-step run that emits ~300 tokens per step decodes 4,500 tokens\n  one after another; every millisecond of TPOT becomes 4.5 s of wall-clock time per run.\n- **TP lowers TPOT; replicas raise runs in parallel.** Pick by what users wait on: one interactive run,\n  or a queue of background runs.\n- **Long agent contexts need cache room.** TP also shards the KV cache, which is often why a 70B agent\n  model runs at TP = 4 rather than TP = 2.\n\n### Background: Megatron-style partitioning of the MLP\nFor a two-matrix MLP `Y = GeLU(X·A)·B`, with A of shape [h, 4h] and B of shape [4h, h]:\n1. **Split A by columns** into N pieces. Each GPU computes `GeLU(X·A_i)`, a [tokens, 4h/N] slice. No\n   communication is needed *because* GeLU is elementwise, so each slice is independent.\n2. **Split B by rows** to match. GPU i computes `GeLU(X·A_i)·B_i`: full output shape [tokens, h], but\n   only its share of the sum.\n3. **One all-reduce** adds the N partial outputs, and every GPU has the full Y.\n\nLlama-style SwiGLU MLPs have three matrices with the same pattern: gate and up projections\ncolumn-parallel, down projection row-parallel, one all-reduce.\n\n### Attention, embeddings and the LM head\n- **Attention is split by heads.** With 64 query heads and TP = 8, each GPU owns 8 query heads, their\n  projections and the K/V heads they attend with, so attention for those heads is entirely local.\n- **Output projection W_O is row-parallel,** producing partial sums that one all-reduce combines.\n- **Embeddings and the LM head** are split along the vocabulary dimension.\n- **Net result:** two all-reduces per layer per forward pass, and every GPU holds 1/N of the weights.\n\n### The KV cache is sharded too\n- **Per-GPU cache per token is the full figure ÷ N,** because each GPU stores K and V only for its heads.\n  The aggregate free memory of all N GPUs serves as cache.\n- **Often the real reason for TP = 4 over TP = 2,** even when the weights fit at TP = 2.\n- **GQA constraint.** Llama-3-70B has 8 KV heads, so TP up to 8 gives each GPU at least one. Beyond that,\n  KV heads are replicated across GPUs that share them, which engines support but which forfeits part of\n  the saving.\n- **Divisibility.** Attention heads, MLP intermediate size and vocabulary should all divide by the TP\n  degree, which is why TP degrees are almost always powers of two.\n\n### Why the interconnect decides everything\n- **Message size at decode.** Each all-reduce exchanges a [tokens_in_step, hidden] activation. With 32\n  sequences, hidden 8,192, bf16: 32 × 8,192 × 2 = 512 KB. An 80-layer model issues 160 per step.\n- **Latency-bound, not bandwidth-bound.** The cost is per-collective latency, dozens of microseconds each\n  on NVLink with NCCL (an illustrative order of magnitude).\n- **Acceptable on NVLink, ruinous elsewhere.** A few milliseconds of overhead per step is fine when the\n  step is 10 ms. On PCIe-only hosts each collective is slower; across nodes over Ethernet or even\n  InfiniBand, latency × 160 per token is ruinous.\n- **Hence the rule:** tensor parallel within a node, pipeline parallel (or independent replicas) across\n  nodes.\n\n### A step-time model\n\n```\nt_step(N) ≈ (weight_bytes + kv_bytes_read) / (N × HBM_bw)  +  2 × n_layers × t_allreduce(N)  +  overhead\n```\n\n- **The first term shrinks as 1/N; the second does not.** It grows slightly with N because a ring\n  all-reduce takes 2(N − 1) steps (each GPU sends about 2(N − 1)/N of the message), so bandwidth cost is\n  nearly flat in N while latency is not. Speedup saturates once collectives rival the memory term.\n- **Illustrative 70B bf16 on H100-class GPUs:**\n\n| | TP = 4 | TP = 8 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Memory term: 141 GB ÷ (N × 3.35 TB/s) | ≈ 10.5 ms | ≈ 5.3 ms |\n| 160 all-reduces at an assumed 20 µs | 3.2 ms | 3.2 ms |\n| Step | ≈ 13.7 ms | ≈ 8.5 ms |\n\n- **So 2× the GPUs buys 1.6×.**\n- **Engines fight the second term.** vLLM, for example, ships a custom all-reduce kernel for small\n  messages over NVLink, and CUDA graphs remove per-kernel launch overhead from the decode step.\n\n### Prefill is a different regime\n- **Messages are large:** [prompt_tokens, hidden], so 4,096 × 8,192 × 2 bytes = 64 MiB each for the model\n  above. Collectives become bandwidth-bound, not latency-bound.\n- **NVLink hides them** behind prefill's large matmuls; over PCIe they can become a large share of time\n  to first token.\n\n### When TP speeds up a model that already fits\n- **TP = 2** roughly halves the weight bytes each GPU reads per step, so single-stream decode gets faster,\n  but less than 2× because of the all-reduces.\n- **Two replicas** (data parallelism) give 2× throughput with zero communication at the same per-token\n  latency.\n- **Rule:** tokens per second per user → TP; aggregate tokens per dollar at acceptable latency →\n  replicas, as long as each replica has enough KV room.\n\n### Related splits\n| Split | What is divided | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pipeline | layer ranges | cross-node friendly, adds latency |\n| Expert | whole experts of a mixture-of-experts model | tokens exchanged via all-to-all |\n| Context / sequence | a very long prompt, for prefill | splits prefill work across GPUs |\n\nAll three are combined with TP in large deployments. At inference on a single node, TP alone is the\ncommon case.","example":"### Step-time model across TP degrees\nPure Python, using the formula and the assumed 20 µs per all-reduce. TP = 1 is hypothetical (the model\ndoes not fit), shown only as the baseline.\n\n```python\nHBM_BW = 3.35e12                     # bytes/s per H100-class GPU (published figure)\n\ndef t_step_ms(n, weight_gb=141, kv_gb=0.0, layers=80, t_allreduce_us=20):\n    mem = (weight_gb + kv_gb) * 1e9 / (n * HBM_BW)          # shrinks as 1/N\n    comm = 0 if n == 1 else 2 * layers * t_allreduce_us * 1e-6  # does not shrink\n    return (mem + comm) * 1e3\n\nbase = None\nfor n in (1, 2, 4, 8):\n    ms = t_step_ms(n)\n    base = base or ms\n    print(f\"TP={n}: step {ms:5.1f} ms, {1e3/ms:4.0f} tok/s per stream, \"\n          f\"speedup {base/ms:3.1f}x on {n} GPUs\")\n# TP=2: 24.2 ms, TP=4: 13.7 ms, TP=8: 8.5 ms -> 5.0x on 8 GPUs, and the gap widens with slower links\n```\n\n### Row- and column-parallel MLP with one all-reduce\nRun with `torchrun --nproc_per_node=2`. Each rank keeps only its weight slice and computes a partial\noutput; the single all-reduce recovers the full result. Rank 0 checks it against the unsharded\ncomputation and prints the bytes the collective moved, the per-layer communication cost discussed above.\n\n```python\n# torchrun --nproc_per_node=2 tp_mlp.py\nimport torch, torch.distributed as dist\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndist.init_process_group(\"nccl\")\nrank, N = dist.get_rank(), dist.get_world_size()\ntorch.cuda.set_device(rank); dev = f\"cuda:{rank}\"\nh, ff, tokens = 4096, 11008, 8                     # hidden, intermediate, tokens in this decode step\ntorch.manual_seed(0)                               # identical full weights on every rank\nA = torch.randn(h, ff, dtype=torch.float16) * 0.02  # up projection  [h, ff]\nB = torch.randn(ff, h, dtype=torch.float16) * 0.02  # down projection [ff, h]\nX = torch.randn(tokens, h, dtype=torch.float16)\n\nA_i = A[:, rank * ff // N:(rank + 1) * ff // N].to(dev)   # column-parallel slice of A\nB_i = B[rank * ff // N:(rank + 1) * ff // N, :].to(dev)   # matching row-parallel slice of B\nX = X.to(dev)\n\nZ_i = F.gelu(X @ A_i)                              # [tokens, ff/N]; elementwise GeLU needs no comm\nY_i = Z_i @ B_i                                    # partial sum with full output shape [tokens, h]\ndist.all_reduce(Y_i, op=dist.ReduceOp.SUM)         # the one collective for this MLP\n\nif rank == 0:\n    ref = F.gelu(X.cpu().float() @ A.float()) @ B.float()\n    print(\"max abs error vs unsharded:\", (Y_i.cpu().float() - ref).abs().max().item())\n    print(\"bytes moved by the all-reduce:\", Y_i.numel() * Y_i.element_size())\n    print(\"weight bytes held per rank:\", (A_i.numel() + B_i.numel()) * 2)\ndist.destroy_process_group()\n```\n\n### Worked sizing: Llama-3-70B-class at TP = 4 vs TP = 2\n80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128: 320 KB of fp16 KV per token. 80 GB GPUs, 0.9 budget, an\nillustrative 2 GB activation reserve.\n\n| | TP = 4 | TP = 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weights per GPU | about 35 GB | about 70 GB |\n| KV per token per GPU | 80 KB | 160 KB |\n| Left for cache (72 GB budget) | about 35 GB, roughly 450k tokens | essentially nothing |\n| Weight-read floor per step | 35 GB ÷ 3.35 TB/s ≈ 10 ms | n/a |\n| Verdict | single-stream decode capped near 95 tokens/s before all-reduce overhead | not a usable deployment in bf16 |\n\n### Worked scenario: a 15-step agent on a 70B model\nEach step decodes ~300 tokens (reasoning plus a tool call). Decode only, using the step times above;\nprefill and tool time are extra. Illustrative, not a benchmark.\n\n| Deployment (8 H100-class GPUs) | TPOT | Decode per step | Decode per run (15 steps) | Runs at once |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| One TP = 8 replica | ≈ 8.5 ms | ≈ 2.6 s | ≈ 38 s | 1 batch |\n| Two TP = 4 replicas | ≈ 13.7 ms | ≈ 4.1 s | ≈ 62 s | 2 batches |\n\n- **Interactive agent, user watching:** TP = 8 cuts ~24 s from every run.\n- **Background agents (nightly triage, batch research):** two TP = 4 replicas finish more runs per\n  hour: two batches each step every 13.7 ms beat one batch every 8.5 ms, about 1.24× the tokens.\n- **Often better than both:** route easy steps to a smaller single-GPU model and keep the 70B for\n  planning, which shortens the chain the big model sees.","patterns":["**Pick TP from the agent's latency budget.** Multiply TPOT by tokens per step and steps per run; if interactive runs miss their budget at the smallest TP that fits, raise TP; if only throughput is short, add replicas. Monitor p95 run wall-clock time alongside TPOT.","**Smallest TP that leaves KV room.** When a model needs more than one GPU, start at the smallest power of two that fits weights plus a healthy cache (`--tensor-parallel-size 4` in vLLM) and scale throughput with replicas. Monitor KV-cache usage and preemptions per replica.","**Verify the topology before benchmarking.** Run `nvidia-smi topo -m` and confirm the GPUs in a TP group show NV# (NVLink) entries, not PHB, NODE or SYS (PCIe and socket hops). `NCCL_DEBUG=INFO` logs which transport NCCL chose at startup.","**TP inside the node, pipeline across nodes.** For models that need more than one node, combine tensor parallel within each NVLink node with pipeline parallel across nodes (vLLM's `--pipeline-parallel-size`). Monitor per-stage utilisation for pipeline bubbles.","**Keep CUDA graphs on for decode.** Avoid eager mode (`--enforce-eager` in vLLM) in production, because CUDA graphs remove per-kernel launch overhead, which grows as a share of the step as TP shortens each rank's compute and adds collective kernels. Monitor TPOT after any engine flag change.","**Benchmark the TP curve per hardware SKU.** Measure tokens/s per request and aggregate tokens/s at TP = 1, 2, 4 (where the model fits) on the exact node type, and record where speedup flattens. Replay real agent traces, since multi-step runs with long contexts behave differently from single chats."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent run is a chain of sequential LLM calls, so per-token latency\n  is multiplied by steps × tokens per step. TP is the main lever that lowers per-token latency for a\n  model too big for one GPU; replicas only add parallel runs.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `--tensor-parallel-size` (and `--pipeline-parallel-size`) in\n  vLLM, the GPU node shape you rent, and the TPOT you see from the endpoint your agent framework calls.\n  With a hosted API the provider makes this choice for you.\n- **What you control.** Model size and precision, TP degree versus replica count, and whether the\n  agent's slow steps (planning, long tool-call JSON) run on the big model at all.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing collectives yourself. Know that TP lives inside one NVLink node, that\n  2× GPUs buys less than 2× speed, and how to check the topology.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosting a 70B-class agent model.** TP inside one NVLink node is how the model fits at all and\n  how each agent step stays fast enough for interactive use.\n- **Latency-bound agent loops.** When a run is 15 sequential calls, cutting TPOT with a higher TP degree\n  shortens the whole run, which replicas cannot do.\n- **Throughput versus latency planning.** Choosing TP = 2 with two replicas or TP = 4 with one decides\n  whether you serve more parallel agents or faster individual ones.\n- **Debugging slow endpoints.** A TP group spanning a PCIe-only link or two nodes explains a TPOT far worse\n  than the GPU count suggests.","mini_project":"**Compare TP degree against replicas for an agent run (2-3 hours, needs a multi-GPU node).**\n- **Goal:** measure how tensor parallelism changes end-to-end agent run time versus how replicas change\n  runs per minute.\n- **Steps:** check the GPU topology with `nvidia-smi topo -m` first; on two GPUs, serve a model that fits\n  one GPU as one `--tensor-parallel-size 2` instance, then as two TP = 1 replicas behind a round-robin\n  client; run the same 10-step scripted agent loop once against each setup and record TPOT and total run\n  time; then run 8 agents concurrently against each setup and record runs per minute.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of TPOT, single-run time and runs per minute per setup, and a one-line\n  recommendation for an interactive agent versus a batch agent workload."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Pipeline parallelism also makes each token faster.\"** It only fits the model: a token still passes through the layer ranges one GPU after another. TP is the split where all N GPUs work on the same token at once, so only TP lowers TPOT.","**\"Doubling the TP degree halves the step time.\"** Only the memory term shrinks as 1/N; the 160 all-reduces per step do not. At an assumed 20 µs each, TP = 4 to TP = 8 goes from about 13.7 ms to 8.5 ms, 1.6× for 2× the GPUs.","**\"Decode all-reduces need more link bandwidth.\"** At decode each message is small (512 KB for 32 sequences at hidden 8,192), so the cost is per-collective latency. That is why TP stays inside an NVLink node; even InfiniBand across nodes multiplies latency by 160 per token.","**\"TP shards the weights but every GPU keeps the full KV cache.\"** Each GPU stores K and V only for its own heads, so per-GPU cache per token is the full figure ÷ N. With 8 KV heads that holds up to TP = 8; beyond it heads are replicated.","**\"If the model fits on one GPU, TP = 2 is how to double throughput.\"** Two replicas give 2× throughput with no communication; TP = 2 gives faster single-stream decode but less throughput per GPU. Choose TP for a user watching one agent run, replicas for a queue of background runs."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    X[\"Hidden state X, same on every GPU\"] --> AT[\"Attention: each GPU owns H/N heads\"]\n    AT --> WO[\"W_O row-parallel: partial sums\"]\n    WO --> AR1[\"All-reduce 1\"]\n    AR1 --> UP[\"Gate and up: column-parallel\"]\n    UP --> ACT[\"Elementwise activation, no comm\"]\n    ACT --> DN[\"Down: row-parallel partial sums\"]\n    DN --> AR2[\"All-reduce 2\"]\n    AR2 --> NX[\"Next layer\"]\n    KV[(\"KV cache: own heads only\")] -.-> AT\n    AR1 -.->|\"latency-bound at decode\"| L[\"Needs fast NVLink\"]","caption":"One transformer layer at TP = N, run for every token of every agent step: each GPU computes its heads\nand its MLP slice locally, and two all-reduces per layer stitch the partial sums back together."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through how a Megatron-style MLP is split across GPUs.","a":"- **First matrix by columns:** each GPU computes its slice of the intermediate activation, and the\n  elementwise nonlinearity needs no communication.\n- **Second matrix by rows:** each GPU produces a full-shape partial sum of the output.\n- **One all-reduce** sums the partials; with the attention output projection that is two per layer."},{"q":"Decode got slower when you moved from TP = 4 to TP = 8. What do you check?","a":"- **Topology first:** `nvidia-smi topo -m` to see whether all eight GPUs share NVLink or some pairs cross\n  PCIe or sockets.\n- **Then the step budget:** the memory term halved, but 160 latency-bound all-reduces per step did not,\n  and slower links make each one cost more.\n- **Also** check whether the KV head count forces replication at TP = 8, and compare against two TP = 4\n  replicas."},{"q":"When would you choose replicas over tensor parallelism for an agent fleet?","a":"- **When the model fits one GPU** and the goal is aggregate throughput: replicas scale linearly with zero\n  communication.\n- **When agents run in the background:** nobody watches a single run, so completed runs per GPU-hour\n  matters more than per-step latency.\n- **When nodes lack NVLink:** collectives over PCIe eat the bandwidth gain, so replicas of a quantized\n  model are often faster and cheaper."},{"q":"How does TP change KV-cache capacity, and where does GQA limit it?","a":"- **Cache per GPU divides by N,** because each GPU stores K and V only for its heads, so aggregate free\n  memory becomes cache.\n- **GQA caps the split:** with 8 KV heads, beyond TP = 8 heads are replicated and the saving shrinks."},{"q":"Your self-hosted 70B agent takes a minute per run. Would you raise TP or add replicas?","a":"- **Decompose the minute:** steps × (prefill + tokens per step × TPOT) + tool time. If decode dominates,\n  TPOT is the lever.\n- **Raise TP** (within one NVLink node) when interactive runs are too slow; it cuts TPOT, about 13.7 ms\n  to 8.5 ms going from TP = 4 to TP = 8.\n- **Add replicas** when runs are fast enough but too many queue. Also consider a smaller model for easy\n  steps."},{"q":"Estimate per-token decode latency for a 70B bf16 model at TP = 8 on H100-class GPUs.","a":"- **Memory term:** 141 GB ÷ (8 × 3.35 TB/s) ≈ 5.3 ms.\n- **Communication:** 2 × 80 layers = 160 all-reduces at an assumed ~20 µs ≈ 3.2 ms.\n- **Total** about 8.5 ms plus overhead, versus about 13.7 ms at TP = 4: 1.6× for twice the GPUs."}],"tradeoffs":["**Lower per-token latency vs all-reduce overhead.** Pooled bandwidth makes latency fall with TP, but each extra GPU adds collective overhead; the gain flips negative once collective latency rivals the shrinking step time.","**Fits big models vs a larger failure domain.** Weights and KV cache divide by N, so a large model fits and serves long contexts, but N GPUs are tied to one request stream and a single GPU failure takes the whole replica down.","**TP vs data-parallel replicas.** TP gives faster single-stream decode; replicas give more total throughput per GPU-hour. Which wins depends on whether the SLO is per-user speed or cost.","**Interactive agents vs background agents.** Higher TP shortens every step of a run a user is watching, but buys fewer runs per GPU-hour than replicas. The choice flips when the agent runs offline and only completion count matters.","**TP vs pipeline parallelism.** TP has no pipeline bubble and lower latency, but demands a fast intra-node interconnect; pipeline parallelism tolerates slow links between nodes."],"pitfalls":["**TP across PCIe-only GPUs or across nodes.** Decode can end up slower than a single GPU with a quantized model. Symptom: tokens/s barely improves or regresses as TP grows. Fix: keep each TP group inside one NVLink node and scale with replicas or pipeline parallelism beyond it.","**A TP degree that does not divide the KV head count.** It forces KV replication. Symptom: less cache capacity than the per-GPU arithmetic predicted.","**Assuming TP = 2 doubles throughput.** Throughput per GPU usually drops relative to two replicas, because communication is pure overhead when the batch already saturates one GPU. Fix: benchmark against two replicas before choosing.","**Forgetting per-rank overhead.** Each TP rank keeps its own CUDA context, NCCL buffers and activation workspace, so per-GPU cache room is less than weights ÷ N suggests.","**Judging an agent deployment by single-request TPOT.** A 10 ms TPOT looks fine until a 15-step run makes the user wait a minute. Symptom: good engine dashboards, complaints about slow agents. Fix: track run-level latency and size TP or model choice from it.","**Mixing GPU models or driver versions in a TP group.** Collectives run at the pace of the slowest device, and some combinations fail to initialise at all. Keep each TP group homogeneous."],"when":"- **Use when** the model does not fit one GPU at an acceptable precision, or it fits but single-stream\n  decode latency is the binding SLO and the GPUs share NVLink.\n- **Use for agents when** a self-hosted large model drives interactive multi-step runs and run latency,\n  not cost, is the complaint. With a hosted API this is the provider's decision.\n- **Keep the degree small:** usually the smallest power of two that leaves healthy KV room; prefer\n  replicas for scaling throughput beyond that.\n- **Avoid across nodes:** use pipeline parallelism or more replicas instead.\n- **Measure:** tokens/s per request and aggregate tokens/s at TP = 1, 2, 4 before committing; the curve\n  shows where communication starts to dominate on your hardware.\n- **Interviews:** draw the column/row split, count the all-reduces per layer, and explain why the\n  interconnect, not FLOPs, limits TP.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks `tensor_parallel_size`, verifies NVLink topology with `nvidia-smi topo -m`, and benchmarks per-request versus aggregate throughput across TP degrees before locking a config.","architect":"Decides the node shape and parallelism strategy per model tier: TP within NVLink nodes, replicas for scale, pipeline across nodes, and the failure domain each implies.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's slow 70B deployment on PCIe-attached GPUs and proposes a quantized single-GPU or NVLink-node alternative with measured tokens/s to back it."},"check":{"q":"A customer has eight A10G-class GPUs (24 GB each, PCIe only, no NVLink) and wants to serve a 70B\nmodel with sub-50 ms time per output token. Will TP = 8 get them there, and what would you propose?","a":"Fitting is possible: 140 GB of bf16 weights divided eight ways is 17.5 GB per GPU, leaving a few\nGB each for KV cache, which is thin but workable with a short context cap. The latency target is the\nproblem. An 80-layer model issues about 160 all-reduces per decode step; over PCIe each one costs far\nmore than on NVLink, and the per-GPU weight read of 17.5 GB at A10G-class bandwidth is already tens of\nmilliseconds. Sub-50 ms per token is unrealistic on that box. I would propose a 4-bit quantized 70B at\nTP = 2 or 4 to cut both bytes and collectives, evaluate its quality on their tasks, or move to an\nNVLink-connected node, and I would benchmark TP = 2, 4 and 8 on their hardware rather than argue from\nfirst principles alone."},"quiz":[{"q":"In Megatron-style tensor parallelism, why is the first MLP matrix split by columns and the second by rows?","options":["Because column splits are faster for cuBLAS and row splits save memory","So the elementwise activation between them needs no communication and one all-reduce finishes the layer","Because the second matrix is transposed in memory in most checkpoints","So that each GPU can hold a different attention head"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Splitting A by columns yields independent slices of the intermediate activation; the nonlinearity acts on each slice alone. Splitting B by rows lets each GPU produce a partial sum of the output that one all-reduce completes."},{"q":"What primarily limits how many GPUs you can usefully put in a tensor-parallel group at decode time?","options":["The total FLOPs of the GPUs","The size of the vocabulary","The latency of the two all-reduces per layer relative to the shrinking per-step compute","The number of transformer layers, since each GPU needs at least one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Decode messages are tiny, so collectives are latency-bound. As N grows each step gets shorter while the number of collectives stays fixed, so communication becomes the dominant cost, especially without NVLink."},{"q":"A model fits comfortably on one GPU. You need twice the aggregate throughput at the same per-token latency. Which option is usually best?","options":["Tensor parallelism across two GPUs","Two independent replicas behind a load balancer","Pipeline parallelism across two GPUs","CPU offloading half the layers to free memory for a larger batch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Replicas double throughput with no communication overhead and keep latency unchanged. TP would lower per-token latency but spends bandwidth and time on all-reduces, so throughput per GPU is lower."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/cpu-offloading","sources":["Sheng et al., 2023 — FlexGen: High-Throughput Generative Inference of Large Language Models with a Single GPU","Rajbhandari et al., 2021 — ZeRO-Infinity: Breaking the GPU Memory Wall for Extreme Scale Deep Learning","Official docs: llama.cpp","Official docs: Hugging Face Accelerate"],"why":"- **The model does not fit.** A 70B model is about 140 GB in bf16 and about 40 GB at 4-bit, and the GPU on\n  the desk has 24 GB. Options: a smaller model, more aggressive quantization, more GPUs, or putting the\n  overflow somewhere else and fetching it on demand.\n- **Host RAM looks like free capacity.** It is plentiful and cheap (hundreds of GB on a workstation), and\n  PCIe can move data both ways while the GPU computes.\n- **The catch is decode arithmetic.** Each token needs every weight once, so non-resident weights cross\n  PCIe every step. Gen4 x16 moves about 25 GB/s in practice (theoretical a little above 30 GB/s; Gen5\n  roughly doubles it). An H100's HBM delivers around 3.35 TB/s; even a consumer GPU around 1 TB/s.\n- **So small offloads hurt a lot.** Streaming 20 GB of weights per token costs about 0.8 s before any\n  compute. Offloading just 10% of a 40 GB model still adds roughly 160 ms of transfer to every token,\n  several times the resident compute.\n- **Three cases change the arithmetic.** The offloaded share is a rounding error; the workload is offline\n  and a huge batch amortises each transfer over thousands of tokens (FlexGen's design); or what is\n  offloaded is KV cache for idle or preempted sequences, read once on resume rather than every step.","how":"### Two very different offloads for an agent\n- **Weights off the GPU: every step pays.** Each decoded token of every tool call and every reasoning\n  step re-reads the offloaded bytes over PCIe. Avoid it for any agent a user waits on.\n- **Idle KV off the GPU: paid once per resume.** While an agent waits on a 30 s tool call or a human\n  approval, its cache does nothing. Parking it in host RAM frees blocks for active agents, and costs one\n  transfer when the agent resumes.\n- **The rest of this section** covers both mechanisms and the arithmetic to choose.\n\n### What can live off the GPU\n1. **Weights**, usually at layer granularity.\n   - Hugging Face Accelerate's `device_map=\"auto\"` places layers on GPU until full and the rest on CPU (or\n     disk via `offload_folder`).\n   - vLLM has a `--cpu-offload-gb` option.\n   - DeepSpeed's ZeRO-Inference and FlexGen stream weights with prefetching.\n2. **KV cache**, at block granularity.\n   - vLLM's `--swap-space` (its V0 scheduler; the V1 engine preempts by recomputation only) reserves\n     pinned host memory to swap the blocks of preempted sequences.\n   - Tiered prefix caches keep warm system-prompt blocks in CPU RAM.\n3. **Activations and optimizer state**, which matter for training (ZeRO-Offload, ZeRO-Infinity) and barely\n   at all for inference.\n\n### Style 1: streaming offload\n- **Mechanism.** Compute stays on the GPU; each offloaded layer's weights are copied in before it runs,\n  ideally prefetching layer i+1 on a second CUDA stream while layer i computes.\n- **Pinned memory is required** *because* DMA engines cannot read pageable memory. An unpinned transfer\n  goes through a staging copy and roughly halves effective bandwidth.\n- **Decode cannot hide the transfer.** Compute per layer for one token is microseconds, so step time is\n  essentially offloaded bytes ÷ PCIe bandwidth.\n- **Where it works.** Prefill of long prompts, or decode of very large batches, has enough compute per\n  layer to overlap the copies: exactly FlexGen's regime.\n\n### Style 2: split execution\n- **Mechanism.** CPU-resident layers are computed on the CPU. llama.cpp's `-ngl` (number of GPU layers)\n  works this way: the first N layers run on the GPU, the rest on CPU cores using host memory bandwidth.\n- **Host bandwidth.** Typically tens of GB/s on a dual-channel desktop and a few hundred on a server.\n- **Only boundary activations cross PCIe,** so the cost is CPU memory bandwidth and matmul speed, not bus\n  speed.\n- **Why it usually beats streaming at batch size 1.** Host bandwidth (tens to hundreds of GB/s) is at\n  least comparable to, and on a server well above, the 25–60 GB/s of a PCIe link, and weights are read\n  where they already live.\n- **The costs add,** because the two halves run one after the other for each token:\n\n```\nstep ≈ gpu_bytes / HBM_bw + cpu_bytes / host_bw\n```\n\n- **Worked:** half of a 40 GB model on a desktop with ~60 GB/s of host bandwidth is about 20 ms + 330 ms.\n  A many-channel server is far faster, but the CPU half still dominates the step.\n\n### A timing model for streaming\n```\noverlapped:      step_time ≈ max(resident_bytes / HBM_bw, offloaded_bytes / PCIe_bw) + overhead\nnot overlapped:  add the two terms\n```\n- **The second term dominates** as soon as more than a few percent of weights are offloaded, because PCIe\n  bandwidth is so much lower.\n- **Solve it backwards** to find how much you can offload within a latency target. The answer is usually\n  \"less than you hoped\", which is why quantizing first is nearly always the better lever.\n\n### KV cache swap versus recompute\nWhen the block pool runs dry, a preempted sequence is either swapped to pinned host memory and back, or\ndropped and recomputed by re-running prefill.\n\n| 8k-token context, Llama-3-8B-class | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Swap | about 1 GB moved, roughly 40 ms over Gen4 |\n| Recompute | on the order of a hundred TFLOP, several hundred milliseconds |\n\n- **Short prompts:** recompute is simpler and nearly free. **Long prompts:** swap wins. The crossover moves\n  with GPU and link speed.\n- **It is an engine policy, not a per-request choice.** vLLM's V0 scheduler recomputed single-sequence\n  requests by default and swapped multi-sequence groups (beam search, `n > 1`), for which it did not\n  implement recomputation.\n- **Swap space size bounds** how many sequences can be parked.\n- **Agents waiting on tools look like this.** A paused agent's next step re-sends the transcript; if\n  its blocks were evicted, prefix caching misses and the engine re-prefills, the recompute row above.\n\n### Mixture-of-experts changes the arithmetic\n- **Each token reads only routed experts,** a few per layer, not the whole expert set.\n- **So a split hopeless for a dense model becomes workable:** keep attention, shared layers and the KV\n  cache on the GPU; keep the large expert FFNs in host RAM and compute routed experts on the CPU.\n- **Right match for cheap RAM.** CPU bytes read per token scale with *active* expert parameters, memory\n  with *total*.\n- **Tooling.** llama.cpp supports this placement through its tensor-override options.\n- **Batching erodes it,** because a batch routes to many different experts and the CPU ends up reading\n  most of them each step.\n\n### NVMe and unified memory\n- **NVMe tier.** ZeRO-Infinity and FlexGen extend the hierarchy to GPU ← CPU ← SSD, with GPUDirect Storage\n  able to DMA from disk to GPU, at a few GB/s per drive.\n- **Unified memory.** Apple silicon and Grace Hopper systems share memory between CPU and GPU, so a 40 GB\n  model on a 64 GB Mac is not \"offloaded\" at all. It is bandwidth-limited by that machine's memory, which\n  is why llama.cpp runs large models acceptably there.","example":"### Worked scenario: 4-bit 70B on a 24 GB GPU\nA 70B model at about 4.5 bits per weight (the 4-bit `Q4_0` layout: 32 weights × 4 bits plus a 16-bit\nscale is 144 bits per 32 weights) is roughly 40 GB across 80 layers, about 0.5 GB per layer.\n\nThe GPU has about 1 TB/s and a Gen4 x16 slot. Reserving 4 GB for KV cache, activations and the CUDA\ncontext leaves 20 GB for weights.\n\n| Placement | Resident time | Bus time | Step (overlapped) | Speed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 40 layers resident, 40 streamed | 20 GB ÷ 1 TB/s = 20 ms | 20 GB ÷ 25 GB/s = 800 ms | max(20, 800) = 800 ms | about 1.2 tokens/s |\n| Only 10% (4 GB) offloaded | 36 ms | 160 ms | max(36, 160) = 160 ms | a 4× slowdown |\n| Whole model resident (hypothetical) | ~40 ms | 0 | ~40 ms | roughly 25 tokens/s |\n\nThe calculator reproduces this and solves for the offload a latency budget allows:\n\n```python\ndef step_ms(total_gb, gpu_frac, hbm_tbs=1.0, pcie_gbs=25.0, overlap=True):\n    resident, offloaded = total_gb * gpu_frac, total_gb * (1 - gpu_frac)\n    t_gpu = resident / hbm_tbs                 # GB / (TB/s) = ms, since 1 TB/s is 1 GB per ms\n    t_bus = offloaded / pcie_gbs * 1000        # GB / (GB/s) = s, converted to ms\n    return max(t_gpu, t_bus) if overlap else t_gpu + t_bus\n\nfor frac in (1.0, 0.9, 0.5):\n    ms = step_ms(40, frac)\n    print(f\"{frac:.0%} resident -> {ms:6.0f} ms/token, {1000/ms:5.1f} tok/s\")\n\nbudget_ms = 100                                  # latency target per token\nmax_offload_gb = budget_ms / 1000 * 25.0         # bytes PCIe can move within the budget\nprint(f\"a {budget_ms} ms budget tolerates at most {max_offload_gb:.1f} GB offloaded\")\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a local coding agent on that 24 GB GPU\nA 15-step agent run that decodes ~300 tokens per step (reasoning plus a tool call) is 4,500 output\ntokens. Decode time only, from the step times above; prefill and tool time are extra.\n\n| Placement | Speed | Decode per run |\n|---|---|---|\n| 40 layers resident, 40 streamed | 800 ms per token, about 1.2 tokens/s | about 60 min |\n| Only 10% (4 GB) offloaded | 160 ms per token, about 6 tokens/s | about 12 min |\n| Whole model resident (hypothetical) | roughly 25 tokens/s | about 3 min |\n\n- **The agent becomes unusable long before the chat does.** 1.2 tokens/s is tolerable for one answer,\n  not for 15 dependent steps.\n- **Better options:** a smaller model that fits fully resident for the agent loop, or a hosted model for\n  development, keeping the 70B for offline evaluation runs where an hour does not matter.\n\n### Catch accidental offloading at load time\n`device_map=\"auto\"` falls back to CPU or disk silently. This production guard caps GPU memory\nexplicitly and fails the deploy if any module landed off the GPU.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM\n\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\",\n    torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16,\n    device_map=\"auto\",\n    max_memory={0: \"20GiB\", \"cpu\": \"64GiB\"},   # GPU 0 budget, host budget for overflow\n    offload_folder=\"offload\",                  # used only if even host RAM runs out\n)\nplacement = model.hf_device_map                # module name -> 0, \"cpu\" or \"disk\"\noff_gpu = [name for name, dev in placement.items() if dev in (\"cpu\", \"disk\")]\nif off_gpu:\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"{len(off_gpu)} modules offloaded, e.g. {off_gpu[:3]}: \"\n                       \"expect seconds per token. Quantize or use a bigger GPU.\")\nprint(\"fully resident on GPU:\", set(placement.values()))\n```","patterns":["**Plan for paused agents' KV.** When agents wait seconds to minutes on tools or approvals, decide whether their cache stays resident, is parked in host RAM (where the engine supports a CPU tier or swap), or is recomputed on resume. Monitor prefix-cache hit rate on resumed steps and TTFT after long tool calls.","**Fail the deploy on silent offload.** For interactive services, check the placement after loading (as above, or the engine's startup log) and refuse to serve if weights are off the GPU. Monitor GPU utilisation: single digits with host RAM full is the signature.","**Quantize before offloading.** When a model is slightly too big, try a 4-bit or 8-bit build and an eval run first, because it removes bytes instead of moving them over the bus. Monitor task quality, not just tokens/s.","**Tune `-ngl` to the largest value that fits** for llama.cpp pilots on owned hardware. Raise it until VRAM is nearly full with your target context, then back off one or two layers for headroom. Monitor tokens/s and VRAM at the longest context you allow.","**Park MoE experts in host RAM.** For a large MoE model on one GPU, use llama.cpp's tensor-override option (`--override-tensor`, for example a pattern matching the `ffn_.*_exps` tensors set to CPU) to keep expert FFN tensors on CPU and everything else on GPU. Monitor tokens/s as concurrency rises, since batching erodes the win.","**Use pinned buffers and side streams for custom streaming.** Allocate host buffers with `pin_memory=True`, copy with `non_blocking=True` on a separate CUDA stream, and prefetch layer i+1. Monitor measured transfer GB/s against the PCIe spec."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent runs multiply per-token latency by steps and tokens per step, so\n  the seconds-per-token of offloaded weights turns a 3-minute run into an hour. The useful case is the\n  opposite: agents spend long stretches waiting on tools or humans, and their idle KV can leave the GPU.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Local agent development with llama.cpp (`-ngl`) or Hugging Face\n  `device_map=\"auto\"`, vLLM's `--cpu-offload-gb` and swap options, and tiered prefix caches that keep\n  shared system-prompt blocks in CPU RAM.\n- **What you control.** Model size and quantization for the agent, a load-time guard against silent\n  offload, and context caps that decide whether a paused agent's KV must be swapped or recomputed.\n- **What you can skip.** ZeRO-Infinity and NVMe tiers, which matter for training and offline research\n  jobs, not for serving agents.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Local agent development.** Partial GPU offload with llama.cpp lets you prototype an agent against a\n  model larger than your laptop GPU, as long as you accept slow steps.\n- **Paused and waiting agents.** Moving the idle KV of agents blocked on tools or humans to host RAM frees\n  GPU blocks for agents that are actively decoding.\n- **Offline agent evals and batch runs.** Large batches amortise each PCIe transfer, so offloading can make\n  an overnight eval sweep possible on hardware that cannot serve the model interactively.\n- **Load-time guards.** Detecting a silent `device_map=\"auto\"` spill to CPU explains an agent that suddenly\n  takes minutes per step.","mini_project":"**Measure what offloading does to an agent step (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** quantify the per-token and per-run cost of offloading part of a model behind a small agent.\n- **Steps:** run a quantized GGUF model in llama.cpp with `-ngl` set to all layers, then to about 90% and\n  50% of layers; drive a 5-step scripted tool-calling loop against each; record tokens per second and\n  total run time; add a startup check that parses the load log's \"offloaded N/M layers to GPU\" line and\n  fails if N is less than M when you expect full GPU.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of layers on GPU versus tokens per second and run time, and the guard\n  fails loudly on the 50% setup."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Offloading 10% of the weights costs about 10% of the speed.\"** Every token re-reads the offloaded bytes over PCIe. For the 40 GB model in the worked example, 4 GB offloaded makes each token take 160 ms of bus time against about 40 ms fully resident, a 4× slowdown, not 10%.","**\"Prefetching on a side stream hides the transfer.\"** Overlap needs compute to hide behind. One decode token spends microseconds per layer, so step time is offloaded bytes ÷ PCIe bandwidth. Overlap works for long prefills and huge offline batches, FlexGen's regime.","**\"Offloading and quantization are two ways to do the same thing.\"** Quantizing fp16 to 4-bit removes 75% of the bytes for a modest quality cost; offloading 75% moves them over the bus on every token and multiplies latency by tens. Quantize first.","**\"Offloading KV cache is as bad as offloading weights.\"** A paused agent's KV is read back once on resume, about 40 ms for an 8k-token Llama-3-8B-class context over Gen4, not on every step. That is the case where offloading pays.","**\"`device_map=\"auto\"` will fail if the model does not fit.\"** It falls back to CPU or disk silently, so the model \"works\" at a token or two per second. Cap `max_memory` and fail the load when any module is off the GPU."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Model plus KV exceeds GPU\"] --> Q[\"Quantize first\"]\n    Q --> F{\"Fits now?\"}\n    F -->|\"yes\"| D[\"Serve fully resident\"]\n    F -->|\"no\"| I{\"Interactive latency SLO?\"}\n    I -->|\"yes\"| T[\"More GPUs, TP, or smaller model\"]\n    I -->|\"no, offline batch\"| B[\"Streaming offload, huge batch\"]\n    I -->|\"local or pilot\"| L[\"Split execution, CPU layers\"]\n    M{\"MoE model?\"} -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Experts in host RAM\"]\n    L --> M\n    K[\"Idle or preempted KV\"] -.->|\"swap to pinned host RAM\"| H[(\"Host memory\")]","caption":"The decision path for an agent model that does not fit: quantize first, and offload weights only when\nlatency does not matter or the offloaded share is tiny. Idle KV of paused agents is the cheap case."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is offloading weights so much worse for decode than for prefill?","a":"- **Decode reads every weight per token** with microseconds of compute per layer, so transfer time cannot\n  hide behind compute and step time becomes offloaded bytes ÷ PCIe bandwidth.\n- **Prefill has thousands of tokens per layer,** enough compute to overlap the copies, which is the regime\n  FlexGen exploits with huge offline batches."},{"q":"A model \"works\" after deployment but generates one token per second. How do you diagnose it?","a":"- **Check placement:** `hf_device_map` or the engine log for layers on CPU or disk.\n- **Check utilisation:** a GPU near idle with host RAM full and PCIe busy means streaming offload.\n- **Fix:** quantize, cap context to shrink the KV reserve, or move to a GPU that fits; then add a load-time\n  guard so it cannot recur silently."},{"q":"When is CPU offloading the right call?","a":"- **Offline batch jobs** where tokens per hour matter and a big batch amortises each transfer.\n- **Pilots on owned hardware,** local experiments, or a model a few percent too big for a short-term fix.\n- **MoE models on one GPU,** with expert FFNs in host RAM, because each token reads only its routed experts."},{"q":"A developer wants to run your 15-step coding agent locally on a partially offloaded 70B. What do you tell them?","a":"- **Do the arithmetic:** at about 1.2 tokens/s, 4,500 output tokens per run is about an hour of decode.\n- **Recommend:** a smaller model that fits fully resident for the inner loop, or a hosted endpoint, and\n  keep the 70B for offline eval runs."},{"q":"Your agents pause for minutes on human approval, and the GPU runs out of KV blocks. What are the options?","a":"- **Leave it to eviction:** blocks of waiting agents get evicted, and the resumed step pays a re-prefill.\n- **Park the cache in host RAM** where the engine supports it: about 40 ms to bring back 8k tokens versus\n  several hundred milliseconds to recompute.\n- **Or shrink what is parked:** compact the transcript before pausing, so the resume prefill is short."},{"q":"Swap or recompute for a preempted 8k-token sequence?","a":"- **Swap:** about 1 GB over Gen4, roughly 40 ms, versus several hundred milliseconds to re-run prefill.\n- **But** it is an engine policy: vLLM's V1 engine recomputes only, so on it the lever is fewer\n  preemptions (smaller context caps, lower concurrency), not swap space."}],"tradeoffs":["**Possible deployment vs per-token cost.** Offloading turns an impossible deployment into a possible one, but every offloaded gigabyte of weights adds roughly 40 ms per token over Gen4 PCIe. Worthwhile only when the share is tiny or latency is irrelevant.","**Offline throughput vs interactive latency.** Large-batch generation amortises weight transfers over many tokens per step, so throughput can be acceptable where single-stream latency is not. It flips back to useless when requests must be answered one at a time.","**KV swap vs reserved host memory.** Swap preserves long contexts cheaply compared with recomputation, but pinned host memory is reserved up front and PCIe traffic competes with any weight streaming on the same link.","**Resident KV for paused agents vs capacity for active ones.** Keeping a waiting agent's cache on the GPU makes its next step fast, but blocks other agents. Parking it frees blocks for one ~40 ms transfer per 8k-token resume; it flips when tool waits are short and resumes frequent.","**Split execution vs concurrency.** Computing on the CPU avoids bus traffic and suits a few layers on a fast machine, yet CPU matmul throughput caps batch size, so it does not scale to many users."],"pitfalls":["**`device_map=\"auto\"` silently placing layers on CPU** when the GPU is slightly too small. Symptom: a model that \"works\" at one or two tokens per second with the GPU at 5% utilization and host RAM full. Fix: set `max_memory` explicitly and fail the load if any module is off the GPU.","**Streaming from pageable instead of pinned memory.** It halves effective bandwidth and adds CPU copy overhead, visible as transfer time far above the PCIe spec. Fix: allocate host buffers pinned.","**Swap space too small for the long-context tail.** Depending on the engine, preemptions then fail outright or fall back to recomputation. Symptom: aborted requests, or a throughput cliff with prefill-heavy GPU traces, when concurrency rises.","**Benchmarking on a Gen5 x16 workstation** and deploying to a cloud instance whose GPU sits on a shared or narrower link: identical settings, half the speed. Fix: benchmark on the target instance type.","**Evaluating an agent on a partially offloaded local model.** Runs take an hour, so the team tests a handful of tasks and calls it done. Symptom: tiny eval sets and timeouts in the agent harness. Fix: run evals on a fully resident or hosted model, and offload only for offline batch jobs.","**Treating offloading as an alternative to quantization.** Quantizing from fp16 to 4-bit removes 75% of the bytes with modest quality cost; offloading 75% of the bytes multiplies latency by tens."],"when":"- **Use weight offloading when** a model is a few percent too large and a short-term fix is acceptable,\n  for offline batch jobs where tokens per hour matter and latency does not, for local experimentation on\n  a machine that serves no one, or when remaining layers can run on a strong CPU via split execution.\n- **Avoid for anything interactive:** prefer quantization, a smaller model, or tensor parallelism. Agent\n  loops are the strictest case, because every step pays the penalty again.\n- **Use KV offloading deliberately:** size the swap space for your long-context tail and watch preemption\n  and swap metrics.\n- **Measure** tokens/s with and without offload before deciding; the formula above predicts it well.\n- **Interviews:** the test is whether you can compute the PCIe penalty rather than assert that\n  offloading \"makes it fit\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures layer placement or swap space, pins host buffers, and reads GPU utilization and transfer timings to spot accidental offloading before it ships.","architect":"Decides where offloading is permitted (batch pipelines, dev boxes) and where it is banned (interactive SLOs), and chooses quantization or more GPUs as the standard remedy.","fde":"Gets a customer's oversized model running on the hardware they already own for a pilot, with explicit latency expectations, and a plan to move to properly sized GPUs for production."},"check":{"q":"A customer runs a 4-bit 70B model on a single 48 GB GPU by offloading the overflow to CPU and\nreports 3 tokens/s. They ask whether a faster CPU would help. What is your analysis and recommendation?","a":"A 4-bit 70B model is around 40 GB of weights; with KV cache and runtime overhead it slightly\nexceeds 48 GB, so a few gigabytes are offloaded. If the engine streams those layers over PCIe, step time\nis dominated by offloaded bytes divided by bus bandwidth, roughly 40 ms per gigabyte on Gen4, and a\nfaster CPU changes nothing; 3 tokens/s is about 330 ms per token, consistent with roughly 7–8 GB crossing\na Gen4 link every step. If it computes them on the CPU, the offloaded share runs at host memory\nbandwidth, so more memory channels help more than a faster core clock, and neither reaches GPU-class\nspeed. The real fix is to remove the\noverflow: cap context length so the KV cache shrinks, use a slightly smaller quantization or a model\nwith fewer KV heads, or add a second GPU with tensor parallelism. I would measure the offloaded size and\ntransfer time first, then predict the gain of each option before they buy anything."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does offloading even a small fraction of weights hurt decode latency so much?","options":["The CPU must re-quantize the weights on every transfer","GPU kernels cannot start until all layers are resident","Every decode step needs every weight once, and PCIe bandwidth is orders of magnitude below GPU memory bandwidth","Offloaded layers lose their KV cache and must recompute attention"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Decode streams the full weight set per token. Bytes that live across PCIe move at tens of GB/s rather than terabytes per second, so a small offloaded share can take longer than the entire resident computation."},{"q":"When is swapping a preempted sequence's KV cache to CPU preferable to recomputing it?","options":["Always, because recomputation wastes GPU memory","When the context is long, so moving the cache over PCIe is cheaper than re-running prefill","When the context is short, because small transfers are nearly free","Only when the model uses grouped-query attention"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Swap cost scales with cache bytes, recompute cost with prefill FLOPs over the prompt. For long prompts the transfer is tens of milliseconds while prefill is hundreds; for short prompts recompute is simpler and about as fast."},{"q":"Why must host buffers used for GPU transfers be pinned?","options":["Pinned memory is compressed, so fewer bytes cross the bus","The GPU cannot address memory above 4 GB otherwise","DMA engines need page-locked memory; otherwise data goes through a staging copy and bandwidth drops","Pinned memory lets the GPU execute CPU layers directly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Page-locked memory cannot be swapped out, so the DMA engine can read it directly. Pageable memory forces the driver to copy into a pinned staging buffer first, roughly halving effective transfer bandwidth."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","sources":["Dettmers et al., 2022 — LLM.int8(): 8-bit Matrix Multiplication for Transformers at Scale","Frantar et al., 2022 — GPTQ: Accurate Post-Training Quantization for Generative Pre-trained Transformers","Lin et al., 2023 — AWQ: Activation-aware Weight Quantization for LLM Compression and Acceleration","Official docs: llama.cpp"],"why":"- **The pressure to quantize is constant.** A 4-bit checkpoint lets a 70B model fit where a 16-bit one did\n  not and roughly quarters the bytes decode must stream. The Training & adaptation lesson covers *how*\n  GPTQ, AWQ, int8 and fp8 work; this one is about *whether the result is still the model you tested*.\n- **Model cards invite a bad inference.** A card says something like \"perplexity 5.47 → 5.62 on\n  WikiText-2\" (illustrative numbers), and teams read that as \"lossless enough\" and deploy.\n- **Perplexity averages away the decisive tokens.** It averages log-loss over every token of generic text,\n  and most tokens are easy. A task often hinges on a few (a digit, a function name, a closing brace) whose\n  probabilities can shift a lot while the average barely moves.\n- **Error concentrates where you were not looking.** GPTQ or AWQ calibration data, often a few hundred\n  segments of web text, may not resemble your domain. Outlier activation channels, which LLM.int8() showed\n  emerge above a few billion parameters, make some layers far more fragile than others.\n- **Small eval sets are noisy.** With a few hundred items the noise exceeds the effect you are hunting, so\n  a naive \"accuracy dropped 1%\" is often meaningless either way. Trusting vendor benchmarks or eyeballing\n  chats fails on the rare, high-stakes behaviours production exposes at scale.","how":"A layered measurement from cheap distribution fidelity to expensive task evidence, always with the\nfull-precision model as the control.\n\n### Step 1: distribution fidelity with teacher forcing\nRun the same fixed text through both models and compare next-token distributions position by position.\nThe forced identical context isolates quantization error from sampling and from divergence after a first\nwrong token. One pass gives three numbers:\n\n| Signal | What it is | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Perplexity delta | on held-out text: WikiText-2 and C4 are conventional, plus a sample of *your* domain text | calibration mismatch shows on your domain |\n| Mean KL divergence | per-token KL from the fp16 distribution to the quantized one | catches distribution moves even when perplexity improves by accident; the most sensitive cheap signal |\n| Top-1 agreement | fraction of positions where both argmax tokens match | under greedy decoding, a direct proxy for how often the quantized model goes a different way |\n\n- **The KL formula** is `KL(P_fp16 || P_quant)`, summed over the vocabulary at each position and averaged\n  over positions.\n- **Look at the KL tail too** (99th-percentile or maximum per-token KL), *because* damage concentrates on\n  a few positions and a small mean can hide a handful of badly wrong ones.\n- **Tooling.** llama.cpp's perplexity tool can save full-precision logits and report KL statistics,\n  including percentiles, against a quantized run.\n- **Put it in CI.** These are deterministic and take minutes, so they belong in CI for every new quantized\n  artifact.\n\n### Step 2: public benchmarks, run yourself\n- **Re-run a standard harness** (EleutherAI's lm-evaluation-harness is the usual one) on both models with\n  identical settings.\n- **What survives:** knowledge-style multiple choice (MMLU-type) tends to survive 4-bit well.\n- **What breaks first:** multi-step arithmetic (GSM8K-type), code generation (HumanEval-type) and\n  instruction-following format checks (IFEval-type).\n- **Report the delta, not the absolute score,** with a confidence interval.\n\n### Step 3: your golden set, your graders\n1. **Same everything:** the release golden dataset, *same* prompts, *same* system prompt, *same* decoding\n   parameters.\n2. **Greedy first** to remove sampling noise, then repeat with the production configuration over several\n   seeds.\n3. **Same task graders.** For open-ended outputs, use pairwise LLM-as-judge with the fp16 output as the\n   reference and randomised position.\n4. **Paired statistics** (paired bootstrap or a sign test on per-item wins), because every prompt is\n   scored under both models. Do not compare two independent means.\n5. **Count the flips:** items that went correct → wrong and wrong → correct.\n\nWhy pairing matters, on 300 items at 80% accuracy:\n\n| Analysis | Standard error of the difference | 95% interval |\n|---|---|---|\n| Two independent runs | √(2 × 0.8 × 0.2 / 300) ≈ 3.3 points | about ±6.4 |\n| Paired, models disagree on 15 items (5%) | √(0.05 / 300) ≈ 1.3 points | near ±2.5 |\n\nThe same data now resolves effects less than half the size.\n\n- **Flips hide in the average.** A model can hold accuracy flat while 10 answers break and 10 others\n  happen to improve; the users who owned those 10 broken answers see a regression.\n- **Flips cost nothing extra.** The flip count is the discordant count that drives the paired statistics.\n\n### Step 4: stress the known weak spots\nAdd targeted slices *because* average scores mask them:\n- **Long-context retrieval:** needle-style prompts at your maximum context, especially if the KV cache is\n  quantized too.\n- **Multilingual prompts, numbers and dates.**\n- **JSON or tool-call validity rates.**\n- **Refusal or safety behaviour,** which can shift in either direction.\n- **Agent trajectories:** tool selection, argument correctness and run completion on replayed agent\n  tasks, because per-step errors compound over a run (see the worked scenario below).\n- **Speed:** weight-only 4-bit helps bandwidth-bound decode, but some kernels slow prefill through\n  dequantisation overhead, so a \"faster\" model can raise time to first token.\n\n### Step 5: production confirmation\n- **Canary or shadow** the quantized model and compare task metrics and user-facing signals against the\n  fp16 fleet before full rollout.\n- **Register it as a new model version** with its own evaluation record, because that is what it is.\n\n### Knowing where damage comes from\n| Scheme | Behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| Weight-only (W4A16: GPTQ, AWQ, NF4) | activations stay 16-bit; usually little loss on 7B-and-larger models; smaller models lose more at the same bit width |\n| Weight-and-activation (W8A8 int8, fp8) | faster tensor-core math for prefill and large batches, but must handle activation outliers (what SmoothQuant-style scaling and fp8's dynamic range address) |\n| KV cache fp8 | generally reported as low-impact |\n| KV cache 4-bit | where long-context retrieval tends to degrade |\n\n- **\"4-bit\" is not one thing.** Group size, per-channel versus per-tensor scales and the calibration set\n  all move the result, so two \"4-bit\" files of the same model can differ more from each other than either\n  differs from fp16.","example":"### Paired bootstrap with flip counts on a golden set\nPure Python, runnable as is. Feed it per-item correctness for both models on the same prompts; it\nreports the delta, a 95% interval and the list of broken items someone must read.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef paired_report(fp16_ok, quant_ok, n_boot=5000, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"fp16_ok, quant_ok: per-item 0/1 correctness on the SAME prompts and decoding.\"\"\"\n    assert len(fp16_ok) == len(quant_ok)\n    n = len(fp16_ok)\n    diffs = [q - f for f, q in zip(fp16_ok, quant_ok)]       # -1 broke, +1 fixed, 0 same\n    broke, fixed = diffs.count(-1), diffs.count(1)\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    boots = sorted(sum(rng.choice(diffs) for _ in range(n)) / n for _ in range(n_boot))\n    lo, hi = boots[int(0.025 * n_boot)], boots[int(0.975 * n_boot)]\n    print(f\"n={n} delta={sum(diffs)/n:+.1%} 95% CI [{lo:+.1%}, {hi:+.1%}] \"\n          f\"broke={broke} fixed={fixed}\")\n    return [i for i, d in enumerate(diffs) if d == -1]       # items someone must read\n\n# Illustrative: 300 items, fp16 at 80%, 15 disagreements (10 broke, 5 fixed)\nfp16 = [1] * 240 + [0] * 60\nquant = fp16.copy()\nfor i in range(10):\n    quant[i] = 0                                            # correct -> wrong\nfor i in range(240, 245):\n    quant[i] = 1                                            # wrong -> correct\nprint(\"read these:\", paired_report(fp16, quant))\n# n=300 delta=-1.7% 95% CI [-4.3%, +0.7%] broke=10 fixed=5\n```\n\nThe interval straddles zero, so the net number alone is inconclusive, yet ten specific answers broke.\nRead them: if they share a pattern (dates, long inputs, JSON), that slice is the real finding.\n\n### Teacher-forced comparison: KL, top-1 agreement, perplexity\nCompares a full-precision model and its 4-bit counterpart on domain text. Run it on your own text, not\nonly WikiText, and gate on the deltas before investing in slower task evals.\n\n```python\nimport math, torch, torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, BitsAndBytesConfig\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"                        # swap in the checkpoint you plan to ship\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nfp16 = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float16, device_map=\"cuda\").eval()\nq4 = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    name, device_map=\"cuda\",\n    quantization_config=BitsAndBytesConfig(load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n                                           bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.float16)).eval()\n\ntexts = open(\"domain_sample.txt\").read().split(\"\\n\\n\")[:200]   # YOUR domain, same text for both\nkl = agree = nll16 = nll4 = n = 0\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for t in texts:\n        ids = tok(t, return_tensors=\"pt\", truncation=True, max_length=1024).input_ids.cuda()\n        lp = F.log_softmax(fp16(ids).logits[0, :-1].float(), -1)   # predictions for positions 1..T\n        lq = F.log_softmax(q4(ids).logits[0, :-1].float(), -1)     # identical context: teacher forced\n        tgt = ids[0, 1:]\n        kl += (lp.exp() * (lp - lq)).sum(-1).sum().item()           # KL(P_fp16 || P_q4), summed\n        agree += (lp.argmax(-1) == lq.argmax(-1)).sum().item()\n        nll16 += -lp.gather(1, tgt[:, None]).sum().item()\n        nll4 += -lq.gather(1, tgt[:, None]).sum().item()\n        n += tgt.numel()\nprint(f\"tokens={n}  mean KL/token={kl/n:.4f}  top-1 agreement={agree/n:.2%}\")\nprint(f\"ppl fp16={math.exp(nll16/n):.3f}  ppl 4bit={math.exp(nll4/n):.3f}\")\n# Next: run the golden set under both models with identical decoding and compare with paired bootstrap.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: per-step losses compound over an agent run\nAssume each step's tool call must be correct for the run to succeed and errors are independent (a\nsimplification; real agents sometimes recover). Run success is per-step success raised to the number\nof steps.\n\n| Per-step tool-call success | 5-step run | 15-step run |\n|---|---|---|\n| 99.5% | 97.5% | 92.8% |\n| 99% (fp16 baseline, illustrative) | 95.1% | 86.0% |\n| 98% (quantized, illustrative) | 90.4% | 73.9% |\n\n- **One point per step is twelve points per run** at 15 steps (86.0% → 73.9%), a regression no\n  single-turn benchmark would flag.\n- **So evaluate at the run level.** Replay the same agent tasks under both models with identical tools\n  and decoding, score run success per task, and feed the paired results to the bootstrap above.","patterns":["**Agent-run gate with paired tasks.** Before a quantized model serves the agent, replay a fixed suite of agent tasks against both builds with the same tools (mocked or sandboxed), prompts and decoding. Gate on paired run success and tool-call validity, and read every task that broke.","**KL gate in CI for every artifact.** When a new quantized file is built, run a teacher-forced pass on a fixed domain sample (the script above, or llama.cpp's perplexity tool saving base logits and comparing KL). Fail the build if mean or 99th-percentile KL exceeds the threshold set from earlier good artifacts.","**Harness runs on both models, same config.** Run lm-evaluation-harness against fp16 and quantized builds in the same job, for example `lm_eval --model hf --model_args pretrained=<path> --tasks gsm8k,ifeval`, and store the deltas with intervals. Watch the reasoning, code and format tasks.","**Paired golden-set gate with a flip review.** Use the release golden set, greedy decoding, paired bootstrap, and require a human to read every correct → wrong flip before approval. Track flip counts per slice over releases.","**Change one thing at a time.** Evaluate weight quantization and KV-cache quantization (for example vLLM's `--kv-cache-dtype fp8`) as separate candidates, so long-context loss is attributed correctly.","**Shadow before rollout.** Mirror a slice of production traffic to the quantized deployment, compare task metrics and JSON validity against the fp16 fleet, and keep the quantized build as its own registry version with its evaluation record."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Tool-call JSON, argument values, long transcripts and multi-step\n  reasoning are exactly what quantization breaks first, and an agent needs all of them on every step.\n  A 1-point drop per step becomes a double-digit drop in run success over 15 steps.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Choosing a 4-bit or fp8 build to self-host the agent model,\n  enabling `--kv-cache-dtype fp8` in vLLM, picking a GGUF file for llama.cpp, or a provider swapping in\n  a cheaper variant behind the same name.\n- **What you control.** The baseline (re-run fp16 yourself), the eval set (your agent's tool-call and\n  trajectory tasks), paired statistics, and the gate that blocks the artifact.\n- **What you can skip.** How GPTQ and AWQ compute their scales; that is the quantization lesson. Here\n  you only need to measure the result.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Choosing a self-hosted agent build.** Paired evals decide between a bf16, fp8 or 4-bit checkpoint\n  before it drives production tool calls.\n- **Turning on an fp8 KV cache.** Long-transcript agent evals show whether recall of early tool results\n  survives the cheaper cache.\n- **Release gates for model swaps.** A CI gate blocks a quantized artifact whose tool-call validity or run\n  success falls outside the agreed margin.\n- **Auditing a provider change.** Re-running the agent eval suite catches a cheaper variant served behind\n  the same model name.","mini_project":"**Build a quantization gate for an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** decide with evidence whether a quantized model may replace the full-precision one behind a\n  tool-calling agent.\n- **Steps:** collect 50-100 agent tasks with known correct tool calls; run them on the full-precision and\n  quantized builds with the same prompts and temperature 0; score tool-call validity, argument accuracy\n  and end-to-end success per item; compute paired differences with a bootstrap confidence interval.\n- **Done when:** a script prints the per-metric deltas with intervals and broke/fixed counts, exits\n  non-zero when any interval's lower bound is below minus your agreed margin, and you have read every\n  task that went from correct to wrong and noted its failing step."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A tiny perplexity delta on the model card means the build is nearly lossless.\"** Perplexity averages log-loss over mostly easy tokens, so the digit, function name or closing brace a task hinges on can shift a lot while the average barely moves. Check mean and tail KL on your own domain text, then task evals.","**\"Comparing against the published fp16 score is good enough.\"** Prompts, templates and harness settings differ, so the \"regression\" may be a harness difference. Re-run fp16 yourself in the same job with identical settings.","**\"Two accuracy numbers on 300 items show whether a 2-point drop is real.\"** Independent runs at 80% carry about ±6.4 points of 95% interval. Pairing per item, with 15 disagreements, narrows it to near ±2.5, so analyse per-item differences, not two means.","**\"Unchanged net accuracy means nothing regressed.\"** Ten broken and ten fixed answers cancel in the mean, and the users who owned the broken ten see a regression. Count correct → wrong flips and read every one.","**\"A one-point drop per call is a one-point drop for the agent.\"** Per-step errors compound: at 15 steps, 99% per step gives 86.0% run success and 98% gives 73.9%. Gate on replayed agent runs, not single-turn scores.","**\"A 4-bit build of a model is a known quantity.\"** Group size, scale granularity and the calibration set all move the result; two 4-bit files of one model can differ more from each other than from fp16. Evaluate the exact artifact you will ship."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"New quantized artifact\"] --> S1[\"1. Teacher-forced KL, PPL, top-1\"]\n    S1 --> G1{\"Deltas within gate?\"}\n    G1 -->|\"no\"| R[\"Reject or re-quantize\"]\n    G1 -->|\"yes\"| S2[\"2. Public harness, both models\"]\n    S2 --> S3[\"3. Golden set, paired stats, flips\"]\n    S3 --> G3{\"CI excludes harmful drop?\"}\n    G3 -->|\"no\"| R\n    G3 -->|\"yes\"| S4[\"4. Weak-spot slices and speed\"]\n    S4 --> S5[\"5. Canary or shadow vs fp16\"]\n    S5 --> V[(\"Registered model version\")]","caption":"The evaluation ladder for a quantized agent model: each rung is more expensive and closer to real\nagent outcomes, and a failure at any gate stops the artifact before the next rung."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is perplexity alone not enough to accept a quantized model?","a":"- **It averages over easy tokens.** Tasks hinge on a few decisive tokens (digits, names, braces) whose\n  probabilities can move a lot while mean log-loss barely changes.\n- **It ignores the rest of the distribution.** KL divergence and top-1 agreement catch shifts in\n  alternative tokens, and task evals catch what actually breaks."},{"q":"Your 4-bit agent model passes all benchmarks but its tool calls now return broken JSON. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Reproduce with a paired slice:** run fp16 and quantized on the same tool-calling prompts with\n  identical decoding and compare validity rates and flips.\n- **Check what changed together:** if the KV cache was also quantized, test it separately; check the\n  calibration data's domain.\n- **Mitigate:** a different group size or method, an 8-bit build, or constrained decoding for the JSON\n  path, then add the slice to the gate."},{"q":"How many golden-set items do you need to detect a 2-point drop?","a":"- **Independent runs** at 80% accuracy on 300 items give about ±6.4 points, far too wide.\n- **Paired analysis** with ~5% disagreement gives about ±2.5 on the same 300 items, still wider than the\n  effect, so 300 is not enough.\n- **Solve for n:** a ±2-point half-width needs 0.05 × (1.96 ÷ 0.02)² ≈ 480 items; detecting a 2-point drop\n  with 80% power needs roughly 0.05 × ((1.96 + 0.84) ÷ 0.02)² ≈ 980, fewer if disagreement is lower."},{"q":"When would you accept a measurable quality drop from quantization?","a":"- **When the error cost is low and the saving is large:** for example a support bot where halving serving\n  cost outweighs a small, interval-backed drop on non-critical slices.\n- **Never silently:** flips on high-stakes slices are read by a human, and the threshold is set per use\n  case, not globally."},{"q":"Two \"4-bit\" builds of the same model behave differently. Why?","a":"- **Different recipes:** group size, per-channel versus per-tensor scales and calibration set all move the\n  error, so two files can differ more from each other than from fp16.\n- **So** evaluate each artifact, and treat each as its own registered model version."},{"q":"How would you decide whether a 4-bit build is good enough to drive a 15-step agent?","a":"- **Measure at two levels:** per-call tool-call validity and argument accuracy, and run success on\n  replayed agent tasks, both paired against fp16 with identical tools and decoding.\n- **Expect compounding:** 99% versus 98% per step is about 86% versus 74% per 15-step run, so small\n  per-call deltas matter.\n- **Read the broken runs** to find the failing step type, then fix it (an 8-bit build, constrained\n  decoding for tool calls) or reject."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cheap signals vs task truth.** Perplexity and KL are cheap and sensitive, but they measure distribution drift on fixed text, not task outcomes; a model can pass them and still fail a format-critical workflow.","**Golden-set evals vs cost and noise.** They are the real acceptance test, yet they cost GPU time and need enough items for paired statistics; on small sets judge disagreement can exceed the quantization effect.","**Greedy vs sampled comparison.** Greedy gives a clean comparison but is not how production runs; sampled comparisons are realistic but need several seeds to average out noise.","**Single-turn evals vs agent-run evals.** Single-turn slices are cheap and repeatable, but miss how errors compound across steps; full agent replays catch compounding at much higher cost per item. The balance flips toward run-level evals as runs get longer.","**Strict thresholds vs serving cost.** Stricter acceptance protects quality but can block a quantization that would have cut serving cost in half; the right threshold depends on what the task's errors cost."],"pitfalls":["**Comparing against a published fp16 score.** Re-run the fp16 model yourself under identical prompts and settings; otherwise the \"regression\" is really a harness difference.","**Judging by chat impressions.** Fluency survives quantization long after arithmetic, code and long-context recall have degraded, so the demo looks fine and the eval does not. Fix: run the ladder, starting with the weak-spot slices.","**Ignoring the calibration set.** A GPTQ or AWQ file calibrated on English web text can degrade noticeably on code or another language. The tell is a domain KL far above the WikiText KL.","**Quantizing weights and KV cache at once** and attributing any loss to the weights. Test them separately, since long-context failures usually trace to the cache.","**Calling a 0.8-point drop \"within noise\" or \"significant\"** without a confidence interval from paired per-item results.","**Accepting from single-turn scores for an agent model.** Per-call accuracy drops a point and looks fine. Symptom: run completion falls sharply on long tasks after rollout. Fix: gate on replayed agent runs, and slice by run length.","**Reporting only net accuracy.** Equal numbers of fixed and broken items cancel in the mean. Symptom: an eval that says \"no change\" followed by specific, reproducible complaints from production users. Fix: report broke and fixed counts and read every broken item."],"when":"- **Run the full ladder** whenever a quantized artifact is a production candidate: a new checkpoint, a new\n  quantization method or group size, a new engine kernel, or KV-cache quantization.\n- **Gates:** make steps 1 and 3 mandatory in the model release pipeline and steps 2 and 4 part of the\n  evaluation record. Skip the expensive steps only for throwaway experiments.\n- **Weight by business cost:** watch the categories that break first; a support bot may tolerate what a\n  code assistant cannot.\n- **For agent models:** add tool-call validity and replayed run success to the mandatory gates, since\n  those are where quantization damage compounds.\n- **Interviews:** the expected answer to \"how do you know 4-bit is fine?\" is this ladder plus why\n  perplexity alone is not enough, not a quoted benchmark number.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the teacher-forced comparison script and the golden-set run, with paired statistics, wired into CI so every quantized artifact gets a delta report automatically.","architect":"Sets the acceptance thresholds per use case and the policy that quantized models are registered, evaluated and rolled out as distinct model versions.","fde":"Builds the domain text sample and golden set from the customer's own data, and shows them the measured quality-versus-cost curve before recommending a quantized deployment."},"check":{"q":"A team reports that their 4-bit model's WikiText perplexity rose by 2% and MMLU dropped by 0.3\npoints, and concludes it is safe for their contract-extraction product. What would you push back on?","a":"Both signals are generic. WikiText perplexity averages over easy tokens of encyclopaedic prose,\nand MMLU is multiple choice, which is the category that survives quantization best. Contract extraction\ndepends on long inputs, exact numbers and dates, and valid structured output, which are exactly the\ncategories that degrade first, and none were measured. I would ask for KL and top-1 agreement on a sample\nof their contracts, a paired comparison of extraction accuracy and JSON validity on their golden set under\nidentical prompts and decoding, a long-context slice at their maximum document length, separate tests if\nthe KV cache is quantized, and confidence intervals on every delta, plus the list of flipped items\nso someone reads what actually broke. A 0.3-point MMLU drop without an interval is not evidence either\nway."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is per-token KL divergence a better early signal of quantization damage than perplexity alone?","options":["KL is cheaper to compute because it does not need the reference text","KL measures how far the whole next-token distribution moved, so it catches shifts that leave average log-loss almost unchanged","Perplexity cannot be computed for quantized models","KL directly predicts task accuracy on downstream benchmarks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Perplexity only scores the probability of the actual next token. KL compares the full distributions, so it flags cases where alternative tokens gain or lose mass even when the target token's probability is similar."},{"q":"Two evaluations of the same quantized model on a 300-item golden set use the same prompts for both models. Which analysis is most appropriate?","options":["Compare the two mean accuracies and call any drop above 1% a regression","Run the quantized model five times and average, ignoring the fp16 run","A paired per-item comparison with a bootstrap confidence interval on the difference","A chi-squared test on the total number of correct answers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because each item is scored under both models, pairing removes item difficulty from the variance and makes the test far more sensitive at the same sample size. Independent-mean comparisons waste that structure."},{"q":"Which category is most likely to show quantization damage first?","options":["Short factual multiple-choice questions","Casual conversational fluency","Multi-step arithmetic, code and long-context retrieval with strict output formats","Translation between high-resource European languages"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tasks that hinge on a few decisive tokens over long chains, or on recalling a detail far back in a quantized KV cache, amplify small distribution shifts. Fluency and recognition-style tasks are robust."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","sources":["Willard & Louf, 2023 — Efficient Guided Generation for Large Language Models","Dong et al., 2024 — XGrammar: Flexible and Efficient Structured Generation Engine for Large Language Models","Tam et al., 2024 — Let Me Speak Freely? A Study on the Impact of Format Restrictions on Performance of Large Language Models","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **Agent code needs parseable output.** A JSON object with known fields, a function call with typed\n  arguments, one of five labels for a router.\n- **\"Mostly complies\" is the problem.** Models wrap JSON in Markdown fences, add a trailing comma, rename a\n  field, emit a comment, or start with \"Sure, here is the JSON\". At a 2% failure rate, a million calls a\n  day break twenty thousand times, clustered on the hardest inputs.\n- **Agents compound the rate.** A run makes many tool calls in sequence, so a small per-call failure rate\n  becomes a large per-run one (see the worked table below).\n- **Validate-and-retry is not enough.** Still worth having for semantic checks, but each retry doubles\n  latency and cost on requests that were already slow, the failure rate never reaches zero, and small or\n  heavily quantized models can loop without converging.\n- **Regex repair is brittle the other way:** it silently accepts malformed intent.\n- **Constrained decoding attacks the root.** The engine knows from the grammar and the output so far which\n  tokens are legal and zeroes the rest before sampling, so invalid output becomes impossible, not\n  unlikely. Open engines (vLLM, SGLang, llama.cpp, TensorRT-LLM) and hosted \"structured outputs\" and\n  strict tool-calling modes all use it; it is now the default.","how":"### Where the constraint sits\n1. Each decode step produces a logits vector over the vocabulary.\n2. A *logits processor* computes the allowed token ids for the current state and adds −∞ to every other\n   entry.\n3. Softmax assigns those tokens zero probability; temperature, top-p and the rest apply only among the\n   allowed ones.\n\nThe model is never asked to do anything; it is simply not permitted to make certain moves.\n\n### Tool calling is the same mechanism\n- **Schema per tool plus a choice of tool.** Function-calling APIs are structured generation: the tool\n  name is an enum and the arguments follow that tool's schema.\n- **Same automaton idea** over the argument schema in hosted \"strict\" tool modes and open engines' tool\n  parsers, which is why argument validity is high while argument *correctness* still needs checks.\n\n### The token-versus-character problem\n- **Grammars are over characters; models emit tokens.** A vocabulary of 100k+ entries has many tokens that\n  straddle grammar boundaries: `\"na`, `me\":`, ` {\"`.\n- **Brute force is too slow.** \"For each of 128k tokens, would appending its characters keep the output\n  valid?\" costs a full grammar walk per token per step, far too slow for a decode step budgeted in\n  milliseconds.\n\n### Outlines' insight: precompute the index\n1. **Compile** the regex (or the regex derived from a JSON schema) into a deterministic finite automaton.\n2. **For every automaton state,** walk every vocabulary token through it and record which tokens are legal\n   and which state each leads to.\n3. **At generation time** track the current state; the allowed set is a table lookup.\n\nThe expensive scan happens at compile time *because* it depends only on the grammar and the tokenizer,\nnot on the request.\n\n### A tiny index\nPattern: \"exactly three digits, then stop\". Toy vocabulary: `7`, `42`, `123`, `1234`, `a`, ` 5`.\n\n| State | Legal tokens → next state | Illegal |\n|---|---|---|\n| start (no digits) | `7` → one digit, `42` → two digits, `123` → accepting | `1234`, `a`, ` 5` |\n| two digits | single-digit tokens only | everything longer |\n| accepting | end-of-sequence only | all others |\n\nMulti-character tokens jump several automaton steps at once, which a character-level check cannot see\nwithout walking each token.\n\n### Regular vs context-free grammars\n- **Non-recursive JSON schemas** compile to regexes.\n- **Recursive or deeply nested grammars** need a pushdown automaton (a context-free grammar): llama.cpp's\n  GBNF grammars and XGrammar implement this. Nested tool arguments (a list of objects with sub-objects)\n  land here.\n- **XGrammar caches** the tokens whose legality does not depend on the stack, so most steps are still a\n  lookup.\n\n### What the automaton controls\n- **No stopping halfway.** End-of-sequence is allowed only in an accepting state.\n- **Forced tokens.** When exactly one token is legal (a closing brace, a fixed key name), some engines skip\n  sampling and append it directly, saving decode steps on boilerplate.\n- **Hard enums, ranges and patterns.** A `\"status\"` field declared as `enum: [\"open\", \"closed\"]` cannot\n  come back as `\"Closed\"`; a tool name cannot be one the agent does not have.\n\n### The cost at serving time\n- **Overlap with the forward pass.** Grammar state advance and mask computation run on the CPU while the\n  GPU computes the same step's forward pass.\n- **Compact application.** The result is a per-sequence bitmask over the vocabulary applied just before\n  sampling. When mask computation is slower than the forward pass, it shows up directly as higher time\n  per output token.\n- **Jump-forward.** Appending runs of forced tokens without sampling is what SGLang calls jump-forward\n  decoding.\n- **Scaling.** Every sequence in a batch can carry a different grammar (each agent has its own tool set),\n  so CPU-side work (compiling grammars, computing each mask) must scale with concurrency; applying the\n  mask on the GPU is cheap.\n\n### What it does not control\n- **Truth.** A valid schema says nothing about whether values are true; a hallucinated invoice number\n  passes a regex for invoice numbers, and a well-formed tool call can target the wrong record. Semantic\n  validation (Pydantic validators, cross-field rules, database lookups) still belongs after generation,\n  with retry-with-feedback for *those* failures.\n- **The distribution.** Where the model wanted to write prose, it must choose whitespace or a quote, and\n  may take a low-probability path.\n- **Not true conditional sampling.** Masking and renormalising each step is not sampling from the model's\n  distribution conditioned on valid output. The mask is greedy and local, so the model can be steered into\n  a prefix whose only legal continuations it finds unlikely, and it can never back up.\n- **Tokenization artefacts.** Forcing output to begin with `{` rather than ` {` can land the model in a\n  token sequence it rarely saw in training.\n- **Reasoning can suffer.** One 2024 study (Tam et al.) reported that strict format restrictions can\n  degrade reasoning performance in some settings, which is why the rules below exist.\n\n### Design rules that follow\n- **Reason before answering.** Put a free-text `reasoning` or `analysis` field *before* the answer fields,\n  or run a two-stage pipeline: think freely, then extract under constraint.\n- **Keep schemas flat and small.** Prefer enums to open strings; order fields the way evidence appears.\n- **Cache grammars by schema hash.** Compilation ranges from milliseconds to seconds for large schemas, and\n  a per-request dynamic schema throws that away every call.\n- **JSON mode is not schema enforcement.** JSON mode guarantees syntactically valid JSON of any shape;\n  structured-output modes enforce your schema's fields, types and enums.\n- **Check backend coverage.** Hosted strict modes and open-engine backends support a *subset* of JSON\n  Schema (for example requiring every field and forbidding additional properties), so validate the schema\n  against the backend before relying on a feature.","example":"### The token index for \"exactly three digits\", in pure Python\nRunnable as is. It builds the state-to-token table from the tiny index above at compile time, then\ndecodes greedily with a fake model that prefers illegal tokens.\n\n```python\nVOCAB = [\"7\", \"42\", \"123\", \"1234\", \"a\", \" 5\", \"<eos>\"]\nACCEPT = 3                                      # DFA for \"exactly three digits\": state = digits seen\n\ndef step(state, ch):\n    return state + 1 if ch.isdigit() and state < ACCEPT else None   # None = dead state\n\ndef build_index(vocab):\n    \"\"\"Compile time: for every DFA state, which tokens are legal and where they lead.\"\"\"\n    index = {}\n    for s in range(ACCEPT + 1):\n        index[s] = {}\n        for tok in vocab:\n            if tok == \"<eos>\":\n                if s == ACCEPT:\n                    index[s][tok] = \"done\"      # EOS only in the accepting state\n                continue\n            cur = s\n            for ch in tok:                      # walk every character of the token\n                cur = step(cur, ch)\n                if cur is None:\n                    break\n            if cur is not None:\n                index[s][tok] = cur\n    return index\n\ndef constrained_greedy(scores, index):\n    \"\"\"Run time: a lookup per step, then argmax among allowed tokens only.\"\"\"\n    state, out = 0, []\n    while state != \"done\":\n        allowed = index[state]\n        tok = max(allowed, key=lambda t: scores.get(t, float(\"-inf\")))\n        out.append(tok)\n        state = allowed[tok]\n    return \"\".join(t for t in out if t != \"<eos>\")\n\nindex = build_index(VOCAB)\nfor s, allowed in index.items():\n    print(s, allowed)       # 0 {'7': 1, '42': 2, '123': 3} ... 3 {'<eos>': 'done'}\n# The \"model\" prefers 1234 and a, both illegal from the start state.\nfake_logits = {\"1234\": 5.0, \"a\": 4.0, \"<eos>\": 3.0, \"42\": 1.0, \"7\": 0.5}\nprint(constrained_greedy(fake_logits, index))   # -> 427\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: parse failures across an agent run\nA 10-step agent makes one tool call per step. Illustrative rates; the point is how per-call rates\ncompound per run (run failure = 1 − (1 − p)^10).\n\n| Setup | Per-call failure | Runs with at least one failure | Extra calls per 1,000 runs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prompt-only JSON, 2% parse failures | 2% | about 18% | about 200 retries, more if retries fail |\n| Prompt-only JSON plus semantic validators (2% parse, 1% wrong values) | about 3% | about 26% | about 300, a full extra call each |\n| Constrained decoding, shape guaranteed | 0% parse | 0% from shape | 0 for shape |\n| Constrained plus semantic validators (say 1% wrong values) | 1% semantic | about 10% | about 100, spent only on meaning |\n\nConstrained decoding removes the shape column entirely; the remaining retry budget goes to errors no\ngrammar can express. Check `finish_reason` too: output cut at `max_tokens` is the one way a constrained\ncall still fails to parse.\n\n### Agent router step: schema-constrained tool choice\nA router picks one of the agent's tools under a JSON Schema via `response_format`, with reasoning first.\nThe enum makes an unknown tool name impossible; Pydantic and a lookup catch wrong-but-valid values.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nTOOLS = {\"search_orders\": lambda q: f\"orders matching {q}\", \"refund\": lambda q: f\"refund for {q}\"}\n\nclass Route(BaseModel):\n    reasoning: str                                        # free text FIRST\n    tool: Literal[\"search_orders\", \"refund\", \"ask_human\"]\n    query: str\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"EMPTY\")\n\ndef route(user_msg: str, retries: int = 1) -> str:\n    msgs = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Pick the next tool for this support agent.\"},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_msg}]\n    for _ in range(retries + 1):\n        resp = client.chat.completions.create(\n            model=\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\", messages=msgs, temperature=0,\n            response_format={\"type\": \"json_schema\",\n                             \"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"route\", \"schema\": Route.model_json_schema()}})\n        r = Route.model_validate_json(resp.choices[0].message.content)   # shape is guaranteed\n        if r.tool == \"ask_human\":\n            return \"escalated\"\n        if r.query.strip():                                              # semantic check, not shape\n            return TOOLS[r.tool](r.query)\n        msgs += [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": resp.choices[0].message.content},\n                 {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"query was empty; give a concrete query\"}]\n    return \"escalated\"\n\nprint(route(\"I was charged twice for order 1182, please fix it.\"))\n```\n\n### A choice constraint from scratch\nThe output must be exactly one of a fixed set of choices, then stop. The allowed-token table is a trie\nbuilt once from the tokenizer, so each step is a lookup rather than a vocabulary scan: the structure\nOutlines and XGrammar generalise to regexes and grammars.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, LogitsProcessor, LogitsProcessorList\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float16).cuda().eval()\n\nclass ChoiceConstraint(LogitsProcessor):\n    \"\"\"Allow only token sequences that spell one of `choices`, then EOS.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, choices, prompt_len):\n        self.trie, self.prompt_len = {}, prompt_len\n        for c in choices:                                   # built ONCE: grammar x tokenizer\n            # one canonical tokenization per choice; real engines allow every token path that spells it\n            node = self.trie\n            for t in tok(c, add_special_tokens=False).input_ids:\n                node = node.setdefault(t, {})\n            node[tok.eos_token_id] = {}                     # accepting state: EOS becomes legal\n    def __call__(self, input_ids, scores):\n        node = self.trie\n        for t in input_ids[0, self.prompt_len:].tolist():  # replay emitted tokens to find the state\n            node = node.get(t, {})\n        allowed = list(node) or [tok.eos_token_id]\n        mask = torch.full_like(scores, float(\"-inf\"))\n        mask[0, allowed] = 0.0\n        return scores + mask                                # every other token gets probability 0\n\nmsgs = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Is the sky green? Answer with one word.\"}]\nids = tok.apply_chat_template(msgs, add_generation_prompt=True, return_tensors=\"pt\").cuda()\nproc = LogitsProcessorList([ChoiceConstraint([\"yes\", \"no\", \"unknown\"], ids.shape[1])])\nout = model.generate(ids, max_new_tokens=5, do_sample=False, logits_processor=proc)\nprint(repr(tok.decode(out[0, ids.shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True)))   # -> 'no'\n```\n\n### Production call: schema-constrained output plus semantic validation\nAgainst vLLM's OpenAI-compatible server (hosted providers accept the same `response_format` shape). The\nreasoning field comes first, and Pydantic re-validates after generation because some backends ignore\nnumeric bounds. In an agent this is the extraction step that fills a ticket before a tool files it.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass Ticket(BaseModel):\n    analysis: str                                         # free-text reasoning FIRST\n    category: Literal[\"billing\", \"bug\", \"account\", \"other\"]\n    priority: int = Field(ge=1, le=4)                     # bounds may be ignored by the backend\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"EMPTY\")\nresp = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Classify this ticket: 'I was charged twice this month.'\"}],\n    response_format={\"type\": \"json_schema\",\n                     \"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"ticket\", \"schema\": Ticket.model_json_schema()}},\n    temperature=0,\n)\nticket = Ticket.model_validate_json(resp.choices[0].message.content)   # shape AND bounds checked\nprint(ticket.category, ticket.priority)\n```","patterns":["**Strict tool calling and native structured outputs, not hand-rolled processors.** When output feeds agent code, pass a JSON Schema via `response_format` or the tool definitions (or the engine's grammar or choice mode) and let the engine's backend, such as XGrammar in vLLM, do the masking. Monitor validity rate; it should sit at 100%, and anything less usually means output truncated at `max_tokens` (check `finish_reason`) or a schema feature the backend does not enforce.","**Static tool schemas, cached grammars.** Keep tool and output schemas versioned in code and identical across requests and agent steps, with no request ids or dates inside enums, so compiled grammars are reused. Monitor TTFT for first-seen schemas versus cached ones.","**Think, then extract.** For planning and reasoning-heavy steps, put an `analysis` field first or split into a free call plus a constrained extraction call. Monitor task accuracy and agent run success against an unconstrained baseline, not just validity.","**Validate semantics with bounded retries per step.** Parse with Pydantic `model_validate_json`, enforce cross-field rules and lookups (does that order id exist?), and retry at most once or twice with the error message fed back into the transcript. Monitor retry rate and the share of steps that exhaust retries and escalate.","**Schema compatibility test in CI.** Run every tool and output schema through the target backend with a few fixture prompts, asserting that unsupported keywords (`format: date`, `minimum`, `pattern`) are either enforced or re-checked after generation."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent loop breaks on the first unparseable tool call or routing\n  decision. Constrained decoding is the mechanism under strict tool calling and structured outputs, so it\n  decides whether step 12 of a run can crash on a stray Markdown fence.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Hosted \"structured outputs\" and strict tool-calling modes, the\n  `response_format` JSON Schema on vLLM's OpenAI-compatible server (with XGrammar as a backend), SGLang,\n  llama.cpp GBNF grammars, and libraries such as Outlines and Instructor on top.\n- **What you control.** Tool and output schemas (flat, static, versioned), field order (reasoning before\n  answer), whitespace rules, the semantic validators behind the parser, and the retry budget per step.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing your own logits processor or automaton compiler. Know that masking\n  happens at the logits, that grammars are compiled and cached, and what the guarantee does not cover.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool-calling agents.** Strict tool modes guarantee every call names a real tool and matches its\n  argument schema, so the loop never crashes on a parse error.\n- **Routers and planners.** An enum-constrained routing step picks the next tool, subagent or model with\n  no free-text parsing.\n- **Extraction into agent memory.** Facts written to a memory store or database come out in a fixed schema\n  that downstream retrieval can rely on.\n- **Small self-hosted agent models.** Constraints make 7B-class models usable for tool calls they would\n  otherwise format wrong a few percent of the time.","mini_project":"**Constrained versus prompted tool calls in a tiny agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure what constrained decoding removes and what it leaves for semantic validation.\n- **Steps:** build a 3-tool support agent (search orders, refund, ask human) against vLLM or a hosted API;\n  run 50 scripted tasks with prompt-only JSON and again with a JSON Schema in `response_format`; log\n  parse failures, semantic failures (unknown order id, empty query), retries and TTFT per step; add a\n  Pydantic validator with one retry.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-setup table of parse failures, semantic failures, retries and task\n  success, with parse failures at zero under constraints and a note on which errors remained."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Constrained decoding means putting the schema in the prompt.\"** Prompting asks; constraining forbids. The engine adds −∞ to every illegal token's logit before sampling, so invalid output is impossible rather than unlikely, whatever the prompt says.","**\"A schema-valid tool call is a correct tool call.\"** The grammar guarantees shape, not truth: a hallucinated order id or wrong amount passes every type check. Semantic validators and lookups still run after parsing, before the tool executes.","**\"The constrained output is what the model would have said, just tidied up.\"** The mask is greedy and local, not sampling conditioned on valid output, so it can steer the model down unlikely paths, and an answer field placed before reasoning commits it early. Put `reasoning` first or think, then extract.","**\"With a strict schema the call can never fail to parse.\"** Output cut at `max_tokens` is still truncated JSON (check `finish_reason`), and some backends ignore keywords such as `minimum`, `pattern` or `format: date`. Re-validate with Pydantic.","**\"JSON mode already enforces my schema.\"** JSON mode guarantees syntactically valid JSON of any shape; structured-output modes enforce your fields, types and enums.","**\"Masking is expensive because every step checks the whole vocabulary.\"** The state-to-token table is compiled once per schema and tokenizer, so a step is mostly a lookup (for nested grammars XGrammar caches the stack-independent tokens and checks only the rest). The real costs are compilation, paid again whenever a request brings a new schema (higher TTFT), and CPU mask work that must keep pace with the batch (higher TPOT when it falls behind)."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    G[\"Tool or output schema\"] --> C[\"Compile once: state to token table\"]\n    C --> K[(\"Grammar cache by schema hash\")]\n    K --> M[\"CPU: allowed-token mask for state\"]\n    F[\"GPU: forward pass, logits\"] --> A[\"Add minus infinity to illegal tokens\"]\n    M --> A\n    A --> S[\"Sample among legal tokens\"]\n    S --> N[\"Advance automaton state\"]\n    N --> E{\"Accepting state and EOS?\"}\n    E -->|\"no\"| M\n    E -->|\"yes\"| V[\"Parse, semantic check, run tool\"]","caption":"One constrained decode step inside an agent's tool call: the CPU computes the grammar mask while the GPU\nruns the forward pass, the mask is applied to the logits, and the sampled token advances the automaton\nuntil the call is complete and goes to semantic validation."},"interview":[{"q":"How does constrained decoding guarantee valid JSON, and why at the logits?","a":"- **Mechanism:** a logits processor adds −∞ to every token the grammar forbids from the current state, so\n  softmax gives them zero probability and the sampler cannot pick them.\n- **Why the logits:** it is the last point before sampling where the engine can remove choices, so\n  validity holds by construction instead of being checked and retried afterwards."},{"q":"Your agent's tool calls always parse now, but it still takes wrong actions. What do you add?","a":"- **Shape is not truth:** strict mode guarantees the call matches the schema, not that the order id\n  exists or the amount is right.\n- **Semantic validators before the tool runs:** Pydantic rules, cross-field checks and lookups against the\n  system of record, with the error fed back for one or two bounded retries, then escalation.\n- **Schema design:** enums for closed sets, a reasoning field first, and a run-level eval of action\n  correctness, not just validity rate."},{"q":"Why can't you just check each vocabulary token against the grammar at every step?","a":"- **Cost:** 100k+ tokens, each needing a grammar walk, per step per sequence, far beyond a millisecond\n  decode budget.\n- **Fix:** precompute, per automaton state, the legal tokens and their next states once per grammar and\n  tokenizer; runtime is a lookup, and XGrammar extends this to context-free grammars."},{"q":"TTFT jumped after you enabled structured outputs for a self-hosted agent fleet. What do you look at?","a":"- **Grammar compilation:** is each request or agent step sending a slightly different schema (tool lists\n  rebuilt per user, dates in enums), defeating the cache?\n- **Schema size and nesting:** large or recursive schemas compile slowly; flatten or version them.\n- **CPU saturation:** mask computation runs on the CPU per sequence, so check CPU utilisation under\n  concurrency and TPOT as well as TTFT."},{"q":"When would you not use constrained decoding?","a":"- **Free-form writing,** where no consumer parses the output, such as the agent's reply to the user.\n- **Reasoning-heavy answers** unless the schema lets the model reason first or you extract in a second\n  call, because strict formats can degrade reasoning.\n- **When only semantics matter:** the grammar cannot catch wrong values, so validation is still needed."},{"q":"What is the difference between JSON mode and schema-enforced structured outputs?","a":"- **JSON mode** guarantees syntactically valid JSON of any shape: fields can be missing or renamed.\n- **Structured outputs** enforce your fields, types and enums, but backends support a subset of JSON\n  Schema, so check which keywords are actually enforced."}],"tradeoffs":["**Guaranteed validity vs distribution shift.** Retries vanish, but the constraint can push the model down low-probability paths; the cost is small for flat schemas and grows with rigid, deeply nested ones.","**Compile-once tables vs dynamic schemas.** Per-step masking becomes nearly free, but a new schema per request (tool lists rebuilt per user) pays compilation each time; the win flips to a loss without a grammar cache.","**Hosted strict modes vs open engines.** Hosted modes are zero-effort, yet support a schema subset and hide the mechanism; open engines expose regex, grammar and choice modes but you own backend selection and version drift.","**Constraints vs validate-and-retry.** Retry handles semantic errors no grammar can express, at a full extra call per failure; combining both gives shape for free and spends retries only on meaning."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting schema-valid tool calls as correct.** Symptom: clean JSON with invented identifiers or wrong numbers that no parser will ever flag, and the agent acts on them. Fix: semantic validators and lookups after parsing, before the tool runs.","**Answer field first, `reasoning` last.** The model commits to an answer before it has \"thought\"; accuracy on reasoning tasks drops compared with unconstrained prompting. Fix: reasoning field first.","**A fresh, slightly different schema per request** (a timestamp or request id baked into an enum). Symptom: high time to first token from grammar compilation on each call. Fix: static, versioned schemas.","**JSON Schema features the backend ignores** (`format: date`, `minimum`, `pattern` in some modes), discovered in production when out-of-range values come through. Fix: a CI compatibility test, and re-validate with Pydantic.","**Forgetting whitespace is part of the grammar.** Unbounded whitespace between tokens lets a model loop on spaces or newlines until `max_tokens`; forbidding it forces minified JSON the model rarely saw in training. Set the whitespace pattern deliberately."],"when":"- **Use when** output is consumed by code: tool arguments, routing decisions, extraction, classification\n  into a fixed label set, memory writes, batch data transformation.\n- **Prefer** the engine's or provider's native structured-output or strict tool mode over hand-rolled\n  logits processors, and keep Pydantic (or equivalent) validation plus bounded retries for semantics.\n- **Avoid** for free-form writing such as the agent's final answer to a user; be careful with\n  reasoning-heavy planning steps, where a two-stage design keeps accuracy.\n- **Measure** validity rate, retry rate, time to first token with and without the grammar, and task\n  accuracy against an unconstrained baseline.\n- **Interviews:** explain why masking happens at the logits, how compile-time precomputation solves the\n  token-boundary problem, and what the guarantee does not cover.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines Pydantic models, passes the schema to the engine's structured-output mode, caches compiled grammars, and adds semantic validators and bounded retries behind them.","architect":"Sets the contract between model outputs and downstream services, chooses hosted strict modes versus open-engine backends, and mandates where free-text reasoning precedes structured answers.","fde":"Turns a customer's extraction target into a schema the backend fully supports, then proves validity and accuracy on their documents against the unconstrained baseline."},"check":{"q":"A team switched an extraction pipeline from prompt-and-retry to constrained JSON decoding.\nValidity went to 100%, but accuracy on a set of hard cases dropped several points. What would you\ninvestigate and change?","a":"Constrained decoding changed the distribution the model samples from, not just the format. I\nwould first check field order: if the answer fields come before any reasoning field, the model is forced\nto commit before working through the evidence, and moving a free-text analysis field to the front or\nsplitting into a think-then-extract two-stage call usually recovers the loss. I would check whether the\ngrammar forces unusual tokenizations (no leading space, escaped newlines) that differ from how the model\nsaw JSON in training, and whether the schema is stricter than necessary, with enums that exclude valid\nanswers. Then I would compare accuracy on the same prompts under unconstrained decoding plus parsing to\nquantify the gap, and keep semantic validators and bounded retries for the residual errors."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do libraries like Outlines precompute a table from automaton states to allowed tokens?","options":["To reduce the model's vocabulary size at load time","Because logits cannot be modified after the forward pass","So each decode step is a lookup instead of checking every vocabulary token against the grammar","To make sampling deterministic regardless of temperature"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Checking 100k+ tokens against a grammar every step is far too slow. The grammar and tokenizer are fixed, so the legality of each token from each state can be computed once and reused for every request."},{"q":"What does constrained decoding guarantee about a generated JSON object?","options":["That every value is factually consistent with the input","That it parses and conforms to the schema's structure, types and enums","That it is the most probable output the model could produce","That it matches what the model would have produced without the constraint"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The automaton only forbids tokens that would break the grammar. Content is still the model's guess, so semantic validation and retries remain necessary for correctness."},{"q":"A reasoning-heavy classification task loses accuracy under a strict schema. What is the most likely effective fix?","options":["Raise the temperature so the model explores more tokens","Remove enums so the model can write any label","Add a free-text reasoning field before the answer field, or extract in a second constrained call","Switch from JSON to YAML output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forcing an immediate answer removes the model's chance to work through the problem in prose. Letting it reason first, then constraining the final fields, restores the behaviour that unconstrained prompting had."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Dao et al., 2022 — FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness","Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: PyTorch (CUDA memory management)"],"why":"- **Failure 1: OOM at peak traffic.** Tests used short prompts and modest concurrency, and allocation is\n  lazy, so the first time a long prompt met a full decode batch the engine died. Agents are the worst\n  case: late steps carry the longest transcripts.\n- **Failure 2: quietly wasted hardware.** An 80 GB GPU leaves 50 GB unused because the concurrency limit\n  stayed at a cautious default, so tokens per dollar is a third of what the hardware allows.\n- **Both come from observing instead of computing.** `nvidia-smi` mixes allocated, cached and reserved\n  memory, and a model that \"uses 18 GB\" at idle can need 60 GB under load.\n- **\"Start it and see if it fits\" answers the wrong question.** Fitting at idle says nothing about\n  capacity at peak, because what matters (tokens in flight, prompt-length distribution) is a property of\n  traffic, not the model.\n- **The arithmetic is short and exact enough to plan on.** Weights are fixed by parameter count and\n  precision; KV cache is a per-token cost times a traffic quantity you cap; activations are bounded by the\n  largest step you allow; overhead is a few gigabytes you measure once.","how":"### Line 1: weights\n`bytes = params × bytes_per_param`, or as a ledger line, `weights = params × bytes_per_param`.\n\n| Precision | Bytes per param |\n|---|---|\n| bf16/fp16 | 2 |\n| fp8/int8 | 1 |\n| 4-bit | 0.5 plus scales and zero points |\n\n- **4-bit is more than 4 bits.** The simple `Q4_0` layout stores 32 weights × 4 bits plus a 16-bit scale,\n  4.5 bits per weight; group-128 schemes such as GPTQ or AWQ land near 4.1–4.2.\n- **Embeddings and LM head.** Published parameter counts include them; if you estimate from per-layer\n  shapes, add them. For a 128k-token vocabulary and hidden size 8,192 in bf16 each is about 2.1 GB, and\n  an untied model has both. Weight-only quantizers often leave them at higher precision, so a \"4-bit\" file\n  is larger than params × 0.5 suggests.\n- **Everything else resident:** a draft model for speculative decoding (plus its own KV cache), a vision\n  encoder for screenshot-reading agents, preallocated LoRA adapter slots for per-agent fine-tunes.\n- **MoE: count all experts,** since every expert must be resident though only a few run per token. A model\n  with 47B total and 13B active parameters costs 94 GB in bf16, not 26 GB.\n\n### Line 2: KV cache\n```\nbytes_per_token = 2 × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element\nKV = 2 × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × bytes × tokens_in_flight\n```\n- **Tokens in flight** = the sum over active sequences of prompt plus generated tokens so far. For agents,\n  each run's transcript at its current step.\n- **With paged allocation** that is the *actual* total, not the sum of per-request maximums.\n- **The levers are the formula read term by term:** fewer KV heads (GQA or MLA architectures), smaller\n  elements (fp8 cache), fewer tokens (context cap, concurrency cap, transcript compaction, prefix sharing\n  of the system prompt and tool schemas), or divide it across GPUs with tensor parallelism.\n\n### Line 3: activations and workspace\n- **Prefill shape.** A chunk of T tokens flows through each layer as [T, hidden], expands to\n  [T, intermediate] inside the MLP, and attention needs its own buffers.\n- **FlashAttention keeps attention linear in T,** because it never materialises the [T, T] score matrix;\n  without it a 32k prompt would need gigabytes of scores per head.\n- **Chunked prefill caps T per step,** which caps this peak regardless of prompt length, including a\n  large tool result or retrieved document pasted into the transcript.\n- **Scale.** With T = 8,192 and a 70B-class intermediate size of 28,672, gate and up projections each\n  produce 8,192 × 28,672 × 2 bytes ≈ 0.47 GB, about 0.94 GB together before a tensor-parallel split. It is\n  one layer's transient, reused layer after layer, not summed.\n- **The LM-head trap.** On every prompt position it would produce 8,192 × 128k × 4 bytes ≈ 4.2 GB of fp32\n  logits, which is why engines compute logits only at positions they sample from.\n- **Sampling buffer.** [batch, vocab] in fp32: 256 sequences × 128k vocabulary × 4 bytes is about 131 MB,\n  small but real.\n- **Engines estimate this line** with a dummy forward pass at the maximum batch and token budget during\n  startup, which is why those limits change the reported KV capacity.\n\n### Line 4: runtime overhead and fragmentation\n- **Per process:** the CUDA context, cuBLAS workspaces, NCCL buffers for tensor parallelism and CUDA-graph\n  captures take a few hundred megabytes to a couple of gigabytes (illustrative range). That is why two\n  processes on one GPU waste more than one.\n- **Allocator reserve.** PyTorch's caching allocator holds freed blocks, so `memory_reserved()` exceeds\n  `memory_allocated()`, and variable-size allocations fragment that reserve over time.\n- **Mitigation.** The `expandable_segments` allocator option in recent PyTorch versions reduces\n  fragmentation.\n- **Engines sidestep most of it** by pre-allocating the KV pool in fixed blocks and profiling the\n  activation peak once: PagedAttention's design logic applied to the whole budget.\n\n### Putting the ledger together\n```\nusable          = VRAM × memory_fraction          (engine budget, e.g. 0.9)\npool_for_kv     = usable − weights − activation_peak − overhead\ntokens_capacity = pool_for_kv / bytes_per_token\nconcurrency     = tokens_capacity / expected_tokens_per_sequence\n```\n- **Under tensor parallelism** run it per GPU, with weights and KV divided by the TP degree but overhead\n  not.\n- **For agents, size by late-step transcripts.** Use the p99 transcript length at the step where runs\n  peak, not the first prompt, and subtract a shared prefix once when prefix caching is on.\n- **Then check latency.** A capacity of 60 concurrent sequences is useless if the step time at 60 breaks\n  the per-token SLO, and an agent run pays that step time many times over.\n\n### Reading the instruments\n| Instrument | Tells you |\n|---|---|\n| `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()` | live tensors |\n| `memory_reserved()` | allocator reserve |\n| `max_memory_allocated()` | the true peak |\n| `torch.cuda.memory_summary()` | fragmentation |\n| engine startup log | KV blocks allocated: the most useful number to compare with the ledger |\n| `nvidia-smi` | unexpected processes and the total, not attribution |","example":"### Worked plan: Llama-3-70B-class on two 80 GB GPUs\n80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, hidden 8,192, about 140 GB in bf16. TP = 2 and a 0.9 fraction give\n72 GB usable per GPU.\n\n| Step | Per-GPU ledger | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| bf16 weights | about 70.6 GB against 72 GB | essentially nothing left for cache (a few GB at best, depending on GB vs GiB): not useful |\n| int8 weights | about 35.3 GB | KV per token 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 320 KB in fp16, 160 KB per GPU after sharding |\n| Reserve 2 GB (illustrative) | 72 − 35.3 − 2 ≈ 34.7 GB pool | 34.7 × 2³⁰ ÷ 160 KB ≈ 227,000 tokens |\n| 8k tokens per conversation | | about 27 concurrent conversations |\n| fp8 KV cache | bytes per token halved | about 55 |\n| Context capped at 4k | tokens per conversation halved | about 111 |\n\nEach step is one term of the ledger. The calculator reproduces it for any model. It blurs GB and GiB, a 7%\ndifference smaller than the uncertainty in the reserve; when the margin is that thin, use the engine's\nlogged block count instead.\n\n```python\ndef vram_plan(vram_gb, params_b, w_bytes, layers, kv_heads, head_dim, kv_bytes,\n              tp=1, fraction=0.9, reserve_gb=2.0, tokens_per_seq=8192, vocab=128_256, hidden=8192):\n    usable = vram_gb * fraction\n    weights = params_b * w_bytes / tp                       # GB per GPU (1e9 params ~ 1 GB at 1 byte)\n    kv_per_token = 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * kv_bytes / tp\n    pool_gb = usable - weights - reserve_gb\n    tokens = int(pool_gb * 2**30 / kv_per_token) if pool_gb > 0 else 0\n    return dict(weights_gb=round(weights, 1), kv_kb_per_token=kv_per_token / 1024,\n                pool_gb=round(pool_gb, 1), tokens=tokens, concurrent=tokens // tokens_per_seq)\n\nbase = dict(vram_gb=80, params_b=70.6, layers=80, kv_heads=8, head_dim=128, tp=2)\nprint(\"bf16 weights, fp16 KV:\", vram_plan(w_bytes=2, kv_bytes=2, **base))\nprint(\"int8 weights, fp16 KV:\", vram_plan(w_bytes=1, kv_bytes=2, **base))\nprint(\"int8 weights, fp8 KV: \", vram_plan(w_bytes=1, kv_bytes=1, **base))\nprint(\"int8, fp8 KV, 4k ctx: \", vram_plan(w_bytes=1, kv_bytes=1, tokens_per_seq=4096, **base))\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: how many tool-calling runs fit\nThe same int8, fp16-KV pool (about 227,000 tokens per GPU pair) serving a research agent. Transcript\nsizes are illustrative: a 4k-token system prompt plus tool schemas, and runs that peak at 12k tokens by\ntheir last steps.\n\n| Policy | Tokens charged per run | Concurrent agent runs |\n|---|---|---|\n| No prefix sharing, size by peak transcript | 12,288 | about 18 |\n| Prefix caching: shared 4k prefix counted once | 8,192 unique | about 27 |\n| Plus compaction: old tool results summarised, unique part capped at 6k | 6,144 unique | about 36 |\n| Plus fp8 KV cache (after an eval) | half the bytes | about 73 |\n\n```python\ndef agent_runs(pool_tokens, shared_prefix, unique_per_run, prefix_cached=True):\n    \"\"\"Concurrent agent runs a KV pool holds, given a shared prefix and per-run transcript growth.\"\"\"\n    if not prefix_cached:\n        return pool_tokens // (shared_prefix + unique_per_run)\n    return (pool_tokens - shared_prefix) // unique_per_run    # prefix blocks stored once\n\npool = 227_000\nprint(agent_runs(pool, 4096, 8192, prefix_cached=False))   # ~18: every run pays for the prefix\nprint(agent_runs(pool, 4096, 8192))                        # ~27: shared prefix counted once\nprint(agent_runs(pool, 4096, 6144))                        # ~36: compaction caps the unique part\n```\n\nThe prefix only shares if it is byte-identical across runs, and compaction trades memory for a recall\nrisk you should eval.\n\n### Check the ledger against a live process\nMeasures weights, predicted KV and the prefill peak for a small model with PyTorch's own counters. The\ngap between peak and live memory is Line 3; the gap between reserved and allocated is Line 4's allocator\nreserve.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM\n\nGB, T = 2**30, 4096\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\", torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16).cuda().eval()\ncfg = model.config\nparams = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())\nprint(f\"Line 1 weights: predicted {params * 2 / GB:.2f} GiB, \"\n      f\"allocated {torch.cuda.memory_allocated() / GB:.2f} GiB\")\n\nhead_dim = cfg.hidden_size // cfg.num_attention_heads\nkv_pred = 2 * cfg.num_hidden_layers * cfg.num_key_value_heads * head_dim * 2 * T\nprint(f\"Line 2 KV for {T} tokens (bf16): predicted {kv_pred / GB:.3f} GiB\")\n\ntorch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\nids = torch.randint(0, cfg.vocab_size, (1, T), device=\"cuda\")\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    out = model(ids, use_cache=True)                   # one prefill; logits for EVERY position\nprint(f\"live after prefill {torch.cuda.memory_allocated() / GB:.2f} GiB, \"\n      f\"peak {torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / GB:.2f} GiB, \"\n      f\"reserved {torch.cuda.memory_reserved() / GB:.2f} GiB\")\nprint(f\"full logits tensor alone: {out.logits.numel() * out.logits.element_size() / GB:.2f} GiB\")\n```\n\nThe last line shows the LM-head trap at small scale: a large vocabulary times every prompt position, which\nserving engines avoid by computing logits only where they sample.","patterns":["**Derive engine limits from the agent ledger.** Before launch, compute the pool from p99 late-step transcript length and set vLLM's `--gpu-memory-utilization`, `--max-model-len` and `--max-num-seqs` from it. Compare the KV-cache size the engine logs at startup with your prediction; a large gap is a forgotten term.","**Load-test the worst agent step, not the average.** Replay full agent runs so the longest transcript (or a huge tool result) arrives during a full decode batch, exercising the activation peak profiling predicted. Monitor peak memory, OOMs and preemptions during the test.","**One engine per GPU, or explicit fractions.** If the RAG embedder or reranker must share a GPU, start it first and give the main engine a lower memory fraction, then re-profile. Alert on unexpected processes in `nvidia-smi`.","**Reduce fragmentation in custom PyTorch servers.** Set `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True` and watch the reserved-minus-allocated gap and `memory_summary()` over long runs.","**Pull levers in a fixed order.** Fewer KV heads or quantized weights first, then context and concurrency caps at the traffic p99 (plus transcript compaction for long agents), then an fp8 cache (`--kv-cache-dtype fp8`) after an eval, then tensor parallelism. Re-run the ledger and the load test after each change."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent transcripts grow every step (system prompt, tool schemas, each tool\n  result), so the KV-cache line, not the weights, decides how many concurrent agent runs fit. A server\n  sized for chat-length prompts OOMs or preempts when long research agents arrive.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** vLLM's `--gpu-memory-utilization`, `--max-model-len`,\n  `--max-num-seqs` and `--kv-cache-dtype fp8`, the KV-cache size the engine logs at startup, and an\n  embedder or reranker for your RAG step sharing the same GPU.\n- **What you control.** Model size and precision, the context cap per agent route, transcript compaction,\n  a byte-identical shared prefix, concurrency caps, and which co-tenants share the GPU.\n- **What you can skip.** Allocator internals beyond reserved versus allocated. With a hosted API the\n  provider does this math; you only need it once you self-host the agent model.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Sizing a self-hosted agent server.** The ledger turns a model, GPU and transcript profile into a\n  concurrent-run number before any hardware is bought.\n- **Choosing context and compaction policy.** Capping or summarising old tool results is a memory lever\n  you can price in concurrent runs.\n- **Co-locating the RAG stack.** Deciding whether the embedder and reranker can share the agent model's\n  GPU, and at what memory fraction.\n- **Debugging OOMs and preemptions.** Comparing the engine's logged KV size and peak memory with the\n  ledger finds the forgotten term.","mini_project":"**Size and verify an agent server on one GPU (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** predict how many concurrent agent runs a small self-hosted model supports, then confirm it.\n- **Steps:** pick a 0.5B to 8B model and your GPU; write the four-line ledger and the `agent_runs`\n  estimate for a transcript profile you choose; start vLLM with limits derived from it and compare the\n  logged KV size; replay N scripted tool-calling runs whose transcripts grow each step, raising N until\n  preemptions appear.\n- **Done when:** you have predicted versus measured KV capacity and concurrent runs in one table, the gap\n  explained by a named term, and engine limits you would ship."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"`nvidia-smi` shows how much memory the model needs.\"** It includes the allocator's reserve and the KV pool the engine pre-allocates, so a serving engine looks nearly full even at idle. Attribute memory with the PyTorch counters and the KV size the engine logs at startup.","**\"If the model loads, the deployment fits.\"** Fitting at idle says nothing about peak: a model that \"uses 18 GB\" at idle can need 60 GB under load. Tokens in flight and the largest prefill step are properties of traffic, so size from capped p99 traffic, not from a successful start.","**\"The weights decide how many agents a GPU serves.\"** Weights are fixed; the KV line scales with traffic. On the same int8 70B pool of about 227,000 tokens per GPU pair, 8k conversations give about 27 concurrent, and an fp8 cache plus a 4k cap gives about 111.","**\"An MoE model needs memory for its active parameters.\"** Every expert must be resident even though only a few run per token. A 47B-total, 13B-active model needs 94 GB of bf16 weights, not 26 GB.","**\"Activation memory adds up across layers and grows with the prompt.\"** It is one layer's transient, reused layer after layer, and it scales with the tokens in one step, T: about 0.94 GB for the 70B-class gate and up projections at T = 8,192. Chunked prefill caps T per step, so a huge tool result does not raise the peak; without it, a long prompt does."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    V[\"VRAM x memory fraction\"] --> W[\"Minus weights\"]\n    W --> A[\"Minus activation peak\"]\n    A --> O[\"Minus overhead\"]\n    O --> P[(\"KV pool\")]\n    P -->|\"divide by bytes per token\"| T[\"Tokens of capacity\"]\n    T -->|\"divide by transcript tokens per run\"| C[\"Concurrent agent runs\"]\n    C --> L{\"Step time meets SLO?\"}\n    L -->|\"no\"| R[\"Lower concurrency or add GPUs\"]\n    L -->|\"yes\"| D[\"Set engine limits\"]\n    Q[\"Quantize, fp8 KV, caps, TP\"] -.->|\"shrink weights\"| W\n    Q -.->|\"fewer bytes or tokens\"| T","caption":"The ledger as a pipeline from VRAM to concurrent agent runs: each subtraction is a lever, and the final\nconcurrency must still pass the latency check."},"interview":[{"q":"What are the four lines of the inference memory ledger, and which one grows with traffic?","a":"- **Weights, KV cache, activation and workspace peaks, runtime overhead plus fragmentation.**\n- **Only the KV cache grows with traffic,** because it is bytes per token times tokens in flight; weights\n  and overhead are fixed, and activations are bounded by the largest step you allow."},{"q":"Your self-hosted agent server is fine at launch but preempts heavily mid-afternoon. How do you size it properly?","a":"- **Agents grow:** tokens in flight are the sum of current transcripts, so many runs reaching late steps\n  together multiply the KV line even at constant run count.\n- **Size by late steps:** use p99 transcript length at the peak step, count a byte-identical shared prefix\n  once with prefix caching, and cap or compact transcripts.\n- **Then verify:** replay real agent runs under load and compare the engine's KV usage and preemption\n  metrics with the ledger."},{"q":"A deployment passed load tests but OOMs in production. How do you find the missing term?","a":"- **Compare the ledger with reality:** the engine's logged KV size, `max_memory_allocated()` and\n  `nvidia-smi` for co-tenant processes.\n- **Check the worst step:** did production send longer prompts than the test, during a full decode batch,\n  with chunked prefill off or a high token budget?\n- **Check additions:** a draft model, LoRA slots or a second process (such as the RAG embedder) that the\n  startup profile never saw."},{"q":"When would you raise the memory fraction, and when would you not?","a":"- **Raise it** when the GPU is dedicated, the activation peak is profiled at your real limits, and KV usage\n  is the bottleneck.\n- **Do not** past about 0.9–0.95, or when other processes, CUDA graphs or unprofiled peaks need headroom;\n  the OOM risk then rises faster than capacity."},{"q":"How much memory does a 47B-total, 13B-active MoE model need for weights in bf16?","a":"- **About 94 GB,** because every expert must be resident even though only a few run per token.\n- **Active parameters predict compute per token,** not the weight footprint; budgeting from 13B (26 GB)\n  means the checkpoint does not load."},{"q":"Estimate concurrency for an int8 70B-class model at TP = 2 on 80 GB GPUs with 8k conversations.","a":"- **Per GPU:** 72 GB usable − 35.3 GB weights − ~2 GB reserve ≈ 34.7 GB pool.\n- **KV:** 320 KB per token in fp16, 160 KB per GPU, so about 227,000 tokens, about 27 conversations.\n- **Levers:** fp8 KV doubles it to about 55; a 4k context cap doubles it again to about 111."}],"tradeoffs":["**Quantized weights vs quality risk.** Quantizing frees the largest line and speeds decode, but every quantized artifact needs its own quality evaluation on agent tasks; the saving is not worth a silent regression in tool-call accuracy.","**fp8 KV cache vs long-context accuracy.** It doubles token capacity at low reported quality cost, yet long-context retrieval is where cache precision bites, so the trade flips as agent transcripts grow.","**Higher memory fraction vs headroom.** More blocks, but less headroom for activation peaks and other processes; past about 0.9–0.95 the OOM risk rises faster than capacity.","**Lower context cap vs legitimate long requests.** Concurrency rises proportionally, but long agent runs are rejected or truncated; the right cap is the measured p99 of real traffic, not the model's maximum.","**Tensor parallelism vs per-GPU overhead.** It divides weights and cache across GPUs but adds per-GPU overhead and interconnect cost, so two GPUs give less than twice the capacity of one."],"pitfalls":["**Sizing from weights alone.** Symptom: a server that starts cleanly, reports healthy, and OOMs or preempts heavily the first time concurrency and context length rise together. Fix: write all four lines and size KV from capped tokens in flight.","**Sizing agents by their first prompt.** Symptom: fine in the morning, preemption storms once many runs reach late steps at once. Fix: size by p99 transcript length at the peak step, and cap or compact.","**Reading `nvidia-smi` as \"memory the model needs\".** It includes the allocator's reserve and the pre-allocated KV pool, so a serving engine reports near-full even at idle. Fix: use the PyTorch counters and the engine's logged KV size.","**Adding a second process without re-profiling** (an embedding model, a reranker). The engine's pool was sized to the free memory at its own startup, so the newcomer OOMs or evicts it. Fix: lower the main engine's memory fraction and restart it.","**Testing only short prompts and small batches.** The activation peak is never exercised; the first 32k-token prompt during a full decode batch triggers the OOM profiling would have predicted. Fix: load-test the worst step.","**Budgeting an MoE model by its active parameters.** The checkpoint then does not load. Fix: budget weights from total parameters."],"when":"- **Do the ledger** before every deployment, model swap, change to context or concurrency limits, new\n  agent route with longer transcripts, and whenever a new co-tenant lands on a GPU.\n- **Pick levers in a fixed order:** a model with fewer KV heads or a quantized weight format first, then\n  context and concurrency caps matched to traffic p99, then fp8 cache, then tensor parallelism, with CPU\n  offloading as a last resort for non-interactive work.\n- **Measure** the engine's reported block count and peak allocated memory under a production-shaped load\n  test and compare with the ledger; a gap means a term you forgot.\n- **Skip it** while your agents call a hosted API; the provider's rate limits are your capacity ceiling.\n- **Interviews:** derive bytes-per-token and walk the four lines for a named model on a named GPU; it is\n  the standard test of serving depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes the ledger in a notebook, sets `gpu_memory_utilization`, `max_model_len` and sequence limits from it, and verifies against the engine's logged block count and peak memory.","architect":"Chooses GPU tier, precision policy and tensor-parallel degree per model from the ledger, and sets the context and concurrency policy that capacity planning depends on.","fde":"Sizes a customer's self-hosted deployment on the hardware they have, showing the four lines and which lever changes their concurrent-user number before any purchase decision."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to serve a Llama-3-8B-class model (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, ~16 GB\nin bf16) on a single 24 GB GPU with 16k-token documents and asks how many users it supports. Walk the\nledger.","a":"Usable memory at a 0.9 fraction is about 21.6 GB. Weights take 16 GB, and an illustrative 1.5 GB\ngoes to activation peak and runtime overhead, leaving roughly 4 GB for KV cache. KV per token is 2 × 32 ×\n8 × 128 × 2 = 128 KB in fp16, so the pool holds around 32k tokens: two 16k documents in flight, not a\nmulti-user service. The levers in order: quantize weights to int8 (8 GB freed, about 96k tokens, six\ndocuments) or 4-bit (another 4 GB, roughly 130k tokens), use an fp8 KV cache to double any of those,\nenable chunked prefill so a 16k prefill does not blow the activation peak, and confirm the step time at\nthe resulting concurrency still meets their latency target. If they need dozens of concurrent documents,\nthe answer is a larger GPU, not more tuning."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which term of the memory ledger grows with traffic rather than with the model?","options":["Weights, because each request loads its own copy of the parameters","The KV cache, because it scales with tokens in flight across all active sequences","Runtime overhead, because the CUDA context grows with each connection","The embedding table, because new tokens are added as prompts arrive"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Weights and overhead are fixed once the model is loaded. The cache is proportional to the total number of prompt and generated tokens currently being served, which is a property of traffic you must cap."},{"q":"Why does a serving engine run a dummy forward pass at startup before allocating its KV pool?","options":["To warm up the CUDA kernels so the first request is fast","To verify the model weights loaded correctly","To measure the peak activation memory at the configured batch and token limits, so the pool can take the rest","To compute the perplexity of the model on a calibration set"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Activation and workspace memory depends on the maximum step the scheduler may build. Measuring it once lets the engine give every remaining byte to KV blocks without risking OOM on the largest step."},{"q":"A 47B-total, 13B-active mixture-of-experts model is planned for a GPU budget sized from 13B parameters. What happens?","options":["It fits, because only active experts are loaded per token","It fits if the KV cache is quantized to fp8","It fails to load, because all experts must be resident and weights cost 47B × bytes per parameter","It loads but runs at one-quarter speed due to expert swapping"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Routing picks experts per token, but every expert can be chosen, so the full set must sit in GPU memory. Active parameters predict compute per token, not the weight footprint."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/streaming","sources":["Official docs: WHATWG HTML Living Standard, server-sent events","Official docs: OpenAI API streaming","Official docs: Anthropic Messages API streaming"],"why":"- **Total time is long; the first token is early.** Decoding produces one token at a time, so total\n  time is roughly `TTFT + (n_tokens − 1) × TPOT`. A 500-token answer at 20 ms per token takes 10 s to\n  finish, but the first token exists after a few hundred milliseconds.\n- **Users judge the first visible change.** For a chat answer the second term dominates: several\n  seconds in which the server already has most of the answer and the user sees nothing. A\n  non-streaming UI feels broken at exactly the output lengths that make LLMs useful.\n- **The naive fixes each fail somewhere.**\n  - *Polling a job endpoint* adds a round trip per poll, gives latency granularity equal to the poll\n    interval and multiplies request volume.\n  - *A raw chunked `text/plain` body* gets tokens out early but has no framing: the client cannot tell\n    a token from a mid-stream error, a tool-call argument fragment or the final usage record.\n  - *WebSockets* give full duplex and framing, but need a protocol upgrade, long-lived connection\n    state and their own reconnect logic, and are the thing most likely to be blocked by a corporate\n    proxy, all for a channel where only one side talks.\n- **SSE sits in the gap.** An ordinary HTTP response with `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, trivial\n  line-based framing with named events, and native browser support. That is why OpenAI- and\n  Anthropic-style APIs stream over it, and why every hop between engine and screen must carry it\n  without buffering.","how":"### The wire format\n- **Events are separated by a blank line.** The body is UTF-8 text; each line is `field: value`.\n- **The fields that matter:**\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `data:` | The payload; several `data:` lines in one event are joined with newlines |\n| `event:` | Optional name the client can dispatch on |\n| `id:` | Lets a reconnecting client send `Last-Event-ID` |\n| `retry:` | Reconnect delay hint |\n| line starting with `:` | A comment the client ignores, which makes `: keep-alive` a free heartbeat |\n\n- **The response stays open.** Under HTTP/1.1 it is sent with `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`; under\n  HTTP/2 as an open stream.\n- **Framing is the blank line, not network packets.** One TCP read can hold half an event or three of\n  them, and can even split a multi-byte UTF-8 character. So a client must decode bytes incrementally,\n  buffer until it sees the blank line, and only then parse the event.\n\nA token stream is just:\n\n```\nevent: delta\ndata: {\"text\": \"The\"}\n\nevent: delta\ndata: {\"text\": \" KV\"}\n\nevent: done\ndata: {\"usage\": {\"output_tokens\": 2}}\n\n```\n\n### What flows through it\nProviders map generation onto events differently, but the shape is the same.\n\n| | OpenAI-style chat completions | Anthropic Messages API |\n|---|---|---|\n| Framing | A sequence of `data:` JSON chunks, each carrying a `delta` | Named events |\n| Start | First chunk typically has only the role and empty content | `message_start` (with input-token usage) |\n| Content | Content fragments in `delta` | `content_block_start`, repeated `content_block_delta` (`text_delta`, or `input_json_delta` carrying partial JSON for tool use), `content_block_stop` |\n| End | A literal `data: [DONE]` sentinel | `message_delta` (stop reason, output-token usage), then `message_stop` |\n| Usage | Only in a final chunk if you ask: `stream_options: {\"include_usage\": true}` | In `message_start` and `message_delta` |\n| Keep-alive | | Periodic `ping` events |\n\nTwo consequences follow:\n- **Tool-call arguments arrive as JSON fragments** that are only parseable at the end of the block.\n- **The authoritative token count is at the tail**, so a client that disconnects early never sees it.\n\n### The path from GPU to screen\n1. **Engine emits.** vLLM, SGLang, TGI or a provider's fleet emits one token per decode step per\n   sequence.\n2. **Serving layer serialises and flushes.** It writes the token as an event and *flushes* it; a\n   framework that buffers generator output defeats the whole point.\n3. **Every intermediary forwards.** Your reverse proxy, an API gateway, a load balancer, maybe a CDN,\n   then the client's HTTP library. HTTP allows each to buffer a response body, and several do by\n   default (nginx `proxy_buffering`, compression layers that wait for a full block).\n4. **Fix it per hop:**\n   - send `X-Accel-Buffering: no` and `Cache-Control: no-cache` headers;\n   - disable compression for `text/event-stream`;\n   - send heartbeats, so idle-connection timers on load balancers (commonly around a minute) do not\n     close a stream during a long prefill or a reasoning pause.\n\n### Semantics that change once you stream\n- **`200` means \"the stream opened\", not \"the answer succeeded\".** The status code is sent before\n  generation starts.\n- **Failures travel in-band.** A model error, content-filter stop or upstream timeout must arrive as an\n  `error` event or a `finish_reason`, and clients must treat the output they have as partial.\n- **Streams are not resumable.** `EventSource` auto-reconnects and sends `Last-Event-ID`, but an LLM\n  server cannot replay generation from an id, so a dropped stream is a cancel-and-restart.\n- **`EventSource` only issues `GET` without custom headers.** Real clients consume the provider's\n  `POST` stream with `fetch` and a `ReadableStream` reader.\n- **Connection limits bite.** On HTTP/1.1 most browsers allow six connections per origin, which runs\n  out if every tab holds a stream open; HTTP/2 multiplexes streams over one connection.\n\n### Streaming an agent run, not just one answer\nAn agent server sits between two streams: it *consumes* the provider's stream for each model call and\n*produces* one stream of its own for the whole run.\n1. **Model call streams in.** Text deltas can be forwarded at once; tool-call argument fragments are\n   accumulated, because they only parse at the end of the block.\n2. **Tool step runs.** No model tokens flow while a tool executes, so the server emits its own\n   `tool_start` and `tool_end` events and heartbeats; a slow search can otherwise trip an idle timer.\n3. **Next model call streams in** with the tool result appended, and the loop repeats.\n4. **The run ends** with one terminal `done` or `error` event for the whole run, not per model call.\n\n| Event you emit | Why the UI or caller needs it |\n|---|---|\n| `delta` | Visible text as it is decoded |\n| `tool_start` / `tool_end` | Progress during the silent tool step, and an audit trail |\n| `step` | Step counter, so a runaway loop is visible |\n| `done` / `error` | The only signal that the run finished, since the status is already `200` |","example":"### SSE endpoint with heartbeats, in-band errors and disconnect handling\nA minimal SSE endpoint that forwards an engine's tokens with correct framing, a connect comment that\nforces intermediaries to commit to the response, heartbeats on idle, in-band errors and a stop on\nclient disconnect. The token source is a stand-in for an engine or provider stream.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef sse(event: str, data: dict) -> str:\n    return f\"event: {event}\\ndata: {json.dumps(data)}\\n\\n\"      # blank line terminates the event\n\nasync def token_source(prompt: str):                             # stand-in for an engine stream\n    for i, tok in enumerate((\"Stream\", \"ing\", \" works\", \".\")):\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.3); yield i, tok\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/answer\")\nasync def answer(req: Request):\n    body = await req.json()\n    q: asyncio.Queue = asyncio.Queue()\n\n    async def pump():                                            # engine -> queue, errors in-band\n        n = 0\n        try:\n            async for i, tok in token_source(body[\"prompt\"]):\n                await q.put((\"delta\", {\"i\": i, \"text\": tok})); n += 1\n            await q.put((\"done\", {\"output_tokens\": n}))\n        except Exception as e:\n            await q.put((\"error\", {\"message\": str(e)}))          # status is already 200\n\n    async def gen():\n        task = asyncio.create_task(pump())\n        yield \": connected\\n\\n\"                                  # comment: flushes proxies, no client event\n        try:\n            while True:\n                try:\n                    ev, data = await asyncio.wait_for(q.get(), timeout=15)\n                except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n                    yield \": keep-alive\\n\\n\"; continue           # heartbeat beats idle timeouts\n                if await req.is_disconnected(): break            # client left: stop forwarding\n                yield sse(ev, data)\n                if ev in (\"done\", \"error\"): break\n        finally:\n            task.cancel()                                        # propagate the stop upstream\n\n    return StreamingResponse(gen(), media_type=\"text/event-stream\",\n        headers={\"Cache-Control\": \"no-cache\", \"X-Accel-Buffering\": \"no\"})\n```\n\n### A client parser that survives split reads\nRunnable without a server: the wire bytes are cut into 11-byte reads, which splits events and the\ntwo-byte `é` across reads. Decoding incrementally and buffering to the blank line still yields clean\nevents. (This sketch handles LF line endings only; a full parser also accepts CRLF and CR.)\n\n```python\nimport codecs, json\n\ndef parse_sse(chunks):\n    dec = codecs.getincrementaldecoder(\"utf-8\")()   # keeps half a character between reads\n    buf = \"\"\n    for chunk in chunks:\n        buf += dec.decode(chunk)\n        while \"\\n\\n\" in buf:                        # an event ends at a blank line, not a read\n            raw, buf = buf.split(\"\\n\\n\", 1)\n            event, data = \"message\", []\n            for line in raw.split(\"\\n\"):\n                if not line or line.startswith(\":\"):\n                    continue                        # comments and heartbeats\n                field, _, value = line.partition(\":\")\n                value = value[1:] if value.startswith(\" \") else value\n                if field == \"event\":\n                    event = value\n                elif field == \"data\":\n                    data.append(value)\n            if data:\n                yield event, \"\\n\".join(data)\n\nwire = ('event: delta\\ndata: {\"text\": \"café\"}\\n\\n: keep-alive\\n\\n'\n        'event: done\\ndata: {\"usage\": {\"output_tokens\": 2}}\\n\\n').encode()\nreads = [wire[i:i + 11] for i in range(0, len(wire), 11)]   # one read ends mid-é\nfor ev, data in parse_sse(reads):\n    print(ev, json.loads(data))\n# delta {'text': 'café'}\n# done {'usage': {'output_tokens': 2}}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what streaming buys, and the buffering signature\nSame 500-token answer at 20 ms per token, TTFT ~300 ms (illustrative).\n\n| Setup | First visible text | Answer complete | What the user sees |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Non-streaming JSON | ~10.3 s | ~10.3 s | Spinner for 10 s |\n| Streaming, every hop unbuffered | ~0.3 s | ~10.3 s | Text from the first second |\n| Streaming, one proxy buffering | ~10.3 s | ~10.3 s | Spinner, then everything at once |\n\nThe third row is why \"all events arrived within the last 100 ms of the response\" is the alert to set:\ngeneration ran at normal speed, but an intermediary held the bytes.\n\n### Agent scenario: assembling a streamed tool call\nThe agent forwards text at once but executes a tool only after its argument JSON is complete. The\nevents are a hand-written stand-in shaped like the Anthropic Messages API stream.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nevents = [  # stand-in for a provider stream; shapes follow the Messages API event types\n    {\"type\": \"content_block_start\", \"index\": 0, \"content_block\": {\"type\": \"text\"}},\n    {\"type\": \"content_block_delta\", \"index\": 0, \"delta\": {\"type\": \"text_delta\", \"text\": \"Checking \"}},\n    {\"type\": \"content_block_stop\", \"index\": 0},\n    {\"type\": \"content_block_start\", \"index\": 1,\n     \"content_block\": {\"type\": \"tool_use\", \"id\": \"tu_1\", \"name\": \"search_orders\"}},\n    {\"type\": \"content_block_delta\", \"index\": 1, \"delta\": {\"type\": \"input_json_delta\", \"partial_json\": '{\"order_'}},\n    {\"type\": \"content_block_delta\", \"index\": 1, \"delta\": {\"type\": \"input_json_delta\", \"partial_json\": 'id\": \"A-17\"}'}},\n    {\"type\": \"content_block_stop\", \"index\": 1},\n    {\"type\": \"message_delta\", \"delta\": {\"stop_reason\": \"tool_use\"}, \"usage\": {\"output_tokens\": 31}},\n]\n\ndef run_step(stream, emit):\n    blocks, calls = {}, []\n    for ev in stream:\n        if ev[\"type\"] == \"content_block_start\":\n            blocks[ev[\"index\"]] = {**ev[\"content_block\"], \"buf\": \"\"}\n        elif ev[\"type\"] == \"content_block_delta\":\n            d = ev[\"delta\"]\n            if d[\"type\"] == \"text_delta\":\n                emit(\"delta\", {\"text\": d[\"text\"]})               # forward text immediately\n            else:\n                blocks[ev[\"index\"]][\"buf\"] += d[\"partial_json\"]   # never parse a fragment\n        elif ev[\"type\"] == \"content_block_stop\":\n            b = blocks[ev[\"index\"]]\n            if b[\"type\"] == \"tool_use\":\n                calls.append((b[\"name\"], json.loads(b[\"buf\"])))   # complete JSON only here\n                emit(\"tool_start\", {\"name\": b[\"name\"]})\n        elif ev[\"type\"] == \"message_delta\":\n            return ev[\"delta\"][\"stop_reason\"], calls, ev[\"usage\"]\n\nprint(run_step(events, lambda e, d: print(\"emit\", e, d)))\n# emit delta {'text': 'Checking '}\n# emit tool_start {'name': 'search_orders'}\n# ('tool_use', [('search_orders', {'order_id': 'A-17'})], {'output_tokens': 31})\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a three-step agent with and without streaming\nIllustrative numbers: two tool calls, then a 400-token answer at 20 ms per token.\n\n| Phase | Time | Non-streaming UI | Streaming UI |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model call 1 (plans, emits tool call) | 0-1.5 s | spinner | \"Looking up order…\" at ~0.4 s |\n| Tool 1 (order API) | 1.5-3.5 s | spinner | `tool_start`, heartbeats |\n| Model call 2 plus tool 2 | 3.5-7 s | spinner | second `tool_start` |\n| Final answer | 7-15 s | spinner | text from ~7.3 s |\n| What the user sees first | | everything at ~15 s | progress at ~0.4 s, can cancel at any point |","patterns":["**Stream agent events, not only tokens.** Agent teams emit `tool_start`, `tool_end` and step events from the agent server alongside model deltas, so users see progress during silent tool steps. Monitor the longest gap between events per run; it usually points at one slow tool.","**Execute tools only on a complete block.** Accumulate `input_json_delta` fragments (or OpenAI-style tool-call argument deltas) and parse at block end, then validate against the tool schema before running anything. Monitor tool-argument parse failures per tool.","**Disable buffering per hop, explicitly.** When SSE crosses nginx, set `proxy_buffering off;` in the streaming location (or send `X-Accel-Buffering: no` from the app) and exclude `text/event-stream` from gzip. Monitor the gap between first and last event arrival against generation time.","**Heartbeat below the shortest idle timer.** Emit `: keep-alive` comments on a timer shorter than any load balancer idle timeout on the path (the example uses 15 s). Monitor stream drops clustered at a fixed duration, such as exactly 60 s.","**Always end with a terminal event.** Every stream ends with `done` or `error` (or the provider's `finish_reason`); the client marks the answer incomplete if the stream closes without one. Monitor stream completion rate and the share of non-normal finish reasons.","**Ask for usage in-stream and reconcile.** Pass `stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}` on OpenAI-style calls and record usage from `message_delta` on Anthropic. Reconcile against the provider's usage reports, because disconnected streams never deliver the tail.","**Propagate disconnects upstream.** On client disconnect, cancel the producer task and close the provider or engine stream. Monitor tokens generated after disconnect and spend versus active streams.","**Coalesce at the edge on busy servers.** Batch tokens into one event every few tens of milliseconds when frame or render overhead shows up. Monitor events per second and inter-event gap distribution."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent run is several model calls plus tool calls. Without streaming\n  the user stares at a spinner for the whole loop; with it they see \"searching…\", partial answers and\n  can cancel a run heading the wrong way.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `stream=True` on OpenAI-style SDKs, the Anthropic Messages API\n  event stream (`input_json_delta` for tool arguments), your FastAPI `StreamingResponse`, and every proxy\n  between your agent server and the browser.\n- **What you control.** Which events you forward (tokens, tool start and end, step boundaries), when you\n  parse streamed tool arguments (only at block end), heartbeats during slow tools, and what a run looks\n  like when it ends without a terminal event.\n- **What you can skip.** The full WHATWG parsing algorithm and HTTP/2 framing details; your SDK parses\n  provider streams. Know the event shape, the buffering signature and the error semantics.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Chat-style assistants with tools.** Streaming the answer and \"calling search…\" events keeps users\n  engaged through multi-step runs and lets them stop a run early.\n- **Coding and research agents.** Long runs forward step boundaries and tool results as events, so the\n  UI shows a live trace instead of a spinner.\n- **Agent backends behind proxies and gateways.** Teams audit every hop for buffering and add heartbeats\n  so slow tools do not trip idle timeouts mid-stream.\n- **Streamed structured output.** Tool arguments and JSON answers arrive as deltas; the agent buffers\n  them and parses only when the block ends.","mini_project":"**Stream an agent run end to end (1-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** serve a small tool-calling agent over SSE that forwards tokens, tool start and end events, and\n  a terminal `done` or `error` event.\n- **Steps:** build a FastAPI endpoint around a two-tool agent loop using the provider's streaming API;\n  buffer tool-argument deltas until each block ends; emit a heartbeat comment during a deliberately slow\n  tool; write a small client that renders events and flags a stream that ends without a terminal event.\n- **Done when:** the client shows the first token before the run finishes, tool events appear in order,\n  and killing the server mid-run is reported as an incomplete run rather than a silent success."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Streaming makes the answer faster.\"** It changes when the user first sees text (about TTFT), not when generation finishes; total time is still `TTFT + (n_tokens − 1) × TPOT`, or slightly more.","**\"A `200` means the call succeeded.\"** The status is sent before the first token. Model errors, filter stops and upstream timeouts arrive later as in-band events, so only a terminal `done` event marks success.","**\"One network read is one event.\"** Framing is the blank line. A read can hold half an event, three events or half a UTF-8 character, so the client buffers bytes until the blank line before parsing.","**\"I can act on tool-call arguments as they stream.\"** `input_json_delta` fragments are not valid JSON until the block ends; execute the tool only after `content_block_stop` and schema validation.","**\"If it streams on localhost, it streams in production.\"** Every proxy, gateway and compression layer may buffer the body; the symptom is all events arriving in the last moment, so check each hop.","**\"Closing the browser tab stops the model.\"** Nothing upstream knows unless the server notices the disconnect and cancels the producer and the provider stream; otherwise you keep paying for tokens."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant C as Client\n    participant P as Proxy or gateway\n    participant S as Agent server\n    participant E as Model API or engine\n    C->>P: POST /v1/agent\n    P->>S: forward request\n    S-->>P: 200 text/event-stream plus connected comment\n    P-->>C: headers and comment, passed straight on\n    E->>S: tokens and tool-call JSON fragments\n    S-->>C: event delta via proxy, unbuffered\n    S-->>C: event tool_start, then keep-alive while tool runs\n    S->>E: next call with tool result\n    E->>S: final answer tokens\n    S-->>C: event done or event error, then close\n    Note over C: no terminal event means partial run","caption":"The 200 is committed before the first token. After that, model deltas, agent tool events, heartbeats\nand the terminal event all travel inside one open response, through every hop."},"interview":[{"q":"Your stream returned 200, sent 300 tokens, then the model failed. How should the system behave?","a":"- **Server:** the status is already committed, so send an in-band `error` event (or a non-normal\n  finish reason) and close the stream.\n- **Client:** keep the partial text, mark it incomplete, and never append a retry's output to it,\n  because that duplicates content; retry from scratch if at all.\n- **Logging:** record it as a failure, not a success, so truncated answers show up in error rates."},{"q":"Streams die after exactly 60 seconds during long reasoning, with no error. Debug it.","a":"- **The tell is the fixed duration.** It points at an idle timer on an intermediary (proxy or load\n  balancer), not at the model.\n- **Confirm** by checking whether any bytes were sent in the minute before the cut; during hidden\n  reasoning there often are none.\n- **Fix** with heartbeat comments on a timer shorter than the idle timeout, and raise the idle setting\n  on hops you own."},{"q":"When would you not stream a response?","a":"- **Short machine calls** such as classification, extraction or embeddings, where the whole answer\n  arrives in well under a second anyway.\n- **Batch pipelines** that validate the complete output before using it, so early tokens buy nothing.\n- **Caveat:** long machine calls can still benefit, because streaming gives early cancellation and an\n  idle-timeout liveness signal."},{"q":"How do you get accurate token usage for streamed calls, including abandoned ones?","a":"- **Ask for it:** OpenAI-style APIs send usage only in a final chunk when you set the include-usage\n  stream option; Anthropic sends it in `message_start` and `message_delta`.\n- **Disconnects lose the tail**, so record received text and treat a tokenized estimate as a lower\n  bound.\n- **Reconcile** against the provider's usage reports, not streamed chunk counts, because servers\n  coalesce or split tokens per event."},{"q":"You are building a chat agent that calls tools. What do you stream to the user, and when do you run the tool?","a":"- **Stream text deltas at once** and emit your own `tool_start` and `tool_end` events around each tool,\n  with heartbeats while it runs, so the run never looks frozen.\n- **Run the tool only after the block closes.** Tool-call arguments arrive as JSON fragments; parse and\n  schema-validate them at block end, never mid-stream.\n- **End the run with one terminal event** so the client can tell a finished run from a dropped one."},{"q":"An agent's SSE stream drops halfway through a multi-step run. What should the system do?","a":"- **Decide cancel or detach up front.** The model's stream cannot be replayed, so by default stop the\n  loop and close the upstream model stream to stop spend. For long background-style runs, some teams\n  instead keep the run going and persist its events, so a client reconnecting with `Last-Event-ID`\n  replays from that log.\n- **Check side effects.** Tools already executed (an email sent, a ticket filed) are not undone by the\n  drop, so log them per run and make retries idempotent.\n- **Restart from a checkpoint** if the agent persists step state; otherwise rerun from scratch."}],"tradeoffs":["**Perceived latency vs total time.** Perceived latency drops to TTFT, but completion time does not improve and may rise slightly from per-token serialisation and small TCP writes. The gain disappears when the consumer is a machine that needs the whole JSON, although early cancellation and idle-timeout liveness still argue for streaming there.","**Simplicity vs two-way talk.** SSE is simple and proxy-friendly because it is one-directional; the moment the client must talk mid-stream (interrupt, barge-in voice, collaborative edits) you need WebSockets or a second request channel.","**Coalescing vs smoothness.** Several tokens per event every few tens of milliseconds cuts frame and render overhead on busy servers, at the cost of visibly choppier output at low concurrency.","**Agent transparency vs noise.** Streaming every tool event makes an agent's work visible and cancellable, but chatty step events clutter the UI and leak internals; forward a curated subset to users and the full stream to traces.","**Latency vs validation.** Structured output cannot be validated until the final event, so a streaming JSON response either needs an incremental parser or loses the latency benefit for machine consumers.","**Visibility vs lost accounting.** Streaming exposes TTFT and inter-token gaps to measurement, but pushes usage and cost data to the end of the stream where disconnects can lose it."],"pitfalls":["**Parsing tool arguments mid-stream.** Symptom: intermittent `JSONDecodeError` or a tool called with truncated arguments. Fix: accumulate fragments and parse only at the end of the tool-use block.","**A proxy or middleware buffers the body.** Symptom: a stream that works on localhost and arrives all at once in production after the full generation time. Fix: walk the hops with `curl -N` and turn buffering and compression off on each.","**Browser `EventSource` against an authenticated `POST` endpoint.** Symptom: 401 or 405 errors, because `EventSource` can only `GET` without custom headers. Fix: `fetch` with a stream reader.","**No heartbeats during long prefill or thinking pauses.** Symptom: streams dying at a fixed interval (often exactly 60 s) with no error from either end. Fix: comment heartbeats below the idle timer.","**Treating the `200` as success.** Ignoring in-band `error` events or stop reasons shows up as truncated answers logged as successes and never retried. Fix: require a terminal event.","**Rendering each delta straight into Markdown.** Symptom: flickering, half-open code fences and broken links until the closing token arrives. Fix: re-render the accumulated text, or hold back an unclosed fence or link.","**Counting SSE chunks as tokens.** Servers may coalesce or split, so usage numbers disagree with the provider's bill. Fix: use the reported usage fields.","**Treating each network read as one event.** Symptom: intermittent JSON parse errors or garbled non-ASCII characters only on slow or distant networks, where reads split events differently than on localhost. Fix: an incremental decoder and a buffer up to the blank line, as in the parser above.","**Assuming a disconnect stops generation.** It does not unless you propagate the stop. Symptom: GPU time and provider spend that stay flat when traffic drops. Fix: cancel the producer and close the upstream stream on disconnect."],"when":"- **Stream by default** for anything a human reads that is longer than a sentence, and for long-running\n  machine calls where early cancellation and liveness matter (agent steps, tool-argument generation).\n- **Stream the whole agent run** to interactive users: one SSE response carrying deltas, tool events and\n  one terminal event, so a multi-step run never looks frozen.\n- **Do not bother** for short classification, extraction or embedding calls, or for batch pipelines\n  that validate the complete output anyway.\n- **Measure** TTFT, the distribution of inter-event gaps, stream completion rate and disconnect rate.\n  Alert on \"all events arrived within the last 100 ms of the response\", the buffering signature.\n- **Interviews:** write the SSE framing from memory, explain why not WebSockets, name the buffering\n  layers, and describe how error semantics change once the status code has been sent.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the generator and client parser, sets the anti-buffering headers and heartbeats, and handles partial JSON for streamed tool calls.","architect":"Decides where streaming terminates (gateway vs service), which hops are allowed to buffer, and whether interactive and batch traffic share the same path.","fde":"Debugs \"it streams in dev but not behind the customer's proxy\", usually a buffering or idle-timeout setting on infrastructure the customer owns."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports that your streaming endpoint \"works on localhost but does not stream in\nproduction, the whole answer just appears after ten seconds\". How do you diagnose it, in what order?","a":"First confirm the server really flushes per token by hitting the service directly with\n`curl -N` from inside the environment; if tokens trickle there, the server is fine. Then walk the\nhops outward: the reverse proxy (nginx buffers upstream responses by default; set\n`X-Accel-Buffering: no` or disable `proxy_buffering`), any compression middleware or gateway that\nwaits for a full block before emitting, a CDN or WAF that caches or inspects bodies, and the load\nbalancer's idle timeout. Finally check the client: awaiting the whole response body instead of\nreading the stream reproduces the exact symptom. Verify by sending a `: connected` comment first; if\neven that arrives late, the problem is an intermediary, not the model."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is SSE preferred over WebSockets for streaming LLM tokens to a browser?","options":["SSE delivers each message with lower latency than a WebSocket frame","Token output is one-directional, and SSE gives framing over ordinary HTTP that proxies, gateways and provider APIs already handle","WebSockets cannot carry JSON payloads reliably","SSE supports binary token ids while WebSockets are text only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SSE is not faster per message; it wins because it is plain HTTP for a one-way channel, so it needs no protocol upgrade or connection state and survives intermediaries. WebSockets are the right tool only when the client must send data mid-stream."},{"q":"A streamed response returned HTTP 200, emitted 300 tokens, then the model errored. What must the client do?","options":["Nothing special; the 200 status already indicates the request succeeded","Automatically retry and append the new output to the 300 tokens it has","Handle the in-band error event or finish reason and treat the 300 tokens as a partial, failed result","Wait for the `[DONE]` sentinel, which is always sent after errors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once streaming starts the status code is fixed, so failures can only be signalled inside the stream. Appending a retry's output would duplicate content; the partial answer must be marked incomplete and the retry started from scratch."},{"q":"Every token of a stream arrives at once, exactly when generation finishes. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Time-to-first-token is unusually high because of a long prompt","An intermediary or middleware is buffering the response body until it completes","The model does not support streaming so the server falls back to a single response","The client negotiated HTTP/2, which does not allow chunked transfer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Buffering is the classic signature: generation ran at normal speed but a proxy, compression layer or the client's own body reader held the bytes. High TTFT would delay the first token, not bunch all of them, and HTTP/2 streams SSE fine without chunked encoding."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/ttft","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Agrawal et al., 2024 — Taming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve","Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized Large Language Model Serving"],"why":"- **End-to-end latency mixes two phases with different physics.** Prefill processes every prompt token\n  in one batched forward pass, dominated by large matrix multiplications and scaling with prompt\n  length. Decode produces tokens one at a time, bound by memory bandwidth and scaling with output\n  length. A regression in \"request latency\" could be either, and you cannot tell which lever to pull.\n- **TTFT isolates the first phase, which users judge.** Once a stream starts, users have something to\n  read and tolerate the rest; before it starts they see nothing and assume the product is broken. A\n  request can have an acceptable total time and an unacceptable TTFT if the first second goes to\n  queueing or a 20k-token retrieved context.\n- **\"Just watch mean latency\" fails twice.**\n  - Means hide the queueing tail, which is where TTFT breaks first under load.\n  - A team that measured TTFT with 200-token test prompts ships a RAG feature with 8k tokens of context\n    and never understands why it feels slow. The answer is in the prefill arithmetic, which this metric\n    forces you to look at.\n- **Speculative decoding and other decode tricks do nothing for it.** They target the second phase.","how":"### What is inside the number\nFrom the client's clock, TTFT is the sum of:\n\n| Term | Size |\n|---|---|\n| Network to the server, plus TCP and TLS handshakes (several round trips) unless the client reuses a pooled connection | Small, fixed |\n| Tokenisation (before admission, because the scheduler needs the prompt length) | Small |\n| Waiting for admission: a free batch slot and free KV-cache memory | **Large, variable** |\n| Prefill forward pass over all prompt tokens | **Large, variable** |\n| Sampling the first token from the logits of the last prompt position | Small |\n| Serialisation and the first flush of the response | Small |\n| Network back | Small |\n\n- **The first token falls out of the prefill pass itself.** There is no separate decode step for it.\n- **Only two terms are large and variable:** the queue and prefill.\n\n### Prefill cost\n- **Rule of thumb.** A dense transformer with *N* parameters costs roughly `2 × N` FLOPs per token for\n  the weight matrices. An attention term grows with the square of prompt length but is small until\n  contexts get very long. So prefill FLOPs ≈ `2 × N × n_prompt`.\n- **Illustrative arithmetic.** An 8-billion-parameter model on a 4,000-token prompt needs about\n  2 × 8e9 × 4e3 = 6.4e13 FLOPs. An H100-class GPU has a dense bf16 peak around 1e15 FLOP/s; at a\n  realistic 40–50 % utilisation that is roughly 130–160 ms of pure prefill.\n- **It scales linearly.** Double the prompt and you double it. A 70B model is nearly nine times more.\n- **So the RAG jump is expected.** TTFT tracks prompt length almost linearly in practice, which is why\n  \"we added RAG and TTFT went from 300 ms to 2 s\" is not a bug.\n\n### Queueing under continuous batching\n- **Admission needs a slot and KV memory.** A continuous-batching engine admits a request only when it\n  has a scheduling slot and enough KV memory for its prompt. When either is exhausted, the request\n  waits for others to finish or be preempted, and that wait lands entirely in TTFT.\n- **TTFT p99 is a capacity signal.** If it climbs while prompt lengths have not changed, you are\n  queueing.\n- **Prefill stalls running streams.** A long prompt entering the batch makes that step expensive,\n  which delays every other stream's next token.\n- **Chunked prefill** splits a long prompt across several steps to protect those streams, at the cost\n  of slightly longer TTFT for the long request itself.\n- **Disaggregated serving** runs prefill on separate GPUs so decode latency and TTFT stop competing at\n  all, paying with a KV-cache transfer between machines.\n\n### Levers, in order of leverage\n1. **Send fewer tokens.** Tighter retrieval (fewer, shorter chunks), summarised history, and no\n   boilerplate. Linear payoff.\n2. **Prefix caching.** If requests share a byte-identical prefix (system prompt, tool schemas, a\n   document in a multi-turn chat), the engine reuses its KV cache and only prefills the suffix.\n   Dynamic content at the top of the prompt (timestamps, user names) breaks the match.\n3. **Admission control and capacity.** Bound concurrency per replica so requests fail fast or queue\n   visibly instead of silently inflating TTFT; scale on queue depth, not GPU utilisation.\n4. **Scheduling.** Chunked prefill, priority classes for interactive traffic, separate pools for\n   long-context jobs.\n5. **Hardware and parallelism.** Prefill is compute-bound, so a faster GPU or tensor parallelism\n   across GPUs does shorten it, unlike decode. Speculative decoding, KV-cache quantisation and\n   weight-only quantisation mostly do not; they target the decode phase.\n\n### TTFT in an agent loop\n- **Each step re-sends the transcript.** System prompt, tool schemas, every earlier tool call and\n  result: the prompt grows by one tool round trip per step, so without caching step 20 prefills far more\n  than step 1.\n- **Prefix caching turns that into a delta.** With an append-only transcript, each step prefills only\n  the new tool result; editing earlier turns or putting a timestamp at the top throws that away.\n- **Short outputs make TTFT the step cost.** A tool call is often a few dozen tokens, so for a planning\n  step TTFT can be most of the model time.\n- **Agent latency is a sum.** Run time ≈ Σ over steps of (TTFT + decode + tool time), so a TTFT\n  regression is multiplied by the step count.\n\n### Measuring it honestly\n- **Stop the clock at the first *non-empty content* delta.** The first SSE frame is often a role-only\n  chunk or a `message_start` event and arrives before any prefill has finished.\n- **Report percentiles bucketed by prompt length**, so a prompt-size regression is distinguishable from\n  a capacity problem.\n- **Track \"time to first visible token\" separately** when a model emits hidden reasoning before its\n  answer, because that gap can be many seconds while TTFT looks fine.\n- **Use server-side TTFT, measured from arrival,** to compare engine configurations; it strips out\n  network.","example":"### Agent scenario: TTFT across a 10-step tool loop\nAn 8B agent with a 4k-token prefix (system prompt plus tool schemas) appends ~1k tokens per step (tool\ncall plus result). Prefill uses the illustrative 2 × N × tokens ÷ (1e15 × 0.45) rule from How it works.\n\n```python\ndef prefill_ms(params, tokens, peak=1e15, util=0.45):\n    return 2 * params * tokens / (peak * util) * 1000\n\nprefix, per_step, steps = 4_000, 1_000, 10\nno_cache = sum(prefill_ms(8e9, prefix + per_step * s) for s in range(steps))\ncached = prefill_ms(8e9, prefix) + sum(prefill_ms(8e9, per_step) for s in range(1, steps))\nprint(f\"no prefix reuse: {no_cache:.0f} ms   append-only + prefix cache: {cached:.0f} ms\")\n# no prefix reuse: 3022 ms   append-only + prefix cache: 462 ms\n```\n\n| Step | Prompt tokens | Prefill without reuse | Prefill with prefix cache |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4,000 | ~142 ms | ~142 ms (cache written) |\n| 5 | 8,000 | ~284 ms | ~36 ms (new 1k only) |\n| 10 | 13,000 | ~462 ms | ~36 ms |\n| Total, 10 steps | | ~3.0 s | ~0.46 s |\n\nQueueing adds to every step on top of this, so under load the gap between the two columns widens.\n\n### Probe TTFT and TPOT against any OpenAI-compatible server\nA short probe against any OpenAI-compatible streaming server (a provider, or vLLM/SGLang locally)\nthat measures TTFT to the first content delta and TPOT over the rest, then shows how TTFT grows with\nprompt length while TPOT stays flat.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")  # any OpenAI-compatible server\nMODEL = \"my-model\"\n\ndef measure(prompt: str, max_tokens: int = 200):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); t_first = t_last = None; chunks = 0; usage_tokens = None\n    stream = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=MODEL, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        max_tokens=max_tokens, stream=True, stream_options={\"include_usage\": True})\n    for chunk in stream:\n        if chunk.usage:                                      # final usage-only chunk, if the server sends one\n            usage_tokens = chunk.usage.completion_tokens\n        if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:  # skip the empty role-only first chunk\n            now = time.perf_counter()\n            t_first = t_first or now\n            t_last = now; chunks += 1\n    n = usage_tokens or chunks                               # chunks ~ tokens only as a fallback\n    ttft = t_first - t0\n    tpot = (t_last - t_first) / max(n - 1, 1)\n    return ttft, tpot, n\n\nfor words in (50, 500, 5000):\n    ttft, tpot, n = measure(\"Summarise this text: \" + \"word \" * words)\n    print(f\"prompt~{words:5d} words  TTFT={ttft*1000:6.0f} ms  TPOT={tpot*1000:5.1f} ms  ({n} out tokens)\")\n# Expect TTFT to rise roughly linearly with prompt length (prefill) and TPOT to stay nearly constant.\n# Run it again with 20 concurrent copies: TTFT climbs (queueing) long before TPOT does.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: predicting the TTFT cost of adding RAG\nPure arithmetic from the prefill rule, before any load test. Assumes ~1e15 FLOP/s bf16 peak at 45 %\nutilisation and no queueing; these are illustrative orders of magnitude, not benchmarks.\n\n```python\ndef prefill_ms(params: float, prompt_tokens: int, peak=1e15, util=0.45) -> float:\n    return 2 * params * prompt_tokens / (peak * util) * 1000\n\nfor label, n, toks in [(\"8B, chat 500\", 8e9, 500), (\"8B, RAG 8k\", 8e9, 8_000),\n                       (\"8B, RAG 20k\", 8e9, 20_000), (\"70B, RAG 8k\", 70e9, 8_000)]:\n    print(f\"{label:14s} {prefill_ms(n, toks):7.0f} ms\")\n```\n\n| Workload | Prompt tokens | Prefill (illustrative) | Takeaway |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8B, plain chat | 500 | ~18 ms | Network and queue dominate |\n| 8B, RAG | 8,000 | ~280 ms | Prefill now dominates TTFT |\n| 8B, wide RAG | 20,000 | ~710 ms | Trim chunks before buying GPUs |\n| 70B, RAG | 8,000 | ~2.5 s | Nearly nine times the 8B cost |\n\nReal numbers will be worse once queueing and attention overhead are added; the ratios are the point.","patterns":["**Append-only agent transcripts.** Keep system prompt and tool schemas first and fixed, append tool calls and results, and never edit earlier turns mid-run, so each step prefills only its delta. Monitor cached versus uncached input tokens per step from the usage fields.","**Trim tool results before they enter context.** Truncate or summarise large tool outputs (search pages, logs, SQL rows) to what the next step needs, because every token is prefilled on every later step. Monitor prompt tokens per step and TTFT per step index.","**Stable prefix first, cached.** Put the system prompt, tool schemas and shared documents first and byte-identical, with timestamps and user fields after them, and run the engine with automatic prefix caching on (vLLM `--enable-prefix-caching`, already the default in the V1 engine). Monitor prefix-cache hit rate and TTFT per route.","**Cap concurrency and fail fast.** Set the per-replica limit (vLLM `--max-num-seqs`) from a load test and return a fast 429 with `Retry-After` beyond it. Monitor the engine's waiting-queue length, which vLLM exports as `vllm:num_requests_waiting`.","**Chunked prefill for mixed traffic.** When long prompts share a replica with live streams, enable chunked prefill (vLLM `--enable-chunked-prefill`, on by default in the V1 engine) and tune the per-step token budget (`--max-num-batched-tokens`): smaller protects streams, larger favours TTFT. Monitor p99 inter-token latency for running streams and TTFT for the longest prompt bucket.","**Bucketed TTFT SLOs.** Alert on TTFT p95 per prompt-length bucket (for example under 1k, 1k-8k, over 8k tokens) and per concurrency band, so you know whether a breach is prompt size or capacity.","**Separate pool for long context.** Route requests above a token threshold to their own replicas or a disaggregated prefill tier, so a few huge prompts cannot queue every short chat. Monitor queue depth per pool.","**Reuse one HTTP client per process.** Create the SDK client once so connections are pooled and handshakes leave the TTFT path. Monitor TTFT for tiny prompts from each region as the network floor."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A 10-step agent makes 10 model calls, and each one waits TTFT before\n  emitting a tool call. Tool-call outputs are short, so on long transcripts TTFT is often the largest\n  share of per-step model time (less so when the step reasons at length first).\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider prompt caching and the cached-token fields in usage\n  responses, vLLM `--enable-prefix-caching` and `vllm:num_requests_waiting` when self-hosting, and the\n  first-content-delta timestamp in your agent traces.\n- **What you control.** Transcript size (tool results trimmed or summarised before appending), a stable\n  prefix (system prompt and tool schemas first, byte-identical), retrieval width in RAG steps, and the\n  number of steps.\n- **What you can skip.** Exact FLOP counts per GPU and disaggregated-serving internals unless you run\n  your own fleet; the `2 × N × n_prompt` rule and \"queueing shows in p99\" are enough.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Multi-step tool-calling agents.** Per-step latency is mostly TTFT, so teams track it per step and\n  watch it grow as the transcript grows.\n- **RAG agents.** Retrieval width sets prompt length, so choosing how many chunks to insert is a TTFT\n  decision as well as a quality one.\n- **Agents with long system prompts and many tools.** A stable, byte-identical prefix lets prompt or\n  prefix caching skip most of the prefill on every step.\n- **Self-hosted agent models.** Queue depth and TTFT p99 tell operators when to add replicas before users\n  feel the slowdown.","mini_project":"**Profile TTFT across an agent run (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show how TTFT changes step by step in a 10-step tool-calling loop, with and without a stable\n  prefix and trimmed tool results.\n- **Steps:** build a small agent with two fake tools that return large outputs; stream every call and log\n  the time to the first content delta, input tokens and cached input tokens; run it three ways: raw tool\n  output appended, tool output trimmed before appending, and trimmed but with a timestamp at the top of\n  the system prompt (which breaks prefix reuse).\n- **Done when:** you have a per-step table and chart of TTFT, prompt size and cached tokens for all three\n  runs, plus a short note on which change saved the most time."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"TTFT is mostly network latency.\"** Network and handshakes are a small fixed floor on a pooled connection. The two large, variable terms are the admission queue and prefill over every prompt token.","**\"The first token is one decode step.\"** It falls out of the prefill pass itself, so its cost scales with the whole prompt (about `2 × N × n_prompt` FLOPs), not with output length.","**\"Speculative decoding or KV quantisation will fix slow TTFT.\"** Both target decode; KV quantisation helps TTFT only indirectly, by freeing memory so fewer requests wait for admission. TTFT moves with fewer prompt tokens, prefix caching, capacity and admission control, and (since prefill is compute-bound) faster GPUs or tensor parallelism.","**\"An agent pays TTFT once per run.\"** It pays it on every step, over a transcript that keeps growing, so a regression is multiplied by the step count unless an append-only prefix stays cached.","**\"The first SSE frame marks TTFT.\"** That frame is often a role-only chunk or `message_start` sent before prefill ends; stop the clock at the first non-empty content delta.","**\"Rising p99 TTFT means prompts got longer.\"** If prompt-length buckets are unchanged, it means queueing: a capacity signal, which is why TTFT is reported per prompt-length bucket."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Client sends request\"] --> B[\"Network and TLS handshake\"]\n    B --> D[\"Tokenise prompt\"]\n    D --> C[\"Admission queue\"]\n    C -->|\"slot and KV memory free\"| E[\"Prefill: all prompt tokens\"]\n    E --> F[\"Sample first token\"]\n    F --> G[\"Serialise and flush\"]\n    G --> H[\"First content delta at client\"]\n    C -.->|\"grows with load\"| Q[\"Queueing tail, p99\"]\n    E -.->|\"grows with prompt length\"| R[\"Linear prefill cost\"]\n    H -.->|\"tool result appended\"| A","caption":"Follow one agent step from send to first visible token. The two boxes that move with load and prompt\nsize, the admission queue and prefill, are where almost all of TTFT goes; the dotted loop is why an\nagent pays it again every step."},"interview":[{"q":"What is TTFT made of, and why does speculative decoding not improve it?","a":"- **Definition:** network plus admission wait plus tokenisation plus prefill over the whole prompt,\n  then sampling the first token from the last prompt position's logits.\n- **Speculative decoding** drafts and verifies several output tokens per decode step, so it shortens the\n  decode phase only; the first token still waits for the full prefill."},{"q":"Latency doubled after we added retrieval. Walk me through it.","a":"- **Decompose first:** compare TTFT and TPOT before and after. Retrieval adds prompt tokens, so expect\n  TTFT to rise and TPOT to stay roughly flat.\n- **Check the arithmetic:** prefill FLOPs ≈ 2 × parameters × prompt tokens, so going from hundreds to\n  thousands of tokens moves prefill from tens to hundreds of milliseconds.\n- **Then the levers:** fewer or shorter chunks (checked on an eval set), the stable prefix first for\n  prefix caching, and chunked prefill if long prompts stall other streams."},{"q":"Your agent gets slower with every step even though tool results are small. What do you check?","a":"- **Cached-token share per step.** It should rise toward the prefix length after step 1; if it stays\n  near zero, something breaks prefix reuse (a timestamp at the top, reordered tool schemas, edited\n  history).\n- **Prompt tokens per step.** A growing transcript with no reuse makes TTFT grow linearly with the step\n  index; trim or summarise old tool results.\n- **Queue depth.** If TTFT rises with concurrency rather than step index, it is capacity, not context."},{"q":"How would you set a latency budget for a 10-step agent?","a":"- **Decompose per step:** TTFT plus decode for the short tool-call output plus tool time, summed over the\n  expected step count.\n- **Spend the budget where it moves:** prefix caching and trimmed tool results cut TTFT on every step;\n  fewer steps (better tools, parallel tool calls) cut all three terms.\n- **Track p95 per step index**, so you see whether later steps are drifting as context grows."},{"q":"Roughly what TTFT should a 70B model have on an 8k-token prompt with no queue?","a":"- **FLOPs:** 2 × 70e9 × 8e3 ≈ 1.1e15.\n- **Throughput:** about 1e15 FLOP/s bf16 peak at 40–50 % utilisation is roughly 4.5e14 FLOP/s, so about\n  2.5 s on one GPU (illustrative).\n- **Implication:** tensor parallelism across GPUs or a smaller prompt is required for a sub-second\n  target, because prefill is compute-bound and does scale with more compute."},{"q":"How do you measure TTFT for a reasoning model that thinks before answering?","a":"- **Two clocks:** TTFT to the first generated token, and time to first visible token for the answer\n  the user reads; the gap can be many seconds.\n- **Client side,** stop at the first non-empty content delta, not the role-only first frame or\n  `message_start`.\n- **Report percentiles** bucketed by prompt length, with cold starts tracked separately."}],"tradeoffs":["**Prefix caching vs one-off prompts.** It slashes TTFT for repeated prefixes, but only when they are byte-identical and still resident; it buys nothing for one-off prompts or when dynamic fields sit before the shared part.","**Chunked prefill vs the long prompt's TTFT.** It protects the inter-token latency of running streams but lengthens TTFT for the long prompt being chunked. The balance flips for prefill-heavy workloads with few concurrent decodes, such as summarisation batches, where you would rather prefill at full speed.","**Short-prompt priority vs starvation.** Prioritising short prompts improves median TTFT at the risk of starving long ones; it only works with ageing or a separate pool for long-context requests.","**Less context vs quality.** Trimming retrieved context lowers TTFT linearly but can cut answer quality, so the cut must be checked against an eval set rather than assumed harmless.","**Disaggregation vs cost.** Splitting prefill and decode isolates TTFT from decode load, but adds machines and a KV-cache transfer whose cost is only worth it at high, steady traffic."],"pitfalls":["**Measuring to the first SSE frame.** Symptom: a TTFT that looks great and does not move when prompt length triples. Fix: stop the clock at the first non-empty content delta.","**Reporting mean TTFT.** Symptom: a green dashboard while users complain, because queueing shows up only in p95 and p99. Fix: report p50, p95 and p99 per prompt-length bucket.","**Blaming the model when it is queueing.** The tell: TTFT correlates with concurrency, not with prompt length, and drops at night. Fix: cap concurrency and scale on queue depth.","**Load-testing with short prompts, shipping long contexts.** Symptom: a product that \"got slow when we added RAG\" with no change in the serving stack. Fix: load-test with the production prompt-length distribution.","**Ignoring hidden reasoning tokens.** TTFT is fine but the user sees a blank screen for ten seconds, so measure time to first visible token separately.","**A new HTTPS connection per request from a distant region.** Symptom: a TTFT floor of a few hundred milliseconds even for tiny prompts, which disappears with a reused client.","**Counting cold starts inside TTFT.** Model load on a fresh replica or serverless GPU wake-up shows up as a p99 dominated by a handful of 30-second outliers after each scale-out. Fix: tag cold-start requests and track them separately, and keep warm capacity ahead of scale-out."],"when":"- **Set a target per surface before launch.** A chat UI usually aims for well under a second at p95,\n  inline completion needs a few hundred milliseconds, and a nightly batch job has no TTFT target at all.\n- **Budget it as network + queue + prefill** and check which term you can actually control.\n- **Watch it bucketed** by prompt length and by concurrency, to tell a prompt-size regression from a\n  capacity problem.\n- **First number to check** when \"the assistant feels slow\", and per step when an agent run is slow.\n- **Interviews:** the expected answer to \"latency doubled after we added retrieval\" is a decomposition\n  of TTFT into prefill arithmetic plus the levers above.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments TTFT to the first content delta, enables prefix caching for the shared system prompt, and trims retrieved context when the prefill term dominates.","architect":"Sets per-surface TTFT SLOs, decides concurrency limits and admission policy, and chooses whether long-context traffic gets its own pool or disaggregated prefill.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their 200-page-document prompt costs seconds before the first word, and offers retrieval or chunking as the fix rather than a bigger GPU alone."},"check":{"q":"Your service's p50 TTFT is 400 ms but p99 is 9 s. The model, prompts and hardware have not\nchanged in a month. What is happening, how do you confirm it, and what do you change?","a":"A gap that large with stable prompts is queueing: requests waiting for a batch slot or KV\nmemory, which lands entirely in TTFT. Confirm by plotting TTFT against concurrent requests and\nengine queue depth; the p99 should rise with load and collapse off-peak, and the engine's\npreemption or waiting-queue metrics should spike at the same times. Prompt-length buckets rule out\na few long prompts as the cause. Fixes, cheapest first: cap per-replica concurrency and return a fast\n429 or visible queue position instead of a silent 9 s wait; enable prefix caching if prompts share a\nsystem prefix; turn on chunked prefill so long prompts stop stalling admission; autoscale on queue\ndepth rather than GPU utilisation; and move long-context or batch traffic to a separate pool."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which change most directly reduces TTFT for requests with a 10k-token prompt?","options":["Enabling speculative decoding with a small draft model","Quantising the KV cache to 8-bit","Caching the KV state of the shared prompt prefix so only the suffix is prefilled","Raising `max_tokens` so the engine schedules the request sooner"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TTFT is dominated by prefill over the prompt; prefix caching removes most of that work when the prefix repeats. Speculative decoding and KV quantisation target the decode phase, and `max_tokens` affects output length, not the time to the first token."},{"q":"Holding hardware and model fixed, TTFT scales primarily with which two quantities?","options":["Output length and sampling temperature","Prompt length and time spent waiting for admission to the batch","Number of KV heads and vocabulary size","Batch size of the decode phase and the stop sequence"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefill cost is roughly linear in prompt tokens, and any wait for a scheduling slot or KV memory is added on top. Output length only affects total time after the first token, and temperature changes which token is sampled, not how long it takes."},{"q":"Which measurement gives a correct client-side TTFT for a streamed chat completion?","options":["Time until the HTTP response headers arrive","Time until the first SSE frame of any kind is received","Time until the first delta containing non-empty content is received","Time until the final usage chunk divided by the number of tokens"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Headers and the first frame (often a role-only chunk or `message_start`) are sent before prefill completes, so they under-report. The first content delta is the first evidence that generation has produced a token."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/tpot","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Yu et al., 2022 — Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models","Leviathan et al., 2023 — Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding"],"why":"- **It dominates total time for real answers.** After the first token, total latency is\n  `TTFT + (n_out − 1) × TPOT`. A 600-token answer at 50 ms per token is 30 s; at 15 ms it is 9 s. The\n  user's sense of \"is this fast\" after the first word, and an agent's time across a dozen chained\n  calls, both hang on this number.\n- **Decode has different physics from prefill.** Prefill is one big matrix multiplication per layer and\n  is compute-bound. Decode processes a single new token per sequence, so arithmetic per step is tiny\n  while the entire weight set still streams from HBM into the compute units every step.\n- **So the levers differ.** Step time is set by bytes moved, not FLOPs. What helps it (fewer bytes per\n  step, more bandwidth, more tokens per step) is not what helps TTFT.\n- **\"Report tokens per second\" is not enough.** It is ambiguous between one stream and the whole\n  server, and hides stalls: a stream averaging 20 ms per token with a 400 ms freeze every few seconds\n  looks fine as a mean and feels broken.\n- **TPOT plus its distribution exposes the cause.** Inter-token latency (ITL) makes stalls visible and\n  points at the engine behaviour behind them.","how":"### Definition and its sibling\n| Metric | Formula | Catches |\n|---|---|---|\n| TPOT | `(t_last − t_first) / (n_out − 1)`: total decode time over the number of gaps, per request | Overall decode speed |\n| ITL | The distribution of individual gaps | Stalls the mean hides (look at p99) |\n| \"Latency per token\" | `end_to_end / n_out` | Nothing useful: it silently folds TTFT back in |\n\n- **Both TPOT and ITL exclude TTFT on purpose**, so a long prompt does not pollute the decode measurement.\n- **Names are inconsistent.** Some dashboards call the per-request mean \"ITL\". Check the formula before\n  comparing two reports.\n\n### The bandwidth floor\nIn one decode step for a batch of *B* sequences, the GPU reads every weight matrix once (the same weights\nserve all *B* tokens) and reads every sequence's KV cache for attention. Ignoring compute:\n\n```\nstep_time ≥ (weight_bytes + Σ kv_bytes_per_sequence) / memory_bandwidth\n```\n\n- **Illustrative numbers.** An 8B-parameter model in fp16 is about 16 GB of weights. An H100 SXM moves\n  roughly 3.35 TB/s from HBM.\n- **Batch 1, short context.** The floor is 16 / 3350 ≈ 4.8 ms per token, a ceiling of about 210 tokens\n  per second for a single stream.\n- **Real systems land below the ceiling** because of kernel launch overhead, imperfect attention\n  kernels, sampling and the host loop. But no amount of extra FLOPs lowers it: a GPU with similar\n  bandwidth and double the FLOPs gives nearly the same TPOT at low batch.\n\n### Why batching is nearly free\n1. **The weight read is paid once per step regardless of *B*.** Going from 1 to 16 sequences multiplies\n   the arithmetic by 16 but adds only KV-cache reads to the bytes moved.\n2. **So per-request TPOT rises gently while aggregate throughput rises almost linearly.**\n3. **It continues until one of two limits:** arithmetic catches up with bandwidth, or the KV cache of\n   all sequences no longer fits in memory.\n4. **The compute crossover can be estimated.** Counting only weight matmuls, a step does `2 × N × B`\n   FLOPs while reading `N × bytes_per_param` bytes, so arithmetic intensity is `2B / bytes_per_param`\n   FLOPs per byte.\n5. **The GPU turns compute-bound when that intensity reaches its ratio** of peak FLOP/s to bandwidth,\n   roughly 1e15 / 3.35e12 ≈ 300 for an H100 in bf16. With fp16 weights (`2B / 2 = B`) the crossover is\n   a batch near 300, independent of model size.\n6. **Int8 weights halve it**, because each byte read now feeds twice the arithmetic. KV reads add bytes\n   without much arithmetic, which pushes the real crossover later still.\n7. **Long contexts pull the memory limit in fast**, because KV bytes scale with `B × context_length`;\n   at long contexts the KV reads, not the weights, dominate the step.\n\n### Levers that move TPOT\n- **Fewer weight bytes.** Weight-only int8 or int4 quantisation cuts the bytes read per step by 2–4×, a\n  direct TPOT win at low batch. At high batch, where compute starts to bind, the gain shrinks and\n  dequantisation cost shows.\n- **Fewer KV bytes.** Grouped-query attention, KV-cache quantisation and shorter contexts reduce the\n  per-sequence term, which matters most for long-context, high-concurrency serving.\n- **More bandwidth per model.** Tensor parallelism splits the weights across *k* GPUs so each reads `1/k`\n  of them per step. The all-reduce after each layer adds a fixed communication cost, so it helps most on\n  NVLink-connected GPUs and large models.\n- **More tokens per step.** Speculative decoding drafts several tokens cheaply and verifies them in one\n  forward pass of the big model, so one weight read can yield two or three accepted tokens.\n  - Effective TPOT falls at low batch; at high batch the wasted verification compute can lower\n    throughput instead.\n  - ITL becomes lumpy: accepted tokens arrive in bursts (near-zero gaps inside a burst, a full step\n    between bursts) even when the mean improves.\n- **A smaller model.** Halving parameters roughly halves bytes per step; whether quality survives is an\n  eval question, not a serving one.\n\n### Where the stalls come from\n- **Prefill inside the shared step.** In continuous batching every running stream advances one token per\n  engine step. A newly admitted request's prefill runs inside that step; a 10k-token prefill can take\n  hundreds of milliseconds, during which every other stream emits nothing.\n- **The signature** is a flat 25 ms ITL punctuated by spikes: a scheduling artefact, not a model\n  property. Chunked prefill caps prefill work per step to bound the stall.\n- **Preemption.** When KV memory runs out the engine evicts a sequence and later recomputes or swaps its\n  cache back; that sequence's ITL shows a multi-second gap.\n\n### How fast is fast enough\n| Consumer | Rough need |\n|---|---|\n| Human reading prose | 200–300 words per minute, about 4–5 words per second; at 1.3 tokens per English word, 5–7 tokens per second, so a TPOT near 150–200 ms keeps pace |\n| Skimming, scrolling, code in blocks | Much lower TPOT, because users skim and want code to appear in blocks |\n| Agentic workloads | As low as you can afford: a planner making eight sequential calls of 300 tokens each turns a 30 ms TPOT into over a minute of waiting before anyone reads a word |\n\n### TPOT in an agent loop\n- **Output tokens are the decode bill.** Per step, decode time ≈ `(n_out − 1) × TPOT`; summed over a run,\n  it is total output tokens times TPOT, plus one TTFT per call.\n- **Steps are sequential.** A planner cannot call the tool until the tool call is fully decoded, so\n  decode time sits on the critical path of every step.\n- **Hidden reasoning counts.** Reasoning tokens are decoded at the same TPOT even when the user never\n  sees them, which is why an agent step with a large thinking budget can be slow with a tiny visible output.\n- **The cheapest lever is fewer tokens.** Terse tool-call formats, capped intermediate outputs and\n  routing simple steps to a smaller, faster model usually save more than serving tweaks, and need no\n  hardware change.","example":"### Worked scenario: predicting TPOT before buying hardware\nTake a Llama-3-8B-style model: 8B parameters in fp16 (16 GB of weights), 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head\ndimension 128.\n- **KV per token** is `2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 131,072 bytes`, i.e. 128 KB.\n- **Hardware:** an H100 SXM with about 3.35 TB/s of HBM bandwidth.\n- **Ignoring compute is safe here.** Even at batch 100 the decode FLOPs per step\n  (`2 × 8e9 × 100 = 1.6e12`) take only about 3 ms at half of the roughly 1e15 FLOP/s peak, well under the\n  bandwidth time at that batch.\n\n```python\nBW, WEIGHTS = 3.35e12, 16e9               # bytes/s, bytes\nKV_PER_TOKEN = 2 * 32 * 8 * 128 * 2       # 131,072 bytes\n\ndef step_ms(batch: int, ctx: int) -> float:\n    return (WEIGHTS + batch * ctx * KV_PER_TOKEN) / BW * 1e3\n\nfor b, ctx in [(1, 4_000), (32, 4_000), (32, 32_000)]:\n    s = step_ms(b, ctx)\n    print(f\"B={b:2d} ctx={ctx:6d}  step {s:5.1f} ms  per-user {1e3/s:4.0f} tok/s  server {b*1e3/s:5.0f} tok/s\")\n```\n\n| Case | Bytes per step | Step (TPOT) | Per user | Server |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Batch 1, 4k context | 16 GB + 4,000 × 128 KB ≈ 16.5 GB | 16.5 / 3350 ≈ 4.9 ms | about 200 tok/s | about 200 tok/s |\n| Batch 32, 4k context | KV = 32 × 4,000 × 131,072 B ≈ 16.8 GB, total ≈ 32.8 GB | about 9.8 ms | roughly 100 tok/s | 32 × 100 ≈ 3,300 tok/s |\n| Batch 32, 32k context | KV = 32 × 32,000 × 131,072 B ≈ 134 GB | about 45 ms if it fit | | |\n\n- **Sixteen times the throughput for twice the TPOT** going from batch 1 to batch 32.\n- **The 32k case does not even fit** next to the weights in 80 GB. Long context, not model size, is what\n  pushes TPOT up at scale.\n- **Measured numbers will be worse** than every figure here; the point is the ratios and which term\n  dominates.\n\n### Mean TPOT hides the stall; ITL p99 shows it\nRunnable with synthetic timestamps: a steady 25 ms gap with one 300 ms prefill stall every 40 tokens.\n\n```python\nimport statistics\n\ngaps_ms = [300 if i % 40 == 39 else 25 for i in range(200)]   # 200 gaps -> 201 tokens\nt = [0.0]\nfor g in gaps_ms:\n    t.append(t[-1] + g)\n\nn_out = len(t)\ntpot = (t[-1] - t[0]) / (n_out - 1)                            # per-request mean\nitl = sorted(gaps_ms)\np50, p99 = itl[len(itl) // 2], itl[int(len(itl) * 0.99)]\nprint(f\"TPOT {tpot:.1f} ms  ITL p50 {p50} ms  p99 {p99} ms\")\nprint(f\"stdev {statistics.pstdev(gaps_ms):.0f} ms\")\n# TPOT 31.9 ms  ITL p50 25 ms  p99 300 ms  -> mean looks healthy, users see freezes\n# stdev 43 ms\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: where a planner's minute goes\nA planner agent makes eight sequential calls; illustrative TTFT 300 ms per call, no tool time counted.\n\n| Design | Output tokens per call | TPOT | Decode time for 8 calls | Plus 8 × TTFT |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Verbose reasoning before each tool call | 300 | 30 ms | 8 × 300 × 30 ms ≈ 72 s | ≈ 74 s |\n| Same, on a faster replica | 300 | 15 ms | ≈ 36 s | ≈ 38 s |\n| Terse tool calls, capped reasoning | 60 | 30 ms | ≈ 14 s | ≈ 17 s |\n| Terse calls on the faster replica | 60 | 15 ms | ≈ 7 s | ≈ 10 s |\n\nHalving TPOT halves decode time; cutting output tokens fivefold beats it, and the two multiply.","patterns":["**Budget output tokens per agent step.** Set `max_tokens` per step type (tight for tool calls and routing, looser for the final answer) and ask for terse tool-call output. Monitor output tokens per step and the share of steps that hit the cap, since truncated tool calls fail to parse.","**Route cheap steps to a faster model.** Use a smaller model for classification, routing and simple tool selection, and the large model for planning and the final answer, gated by your agent eval set. Monitor per-step latency and task success per model.","**Alert on ITL p99, not mean TPOT.** Export per-token latency as a histogram (vLLM exposes a time-per-output-token histogram on its metrics endpoint) and alert on p99 per route. Monitor the p99/p50 ratio; a jump means stalls, not slower decode.","**Chunked prefill when stalls appear.** In vLLM enable chunked prefill and size `--max-num-batched-tokens` so a long prompt is spread over several steps. Monitor ITL p99 for running streams against TTFT of the longest prompts.","**Set the concurrency ceiling from the TPOT SLO.** Run a load curve and set `--max-num-seqs` at the largest batch where per-user TPOT p95 still meets the target. Monitor TPOT p95 against running sequences.","**Keep KV headroom to avoid preemption.** Leave memory for the expected context mix (vLLM `--gpu-memory-utilization`, `--max-model-len`) and watch the engine's preemption counter, which should stay near zero for interactive pools.","**Quantise weights behind an eval gate.** Serve an int8 or int4 checkpoint (for example AWQ or GPTQ via vLLM `--quantization`) only after it passes your eval set. Monitor TPOT at low batch, where the gain is largest, and quality metrics.","**Speculative decoding on the low-batch pool only.** Enable it where concurrency is low and acceptance is high; monitor acceptance rate and aggregate tokens/s at peak, and disable it if throughput falls."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent run time is a sum over steps, and every output token of every step\n  costs TPOT. Verbose reasoning or chatty tool-call preambles multiply it: eight calls of 300 tokens at\n  30 ms is over a minute.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Output-token counts in provider usage fields, `max_tokens` per\n  agent step, model choice (a smaller model for routing steps), and vLLM `--max-num-seqs` and\n  `--quantization` when you self-host the agent's model.\n- **What you control.** How many tokens each step emits (terse tool calls, a reasoning budget, short\n  intermediate outputs), which model handles which step, and concurrency on your own replicas.\n- **What you can skip.** Deriving the compute crossover batch or tuning tensor parallelism, unless you\n  operate the GPUs. Know that decode is bandwidth-bound and that stalls show in ITL p99.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Reasoning-heavy agents.** Every reasoning token costs TPOT, so teams set reasoning or output budgets\n  per step to keep run time predictable.\n- **Model routing inside an agent.** Cheap, fast models handle routing and tool-selection steps; the\n  larger model writes only the final answer.\n- **Streaming final answers.** TPOT sets how fast text appears once it starts; ITL p99 shows the stalls\n  users report as a \"frozen\" stream.\n- **Self-hosted agent fleets.** Concurrency and quantisation settings trade per-stream TPOT for how many\n  agent sessions one replica can serve.","mini_project":"**Cut output-token time in an agent loop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure how much of an agent run is spent decoding and reduce it without hurting task success.\n- **Steps:** run a small tool-calling agent on 10 tasks and log output tokens, TTFT and total time per\n  step; compute decode time as total minus TTFT; then tighten `max_tokens` and ask for terse tool calls\n  (or route tool-selection steps to a smaller model) and rerun.\n- **Done when:** you have a before and after table of output tokens and decode time per run, and the\n  task success count is unchanged or any drop is explained."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A GPU with more FLOPs decodes faster.\"** At low batch each decode step streams every weight from HBM for one token per sequence, so TPOT tracks memory bandwidth; double the FLOPs at similar bandwidth gives nearly the same TPOT.","**\"Batching slows every user down proportionally.\"** The weight read is shared by the whole batch, so batch 1 to 32 raised step time about 2× while server throughput rose about 16× in the worked example, until compute or KV memory binds.","**\"Mean TPOT tells me if streaming feels smooth.\"** A 25 ms stream with a 300 ms stall every 40 tokens averages 31.9 ms; only the ITL distribution (p99) shows the freezes users notice.","**\"Model size is what makes decode slow at scale.\"** At long contexts and high concurrency the KV-cache reads (`B × context_length`) dominate the bytes per step, not the weights.","**\"Speculative decoding always lowers TPOT.\"** It helps at low batch, but at high batch the wasted verification compute can cut throughput, and ITL becomes bursty even when the mean improves.","**\"Hidden reasoning is free because the user never sees it.\"** Reasoning tokens decode at the same TPOT and sit on the critical path of every agent step; cutting output tokens is usually the biggest lever."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[(\"Weights in HBM, read once\")] --> S[\"Decode step for batch B\"]\n    K[(\"KV cache, read per sequence\")] --> S\n    S -->|\"bytes over bandwidth\"| T[\"Step time is TPOT\"]\n    T --> O[\"One token per sequence\"]\n    O -->|\"next step\"| S\n    P[\"New request prefill\"] -.->|\"runs inside step\"| S\n    X[\"Preemption, KV full\"] -.->|\"multi-second gap\"| O\n    S -.->|\"spikes show in\"| I[\"ITL p99\"]","caption":"One decode step, which every output token of every agent call passes through: the weight read is\nshared by the whole batch, KV reads grow per sequence (long agent transcripts make them large), and a\nprefill or preemption landing in the same step is what produces stalls."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is LLM decode memory-bandwidth bound rather than compute bound?","a":"- **One token per sequence per step** means the arithmetic is tiny, roughly 2 FLOPs per parameter per\n  token.\n- **But every weight is read from HBM each step**, plus the KV cache, so bytes moved set the step time.\n- **Consequence:** TPOT tracks bandwidth, and batching shares the weight read across sequences until\n  intensity reaches the GPU's FLOP-to-byte ratio, around 300 for an H100 in bf16."},{"q":"Estimate the best-case TPOT for an 8B fp16 model on an H100 at batch 1.","a":"- **Bytes per step:** about 16 GB of weights plus a small KV term at short context.\n- **Bandwidth:** about 3.35 TB/s, so 16 / 3350 ≈ 4.8 ms per token, a ceiling near 210 tokens per second.\n- **Reality check:** measured TPOT will be higher because of kernel launches, attention kernels,\n  sampling and the host loop; no extra FLOPs lower the floor."},{"q":"Users report the stream \"freezes\" but the dashboard says TPOT is 25 ms. What do you look at?","a":"- **The ITL distribution:** a flat 25 ms with periodic spikes of hundreds of milliseconds is the\n  signature of prefill running inside the shared step, or of preemption.\n- **Correlate spikes** with request admissions of long prompts and with the engine's preemption counter.\n- **Fix:** chunked prefill for admission stalls, more KV headroom or a lower concurrency cap for\n  preemption."},{"q":"When would speculative decoding make things worse?","a":"- **At high batch**, where the GPU is near compute-bound, verification of rejected drafts spends compute\n  the batch needs, so aggregate throughput can fall.\n- **With low acceptance**, the drafts rarely pay for the extra forward work.\n- **Also note** it makes ITL lumpy, so gap-based alerts need adjusting."},{"q":"Your self-hosted agents decode more slowly as their transcripts grow. Why, and what do you do?","a":"- **KV bytes scale with batch times context length**, while weight bytes are fixed per step; long agent\n  transcripts at high concurrency make KV reads, not model size, dominate the step.\n- **Example:** an 8B model at batch 32 and 32k context would read about 134 GB of KV per step against\n  16 GB of weights, roughly 45 ms per step if it fit at all.\n- **Levers:** grouped-query attention, KV-cache quantisation and context caps attack that term directly."},{"q":"An agent run takes 70 seconds and the team wants a bigger GPU. What do you check first?","a":"- **Split the run:** per-step TTFT, output tokens times TPOT, and tool time from the trace.\n- **If decode dominates,** count output tokens per step first: verbose reasoning or preambles before tool\n  calls are usually the biggest term, and cutting them is free.\n- **Only then hardware:** a GPU with more bandwidth lowers TPOT roughly by the bandwidth ratio, not the\n  FLOPs ratio, and routing simple steps to a smaller model may help more."}],"tradeoffs":["**Concurrency vs per-user latency.** Raising concurrency multiplies throughput for a small TPOT cost while weights dominate the step; once KV reads dominate (long contexts) or compute binds, each added sequence costs every user real latency.","**Quantisation vs quality.** Weight quantisation lowers TPOT at low batch and can cut quality on some tasks; at high batch the speed gain fades while the quality cost stays, so evaluate both before shipping.","**Tensor parallelism vs communication.** It cuts per-GPU bytes but adds a per-layer all-reduce; on PCIe-connected GPUs the communication can eat the gain, so it pays mainly with NVLink and large models.","**Speculative decoding vs load.** It reduces effective TPOT when acceptance is high and the batch is small; with low acceptance or a saturated GPU it burns compute and can reduce throughput.","**Chunked prefill vs TTFT.** It smooths ITL for running streams at the price of slower TTFT for long prompts; for a prefill-heavy, low-concurrency workload you may prefer the stalls."],"pitfalls":["**Expecting a faster-FLOPs GPU to speed single-stream decode proportionally.** Symptom: a costly upgrade that moves TPOT only as much as the bandwidth ratio. Fix: compare GPUs on HBM bandwidth.","**Reporting TPOT only as a mean.** Symptom: users describe \"freezes\" while the dashboard shows a healthy 25 ms, because prefill stalls and preemptions live in p99 ITL. Fix: chart the ITL histogram.","**Including TTFT in the TPOT calculation.** Symptom: TPOT that mysteriously grows with prompt length even though decode speed has not changed. Fix: start the clock at the first token.","**Benchmarking at batch 1 and sizing concurrency from it.** Symptom: per-user speed that halves the moment real traffic arrives. Fix: run a load curve at realistic concurrency.","**Ignoring context length in capacity math.** Symptom: TPOT and preemptions climbing as conversations get longer during the day. Fix: size KV memory for the real context mix.","**Counting streamed chunks as tokens.** Servers can coalesce or split tokens per chunk, so a chunk-based TPOT disagrees with the provider's usage numbers (coalescing inflates it, splitting flatters it). Fix: divide by the usage-reported output token count."],"when":"- **Track TPOT and p99 ITL** for every surface where output length is non-trivial: chat, code\n  generation, long-form drafting, and above all agent loops where total time is a sum of many decode\n  phases.\n- **Budget agent output first.** Before tuning TPOT, cap output tokens per agent step; the product of\n  tokens and TPOT is what the user waits for.\n- **Set the target from the consumer.** A human reader is satisfied by a few hundred milliseconds per\n  token, a code editor wants tens of milliseconds, and an orchestrator chaining calls wants the lowest\n  you can afford.\n- **Sanity-check first, then measure.** Use the bandwidth floor to check vendor claims and hardware\n  choices before testing, then measure under realistic concurrency and context length.\n- **Interviews:** derive the floor from weights, KV size and bandwidth, then explain why batching barely\n  changes it; that is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Measures TPOT and p99 ITL from the stream, turns on chunked prefill when stalls appear, and tests weight quantisation against an eval set before enabling it.","architect":"Picks GPU class by memory bandwidth and capacity rather than FLOPs for decode-heavy workloads, and sets the concurrency ceiling where per-user TPOT still meets the SLO.","fde":"Explains to a customer with an on-prem A100 why their chat streams at half the speed of the demo on H100s, and which of quantisation, TP or a smaller model is viable for them."},"check":{"q":"Product wants chat tokens to stream twice as fast. The team proposes moving from A100 to H100\nGPUs. Is that enough, what else would you consider, and what are the catches of each option?","a":"Decode is bandwidth-bound, so the upgrade buys roughly the ratio of memory bandwidth, about\n1.6× from A100-80GB to H100 SXM, not 2×, and less if the batch is large enough to be partly\ncompute-bound. To close the gap: weight-only int8 or int4 quantisation halves or quarters the bytes\nper step, with a quality check required; tensor parallelism across two NVLink GPUs halves per-GPU\nreads but adds all-reduce latency and doubles hardware per replica; speculative decoding can give\n2–3× at low batch if the draft model's acceptance rate is high, but wastes compute at high load;\nlowering per-replica concurrency raises per-user speed at a throughput cost. Before any of it, check\np99 ITL: if the complaint is really periodic stalls from prefill or preemption, chunked prefill and\nmore KV memory headroom fix it with no hardware change."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does per-request TPOT rise only slightly when the batch grows from 1 to 16 sequences?","options":["The GPU has 16 streaming multiprocessors, one per sequence","The model weights are read from memory once per step and shared by all sequences, so only the KV-cache reads grow","Continuous batching pauses other sequences while each one decodes","Larger batches switch the engine from fp16 to int8 automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A decode step is dominated by streaming the weights from HBM; that cost is paid once regardless of batch size. Each extra sequence adds only its KV reads and a little arithmetic, so throughput scales nearly linearly while per-token latency creeps up."},{"q":"Which of these does NOT reduce TPOT for a single stream at batch 1?","options":["Weight-only int4 quantisation","Speculative decoding with a well-matched draft model","Prefix caching of the system prompt","Tensor parallelism across two NVLink-connected GPUs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prefix caching avoids prefill work and therefore helps TTFT, but every decode step still reads the full weights. Quantisation and tensor parallelism cut bytes per step, and speculative decoding yields several tokens per weight read."},{"q":"A stream shows a steady 25 ms between tokens with occasional 300 ms gaps. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Network jitter between the client and the server","The sampling temperature is too high for stable decoding","Prefill of newly admitted requests, or preemption, running inside the shared engine step","The tokenizer is slow on rare words"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In continuous batching, a long prompt entering the batch makes that step expensive and every other stream waits for it; preemption when KV memory is exhausted causes the same signature. Chunked prefill and more memory headroom are the fixes, not network or sampling changes."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/tokens-sec","sources":["Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized Large Language Model Serving","Official docs: vLLM benchmarking"],"why":"- **Serving is selling tokens per GPU-second.** The business, the capacity plan and the user experience\n  all need a rate, and the obvious one is overloaded.\n- **Three people, three quantities, same words.** A vendor slide says \"1,200 tokens/s\"; a user complains\n  the assistant \"only does 30 tokens/s\"; an engineer sizes a cluster from a benchmark at concurrency 1.\n- **The mistakes compound.** Sizing from single-stream speed can undershoot capacity by an order of\n  magnitude, while judging user experience from aggregate throughput hides that each stream is crawling.\n- **One number per deployment does not exist.** It depends on requests in flight, prompt and answer\n  lengths, whether prompt tokens count (prefill chews through them tens of times faster than decode\n  produces output), and which tokenizer defines a token.\n- **Tokenizers alone move it a lot.** Two models given the same English text can differ in token count\n  by double-digit percentages, and far more on code or non-Latin scripts. Without pinning these down,\n  tokens/s is marketing, not measurement.","how":"### The four numbers hiding in one phrase\n1. **Per-stream output tokens/s.** One request's decode rate after its first token: about `1 / TPOT`.\n   This is what a reader perceives.\n2. **Aggregate output tokens/s.** Output tokens produced by the whole replica per second across all\n   concurrent requests. In steady state it is roughly `concurrency × per-stream rate`, and it is the\n   number that sets cost per token.\n3. **Input (prefill) tokens/s.** Prompt tokens processed per second. Prefill is compute-bound and\n   batched, so this is typically one to two orders of magnitude higher than output rate on the same\n   hardware.\n4. **\"Total\" tokens/s.** Input plus output over wall time. It is dominated by the cheap input term, so it\n   makes any system look fast; treat a quote that does not say \"output\" as this number.\n\nAlongside these:\n- **Requests per second.**\n- **Goodput:** the rate of requests (or tokens) served *within* the latency SLO. It is the honest\n  capacity figure because it refuses to count tokens that arrived too late to matter.\n\n### How they relate\n- **Per-stream rate falls slowly with concurrency**, because each decode step streams the weights once\n  for all sequences and only KV-cache reads grow with the batch.\n- **Aggregate rate climbs almost linearly at first**, then bends as KV reads dominate or the GPU becomes\n  compute-bound, and stops when KV memory is full and new requests queue.\n- **The sellable capacity is where the curves cross your SLO**, not the plateau, for interactive\n  traffic.\n\n### Little's law makes it exact\n\"Aggregate ≈ concurrency × per-stream rate\" is only the decode-phase view. In steady state, requests per\nsecond = concurrency / mean request latency, so\n\n```\naggregate_output_tok/s ≈ concurrency × n_out / (TTFT + (n_out − 1) × TPOT)\n```\n\n| 64 in flight, TPOT 25 ms, TTFT 500 ms | Request time | Aggregate output |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1,000-token answers | about 25.5 s | 64 × 1000 / 25.5 s ≈ 2,500 tokens/s |\n| 50-token answers | about 1.7 s | 64 × 50 / 1.7 s ≈ 1,900 tokens/s |\n\nSame engine, fewer tokens per second for short answers, because each slot spends almost a third of its\nlife in queue and prefill rather than decoding.\n\n### What moves the numbers\n| Factor | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| **Bytes per step** | Model size and weight precision set the floor of per-stream speed |\n| **Memory bandwidth** | The hardware term; FLOPs matter only once compute binds at high batch |\n| **Concurrency** | Trades per-stream for aggregate, as above |\n| **Context length** | More KV bytes per step and fewer sequences that fit, so both per-stream and aggregate fall as conversations grow |\n| **Output length distribution** | Many short answers mean more scheduling churn and a larger prefill share; aggregate output falls even though the GPU is busy |\n| **Tokenizer** | Changes the denominator. Comparing models by tokens/s rewards the coarser vocabulary; compare characters or words per second for user-facing speed |\n\n### Measuring it without fooling yourself\n- **Count tokens from the server's usage report**, not from the number of streamed chunks.\n- **Warm up and measure in steady state**, excluding model load and cache fill.\n- **Choose the loop deliberately.**\n  - *Closed-loop:* fixed concurrency; each client sends the next request when the previous finishes.\n    It can never show queue build-up, so its capacity numbers are optimistic.\n  - *Open-loop:* requests arrive at a set rate regardless of completion, which exposes queueing.\n- **Use realistic prompt and output length distributions**, report failures and timeouts as part of the\n  run, and publish TTFT and TPOT percentiles next to the throughput figure.\n- **Tools:** vLLM ships a serving benchmark script and NVIDIA's GenAI-Perf exists for the same job; a\n  30-line asyncio harness is enough to start.\n\n### From tokens/s to money\nCost per million output tokens on self-hosted hardware is\n`gpu_hourly_cost / (aggregate_output_tokens_per_second × 3600) × 1e6`. Illustrative:\n\n| Replica setting | Output tokens/s | Tokens per hour at $3 per GPU-hour (assumed) | Cost per million output tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Batched, high concurrency | 3,000 | 10.8 million | about $0.28 |\n| Concurrency 1 | 200 | 0.72 million | $4.17 |\n\n- **Before idle time, redundancy and prefill.** Real costs are higher than both rows.\n- **That spread is the entire economics of batching**, and why the concurrency you choose is a pricing\n  decision.\n\n### Agent traffic changes the shape\n- **Short outputs, long prompts.** Tool-call steps emit tens of tokens over prompts of thousands, so the\n  short-answer row of the Little's law table is the agent's normal case.\n- **Uncached input tokens/s is often the binding number.** Each step re-sends the transcript, so input\n  volume can be tens of times output volume; track it separately (net of prefix-cache hits) or output\n  tokens/s looks low while prefill is saturated.\n- **The unit is the task.** Users and budgets care about seconds and dollars per completed agent run,\n  which is tokens per step × steps, divided by the rates above.","example":"### Agent scenario: sizing a replica for agent runs\nIllustrative: 50 agent runs per minute, 10 steps each, 8k input and 150 output tokens per step, on a\nreplica with 64 in flight, TTFT 500 ms and TPOT 25 ms.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef aggregate_tok_s(concurrency, n_out, ttft_s, tpot_s):\n    return concurrency * n_out / (ttft_s + (n_out - 1) * tpot_s)\n\nruns_per_min, steps, inp, out = 50, 10, 8_000, 150\nneed_out = runs_per_min * steps * out / 60          # output tok/s the agents need\nneed_in = runs_per_min * steps * inp / 60           # input tok/s re-sent by the agents\nagg = aggregate_tok_s(64, out, 0.5, 0.025)          # short agent outputs: ~2,272 tok/s\nprint(f\"need {need_out:,.0f} out tok/s and {need_in:,.0f} in tok/s ({need_in / need_out:.0f}x)\")\nprint(f\"replica gives {agg:,.0f} out tok/s -> {math.ceil(need_out / agg)} replica by output alone\")\n# need 1,250 out tok/s and 66,667 in tok/s (53x)\n# replica gives 2,272 out tok/s -> 1 replica by output alone\n```\n\n| Question | Answer | Lesson |\n|---|---|---|\n| Is output capacity enough? | Yes, about 1,250 of ~2,272 tok/s | Output tokens/s says \"one replica\" |\n| What else is needed? | ~66,667 input tok/s, 53× the output | Measure prefill load before trusting that |\n| Cheapest lever | Prefix caching, so each step prefills only its new tokens | Cuts the input term, not the output one |\n\n- **Why the input term matters.** For an 8B model, the TTFT lesson's illustrative prefill rule (about\n  4.5e14 effective FLOP/s, `2 × N` FLOPs per token) gives roughly 28,000 prefill tokens/s per GPU, so\n  66,667 uncached input tok/s is over two GPUs of prefill alone.\n- **With prefix caching** most of each step's 8k tokens are already cached, and the uncached input rate\n  can fall several-fold.\n\n### Closed-loop load generator for the concurrency curve\nA small closed-loop load generator against any OpenAI-compatible streaming server: *c* workers each\nsend a request, wait for it to finish, and send the next, so concurrency stays near *c* for the\nwhole run.\n- **It reports** aggregate output tokens/s (total tokens over wall time, not an average of\n  per-request rates), median per-stream rate and TTFT percentiles at several concurrency levels.\n- **That is the curve** you need before setting a per-replica concurrency limit.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, statistics, time\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\nclient = AsyncOpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")\nMODEL, PROMPT = \"my-model\", \"Explain how a KV cache works in detail.\"\n\nasync def one(max_tokens=256):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); t_first = None; n = 0\n    stream = await client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=MODEL, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": PROMPT}], max_tokens=max_tokens,\n        stream=True, stream_options={\"include_usage\": True})\n    async for ch in stream:\n        if ch.usage:                      # authoritative count, sent in the final chunk\n            n = ch.usage.completion_tokens\n        if ch.choices and ch.choices[0].delta.content and t_first is None:\n            t_first = time.perf_counter()\n    return t_first - t0, n, time.perf_counter() - t0\n\nasync def level(c: int, per_worker: int = 4):\n    res = []\n    async def worker():                    # closed loop: send the next request when one finishes\n        for _ in range(per_worker):\n            res.append(await one())\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    await asyncio.gather(*(worker() for _ in range(c)))\n    wall = time.perf_counter() - t0\n    out = sum(n for _, n, _ in res)                                   # total output tokens / wall time\n    per_stream = [n / (total - ttft) for ttft, n, total in res if n > 1]\n    ttfts = sorted(ttft for ttft, _, _ in res)\n    print(f\"c={c:3d}  aggregate={out / wall:7.0f} tok/s  per-stream p50={statistics.median(per_stream):5.0f} tok/s\"\n          f\"  TTFT p50={ttfts[len(ttfts)//2]*1e3:5.0f} ms  p95={ttfts[int(len(ttfts)*.95)]*1e3:5.0f} ms\")\n\nasync def main():\n    await one()                                                       # warm-up\n    for c in (1, 4, 16, 64):\n        await level(c)\nasyncio.run(main())\n# Typical shape: aggregate climbs steeply then flattens; per-stream falls gently then faster;\n# TTFT p95 jumps when the engine starts queueing. Pick the largest c that still meets your SLOs.\n# A closed loop never offers more than c requests, so rerun open-loop before trusting the limit.\n```\n\n### From a benchmark result to replicas and price\nRunnable arithmetic: Little's law for aggregate output, then replicas and cost per million. Inputs are\nillustrative; replace them with your measured TTFT, TPOT and traffic.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef aggregate_tok_s(concurrency, n_out, ttft_s, tpot_s):\n    return concurrency * n_out / (ttft_s + (n_out - 1) * tpot_s)\n\ndef cost_per_million(gpu_hourly, agg_tok_s):\n    return gpu_hourly / (agg_tok_s * 3600) * 1e6\n\nagg = aggregate_tok_s(64, 1000, 0.5, 0.025)        # ~2,512 output tok/s per replica\npeak_demand = 20_000                                # output tok/s needed at peak (assumed)\nprint(f\"aggregate {agg:,.0f} tok/s, replicas {math.ceil(peak_demand / agg)}\")\nprint(f\"${cost_per_million(3.0, agg):.2f} per million at c=64\")\nprint(f\"${cost_per_million(3.0, 200):.2f} per million at c=1 (200 tok/s)\")\n# aggregate 2,512 tok/s, replicas 8\n# $0.33 per million at c=64\n# $4.17 per million at c=1 (200 tok/s)\n```","patterns":["**Report cost and time per agent run.** Sum input and output tokens per run from usage fields and convert to dollars and seconds per completed task, with failed runs counted. Monitor both per agent version, since a prompt change can double input tokens without changing output.","**Load-test with agent-shaped traffic.** Replay real agent traces (long prompts, short outputs, sequential calls per run) instead of chat-shaped prompts when benchmarking a replica. Monitor input tokens/s and TTFT next to output tokens/s.","**Benchmark with your length mix, open-loop.** Run the engine's serving benchmark (vLLM's ships with it; `vllm bench serve` in recent versions) with your real prompt and output length distribution and a fixed request rate, not only fixed concurrency. Record TTFT and TPOT percentiles next to tokens/s.","**Publish goodput, not peak.** Report capacity as output tokens/s (or requests/s) served within the TTFT and TPOT SLO at the concurrency limit you will deploy. Monitor goodput per replica in production against that number.","**Count from usage fields.** Read `usage.completion_tokens` (with the include-usage stream option on OpenAI-style APIs) rather than chunk counts. Monitor drift between your token counts and the provider's billing.","**Price from the chosen operating point.** Compute cost per million output tokens from aggregate tokens/s at the deployed concurrency, plus idle and redundancy overhead. Monitor GPU-hours per million output tokens weekly.","**Compare models by task time, not tokens/s.** For model selection, measure characters per second or seconds to complete a fixed task set, because tokenizers differ. Monitor end-to-end task latency."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent calls are prompt-heavy and output-light: a 10-step run might send\n  80k input tokens and emit 1.5k output tokens. Output tokens/s alone hides the prefill load that\n  actually fills your GPUs.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider usage fields (`usage.completion_tokens` and input-token\n  counts) in agent traces, vendor speed claims when picking the agent's model, and `vllm bench serve`\n  when sizing self-hosted replicas.\n- **What you control.** Which model serves which step, concurrency on your own replicas, and how many\n  input tokens each step re-sends (prefix caching and trimmed tool results).\n- **What you can skip.** Closed- versus open-loop benchmark design, if you only call hosted APIs; you\n  still need per-stream versus aggregate and the tokenizer caveat.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Choosing the agent's model.** Teams convert vendor speed claims into seconds per completed task on\n  their own prompts, not on a generic benchmark.\n- **Sizing self-hosted replicas.** Aggregate tokens/s at the target latency, measured with prompt-heavy\n  agent traffic, sets how many replicas a launch needs.\n- **Cost per successful task.** Input and output token counts from usage fields, priced and divided by\n  successful runs, are the number finance and product teams compare.\n- **Comparing models with different tokenizers.** Teams normalise to characters or tasks so a tokenizer\n  that splits text finer does not look faster.","mini_project":"**Turn tokens/s into seconds and dollars per task (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** compare two models for one agent by time and cost per completed task, not raw tokens/s.\n- **Steps:** run the same small tool-calling agent on 10 tasks with each model; log input tokens, output\n  tokens, wall time and success per run from usage fields; compute per-stream output tokens/s, seconds\n  per task and cost per successful task using the provider's published prices.\n- **Done when:** you have one table per model with all three numbers and a short note on whether the\n  faster tokens/s model also wins per task."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A vendor's tokens/s tells me how fast my users will see text.\"** It is usually aggregate output, or even input plus output, at high concurrency. A user sees the per-stream rate, about `1 / TPOT`, which falls as concurrency rises.","**\"Single-stream speed is enough to size a cluster.\"** One replica at concurrency 64 can produce over ten times the output of concurrency 1, so sizing from a concurrency-1 benchmark overshoots replicas and cost by about that factor.","**\"Output tokens/s is the load my agents put on the GPU.\"** Agent steps re-send long transcripts and emit short outputs; in the worked example input is 53× output, so uncached prefill is often the binding number.","**\"Higher tokens/s means the faster model.\"** A coarser tokenizer emits fewer tokens for the same text, so compare characters, words or seconds per completed task, not raw tokens/s.","**\"Throughput at the plateau is my capacity.\"** Tokens that arrive after the TTFT or TPOT SLO do not count; the sellable figure is goodput at the concurrency where the curves still meet the SLO."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Quoted tokens per second\"] --> T{\"Which tokens?\"}\n    T -->|\"input plus output\"| F[\"Flattering total, prefill dominated\"]\n    T -->|\"output only\"| S{\"One stream or whole server?\"}\n    S -->|\"one stream\"| P[\"Per-stream, about 1 over TPOT\"]\n    S -->|\"whole server\"| A[\"Aggregate at stated concurrency\"]\n    A --> L[\"Pin lengths, hardware, tokenizer\"]\n    L --> G[\"Goodput within SLO\"]\n    G --> C[\"Cost per million output tokens\"]","caption":"Before trusting any tokens/s figure for your agent's model, walk it down this path; each question\nchanges the number, and only the last two predict capacity and cost per agent run."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between per-stream and aggregate tokens per second?","a":"- **Per-stream** is one request's decode rate after its first token, about 1 / TPOT; it is what a user\n  perceives.\n- **Aggregate** is all output tokens a replica produces per second across concurrent requests, roughly\n  concurrency × per-stream rate; it sets cost per token.\n- **They diverge with concurrency** because the weight read is shared: aggregate rises while each\n  stream slows."},{"q":"You need to size a fleet for launch. How do you benchmark?","a":"- **Open-loop at realistic lengths:** fixed arrival rates with the production prompt and output mix,\n  after warm-up, counting tokens from usage.\n- **Find goodput:** the highest rate where TTFT and TPOT percentiles meet the SLO, including failures\n  and timeouts.\n- **Divide peak demand by goodput per replica**, then add headroom for redundancy and bursts."},{"q":"How would you size self-hosted capacity for an agent product?","a":"- **Start from runs:** runs per minute × steps × input and output tokens per step gives input and output\n  tokens/s needed.\n- **Check both terms:** agent traffic can need tens of times more input tokens/s than output, so a\n  replica that has output headroom may still be prefill-saturated.\n- **Benchmark with replayed traces** and take goodput at the TTFT and TPOT SLO, then add headroom."},{"q":"What does batching do to cost per token? Give numbers.","a":"- **Formula:** GPU hourly cost / (aggregate output tokens/s × 3600) × 1e6.\n- **Illustrative:** at an assumed $3 per GPU-hour, 3,000 tokens/s is about $0.28 per million; the same\n  GPU at concurrency 1 and 200 tokens/s is $4.17 per million.\n- **So** the concurrency limit is a pricing decision, constrained by the latency SLO."},{"q":"You are choosing between two models for an agent. Model A posts higher tokens/s. Is it faster?","a":"- **Not necessarily.** Agent steps are short outputs on long prompts, so TTFT and input handling can\n  matter more than decode rate.\n- **Tokenizers differ,** so higher tokens/s may mean more tokens for the same text.\n- **Measure the agent:** run your eval task set on both and compare seconds, dollars and success rate\n  per completed run."},{"q":"When is a tokens-per-second comparison between two models misleading?","a":"- **Different tokenizers:** a coarser vocabulary emits fewer tokens for the same text, so the slower\n  model can post the higher figure.\n- **Different conditions:** concurrency, lengths, precision or input-plus-output counting.\n- **Use instead** characters or words per second, or task completion time and cost at the SLO."}],"tradeoffs":["**Aggregate vs per-stream.** Raising concurrency raises aggregate tokens/s and lowers cost per token, but lowers per-stream speed. The right point flips from \"maximise aggregate\" to \"cap concurrency\" the moment a per-stream or TTFT SLO exists.","**Total vs output tokens/s.** Quoting total (input + output) gives a bigger, more flattering number that is fair for prefill-heavy jobs like summarisation, and misleading for chat where output dominates the cost.","**Peak vs goodput.** Comparing engines on peak throughput is cheap to run but rewards configurations that queue aggressively; goodput at your SLO is harder to measure and the only figure that predicts capacity.","**Speculative decoding by concurrency.** It raises per-stream tokens/s at low concurrency but can lower aggregate at high concurrency, so a benchmark at one setting can reverse at the other.","**Short vs long context.** Long-context workloads cut both numbers because KV reads grow and fewer sequences fit; a model that wins at 1k context can lose at 32k."],"pitfalls":["**Comparing tokens/s across different tokenizers.** Symptom: the \"faster\" model takes longer to finish the same paragraph. Fix: compare characters per second or task time.","**Benchmarking at concurrency 1 and dividing a fleet by that.** Symptom: capacity wildly under- or over-provisioned depending on which number was used. Fix: size from goodput at the deployed concurrency.","**Counting streamed chunks as tokens.** Symptom: tokens/s that disagrees with usage and with the bill, since servers coalesce or split tokens per event. Fix: read the usage fields.","**Closed-loop load tests only.** Symptom: a capacity estimate that collapses in production when arrivals burst and queues form. Fix: add an open-loop run at fixed arrival rates.","**Ignoring prompt tokens in capacity math.** Symptom: a RAG workload saturating prefill compute while the output tokens/s dashboard says the GPU has headroom. Fix: track input tokens/s alongside output.","**Averaging per-request tokens/s.** Symptom: an \"aggregate\" figure that short requests skew and that no capacity model can reproduce. Fix: divide total output tokens by wall time."],"when":"- **Use for capacity or cost questions:** how many replicas for a launch, what a GPU-hour yields,\n  whether a quantised model pays for itself, whether a vendor's claim covers your workload.\n- **Always state which of the four numbers** you mean and the conditions behind it.\n- **Use TPOT and TTFT instead** when the question is how one request feels.\n- **Use per-run time and cost** when the question is how an agent performs; tokens/s is an input to it.\n- **Day to day** it appears in capacity reviews, vendor comparisons and pricing models.\n- **Interviews:** refuse to answer \"how many tokens/s does it do\" until concurrency, lengths, hardware\n  and which tokens are specified, then connect the number to cost per million tokens.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the load harness, reads usage rather than chunk counts, and produces the concurrency-versus-throughput curve for each model and hardware pairing.","architect":"Converts aggregate tokens/s at the SLO into replica counts and cost per token, and rejects vendor numbers that omit concurrency and lengths.","fde":"Re-runs the benchmark on the customer's hardware and prompt mix, because their tokenizer, context lengths and traffic pattern invalidate the reference numbers."},"check":{"q":"A vendor claims their endpoint delivers 1,200 tokens per second. What questions do you ask\nbefore deciding whether it will serve your product, and why does each one matter?","a":"Which tokens: output only, or input plus output, since prefill inflates totals? At what\nconcurrency: one stream or the whole deployment, and is 1,200 what one user sees or what a GPU\nproduces? With what prompt and output lengths, because long contexts shrink both numbers and short\noutputs raise scheduling overhead? On which hardware and precision, so I can compare to a bandwidth\nfloor? Closed- or open-loop, and what were TTFT and TPOT percentiles at that rate, since throughput\nwithout an SLO is not capacity? Which tokenizer, since I will compare to my current model by words\nor characters, not tokens? Finally, is it sustained or peak, and does it include failures? The answers\nturn a slogan into a point on a curve I can place my workload on."},"quiz":[{"q":"A replica produces 4,000 output tokens/s in aggregate at a steady concurrency of 64. Roughly what does each user see?","options":["About 4,000 tokens/s, since every stream runs at full speed","About 60 tokens/s, because the aggregate is shared across the concurrent streams","About 256,000 tokens/s, the product of the two numbers","It cannot be estimated without the TTFT distribution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In steady state aggregate ≈ concurrency × per-stream rate, so 4,000 / 64 ≈ 62 tokens/s per user. TTFT affects when a stream starts, not its decode rate once running."},{"q":"Which comparison between two models is NOT apples-to-apples?","options":["Aggregate output tokens/s on the same GPU at the same concurrency and lengths","Output tokens/s when the two models use different tokenizers","Characters of output per second on the same prompts","Cost per million output tokens at the SLO on the same hardware"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tokenizer with a coarser vocabulary emits fewer tokens for the same text, so a slower model can post a higher tokens/s figure. Characters or words per second, or cost at the SLO, remove that distortion."},{"q":"Which single change increases a replica's aggregate output tokens/s while lowering each user's per-stream rate?","options":["Weight-only int4 quantisation of the model","Moving to a GPU with higher memory bandwidth","Raising the maximum number of concurrent sequences","Enabling speculative decoding at batch size 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Higher concurrency shares each weight read among more sequences, raising total output while each step takes slightly longer. Quantisation and more bandwidth raise both numbers, and speculative decoding at batch 1 raises per-stream speed."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","sources":["Yu et al., 2022 — Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models","Agrawal et al., 2024 — Taming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve","Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW"],"why":"- **A GPU at concurrency 1 is mostly idle.** It streams 16 GB of weights through its memory system to\n  produce a single token, then does it again. Producing 32 tokens per pass instead costs almost nothing\n  extra, which is why continuous batching exists and why self-hosting is affordable at all.\n- **The same arithmetic makes 32 users share one pipeline.** Each step is a little slower, new arrivals\n  wait for a slot, and under bursty traffic the wait, not the step, becomes the latency.\n- **Not choosing gets the worst of both.**\n  - Run at high utilisation for low cost per token and p99 explodes, because queueing delay grows\n    without bound as utilisation approaches 100 %.\n  - Run at low concurrency for tight latency and you pay for GPUs that are 80 % idle.\n- **\"Autoscale on GPU utilisation\" does not resolve it.** The usual metric (what `nvidia-smi` reports)\n  is the fraction of time any kernel is running, not compute in use, so a decode loop at batch 4 and one\n  at batch 200 can both read near 100 %. It says the GPU is busy, not how much headroom remains or how\n  long the queue is.\n- **Providers price the trade-off explicitly.** Interactive endpoints are tuned for latency and cost\n  more; batch endpoints run at the throughput end with multi-hour completion windows at roughly half\n  the interactive price at the time of writing. Self-hosting, you make that choice per workload.","how":"### The shape of the curve\n- **Throughput** (aggregate output tokens/s) against concurrency rises almost linearly, bends, and\n  plateaus.\n- **Per-request latency** (TPOT, and TTFT once queueing starts) stays nearly flat, turns upward at a\n  knee, then climbs steeply.\n- **The decode-phase mechanism** is a single line:\n\n```\nstep_time ≈ (weight_bytes + B × kv_bytes_per_seq) / bandwidth        (bandwidth-bound regime)\nthroughput ≈ B / step_time          per-request TPOT ≈ step_time\n```\n\n1. **While `weight_bytes` dominates**, *B* can grow with little effect on step time, so throughput\n   scales with *B*.\n2. **As `B × kv_bytes` catches up** (long contexts do this quickly), each new sequence costs every other\n   one real latency.\n3. **Eventually** arithmetic overtakes bandwidth and the step becomes compute-bound, or KV memory fills\n   and the engine can admit no one. From there additional load only lengthens the queue.\n\n### Queueing sits on top\n- **Arrivals are random even below the knee.** Little's law: average requests in the system equal\n  arrival rate times average time in system (`L = λW`).\n- **Time in system grows like `service_time / (1 − ρ)`** for a simple single-server queue, where ρ is\n  utilisation.\n\n| Utilisation ρ | Time in system vs service time |\n|---|---|\n| 0.5 | 2× |\n| 0.9 | 10× |\n\n- **LLM service times are long and highly variable** (output length is unknown in advance), which makes\n  the tail worse than the simple model predicts.\n- **So** interactive services target moderate utilisation, and TTFT p99 is the first thing to break\n  under load.\n\n### Choosing the operating point\n1. **Write the SLO as percentiles**, for example TTFT p95 ≤ 1 s and TPOT p95 ≤ 50 ms for chat.\n2. **Run a load curve** at realistic prompt and output lengths and find the largest concurrency at which\n   both hold. That is the replica's *goodput* capacity.\n3. **Set the engine's per-replica concurrency limit** (`max_num_seqs` in vLLM) and token budget there,\n   with headroom for bursts.\n4. **Shed or queue beyond it visibly:** a fast 429 with `Retry-After`, or a queue position, instead of a\n   silent 10-second TTFT.\n5. **Autoscale on queue depth or TTFT**, not on GPU utilisation.\n6. **Separate pools:** interactive traffic at the knee, batch and background jobs at the plateau where\n   each token is cheapest and nobody is waiting.\n\n### Techniques move the curve; they do not remove it\n| Technique | What it shifts |\n|---|---|\n| Continuous batching | Sequences join and leave every step instead of waiting for the slowest |\n| PagedAttention | Stops KV fragmentation, so more sequences fit before memory binds |\n| Chunked prefill | Bounds prefill work per step, so new arrivals do not stall running streams |\n| Disaggregated prefill and decode | Puts the phases on different GPUs, so one workload's TTFT stops competing with another's TPOT |\n| Quantisation | Shrinks bytes per step and KV footprint |\n| Speculative decoding | Lowers latency at low concurrency but spends compute the plateau needs, so it can reduce peak throughput |\n\nEach shifts the knee rightward or the floor downward; none lets you run at 100 % utilisation with flat\nlatency.\n\n### If you do not run the engine\n- **You still own the trade-off.** You do not set the batch size, but you choose between interactive\n  and batch tiers, how many requests to keep in flight per key, which timeouts to accept, and whether\n  to pay for provisioned or dedicated capacity when tail latency matters.\n- **The provider's rate limits are**, in effect, the operating point they chose for you.\n\n### Agents on the curve\n- **Sequential steps multiply latency.** Run time ≈ steps × (queue wait + TTFT + decode) + tool time, so\n  operating near the knee costs an agent several times what it costs a single chat turn.\n- **Agents generate their own load.** Parallel tool calls, sub-agents and retries put several requests\n  in flight per user, so concurrency rises faster than user count; cap in-flight calls per run.\n- **Two traffic classes, two points.** Interactive agents sit at the knee; background agents, evals and\n  trace backfills belong at the plateau.\n- **Batch tiers fit single calls, not loops.** A provider batch API returns results within hours, so it\n  suits one-shot work (LLM-judge grading of finished trajectories, re-scoring traces); a multi-step run\n  would wait a batch window per step, so run whole agent loops on a high-concurrency pool instead.","example":"### Worked scenario: what a latency target costs in throughput\nOne replica, using the illustrative bandwidth model from the TPOT lesson: an 8B fp16 model (16 GB of\nweights, 128 KB of KV cache per token) on an 80 GB GPU with about 3.35 TB/s of memory bandwidth. Every\nrequest has 4k tokens of context, so each sequence holds about 0.5 GB of KV cache.\n- **Memory caps concurrency first.** After weights and a few GB of working space, roughly 60 GB remain,\n  so at most about 120 sequences can be resident.\n- **The model.** Bytes per step at *B* sequences are `16 GB + B × 0.5 GB`, so TPOT ≈ `(16 + 0.5B) / 3350`\n  seconds and throughput ≈ `B / TPOT`.\n\n| TPOT SLO | Bandwidth limit on *B* | Binding limit | Step time | Throughput |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ≤ 50 ms (chat) | `16 + 0.5B ≤ 167 GB`, *B* up to about 300 | Memory, ~120 | `(16 + 60) / 3350 ≈ 23 ms` | about 120 / 0.023 ≈ 5,300 tokens/s |\n| ≤ 15 ms (code completion) | `16 + 0.5B ≤ 50 GB`, *B* ≤ 68 | Latency | 15 ms | about 68 / 0.015 ≈ 4,500 tokens/s |\n| ≤ 10 ms | *B* ≤ 35 | Latency | 10 ms | about 3,500 tokens/s |\n\n- **At 50 ms, latency is not the limit**: the step is comfortably inside the SLO, so run the replica full.\n- **At 10 ms, the same hardware delivers about a third fewer tokens per hour**, hence roughly 50 % higher\n  cost per token, purely because of the latency target.\n- **That is the trade-off as a budget line**, before any queueing delay is added; real measurements will\n  sit below every figure here.\n\n```python\nBW_GBPS, WEIGHTS_GB, KV_GB_PER_SEQ, MEM_CAP = 3350, 16, 0.5, 120\n\ndef best_batch(tpot_slo_s: float) -> int:\n    by_latency = int((tpot_slo_s * BW_GBPS - WEIGHTS_GB) / KV_GB_PER_SEQ)\n    return min(by_latency, MEM_CAP)\n\nfor slo in (0.050, 0.015, 0.010):\n    b = best_batch(slo)\n    step = (WEIGHTS_GB + KV_GB_PER_SEQ * b) / BW_GBPS\n    print(f\"SLO {slo*1e3:4.0f} ms  B={b:3d}  step {step*1e3:4.1f} ms  {b/step:5.0f} tok/s\")\n# SLO   50 ms  B=120  step 22.7 ms   5289 tok/s\n# SLO   15 ms  B= 68  step 14.9 ms   4556 tok/s\n# SLO   10 ms  B= 35  step 10.0 ms   3500 tok/s\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: one replica, interactive versus background agents\nSame illustrative replica; an agent run emits 1,500 output tokens across 10 steps (decode only, no TTFT,\nqueue or tool time).\n\n| Operating point | Step time (TPOT) | Decode time per run | Replica throughput | Runs per replica-hour |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Full replica, B ≈ 120 | ~23 ms | 1,500 × 23 ms ≈ 34.5 s | ~5,300 tok/s | ~12,700 |\n| Latency-capped, B ≈ 35 | 10 ms | 1,500 × 10 ms = 15 s | ~3,500 tok/s | ~8,400 |\n\n- **An interactive agent** where a user waits wants the second row: less than half the decode time per\n  run, at about a third fewer runs per hour.\n- **A nightly research or eval agent** wants the first row, because only runs per dollar matter.\n\n### Why the tail explodes near saturation\nRunnable: a single-server FIFO queue with random arrivals and service times (Lindley's recursion),\ncompared with the `service_time / (1 − ρ)` formula.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef mean_time_in_system(rho, service=1.0, n=200_000, seed=0):\n    rnd = random.Random(seed)\n    wait = total = 0.0\n    for _ in range(n):\n        s = rnd.expovariate(1 / service)          # this request's service time\n        total += wait + s\n        gap = rnd.expovariate(rho / service)      # time until the next arrival\n        wait = max(0.0, wait + s - gap)           # next request's queueing delay\n    return total / n\n\nfor rho in (0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95):\n    print(f\"rho={rho:.2f}  simulated {mean_time_in_system(rho):5.1f}x  formula {1/(1-rho):5.1f}x\")\n# rho=0.50 ~2x, 0.70 ~3.3x, 0.90 ~10x, 0.95 ~20x the service time\n```","patterns":["**Split agent traffic by who is waiting.** Route user-facing agent runs to the latency pool or interactive tier, multi-step eval sweeps and background agents to a high-concurrency pool, and single-call work such as trace re-scoring to a provider batch API. Monitor interactive per-step TTFT while background jobs run.","**Cap in-flight calls per agent run.** Limit parallel tool calls and sub-agents per run with a semaphore or the framework's concurrency setting, so one run cannot fill a replica. Monitor in-flight requests per run and per tenant.","**Concurrency cap from the load curve.** Set vLLM `--max-num-seqs` (and the per-step token budget `--max-num-batched-tokens`) at the largest concurrency that met the SLO, minus burst headroom. Monitor TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 against running sequences.","**Shed at the edge with `Retry-After`.** When a replica's queue exceeds what fits in the first-token budget, return 429 with `Retry-After` from the gateway instead of queueing silently. Monitor shed rate per tenant and the share of admitted requests inside the SLO.","**Autoscale on queue depth.** Feed the engine's waiting-request gauge (vLLM `vllm:num_requests_waiting`) into the autoscaler, for example a Kubernetes HPA on a custom metric or a KEDA Prometheus trigger. Monitor queue depth and scale-out lag.","**Separate interactive and batch pools.** Run interactive traffic on replicas held at the knee and send offline work to a pool run at the plateau, or to a provider batch API (OpenAI Batch, Anthropic Message Batches). Monitor interactive TTFT during batch job start times.","**Measure goodput per replica.** Track tokens or requests served within SLO per GPU-hour as the capacity KPI. Monitor it next to cost per token in capacity reviews."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A 10-step agent pays queue wait and step time ten times in a row, so a\n  latency regression that is mild for chat is multiplied for agents. Background agents and eval runs are\n  the opposite case: nobody waits, so run them at the throughput end.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider interactive versus batch tiers (OpenAI Batch, Anthropic\n  Message Batches), rate limits per API key, your agent framework's max parallel tool calls or\n  sub-agents, and vLLM `--max-num-seqs` when self-hosting.\n- **What you control.** How many calls each agent keeps in flight, which traffic goes to a batch tier,\n  per-step timeouts, and admission limits in front of your own replicas.\n- **What you can skip.** Deriving the M/M/1 formula; remember ρ = 0.9 means about 10× service time and\n  that the tail breaks first.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Interactive versus background agents.** Teams route user-facing runs to a low-latency path, nightly\n  or bulk agent loops to a high-concurrency replica, and one-shot grading calls to a batch tier.\n- **Eval and data-generation runs.** Thousands of agent runs where nobody waits are run at the throughput\n  end to cut cost and finish overnight.\n- **Parallel tools and sub-agents.** Fan-out width per run is a throughput choice that raises queue wait\n  for every other user on the same capacity.\n- **Admission control on shared replicas.** Concurrency caps keep the latency SLO for interactive agents\n  while the rest of the load queues or sheds.","mini_project":"**Find the knee for an agent workload (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** chart run latency against throughput for one agent and pick an operating point.\n- **Steps:** replay the same 10-step agent task at increasing concurrency against a self-hosted model (a\n  hosted API usually shows rate-limit errors before it shows the knee); record run latency p50 and p99 and completed runs per minute at\n  each level; mark the point where p99 starts to climb steeply.\n- **Done when:** you have a latency-versus-throughput chart with the knee marked, and a one-paragraph\n  recommendation for the interactive cap and the background setting."},"typed":{"type":"decision","scenario":"A support-automation company self-hosts an 8B agent model for two workloads on the same illustrative\nreplica as the worked example below (80 GB GPU, ~3.35 TB/s, 4k-token contexts).\n- **Interactive support agents.** Users wait on 10-step runs of about 1,500 output tokens. Daytime peak\n  is 12,000 output tok/s; overnight it falls to about 1,500 tok/s.\n- **Nightly eval and back-office agents.** 400M output tokens per night, finished inside an 8-hour window\n  (28,800 s), so about 13,889 tok/s on average.\n- **Constraints.**\n  - *Latency:* interactive TPOT p95 ≤ 15 ms, so decode per run stays near 22 s.\n  - *Budget:* ≤ $300 per day, at an assumed $3 per GPU-hour, so 4 GPUs ($288 per day).\n  - *Data residency:* traces may not leave the company's cloud account, which rules out a provider\n    batch API.\n  - *Team and deadline:* two platform engineers who already run Kubernetes; six weeks.\n- **Capacity per replica** (from the bandwidth model below): capped at B = 68, step ~14.9 ms and about\n  4,556 tok/s; full at B ≈ 120, step ~22.7 ms and about 5,289 tok/s. Interactive replicas are planned at\n  75 % of capped capacity (about 3,417 tok/s) for burst headroom.","options":"| Option | How it works | Latency SLO | Night throughput | Budget |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **1. Shared pool, run full** | 4 replicas at B ≈ 120 all day | Fails: ~23 ms TPOT, ~34 s decode per run | 21,156 tok/s, ample | 4 GPUs, $288 |\n| **2. Shared pool, capped** | 4 replicas at B = 68; batch jobs fill spare slots at night | Step time meets 15 ms, but at night (ρ ≈ 0.84) batch requests hold the slots and interactive runs queue for admission | 18,224 − 1,500 = 16,724 tok/s, done in ~6.6 h | 4 GPUs, $288 |\n| **3. Dedicated pools** | 4 capped interactive + 3 full batch replicas, always on | Meets it, isolated | 15,867 tok/s, done in ~7.0 h | 7 GPUs, $504: over budget |\n| **4. Time-split pools** | Day: 4 capped interactive. Night: 1 capped interactive + 3 replicas relaunched at full batch | Meets it; night interactive load is 1,500 of 3,417 tok/s on its own replica | 15,867 tok/s, done in ~7.0 h | 4 GPUs, $288 |","decision":"**Option 4, time-split pools.**\n- **Latency** rules out option 1, and the queueing argument rules out option 2: even with a fast step,\n  a slot held by a batch request lands in interactive TTFT, and that wait is paid on every agent step.\n- **Budget** rules out option 3; option 4 buys the same isolation with the same 4 GPUs by moving three\n  replicas between operating points twice a day.\n- **Day sizing holds.** 12,000 ÷ 3,417 ≈ 3.5, so 4 capped replicas run at ρ ≈ 0.66.\n- **Night sizing holds.** 3 × 5,289 = 15,867 tok/s finishes 400M tokens in about 7.0 h, leaving about an\n  hour of slack in the window.\n- **Team fit.** Two Kubernetes deployments with different `--max-num-seqs` values, scaled on a schedule,\n  are within a two-person team's six weeks.\n- **What would change it.**\n  - *Residency relaxed:* send the nightly work to a provider batch tier (roughly half the interactive\n    price at the time of writing) and shrink the fleet.\n  - *Nightly volume above ~457M tokens* (15,867 × 28,800) or a shorter window: add a fourth batch GPU.\n  - *Night interactive load above ~3,400 tok/s:* keep two interactive replicas at night.\n  - *Engine priority scheduling that load tests show protects interactive TTFT:* option 2 becomes viable\n    and drops the twice-daily switch.","tradeoffs":"- **Two switchovers a day.** Relaunching replicas drains in-flight work and costs warm-up time. Watch\n  switchover duration and batch requests failed during the drain.\n- **Thin night margin.** About one hour of slack: watch nightly completion time and tokens per night,\n  and alert when the projected finish passes the window.\n- **Lower daytime efficiency.** Capped replicas give about 14 % fewer tokens per GPU than running full\n  (4,556 vs 5,289). Watch goodput per GPU-hour in capacity reviews.\n- **One interactive replica at night.** No redundancy for night users; watch night TTFT p95 and replica\n  restarts, and fail over to a batch replica if it goes down.","rubric":"- **Constraints first.** The write-up states the latency SLO, budget, residency rule and window as\n  numbers before naming any option.\n- **Sizing is shown.** Replica counts come from per-replica capacity at the SLO-capped batch, with\n  headroom stated, and every figure can be recomputed from the inputs.\n- **Queueing is addressed.** It explains where interactive requests wait (admission, not only step time)\n  and why utilisation near 1 is acceptable for batch but not for interactive traffic.\n- **Each rejected option names its failing constraint**, not a vague preference.\n- **Reversal triggers are measurable**, such as a token volume, a load level or a test result.\n- **Monitoring is named** for every accepted trade-off: the metric and the threshold that would trigger\n  a change."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"SLO as percentiles\"] --> L[\"Load curve at real lengths\"]\n    L --> K[\"Largest concurrency meeting SLO\"]\n    K --> C[\"Set per-replica limit\"]\n    C --> R{\"Request arrives\"}\n    R -->|\"under limit\"| A[\"Admit to batch\"]\n    R -->|\"over limit\"| X[\"Fast 429 or visible queue\"]\n    R -->|\"background agents, evals\"| B[\"Throughput pool at plateau\"]\n    X -.->|\"queue depth\"| H[\"Autoscale replicas\"]","caption":"How an operating point is chosen and enforced: the SLO picks the concurrency, and everything above that\nlimit is shed, queued visibly or sent to a cheaper pool, which is where background agents and evals go."},"interview":[{"q":"How would you choose the concurrency limit for a new chat deployment?","a":"- **Start from the SLO** as percentiles, such as TTFT p95 ≤ 1 s and TPOT p95 ≤ 50 ms.\n- **Run an open-loop load curve** at production prompt and output lengths, and take the largest\n  concurrency at which both percentiles hold, minus burst headroom.\n- **Enforce it** with the engine's max-sequences setting, shed the excess with 429s, and autoscale on\n  queue depth."},{"q":"Your agent runs got 3× slower after traffic doubled, but single chat calls only got slightly slower. Why?","a":"- **Agents pay the queue every step.** Near the knee, queue wait and step time rise per call; a 10-step\n  agent pays that increase ten times sequentially.\n- **Agents add their own load:** parallel tool calls and retries push concurrency up faster than users.\n- **Fix:** cap per-replica concurrency below the knee, cap in-flight calls per run, autoscale on queue\n  depth, and move background agents off the interactive pool."},{"q":"How do you run a nightly eval of 10,000 agent trajectories without hurting live users?","a":"- **Separate the traffic:** run the agent loops on a separate pool at the plateau, not the interactive\n  replicas; a batch API suits only the single-call parts, such as LLM-judge grading of finished runs,\n  because each agent step would otherwise wait a batch window.\n- **Throughput is the goal:** high concurrency per replica, because nobody waits on a single run.\n- **Watch the live pool's** TTFT p95 during the run as the guardrail."},{"q":"When would you deliberately run a replica at low utilisation?","a":"- **Interactive tiers with strict tails:** at ρ = 0.9 time in system is about ten times service time, so\n  a voice or completion product needs headroom.\n- **Not for offline work:** evaluation runs and backfills should run at the plateau, ideally on a\n  separate pool or a provider batch tier at roughly half price."},{"q":"What does tightening TPOT from 50 ms to 10 ms cost on one replica?","a":"- **In the illustrative 8B example**, memory caps the batch at about 120 at 50 ms, for about 5,300 tokens/s.\n- **At 10 ms** latency binds at a batch of about 35, about 3,500 tokens/s: a third fewer tokens and\n  roughly 50 % higher cost per token.\n- **So** a latency target is a line in the infrastructure budget, and should be negotiated as one."},{"q":"GPU utilisation reads 100 % at both light and heavy load. Why, and what do you scale on instead?","a":"- **The metric is time-busy**, the fraction of time any kernel runs, not the fraction of compute used,\n  so batch 4 and batch 200 decode loops both look saturated.\n- **Scale on** engine queue depth or TTFT percentiles, which directly measure the wait users feel."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cost per token vs per-request latency.** Higher concurrency lowers cost per token and raises per-request latency; the balance flips from \"fill the GPU\" to \"cap the batch\" the moment a per-request SLO exists for that traffic.","**Flat tails vs wasted hardware.** Low utilisation buys flat tail latency and wastes hardware; right for interactive tiers, wrong for offline jobs that can wait hours.","**Speculative decoding by pool.** It improves latency when the GPU has spare compute (low concurrency) and can cut peak throughput when it does not, so it belongs on the interactive pool, not the batch pool.","**Shared vs separate pools.** Sharing one pool maximises utilisation but lets batch arrivals push interactive TTFT past the knee; separate pools cost headroom and buy predictability.","**Shedding vs queueing.** Fast 429s protect the latency of admitted requests at the cost of failed ones; queueing admits everyone and lets latency drift, which is better only when callers can wait."],"pitfalls":["**Autoscaling on GPU utilisation.** Symptom: a metric pinned near 100 % at both light and heavy load, so replicas scale out late or never while users wait through a queue. Fix: scale on queue depth.","**Closed-loop throughput benchmarks with no SLO.** Symptom: a \"capacity\" figure production never reaches without blowing p99. Fix: report goodput from an open-loop run.","**Sizing from mean latency.** Symptom: on-call pages for p99 while the dashboard average looks fine, since queueing lives entirely in the tail. Fix: size against p95 and p99.","**One batch job on the interactive pool.** Symptom: TTFT spikes at the exact minute a cron job starts. Fix: a separate pool or a provider batch API.","**Raising `max_num_seqs` without checking KV memory.** Symptom: preemptions and recompute stalls that lower throughput and raise latency at once. Fix: check KV occupancy and the preemption counter first.","**Treating speculative decoding as free throughput.** Symptom: lower aggregate tokens/s at peak than without it. Fix: keep it on the low-concurrency pool."],"when":"- **Every time** you configure a replica, choose a provider tier, or set a rate limit, you are making\n  this decision.\n- **Latency end** whenever a person or latency-sensitive agent is waiting: chat, completions, voice,\n  interactive tools, user-facing agent runs whose steps compound.\n- **Throughput end** for evaluation runs, bulk extraction, embeddings backfills, nightly summaries and\n  anything with a deadline measured in hours.\n- **Measure** throughput, TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 against concurrency for each model and hardware pairing,\n  pick the point, and enforce it with limits and alerts on queue depth.\n- **Interviews:** draw the curve, explain why it bends (weights shared per step, then KV and compute\n  binding), add the queueing tail, and name the concurrency limit as the control.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the load curve, sets per-replica concurrency and token budgets, and wires 429 shedding and queue-depth metrics into the serving layer.","architect":"Owns the operating point per traffic class, decides on separate pools or tiers, and translates the SLO into replica counts and cost per token.","fde":"Negotiates a customer's latency target against their GPU budget with the curve in hand, often by moving their batch workloads off the interactive pool."},"check":{"q":"One replica reports 85 % GPU utilisation and p99 TTFT of 6 s. Finance will not approve more\nGPUs. What do you do, and in what order?","a":"First establish which constraint binds: check KV-memory occupancy, preemption counts and\nengine queue depth; 85 % utilisation in a bandwidth-bound decode loop says little about headroom.\nThen protect latency with a per-replica concurrency cap set from a load curve, shedding the excess\nwith fast 429s so admitted requests stay inside the SLO instead of everyone waiting 6 s. Next\nrecover capacity without hardware: enable prefix caching for the shared system prompt, turn on\nchunked prefill so long prompts stop stalling admission, cap `max_model_len` to what the product\nneeds so more sequences fit, and evaluate weight or KV quantisation. Move any batch or background\ntraffic to off-peak hours or a separate cheap pool. Finally measure goodput at the SLO and present\nfinance with cost per token at the required latency, which is the real request."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does raising the batch size increase throughput almost linearly at first?","options":["Each sequence gets its own copy of the weights in GPU memory","The weights are read from memory once per decode step and shared by all sequences in the batch","The GPU clock increases under heavier load","Continuous batching compresses the KV cache as the batch grows"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A decode step is bound by streaming the weights from memory, a cost paid once per step regardless of batch size. More sequences per step mean more tokens for nearly the same time until KV reads or compute start to dominate."},{"q":"Which statement about queueing delay in a loaded LLM service is correct?","options":["Average wait grows linearly with utilisation, so 90 % load means 90 % more delay","Average time in the system grows roughly like 1 / (1 − utilisation), so tail latency explodes as load nears saturation","Queueing only matters once GPU memory is completely full","Mean latency captures queueing well because waits are evenly distributed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The simple single-server result `W ≈ service_time / (1 − ρ)` already gives ten times the service time at ρ = 0.9, and LLM service times are long and variable, which makes the tail worse. The damage shows up in p95 and p99 long before memory is exhausted."},{"q":"Which technique improves per-request latency at low load but can reduce peak throughput at high load?","options":["PagedAttention","Prefix caching","Speculative decoding","Chunked prefill"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Speculative decoding spends extra compute drafting and verifying several tokens per step; that compute is spare at low concurrency but is exactly what a saturated GPU needs, so peak throughput can fall. PagedAttention, prefix caching and chunked prefill shift the curve without that penalty."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/timeouts","sources":["Dean and Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale","Official docs: httpx timeouts","Official docs: OpenAI Python SDK"],"why":"- **Legitimate durations vary by 100×.** Ordinary HTTP timeouts assume tens of milliseconds. An LLM\n  response at 20 ms per token takes 20 s for 1,000 tokens when healthy; a reasoning model can pause\n  before emitting anything; a request queued behind a burst can wait seconds before prefill starts.\n- **Both flat choices fail.** \"30 seconds and give up\" silently fails a large share of long outputs.\n  \"Ten minutes to be safe\" lets a dead upstream pin a worker, a connection-pool slot and a user for ten\n  minutes.\n- **The damage compounds into an overload spiral.** Hung requests hold slots, so healthy ones queue;\n  clients time out and retry, adding load to an already slow upstream; the retries time out too. LLM\n  backends are unusually prone to this because each request is expensive and long.\n- **Forgotten infrastructure has its own timers.** Reverse proxies and load balancers ship with idle\n  timeouts of about a minute, so a non-streaming generation that takes 70 s is cut off with a gateway\n  error while the engine keeps generating for nobody.\n- **\"Stream and never time out\" is not enough.** Streaming turns \"no bytes for N seconds\" into a\n  liveness signal, but you still need a total bound so a stuck stream cannot run to `max_tokens`, and\n  every downstream call in an agent step must know how much of the user's patience remains.","how":"### The timeout stack\nA single call crosses several layers, each with its own timer:\n\n| Layer | Default or behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| SDK | The OpenAI Python client defaults to a 10-minute timeout and two retries |\n| HTTP library under it | httpx splits a timeout into connect, read, write and pool phases, each defaulting to five seconds |\n| Your server's handler | Whatever you configured |\n| Proxy or load balancer | nginx's `proxy_read_timeout` and an AWS ALB's idle timeout both default to 60 s |\n| Provider's gateway | The provider's own limits |\n| Engine scheduler | May hold a request in its admission queue |\n\n- **The effective timeout is the *shortest* of these**, and the one that fires is rarely the one you\n  configured.\n- **Retries stack on top.** Because that SDK retries a timed-out request by default, its worst case is\n  three full timeouts, half an hour, before your code sees an error, unless you lower `timeout` or\n  `max_retries`.\n\n### Which timers, and what each one means\n| Timer | Covers | Setting | When it fires |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Connect** | TCP and TLS handshake | Short, a few seconds | Host unreachable; retrying elsewhere is cheap and safe |\n| **First byte / first token** | Queueing plus prefill | From TTFT p99 with margin (illustratively 10–30 s for long-prompt workloads) | Little consumed, so a fallback or retry on another replica is reasonable |\n| **Idle (read) between chunks** | Liveness of a stream | 15–30 s, since a healthy stream emits a token every few tens of milliseconds | Upstream is stuck, provided the server heartbeats through legitimate pauses such as hidden reasoning |\n| **Total deadline** | The whole call | Must cover the longest *legitimate* response | For non-streaming calls, the only timer that tells \"slow\" from \"dead\" |\n\n### Budget arithmetic\n- **Non-streaming deadline:** `TTFT_p99 + max_tokens × TPOT_p99 + margin`.\n- **Example:** with `max_tokens = 4096` and a TPOT of 30 ms that is already over two minutes before\n  queueing. That is why non-streaming calls with large `max_tokens` collide with 60-second proxies, and\n  why some SDKs refuse very long non-streaming requests and tell you to stream.\n- **Derive it per call** from `max_tokens` rather than hard-coding one constant.\n- **Cap `max_tokens`** to what the product needs; a smaller cap is the cheapest timeout fix there is.\n\n### Deadline propagation\n1. **Set one absolute deadline at entry.** An agent step that must answer within 60 s and makes four\n   LLM calls and two tool calls cannot give each of them 60 s.\n2. **Pass it down the call chain.** Every downstream call gets `remaining = deadline − now`.\n3. **Fail fast near the end.** When `remaining` falls below the minimum useful time for a step, return\n   a partial result rather than start work that cannot finish.\n4. **Carry it on the wire.** gRPC carries deadlines natively; over HTTP pass it as a header or in your\n   own context object.\n\n### Timeouts inside an agent loop\n- **Tools need timers too.** A web fetch, SQL query or code sandbox can hang as easily as a model call;\n  wrap each in a timeout from its own latency percentiles, inside the run deadline.\n- **A timed-out tool is information for the model.** Return a short \"tool timed out\" result to the next\n  step so the agent can try another tool or answer with what it has, rather than crashing the run.\n- **Step limits are the loop's total timer.** A max-steps cap bounds runs that loop without progress,\n  which no per-call timeout catches.\n- **Side-effecting tools need care.** A timed-out write may have happened; retry only with an\n  idempotency key, or check state first.\n\n### What happens when a timer fires\n- **Nothing stops upstream unless you make it.** A client-side timeout that merely raises leaves the\n  engine decoding, the provider billing, and the connection half-open until the server's own timers\n  notice.\n- **So every timeout must close the connection or abort the request**, so the signal reaches the engine.\n  That is the cancellation lesson's job, and timeouts are its most common trigger.\n\n### Retrying after a timeout\n- **It is the most expensive failure to retry.** The first attempt may still be running, timeouts signal\n  overload more often than a transient glitch, and the retry needs the same long budget again.\n- **Retry only** when the remaining deadline allows it, with jitter, and only for idempotent requests.\n- **Prefer falling back:** a smaller or different model, a cached answer, a degraded response.\n- **Open a circuit breaker** when timeouts cluster, so a sick upstream is not hammered.\n\n### Server-side timers if you run the engine\n- **Bound the admission queue.** A request that has waited longer than its first-token budget should get\n  a fast 429 or 503 rather than be served late.\n- **Cap per-request generation time and `max_tokens`** at the API boundary.\n- **Expose queue wait as a metric**, so you can see the budget being spent before clients time out.","example":"### One deadline shared by an agent's steps\nRunnable with sleeps standing in for LLM and tool calls: a 1 s budget for four steps. Each step gets at\nmost what is left, and the last one is skipped because it could not finish usefully.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nclass Deadline:\n    def __init__(self, seconds: float):\n        self.at = time.monotonic() + seconds          # absolute, so it survives being passed around\n    def remaining(self) -> float:\n        return self.at - time.monotonic()\n\nasync def step(name, duration, deadline, min_useful=0.2):\n    left = deadline.remaining()\n    if left < min_useful:                             # fail fast instead of starting doomed work\n        raise TimeoutError(f\"skipped {name}: only {left:.1f}s left\")\n    await asyncio.wait_for(asyncio.sleep(duration), timeout=left)   # never longer than what is left\n    return name\n\nasync def agent(budget_s=1.0):\n    d, done = Deadline(budget_s), []\n    try:\n        for name, dur in [(\"plan\", 0.3), (\"search\", 0.2), (\"draft\", 0.4), (\"review\", 0.3)]:\n            done.append(await step(name, dur, d))\n    except (TimeoutError, asyncio.TimeoutError) as e:\n        return done, f\"partial ({e})\"\n    return done, \"complete\"\n\nprint(asyncio.run(agent()))\n# (['plan', 'search', 'draft'], 'partial (skipped review: only 0.1s left)')\n```\n\n### Layered timeouts around a streaming call\nLayered timeouts around a streaming call with `httpx`: a short connect timeout, a cumulative first-token\ntimer, an idle timer that resets on every line (heartbeats included), and a total deadline derived from\n`max_tokens` and measured TPOT, capped by the caller's absolute deadline.\n- **Why the cap:** an agent can share one budget across several steps.\n- **With these numbers** a 512-token call gets `8 + 512 × 0.05 × 1.5 ≈ 46 s`, and the first-token timer\n  turns a stuck queue into an error after 15 s instead of 46.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json, time\nimport httpx\n\nTTFT_P99, TPOT_P99 = 8.0, 0.05            # measured for this model and traffic, in seconds\nFIRST_TOKEN, IDLE = 15.0, 20.0\n\ndef call_budget(max_tokens: int) -> float:\n    return TTFT_P99 + max_tokens * TPOT_P99 * 1.5    # 1.5x margin on decode\n\nasync def stream_completion(client: httpx.AsyncClient, payload: dict, deadline: float) -> str:\n    budget = min(call_budget(payload[\"max_tokens\"]), deadline - time.monotonic())\n    if budget < 2.0:\n        raise TimeoutError(\"not enough budget left to start this call\")\n    first_by = time.monotonic() + FIRST_TOKEN\n    timeout = httpx.Timeout(connect=3.0, read=IDLE + 5, write=10.0, pool=3.0)  # socket backstop\n    text = []\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(budget):                       # total deadline (Python 3.11+)\n            async with client.stream(\"POST\", \"/v1/chat/completions\",\n                                     json={**payload, \"stream\": True}, timeout=timeout) as r:\n                r.raise_for_status()\n                lines = r.aiter_lines()\n                while True:\n                    wait = IDLE if text else min(IDLE, first_by - time.monotonic())\n                    try:\n                        line = await asyncio.wait_for(anext(lines), max(wait, 0.0))\n                    except StopAsyncIteration:\n                        break\n                    if not line.startswith(\"data: \") or line == \"data: [DONE]\":\n                        continue                                  # heartbeats reset IDLE only\n                    delta = json.loads(line[6:])[\"choices\"][0][\"delta\"].get(\"content\")\n                    if delta: text.append(delta)\n    except (TimeoutError, httpx.TimeoutException) as e:\n        # leaving `client.stream` closed the connection: the upstream's only signal to stop decoding\n        phase = \"first-token\" if not text else \"idle-or-total\"\n        raise TimeoutError(f\"{phase} timeout after {len(text)} chunks\") from e\n    return \"\".join(text)\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: a tool timeout returned to the model\nA hung tool becomes a tool result, so the agent continues within the run deadline instead of failing.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def slow_search(q):          # stand-in for a tool whose upstream hangs\n    await asyncio.sleep(10); return \"results\"\n\nasync def call_tool(fn, args, remaining_s, tool_timeout_s=3.0):\n    try:\n        return await asyncio.wait_for(fn(**args), timeout=min(tool_timeout_s, remaining_s))\n    except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n        return {\"error\": \"tool_timeout\", \"tool\": fn.__name__,\n                \"hint\": \"try another source or answer with what you have\"}\n\nprint(asyncio.run(call_tool(slow_search, {\"q\": \"refund policy\"}, remaining_s=20)))\n# {'error': 'tool_timeout', 'tool': 'slow_search', 'hint': 'try another source or answer with what you have'}\n```\n\n| Budget item (60 s run SLO, illustrative) | Allocation |\n|---|---|\n| 6 LLM calls, `max_tokens` 300, TTFT p99 2 s, TPOT 30 ms | about 6 × (2 + 9) = 66 s at worst, so cap tokens or steps |\n| Same with `max_tokens` 150 | about 6 × (2 + 4.5) = 39 s |\n| 3 tools at 3 s timeout each | 9 s |\n| Remaining margin, with the 150-token cap | 60 − 39 − 9 ≈ 12 s for queueing and the final answer |\n\n### Worked scenario: the 60-second wall\nA non-streaming endpoint behind a load balancer with the default 60 s idle timeout, TTFT p99 8 s, TPOT\n30 ms (illustrative).\n\n| `max_tokens` | Needed deadline (8 s + tokens × 30 ms, before margin) | Behind a 60 s idle timeout |\n|---|---|---|\n| 512 | about 23 s | Fine |\n| 1,500 | about 53 s | Fails on queueing spikes |\n| 4,096 | about 131 s | Always cut off; engine keeps generating |\n\nFixes, cheapest first: cap `max_tokens`, stream with heartbeats so the idle timer never fires on a live\ncall, then raise the idle timeout on hops you own.","patterns":["**One run deadline, many child budgets.** Create the deadline when the agent run starts, store it on the run context, and give every model and tool call `min(own budget, remaining)`. Monitor which step type consumes the budget in traces.","**Per-tool timeouts with structured timeout results.** Wrap each tool with its own timeout and return a typed timeout result to the model instead of raising. Monitor tool timeout rate per tool and how often the agent recovers after one.","**Configure SDK timeouts and retries explicitly.** Construct the client with a timeout and retry count you chose (the OpenAI Python SDK accepts `timeout`, including an `httpx.Timeout`, and `max_retries`, and `client.with_options(...)` overrides them per call). Monitor timeout counts per call site.","**Audit and raise idle timeouts on streaming routes.** Set nginx `proxy_read_timeout` for the streaming location and the ALB `idle_timeout.timeout_seconds` attribute above your longest heartbeat gap. Monitor disconnects clustered at exactly the old default.","**Propagate one deadline.** Put an absolute deadline on the request context at the edge, pass it to services in a header of your own (or as a gRPC deadline), and compute each call's timeout as `min(derived budget, remaining)`. Monitor which step consumes the budget.","**Bound the admission queue.** Reject requests that have waited past their first-token budget with 429 or 503 at the serving layer. Monitor queue wait percentiles next to client timeout rate.","**Circuit-break on clustered timeouts.** When the timeout rate for an upstream crosses a threshold, open a breaker and route to a fallback model for a cool-down period. Monitor breaker state changes and fallback share.","**Label timeouts by cause.** Emit first-token, idle and total timeouts as separate metric labels per layer; a rise in first-token timeouts is usually the first visible sign of upstream overload."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A run is a chain: six model calls and three tools under one user SLO.\n  If each call gets the full budget, every call \"succeeds within timeout\" and the run still misses it;\n  one hung tool can stall the whole loop.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The SDK client's `timeout` and `max_retries`, `httpx.Timeout`\n  inside it, `asyncio.timeout` around tool calls, your framework's per-run step or time limits, and the\n  60 s idle timers on proxies in front of your agent server.\n- **What you control.** One absolute run deadline passed to every step, per-call budgets from\n  `max_tokens` and measured TPOT, per-tool timeouts from tool latency percentiles, and what the agent\n  returns when time runs out.\n- **What you can skip.** Memorising every proxy's default; audit the hops in your own path and stream\n  with heartbeats.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **User-facing agents with an SLO.** One run deadline passed to every step keeps a chain of model and\n  tool calls inside the promised response time.\n- **Agents calling flaky tools.** Per-tool timeouts from measured latency percentiles stop one hung API\n  from stalling the whole loop.\n- **Streaming agents behind proxies.** First-token and idle timers catch a stuck stream early, and\n  heartbeats keep slow tools from tripping proxy idle limits.\n- **Graceful degradation.** When the budget runs low the agent returns a partial answer or a cheaper\n  fallback instead of a bare error.","mini_project":"**Put one deadline on an agent run (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make a tool-calling agent finish or fail clearly within a fixed run budget.\n- **Steps:** build a small agent with one fast tool and one tool that sometimes hangs; pass an absolute\n  deadline into the loop; give each model call connect, first-token, idle and total timers derived from\n  the remaining budget; wrap tools in `asyncio.timeout`; cancel the in-flight call on expiry and return a\n  partial answer.\n- **Done when:** across 20 runs no run exceeds the deadline, hung tools are logged as timeouts, and\n  retries happen only when the remaining budget allows."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"The SDK default timeout is a sensible bound.\"** The OpenAI Python client defaults to 10 minutes and two retries, so a hung call can take half an hour to surface; set `timeout` and `max_retries` yourself.","**\"If every call is inside its timeout, the run is inside its SLO.\"** Per-call budgets add up across six model calls and three tools. Only one absolute run deadline, passed down as `remaining`, bounds the whole run.","**\"A longer timeout is the safe choice for slow LLMs.\"** Long flat timeouts let dead upstreams pin slots and feed the retry spiral. Split the timers: a short first-token and idle timer catches dead calls fast, and the total covers long healthy ones.","**\"The timeout I configured is the one that fires.\"** The shortest timer on the path wins, often a 60 s proxy or load balancer idle timeout in front of a non-streaming call.","**\"When my timeout fires, the work stops.\"** Raising an exception leaves the engine decoding and the provider billing until the connection is closed or the request aborted; every timeout must trigger cancellation.","**\"Retrying a timeout is like retrying any transient error.\"** The first attempt may still be running or may have written data, and timeouts usually mean overload. Retry only within the deadline, with an idempotency key, or fall back."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Check run budget left\"] --> C[\"Connect\"]\n    C -->|\"connected\"| F[\"Wait for first token\"]\n    F -->|\"first content\"| R[\"Read stream\"]\n    R -->|\"chunk or heartbeat resets idle\"| R\n    R -->|\"done\"| OK[\"Return answer\"]\n    C -.->|\"connect timeout\"| X[\"Close connection, cancel upstream\"]\n    F -.->|\"first-token timeout\"| X\n    R -.->|\"idle or total timeout\"| X\n    X --> D{\"Budget left and idempotent?\"}\n    D -->|\"yes\"| B[\"Fallback or one retry\"]\n    D -->|\"no\"| P[\"Partial result or clear failure\"]","caption":"One model call inside an agent run: each phase has its own timer, all capped by the run's remaining\nbudget, and every expiry routes through cancellation before any fallback or retry."},"interview":[{"q":"A tool in your agent loop sometimes hangs for minutes. How do you handle it?","a":"- **Wrap the tool in its own timeout** from its latency percentiles, capped by the run's remaining\n  deadline.\n- **Return the timeout to the model** as a structured tool result so it can pick another tool or answer\n  with what it has, instead of failing the run.\n- **Cancel the underlying work** and, for side-effecting tools, retry only with an idempotency key."},{"q":"Why is a single flat timeout wrong for LLM calls, especially inside agents?","a":"- **Legitimate durations span 100×:** queueing, prefill and an unknown output length mean a healthy call\n  can take under a second or several minutes.\n- **A short constant** fails long outputs; **a long one** lets dead upstreams pin workers and pool slots.\n- **Agents chain calls,** so per-call constants add up past the run SLO; layered timers inside one run\n  deadline fix both."},{"q":"What is your effective timeout if the SDK says 10 minutes and nginx sits in front?","a":"- **The shortest timer wins:** nginx `proxy_read_timeout` defaults to 60 s, so a non-streaming call that\n  sends nothing for a minute is cut there, whatever the SDK says.\n- **Retries multiply the worst case:** the OpenAI SDK's default of two retries means up to three full\n  timeouts before your code sees an error.\n- **Fix:** stream with heartbeats, set SDK `timeout` and `max_retries` deliberately, and raise idle\n  timeouts on hops you own."},{"q":"How do you size the total timeout for a non-streaming call with max_tokens 4096?","a":"- **Formula:** TTFT p99 + max_tokens × TPOT p99 + margin.\n- **Numbers:** at 30 ms TPOT, 4096 tokens is about 123 s of decode alone, so over two minutes before\n  queueing, which no 60-second proxy will allow.\n- **Better:** cap max_tokens to what the product needs, or stream so liveness comes from the idle timer."},{"q":"A timed-out call is still running upstream. Do you retry?","a":"- **Not by default:** the first attempt may still be consuming capacity, and timeouts usually signal\n  overload, so a retry adds load to the cause.\n- **Cancel first**, then retry only if the remaining deadline covers a full attempt and the request is\n  idempotent, with jitter.\n- **Prefer fallback** to a cheaper model or a degraded answer, and open a circuit breaker if timeouts\n  cluster."},{"q":"Your timeout rate doubled overnight. How do you find the cause?","a":"- **Split by cause and layer:** first-token timeouts point at queueing or longer prompts; idle timeouts\n  at stalled streams or missing heartbeats; total timeouts at longer outputs or slower decode.\n- **Correlate** with engine queue wait, prompt-length percentiles and any infrastructure change (a new\n  proxy or gateway with a 60 s default).\n- **For agents,** also split by step index and tool: growing transcripts lengthen late-step TTFT, and\n  one degraded tool can account for most run timeouts."}],"tradeoffs":["**Short first-token timeout vs bursts.** It fails fast and enables cheap fallback, but under a traffic burst turns a recoverable queue into a wave of errors; the balance flips when callers can wait and nothing else can serve them.","**Idle timeouts vs heartbeats.** They give a precise liveness signal for streams at the cost of needing heartbeats during legitimate pauses; without heartbeats they are worse than a plain total deadline.","**Derived deadlines vs generous caps.** Deriving from `max_tokens` is accurate but means a generous cap buys a long timeout; tighter caps make timeouts tighter at the cost of truncating rare long answers.","**Retry vs overload.** Retrying can rescue a transient failure and can also double load on an overloaded upstream; the decision flips on whether remaining budget exists and whether the timeout rate is rising fleet-wide.","**Long vs short server-side timeouts.** Long ones maximise the chance a slow request completes and let hung requests hold capacity; shorter ones protect the pool and shed work that would have finished."],"pitfalls":["**Inheriting defaults.** A 60-second proxy or load balancer idle timeout in front of a non-streaming endpoint shows up as gateway errors at exactly 60 s on long generations while the engine keeps running. Fix: audit every hop's timer, then stream or raise the ones you own.","**Treating the HTTP read timeout as a total timeout.** Symptom: a \"timeout\" that never fires on a slow-but-alive stream, since it resets on every chunk. Fix: add an explicit total deadline.","**Timing out without cancelling.** Symptom: provider usage and GPU load that stay high after clients have given up, and tokens billed for answers nobody saw. Fix: close the connection on every expiry.","**One timeout constant regardless of `max_tokens`.** Symptom: short calls waiting minutes to fail and long calls cut off while healthy. Fix: derive the budget per call.","**Agent steps that each get the full user budget.** Symptom: a 60-second SLO breached by a request whose individual calls all \"succeeded within timeout\". Fix: propagate one absolute deadline.","**Retrying timeouts immediately, without a budget check.** Symptom: an overload that worsens as clients time out, the signature of a retry storm. Fix: retry only within budget, with jitter, behind a breaker."],"when":"- **Every call** to a model, a retriever or a tool needs the layered timers above; the question is only\n  the values.\n- **Derive values** from measured percentiles and `max_tokens`, revisit them when models or prompt\n  lengths change, and give agents one propagated deadline rather than per-call constants.\n- **Stream whenever `max_tokens` is large**, so liveness comes from the idle timer instead of a\n  multi-minute total.\n- **Measure** timeout rate by layer and by cause (first-token versus idle versus total), and alert when\n  it rises, because it is usually the first visible sign of upstream overload.\n- **Interviews:** for \"how would you set timeouts for an LLM call\", answer with the stack, the\n  arithmetic and the cancel-and-fallback behaviour, not a number.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures connect, first-token, idle and total timers per call from `max_tokens` and measured TPOT, and makes every expiry close the upstream connection.","architect":"Sets the end-to-end latency budget per surface, mandates deadline propagation across services, and decides where fallback replaces retry.","fde":"Finds the 60-second idle timeout in the customer's load balancer or API gateway that is truncating long answers, and gets streaming enabled through it."},"check":{"q":"An agent makes up to six sequential LLM calls and three tool calls per user request, and the\nuser-facing SLO is 60 seconds. How do you set the timeouts, and what happens when the budget runs out?","a":"Give the request one absolute deadline at entry and pass it to every step; each call gets\n`min(its own derived budget, remaining)`. Derive each LLM call's budget from its `max_tokens` and\nmeasured TPOT and stream it so the idle timer (with heartbeats) is the liveness check; give tools\nbudgets from their own latency percentiles. Before starting a step, check that enough budget remains\nto be useful; if not, stop and return the best partial result or a clear \"could not finish\" rather\nthan start a call that will be cut off. Never retry a timed-out step unless the remaining budget\ncovers it, and prefer a cheaper fallback model. Every expiry must cancel the in-flight call so the\nupstream stops. Measure which step consumes the budget in practice and shrink `max_tokens` or move\nwork earlier accordingly."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the most useful liveness signal for a streaming LLM response?","options":["The connect timeout, since a connected socket means the server is alive","A single total timeout set generously to cover the longest answer","An idle timeout on the gap between chunks, combined with server heartbeats during legitimate pauses","The time to first token, after which the stream can be trusted to finish"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A healthy stream delivers data continuously, so a stalled gap is the earliest and most specific sign of a dead upstream. A total timeout still bounds the worst case, but it reacts far later; TTFT says nothing about what happens after the first token."},{"q":"Long non-streaming generations fail at exactly 60 seconds with a gateway error, and the GPU stays busy afterwards. What is the likely cause?","options":["The model has a built-in 60-second generation limit","An intermediary's default idle timeout cut the connection, and the engine was never told to abort","The client's connect timeout is too short","`max_tokens` is too low for the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reverse proxies and load balancers commonly default to about a minute of idle time, which a non-streaming response exceeds with nothing sent. The engine keeps decoding because no cancellation reached it; streaming with heartbeats or a longer idle setting plus propagated cancellation fixes both halves."},{"q":"A 90-second generation times out while the upstream is clearly overloaded. What is the safest default behaviour?","options":["Retry immediately with the same parameters to recover from the transient failure","Extend the timeout and wait, since the answer is probably almost done","Cancel the in-flight request upstream, then fall back or fail clearly, retrying only if the remaining budget allows","Switch to a non-streaming call so the response arrives in one piece"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retrying a long, timed-out call under overload doubles the load that caused the timeout and may duplicate work still running upstream. Cancelling frees capacity, and a cheaper fallback or an honest failure respects the user's budget."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/cancellation","sources":["Dean and Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale","Official docs: Python asyncio task cancellation","Official docs: MDN AbortController"],"why":"- **LLM requests are expensive, long and often abandoned.** Users close tabs, hit stop, or send a new\n  message mid-answer; agents fan out speculative branches and keep one; timeouts give up on running\n  calls; hedged requests race two providers and need only the winner.\n- **Work continues unless something says stop**, because a GPU engine has no idea whether anyone is\n  reading.\n- **The cost is threefold.**\n  - *Money:* a provider bills every token decoded until the abort arrives; a self-hosted replica burns\n    GPU time on discarded output.\n  - *Capacity:* an abandoned sequence holds its KV blocks and batch place, so live requests queue behind\n    ghosts, TTFT rises and preemptions start. Under a spike with many disconnects, a healthy cluster\n    looks saturated.\n  - *Correctness:* the old answer keeps streaming into a chat thread after the user moved on, and a\n    cancelled agent step may already have fired a tool with side effects.\n- **\"Let it run, it is only a few hundred tokens\" fails on arithmetic.** If a tenth of requests are\n  abandoned halfway through 1,000-token answers, the replica spends about 5 % of its decode capacity on\n  nothing, and the share climbs with longer outputs and more impatient users.\n- **Timeouts without cancellation are worse:** they guarantee the client gives up while guaranteeing the\n  upstream does not.","how":"### The chain, hop by hop\n1. **Trigger.** In the browser an `AbortController` aborts the `fetch`; a stop button, a new message\n   or navigation calls `controller.abort()`. Server-side triggers are timeouts, superseding requests\n   and hedge resolution.\n2. **Transport.** The client closes the connection (or resets the HTTP/2 stream).\n3. **Your server.** The framework learns of the disconnect only when it next touches the socket. A\n   streaming handler finds out at its next write, or by polling a disconnect check; a non-streaming\n   handler that is simply awaiting the model never touches the socket and never finds out.\n4. **Task cancellation.** The handler's task is cancelled (streaming frameworks usually do this on\n   disconnect) or your code cancels it. In Python, `task.cancel()` schedules a `CancelledError` at the\n   task's next `await`; it is cooperative, not a kill, so `finally` blocks run and must not swallow\n   the exception.\n5. **Upstream close.** Cleanup closes the response you were reading from the provider or engine. That\n   close is the only signal the next hop gets; forgetting it leaves the upstream running while your\n   own request looks finished.\n6. **Engine abort.** The serving layer in front of the engine (an OpenAI-compatible server, a\n   provider's gateway) detects its client's disconnect and aborts the request. A continuous-batching\n   engine removes the sequence from the running batch at the next step and releases its KV blocks for\n   other sequences. Without the abort, the sequence runs to end-of-sequence or `max_tokens`.\n\n**Each arrow is a place the signal can be lost:**\n- a proxy configured to keep the upstream alive after the client leaves;\n- a worker that cannot observe the socket;\n- a `finally` that logs and returns;\n- an SDK that buffers the response instead of streaming it.\n\n### Making disconnects observable\n- **Streaming is the practical fix.** A write per token means the server notices a dead client within\n  one token.\n- **For non-streaming work**, run generation as a separate task and race it against a disconnect\n  watcher, or poll the request's disconnect state while waiting.\n- **Frameworks expose this differently:** FastAPI's `request.is_disconnected()`, a disconnect message on\n  the ASGI receive channel. The companion lesson in the FastAPI module covers the mechanics.\n\n### Side effects and accounting\n- **Cancellation interrupts at an `await`**, so anything between awaits either happened or did not. For\n  pure generation that is harmless.\n- **Mid tool-call it is not.** The tool may have sent the email, written the row or charged the card\n  before the cancel landed. Make tools idempotent or two-phase, and record the run as \"cancelled\"\n  rather than \"failed\".\n- **Shield work that must complete**, such as writing the usage record or releasing a lock, so a cancel\n  cannot tear it.\n- **Billing does not match what you received.** A cancelled stream never delivers its final usage event,\n  while a provider generally charges for tokens generated until its gateway sees the abort. That can be\n  more than you received: tokens in flight, buffered at a proxy, or decoded during the\n  disconnect-detection lag.\n- **So estimate and reconcile.** Keep the text you did receive and tokenize it for an estimate, treat it\n  as a lower bound, and reconcile against the provider's usage reports rather than streamed chunk counts.\n\n### Cancelling an agent run\n- **The run is a tree of tasks.** The loop task owns the current model stream, any parallel tool calls\n  and any sub-agents; a stop must reach every leaf, not only the HTTP response.\n- **Structured concurrency does the fan-out.** Start parallel tools inside an `asyncio.TaskGroup` (Python\n  3.11+) so cancelling the run cancels them all, and each tool's `finally` closes its own connection.\n  Catch ordinary tool errors inside each tool wrapper, because a `TaskGroup` also cancels the sibling\n  tools when one child raises.\n- **Check before each step.** Between steps, check a cancel flag or deadline so the loop does not start\n  a new model call after the user pressed stop.\n- **Persist what finished.** Record completed steps and tool results so a restarted run can resume or\n  show the user what already happened.\n\n### Patterns built on cancellation\n| Pattern | How it works | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Supersede** | The newest user message cancels the in-flight generation before starting the next | Two answers never interleave and the slot is freed immediately |\n| **Stop button** | Same mechanism, user-triggered | Keep the partial text, mark it truncated |\n| **Deadline cancel** | A timeout is only useful if it cancels | The timeouts and cancellation lessons are halves of one behaviour |\n| **Hedging** | Issue the same request to two replicas or providers after a short delay; cancel the loser when the first token or full response arrives | Cuts tail latency at the price of duplicate tokens, so reserve it for latency-critical, cheap-to-duplicate calls |\n| **Speculative prefetch** | Start a likely-next generation early | Cancel it if the user goes elsewhere |","example":"### Agent scenario: stop pressed while parallel tools run\nRunnable on Python 3.11+: the run's parallel tools live in a `TaskGroup`, so cancelling the run cancels\nthe slow tool too, while a tool that already finished keeps its result.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nlog = []\n\nasync def tool(name, secs):\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(secs)                    # stand-in for an HTTP tool call\n        log.append(f\"{name}: done\")\n    finally:\n        if asyncio.current_task().cancelling():\n            log.append(f\"{name}: cancelled, connection closed\")\n\nasync def agent_run(steps=5):\n    for step in range(steps):\n        log.append(f\"step {step}: model call\")\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.1)                     # stand-in for a streamed model call\n        async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:        # parallel tool calls share the run's fate\n            tg.create_task(tool(f\"search-{step}\", 0.3))\n            tg.create_task(tool(f\"fetch-{step}\", 1.0))\n\nasync def main():\n    run = asyncio.create_task(agent_run())\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.6)                         # user presses stop during step 0's tools\n    run.cancel()\n    await asyncio.wait([run])\n    print(*log, sep=\"\\n\")\n    print(\"run cancelled:\", run.cancelled())\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n# step 0: model call\n# search-0: done\n# fetch-0: cancelled, connection closed\n# run cancelled: True\n```\n\n### Supersede pattern for a chat session\nThe newest request cancels the previous generation, and the cancelled task's `finally` closes the\nprovider stream, which is the only way the provider learns to stop decoding. The `asyncio.wait` keeps\nthe caller from swallowing its own cancellation.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\nclient = AsyncOpenAI()\nMODEL = \"my-model\"\n\nclass ChatSession:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.current: asyncio.Task | None = None\n        self.received: list[str] = []            # a cancelled stream sends no usage event; tokenize\n                                                 # this later for a lower-bound estimate of billed output\n\n    async def _generate(self, messages, on_delta):\n        stream = await client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages, stream=True)\n        try:\n            async for chunk in stream:\n                if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n                    self.received.append(chunk.choices[0].delta.content)\n                    on_delta(chunk.choices[0].delta.content)\n        finally:\n            await stream.close()                 # runs on CancelledError too: closing the HTTP response\n                                                 # is the provider's only signal to abort the sequence\n\n    async def submit(self, messages, on_delta) -> asyncio.Task:\n        prev = self.current\n        if prev and not prev.done():\n            prev.cancel()                        # supersede: new message or stop button\n            await asyncio.wait([prev])           # wait for its cleanup without swallowing our own cancel\n        self.current = asyncio.create_task(self._generate(messages, on_delta))\n        return self.current\n\nasync def demo():\n    s = ChatSession()\n    show = lambda t: print(t, end=\"\", flush=True)\n    first = await s.submit([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a long essay about rivers.\"}], show)\n    await asyncio.sleep(1.0)                     # user changes their mind one second in\n    second = await s.submit([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Actually, one sentence on rivers.\"}], show)\n    await second\n    print(\"\\nfirst cancelled:\", first.cancelled(), \"| chars received:\", sum(map(len, s.received)))\n\nasyncio.run(demo())\n```\n\n### Racing a non-streaming generation against a disconnect watcher\nRunnable with no server: a fake upstream decodes a token every 10 ms, and the \"client\" leaves after\n0.5 s. The watcher wins the race, the generation task is cancelled, and its `finally` closes the\nupstream, so decoding stops near 50 tokens instead of running to 1,000.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nclass FakeUpstream:                                 # stands in for a provider or engine stream\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.closed, self.tokens = False, 0\n    async def stream(self):\n        while not self.closed:\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n            self.tokens += 1\n            yield \"tok \"\n    async def close(self):\n        self.closed = True                          # real code: close the HTTP response\n\nasync def generate(up, out, max_tokens=1000):\n    try:\n        async for t in up.stream():\n            out.append(t)\n            if len(out) == max_tokens:\n                break\n    finally:\n        await up.close()                            # runs on CancelledError too\n\nasync def handle(disconnect_after):\n    up, out = FakeUpstream(), []\n    gen = asyncio.create_task(generate(up, out))\n    watch = asyncio.create_task(asyncio.sleep(disconnect_after))  # real code: poll is_disconnected()\n    done, _ = await asyncio.wait({gen, watch}, return_when=asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED)\n    if watch in done:\n        gen.cancel()\n        await asyncio.wait([gen])                   # let cleanup finish\n        status = \"cancelled\"\n    else:\n        watch.cancel()\n        status = \"completed\"\n    print(f\"{status}: upstream closed={up.closed}, tokens decoded={up.tokens}\")\n\nasyncio.run(handle(0.5))\n# cancelled: upstream closed=True, tokens decoded=45   (roughly 45-50, timing dependent)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what abandonment costs without cancellation\nDecode capacity wasted when abandoned requests run to completion (illustrative; with working\ncancellation the waste shrinks to the detection lag, about one token for streaming handlers).\n\n| Abandonment rate | Abandoned at | Waste per abandoned request | Share of decode capacity wasted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10 % | halfway through 1,000 tokens | 500 tokens | about 5 % |\n| 10 % | after 100 of 1,000 tokens | 900 tokens | about 9 % |\n| 30 % | halfway | 500 tokens | about 15 % |\n\nThe earlier and more often users leave, the larger the share, which is why long outputs and impatient\nusers make the problem grow.","patterns":["**One cancellable task per agent run.** Keep a handle to the run's task keyed by run id, cancel it from the stop endpoint or on disconnect, and start tools and sub-agents inside a `TaskGroup` so they die with it. Monitor tool calls and model tokens that start after a run was cancelled; it should be zero.","**Idempotency keys on side-effecting tools.** Derive a key from run id and step so a retried or resumed run cannot send the same email or order twice. Monitor duplicate side effects per tool.","**Wire `AbortController` on every client call.** Create one per request, pass `signal` to `fetch`, and call `abort()` on stop, supersede and navigation. Monitor the abandonment rate from client telemetry.","**Close upstream in `finally` or a context manager.** Wrap provider streams so cleanup always closes the HTTP response, and never catch `CancelledError` without re-raising. Monitor open upstream connections against active client streams.","**Keep the proxy from hiding aborts.** In nginx leave `proxy_ignore_client_abort` at its default `off`, so a client abort closes the upstream connection; check equivalent settings on gateways. Monitor whether the serving layer logs disconnects when users press stop.","**Shield bookkeeping.** Run the usage write and lock release inside `asyncio.shield(...)` so a cancel cannot tear them, and record the request as \"cancelled\", not an error. Monitor cancelled share separately from the error rate.","**Hedge with an explicit loser cancel.** For short latency-critical calls, start a second attempt after a delay, take the first result with `asyncio.wait(..., return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)`, and cancel the rest. Monitor duplicate tokens per hedged call and p99 latency.","**Measure disconnect-to-abort time.** Track time from client disconnect to engine abort, tokens generated after disconnect, and the engine's aborted-request count. If provider usage does not fall when abandonment rises, the abort is not reaching them."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Users stop agents that head the wrong way, and agents fan out parallel\n  tools, sub-agents and speculative branches. Each abandoned piece keeps billing, holds capacity and may\n  still fire a side effect unless cancellation reaches it.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `AbortController` in the UI, `task.cancel()` and\n  `asyncio.TaskGroup` in a Python agent loop, `await stream.close()` on provider SDK streams, FastAPI\n  `request.is_disconnected()`, and nginx `proxy_ignore_client_abort`.\n- **What you control.** One cancellable task per run, structured concurrency so tools die with the run,\n  upstream streams closed in `finally`, idempotency keys on write tools, and a \"cancelled\" run status.\n- **What you can skip.** Engine scheduler internals; it is enough that an aborted sequence is dropped at\n  the next step and its KV blocks are freed.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Stop buttons in agent UIs.** A user's stop must end decoding, pending tool calls and sub-agents, not\n  just hide the stream.\n- **Parallel tools and speculative branches.** When one branch wins, the others are cancelled so they\n  stop billing and free capacity.\n- **Agents with write tools.** Idempotency keys and a \"cancelled\" run status make an interrupted email,\n  payment or ticket update safe to inspect and retry.\n- **Deadlines and disconnects.** Timeouts and dropped connections reuse the same cancellation path, so\n  abandoned runs do not keep spending.","mini_project":"**Make stop actually stop an agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove that cancelling a run ends every model stream and tool call within a second.\n- **Steps:** build a FastAPI agent endpoint that streams a run with two parallel tools in an\n  `asyncio.TaskGroup`; check `request.is_disconnected()` in the loop; close the provider stream in\n  `finally`; log a line from every tool and stream when it starts, finishes or is cancelled; disconnect\n  the client mid-run.\n- **Done when:** the log shows every task cancelled shortly after the disconnect, no tool logs work after\n  it, and the run is stored with a \"cancelled\" status."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Closing the tab or hitting stop stops the model.\"** The engine has no idea whether anyone is reading. The stop must cross every hop, and each hop must close its own upstream connection, or decoding runs to `max_tokens`.","**\"`task.cancel()` kills the task.\"** It schedules a `CancelledError` at the next `await`. `finally` blocks run, so that is where the upstream stream must be closed; a `finally` that returns, or an `except CancelledError` that does not re-raise, swallows the cancel and the run carries on.","**\"My server knows when the client leaves.\"** It learns only when it next touches the socket. A streaming handler notices within a token; a non-streaming handler awaiting the model never does unless you race it against a disconnect watcher.","**\"Cancelling the HTTP response cancels the agent run.\"** The run is a tree: model stream, parallel tools and sub-agents. Only structured concurrency such as `asyncio.TaskGroup` and a cancel check between steps reaches every leaf.","**\"A cancelled tool call did nothing.\"** Cancellation lands at an `await`, after the email or charge may already have gone out; write tools need idempotency keys, and the run is recorded as cancelled, not failed.","**\"I am billed only for the tokens I received.\"** A cancelled stream never sends its usage event, and the provider charges until its gateway sees the abort. Treat received text as a lower bound and reconcile against usage reports."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant B as Browser\n    participant A as Your API\n    participant G as Provider or serving layer\n    participant E as Engine\n    B->>A: POST stream, tokens flowing\n    Note over B: user hits stop\n    B->>A: controller.abort closes connection\n    Note over A: run task and its tools cancelled\n    A->>G: finally closes upstream stream\n    G->>E: abort request\n    Note over E: drop sequence at next step, free KV blocks\n    Note over E: queued requests take the freed slot","caption":"Follow the stop signal from the click to freed KV blocks. In an agent the run task and its tools are\ncancelled too; every hop must close the next connection, or the engine keeps decoding for nobody."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does an abandoned LLM request cost more than an abandoned web request?","a":"- **It keeps consuming:** a decode slot, KV-cache memory and billed tokens until end-of-sequence or the\n  token limit, because the engine cannot tell nobody is reading.\n- **It hurts live traffic:** abandoned sequences hold KV blocks, so queued requests wait, TTFT rises and\n  preemptions start.\n- **Numbers:** a tenth of requests abandoned halfway through 1,000-token answers wastes about 5 % of\n  decode capacity."},{"q":"A user hits stop while an agent is mid tool-call. What can go wrong and how do you design for it?","a":"- **The tool may already have acted:** cancellation lands at an await, so an email or payment may have\n  been sent before it arrived.\n- **Design:** idempotency keys or two-phase tools, the run recorded as cancelled, and shielded\n  bookkeeping so usage and locks are written even under cancel.\n- **On retry**, the idempotency key prevents a duplicate side effect."},{"q":"Users press stop on agents, but runs keep calling tools for a minute afterwards. What is wrong?","a":"- **The stop only reached the HTTP response,** not the loop: the run task was never cancelled, or the\n  loop does not check for cancellation between steps.\n- **Tools run outside structured concurrency,** for example fire-and-forget tasks, so cancelling the\n  parent does not reach them.\n- **Fix:** one cancellable task per run, tools in a `TaskGroup`, a cancel check before each step, and a\n  metric for work started after cancellation."},{"q":"When is hedging worth it, and what does it cost?","a":"- **Worth it** for short, latency-critical calls with a bad tail and spare capacity: send a second\n  request after a short delay and cancel the loser.\n- **Cost:** duplicate tokens on every hedged call, and extra load exactly when an upstream is slow.\n- **Requirement:** the loser must actually be cancelled upstream, or the cost doubles with no limit."},{"q":"How would you prove cancellation works end to end in production?","a":"- **Metrics:** time from client disconnect to engine abort, tokens generated after disconnect, and the\n  engine's aborted-request count rising with stop-button presses.\n- **Billing check:** provider usage should fall when abandonment rises; if it does not, the abort is\n  lost at some hop.\n- **Reconcile** received text, tokenized as a lower bound, against the provider's usage reports."}],"tradeoffs":["**Freed capacity vs lost partial work.** Cancelling on disconnect frees capacity and money immediately but throws away partial work that might have been valuable; for long, expensive jobs the better design is an asynchronous job whose lifetime is independent of the HTTP connection.","**Tail latency vs duplicate tokens.** Hedged requests cut tail latency and double the tokens spent on every hedged call; they pay off only for short, latency-critical calls where the tail is bad and capacity is available.","**Immediate cancel vs grace period.** Immediate is cheapest, while a short grace period tolerates mobile network blips and tab switches. The right delay depends on how reconnecting clients resume, which for LLM streams is usually \"they cannot\", so grace periods rarely pay.","**Safe generation vs risky side effects.** Cooperative cancellation is safe for generation and risky around side effects; idempotent tools can be cancelled freely, non-idempotent ones need shielding or compensation.","**Polling overhead vs never stopping.** Polling for disconnects in non-streaming handlers adds a little overhead per request and is the only way those handlers ever stop early."],"pitfalls":["**No disconnect handling at all.** Symptom: GPU utilisation and provider spend that stay flat when users leave, and TTFT that rises during periods of many abandoned requests. Fix: wire the chain above, hop by hop.","**Catching `CancelledError` and continuing.** On Python before 3.8 a plain `except Exception` did this silently, since it was not yet a `BaseException`; a bare `except:` still does. Symptom: tasks that never finish and `finally` cleanup that never runs. Fix: clean up in `finally` and re-raise.","**Closing your own response but not the upstream stream.** Symptom: provider usage higher than your logs can account for. Fix: close the provider stream in `finally` or a context manager.","**A proxy that keeps the upstream connection alive after the client aborts.** Symptom: the serving layer never sees a disconnect even though clients clearly left. Fix: check settings such as nginx `proxy_ignore_client_abort`.","**Assuming non-streaming endpoints cancel on disconnect.** Symptom: a 2-minute generation completing for a client that left after 5 seconds. Fix: race generation against a disconnect watcher.","**Cancelling an agent mid-tool-call with no idempotency.** Symptom: duplicated emails or orders when the user retries after hitting stop. Fix: idempotency keys or two-phase tools.","**Reporting cancelled requests as errors.** Symptom: a noisy error rate that masks real failures and panics on-call. Fix: a separate \"cancelled\" status and metric."],"when":"- **Always, for services fronting a model:** cancellation on disconnect, timeout and supersede is not\n  optional once outputs are longer than a sentence or traffic is bursty.\n- **Hedging** only for short, latency-critical calls with capacity to spare; **speculative prefetch**\n  only when the abandoned cost is clearly less than the latency saved.\n- **Do not tie long batch jobs to a connection** at all.\n- **Cancel the whole agent run,** not just its HTTP response: the loop, in-flight model stream, parallel\n  tools and sub-agents.\n- **Measure** the abandonment rate, time from client disconnect to engine abort, and tokens generated\n  after disconnect; check that the provider's reported usage falls when abandonment rises, and if it\n  does not, the abort is not reaching them.\n- **Interviews:** trace the stop signal hop by hop from `AbortController` to KV-block release; that is\n  the answer that shows you have run this in production.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires `AbortController` on the client, closes upstream streams in `finally`, and makes handlers observe disconnects for both streaming and non-streaming paths.","architect":"Decides which calls may be hedged or prefetched, which jobs are detached from connections, and requires idempotent tools so cancelled agent steps cannot cause duplicates.","fde":"Finds the customer proxy or gateway that swallows client aborts, and proves with engine metrics that abandoned requests were burning their GPU capacity."},"check":{"q":"Users hit the stop button constantly, yet GPU utilisation and the provider bill do not move at\nall. Trace where the cancellation is being lost, and how you would prove each hypothesis.","a":"Start at the browser: confirm `controller.abort()` is called and the fetch actually closes\n(network tab shows the request cancelled). Next the server: does the handler observe the disconnect?\nA non-streaming handler awaiting the model never will; a streaming one should see a failed write or\n`is_disconnected()` within a token, so log it. Then task cancellation: check that the task is\ncancelled and that `finally` runs without swallowing `CancelledError`. Then the upstream: verify the\nprovider or engine response is closed, not merely abandoned to the garbage collector, by watching the\nconnection count or the provider's streamed-token usage. Then infrastructure: a proxy configured to\nignore client aborts keeps the upstream alive, visible as the serving layer never logging a\ndisconnect. Finally the engine: its abort count and freed-block metrics should rise with stop-button\npresses; if everything upstream is right and they do not, the serving layer is not calling abort."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a non-streaming endpoint usually keep generating after the client disconnects?","options":["The engine refuses to abort requests that have already started prefill","The handler is awaiting the model and never touches the socket, so nothing observes the disconnect until it tries to write the response","HTTP does not allow a client to close a connection before the response arrives","Non-streaming responses are cached by the proxy and must be completed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A disconnect is only visible to code that reads from or writes to the connection. A streaming handler writes every token and notices quickly; a non-streaming one discovers the dead client only when the finished answer fails to send, long after the work was wasted."},{"q":"In Python asyncio, what does calling `task.cancel()` on a running generation task actually do?","options":["Kills the task immediately and skips any cleanup code","Sends a SIGTERM to the thread running the coroutine","Schedules a `CancelledError` to be raised at the task's next `await`, so `finally` blocks run and can close upstream streams","Marks the task as done and returns its partial result"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cancellation is cooperative: the exception is delivered at the next suspension point, cleanup in `finally` runs, and code must re-raise rather than swallow it. That is exactly what lets the task close the provider stream on its way out."},{"q":"What happens to an aborted sequence in a continuous-batching engine such as vLLM?","options":["Its KV cache is kept until `max_tokens` would have been reached, in case the client reconnects","It finishes decoding in the background and the output is discarded","It is removed from the running batch and its KV blocks are released for other sequences","It is moved to a low-priority queue and completed when the GPU is idle"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The point of propagating cancellation is to reclaim capacity now. The engine drops the sequence at the next scheduling step and frees its memory, which is why abandoned requests only cost capacity when the abort never reaches the engine."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/backpressure","sources":["Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (Handling Overload; Addressing Cascading Failures)","Official docs: vLLM (engine arguments and metrics)"],"why":"- **Capacity is fixed.** The running batch is capped by KV-cache memory (`max_num_seqs` and free cache\n  blocks in vLLM), and token output is capped by memory bandwidth. Arrivals do not know this: when\n  arrival rate λ exceeds service rate μ for even a few seconds, the difference accumulates somewhere.\n- **An unbounded queue moves the problem.** Little's law, L = λW: requests in system equal arrival rate\n  times time-in-system. Unbounded, W grows without limit and every queued request outlives its client's\n  timeout. The client retries, raising λ on a system already saturated.\n- **Useful throughput falls when demand peaks.** The GPU keeps prefilling 8k-token prompts for\n  connections nobody is listening to. That is the cascading-failure pattern, and LLM work makes it worse\n  because each unit is expensive and long-lived.\n- **\"Just autoscale\" is too slow.** A new GPU node pulls an image, loads tens of gigabytes of weights and\n  warms up, which takes minutes; the spike is over or the service has already collapsed.\n- **What it buys.** The system keeps serving *some* traffic correctly while scaling catches up. Fail fast\n  and selectively at the edge, where rejection costs microseconds, not slowly and universally in the\n  middle, where every failure has already consumed GPU time.","how":"### Where the pressure originates\n- **Scheduler state.** A continuous-batching engine holds a running batch and a waiting queue. Each\n  decode step it tries to admit waiting requests.\n- **Admission fails** when the sequence count would exceed `max_num_seqs` or no free KV blocks remain.\n- **Preemption is the first sign of overload.** When a *running* request needs a block and none is free,\n  the engine preempts a sequence (swapping or recomputing its cache).\n- **Engines expose it as metrics,** for instance vLLM's running and waiting request gauges and its\n  KV-cache usage percentage.\n- **Queue wait time, not GPU utilisation, is the pressure signal.** A GPU at 95% with an empty queue is\n  healthy; at 95% with a queue growing by eight requests a second it is minutes from collapse.\n\n### Propagating it upstream\nThe path is client → gateway → application → engine. Each hop must:\n1. **Bound its own buffer.**\n2. **Translate \"I am full\"** into something the previous hop understands.\n\n| Status | Meaning | Header |\n|---|---|---|\n| 429 | *this caller* exceeded its allowance | `Retry-After` |\n| 503 | *the server* is out of capacity | `Retry-After` |\n\nTwo rules follow from the economics:\n- **Decide before any GPU work starts,** because rejecting after prefill wastes the most expensive part of\n  the request.\n- **Make the decision cheap,** ideally a counter check, because under overload you make it thousands of\n  times a second.\n\n### Admission policies, simplest first\n1. **Concurrency limit.** A semaphore sized from Little's law: at 4 requests/s and 8 s mean latency you\n   need about 32 in flight. Set the limit near the engine's real capacity and reject beyond it. Simple,\n   but one number cannot describe a mix of 500-token and 50,000-token requests.\n2. **Bounded queue with a wait budget.** Allow a short queue, but reject any request that waited longer\n   than a budget (say 1–2 s for interactive traffic). This is the CoDel insight from network queue\n   management: control queue *delay*, not *length*, because length is meaningless when service times\n   vary 100× between requests.\n3. **Token-aware admission.** The real cost is tokens. Admit against a budget of prompt tokens plus\n   `max_tokens`, because one 100k-token document request displaces fifty chat turns and a request count\n   hides that.\n4. **Priority shedding.** Over capacity, drop batch and background work before interactive traffic,\n   because the cost of a dropped request differs by an order of magnitude between them.\n5. **Adaptive limits.** Measure the latency gradient and shrink the limit when it rises (the TCP-Vegas\n   idea used by adaptive concurrency libraries), because any static limit drifts as prompt mix and model\n   change.\n\n### Streaming and slow consumers\n- **Pressure runs in reverse too.** The server produces tokens faster than a slow client reads them, so\n  the application buffers.\n- **Capacity leaks to dead streams.** The engine keeps generating and holding KV memory for a consumer that\n  may have gone away.\n- **Fix:** bound the per-connection buffer, detect disconnects, and cancel the generation (see\n  cancellation).\n\n### The caller's half of the loop\n- **Rejection only helps if the caller slows down.** Well-behaved clients honour `Retry-After`, back off\n  with jitter, and cap retries with a budget (see retry strategies).\n- **AIMD in internal clients.** Shrink concurrency on 503s and grow it slowly on success (additive\n  increase, multiplicative decrease, the TCP rule), because that converges on the server's real capacity\n  without either side knowing the number in advance.\n- **Own callers vs external callers.** Enforce this in the shared client library for your own services;\n  for external callers, assume some retry immediately and size the edge rejection path to absorb it.\n\n| Control | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| Rate limiting | caps one caller's share regardless of server health |\n| Backpressure | the signal that the server as a whole is full |\n| Load shedding | the act of dropping work once that signal fires |\n\nA system needs all three.\n\n### Queues as deliberate backpressure\n- **For non-interactive work,** a durable queue with a bounded consumer pool turns rejection into latency:\n  producers enqueue freely, consumers pull at the GPU's pace.\n- **Only with a maximum depth or age;** an unbounded queue is the original problem wearing a different hat.\n\n### Agent runs: admit the run, not the call\nAn agent run is a chain of dependent calls, so admission has to happen at two levels:\n1. **Run admission at the front door.** Decide once, before step 1, whether the run can start. Rejecting\n   a new run costs nothing; aborting a half-finished run wastes every step already paid for.\n2. **Call-level gate inside the run.** Each model call still passes a shared semaphore or token gate, but\n   admitted runs get priority over new runs, so work in progress drains first.\n3. **Bounded fan-out.** Parallel tool calls and sub-agents share a per-run cap, because one planner that\n   spawns 20 parallel searches can starve every other run.\n4. **Budgets per run.** A maximum step count and token budget turn a looping agent into a bounded cost,\n   which makes run-level admission predictable.\n5. **Honour pushback in the loop.** On 429 or 503 the loop waits for `Retry-After` within the run's\n   deadline, then either continues or ends the run with a clear \"busy\" result, never a tight retry loop.","example":"### Admission gate in front of an engine\nA semaphore sized from Little's law, a wait budget after which the request is shed with a `503` and\n`Retry-After`, a disconnect check so no GPU work is done for a caller who left while queued, and the\nqueue-wait histogram that is the real pressure signal.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request, Response\n\napp = FastAPI()\nMAX_CONCURRENT = 32      # Little's law: ~4 req/s × ~8 s latency, trimmed to the engine's real capacity\nWAIT_BUDGET_S = 1.5      # longest an interactive request may queue before we shed it\nsem = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_CONCURRENT)\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/generate\")\nasync def generate(req: Request):\n    body = await req.json()\n    t0 = time.monotonic()\n    try:\n        await asyncio.wait_for(sem.acquire(), timeout=WAIT_BUDGET_S)\n    except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n        # Shed at the edge: costs us microseconds, and tells the caller exactly what to do.\n        raise HTTPException(503, \"overloaded\", headers={\"Retry-After\": \"2\"})\n    queue_wait_ms = (time.monotonic() - t0) * 1000   # export as a histogram: this IS the pressure signal\n    try:\n        if await req.is_disconnected():               # caller gave up while queued: do no GPU work\n            return Response(status_code=499)          # nginx's \"client closed request\" convention\n        return await call_engine(body)               # vLLM / provider call, with its own timeout\n    finally:\n        sem.release()\n```\n\nTuning the two numbers:\n- **Concurrency limit.** Start from Little's law, then lower it until the engine's waiting queue stays\n  near zero under load.\n- **Wait budget.** From the product's latency budget: if users abandon at 5 s and generation takes 3 s,\n  queueing longer than 1.5–2 s is pointless.\n- **In production** the semaphore is usually token-weighted and per-priority, and the same shape appears\n  inside the engine as `--max-num-seqs`.\n\n### Little's law sizing and a token-weighted gate\nSize the limit, then admit by tokens rather than requests. Pure Python, runs as is.\n\n```python\ndef littles_law(rate_per_s, latency_s):\n    return rate_per_s * latency_s                 # L = λW: requests in flight\n\nprint(littles_law(4, 8))    # 32 in flight at steady state\nprint(littles_law(12, 8))   # 96 needed when traffic triples\n\nclass TokenGate:\n    \"\"\"Admit while in-flight tokens (prompt + max_tokens) stay under a budget.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, budget):\n        self.budget, self.used = budget, 0\n    def try_admit(self, prompt_tokens, max_tokens):\n        cost = prompt_tokens + max_tokens\n        if self.used + cost > self.budget:\n            return False                          # caller returns 503 + Retry-After\n        self.used += cost\n        return True\n    def release(self, prompt_tokens, max_tokens):\n        self.used -= prompt_tokens + max_tokens\n\ngate = TokenGate(budget=200_000)\nprint(gate.try_admit(100_000, 1_000))  # True: one long document\nprint(gate.try_admit(2_000, 500))      # True: a chat turn still fits\nprint(gate.try_admit(100_000, 1_000))  # False: a second document would exceed the budget\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: traffic triples with no backpressure\nIllustrative numbers from 4 req/s at 8 s mean latency, engine capped near 32 concurrent.\n\n| Time | Arrivals | Served | Waiting | Wait for a new arrival |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Before | 4/s | 4/s | ~0 | ~0 s |\n| +10 s | 12/s | 4/s | ~80 | ~20 s |\n| +60 s | 12/s | 4/s | ~480 | ~120 s, past a 30 s client timeout |\n| +60 s with a 1.5 s wait budget | 12/s | 4/s | ≤ ~6 | ≤ 1.5 s, the rest get fast 503s |\n\nThe wait grows by about 2 s every second (8 join, 4 leave), so after about 15 s of overload new arrivals\nalready wait past a 30 s client timeout; from then on the GPU serves mostly clients who have left.\n\n### Agent server: run admission plus bounded tool fan-out\nAdmit whole runs with a cheap check, cap in-flight model calls across all runs, and cap parallel tool\ncalls per run. `llm_step` and `run_tool` stand in for your model client and tool executor.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nMAX_RUNS = 8              # runs in progress; size from Little's law on run duration, not call duration\nMAX_LLM_CALLS = 32        # shared with every run: matches the engine or provider concurrency\nMAX_TOOLS_PER_RUN = 4     # parallel tool calls one run may have in flight\nMAX_STEPS = 12            # per-run step budget keeps a looping agent bounded\n\nruns = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_RUNS)\nllm_calls = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_LLM_CALLS)\n\nclass Busy(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Mapped to 503 + Retry-After by the API layer.\"\"\"\n\nasync def start_run(task):\n    if runs.locked():                      # admit or reject before step 1: no work wasted\n        raise Busy(\"agent capacity full\")\n    async with runs:\n        return await agent_loop(task)\n\nasync def agent_loop(task):\n    tools_gate = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_TOOLS_PER_RUN)\n    history = [task]\n    for step in range(MAX_STEPS):\n        async with llm_calls:              # every model call passes the shared gate\n            action = await llm_step(history)\n        if action.final:\n            return action.answer\n        async def one(call):\n            async with tools_gate:         # a planner asking for 20 searches runs 4 at a time\n                return await run_tool(call)\n        history += await asyncio.gather(*(one(c) for c in action.tool_calls))\n    return \"stopped: step budget reached\"\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: ten users, one agent each\nIllustrative: each run takes about 10 steps of ~4 s, and at each step the planner fans out to 6 parallel\nsub-agent model calls. The engine serves about 32 concurrent calls well.\n\n| Setup | Model calls in flight at peak | What users see |\n|---|---|---|\n| No run admission, no fan-out cap | 10 runs × 6 = 60, nearly 2× capacity | everyone's step latency roughly doubles, many runs time out at step 7-9 |\n| Run admission at 8, fan-out cap 4 | 8 runs × 4 = 32, at capacity | 8 runs finish at normal speed, 2 get an instant \"busy, retry in a few seconds\" |\n| Same, plus priority to admitted runs | in-progress steps go first | no run is abandoned halfway, so no tokens are wasted on unfinished work |","patterns":["**Run-level admission for agents.** Agent teams gate the start of a run, cap parallel tool calls and sub-agents per run, and give in-progress runs priority over new ones at the model-call gate. Monitor runs rejected at start versus runs aborted mid-way; the second number should be near zero.","**Semaphore plus wait budget at the API layer.** Use for every route that fronts a GPU or metered quota. Size it with Little's law, set a 1–2 s budget for interactive traffic, return 503 with `Retry-After`. Monitor the queue-wait histogram and rejection rate.","**Engine-side limits that match the gate.** Set `--max-num-seqs` in vLLM so the engine's own waiting queue stays near zero; the application gate then holds the real queue. Monitor vLLM's running and waiting gauges, KV-cache usage and preemption counts.","**Priority lanes on shared GPUs.** When batch and chat share a deployment, give each class its own limit and shed batch first. Monitor rejections per class so batch is not starved forever.","**AIMD in the shared client library.** Internal callers halve their concurrency on a 503 and add one slot per success window. Monitor client-side concurrency over time; a sawtooth shows it working.","**Autoscale on queue wait, not GPU utilisation.** Trigger scale-out when p95 queue wait crosses part of the budget, because utilisation stays near 95% whether the queue is empty or exploding. For hosted models the equivalent signal is your own gate's wait time and the provider's 429 rate."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A user turn is not one request: a ReAct loop often makes 5-20 model calls\n  (illustrative), and parallel tool calls or sub-agents multiply that. Ten users can look like two\n  hundred requests, and a run\n  shed at step 9 has already wasted eight steps of GPU time and tokens.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider 429s and `Retry-After` reaching your agent loop; the\n  concurrency setting on LangChain or LangGraph batch and parallel execution; vLLM's `--max-num-seqs` and\n  waiting gauges when you self-host; the semaphore in your FastAPI agent server.\n- **What you control.** Admit whole runs at the front door, cap parallel tool and sub-agent fan-out, give\n  each run a step and token budget, and make the loop honour `Retry-After` instead of hammering.\n- **What you can skip.** Queueing-theory proofs and CoDel internals. Little's law and \"bound queue time,\n  not queue length\" are enough to size an agent gateway.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosted agent backends on vLLM.** Admission caps and queue-wait budgets keep a burst of agent runs\n  from pushing every run past its client timeout at once.\n- **Multi-tenant agent platforms.** Per-tenant run caps stop one customer's batch job of 500 agent runs from\n  starving everyone else's interactive sessions.\n- **Agents with parallel tool calls or sub-agents.** A fan-out cap per run bounds how many model and tool\n  calls a single user turn can put in flight.\n- **Agents calling hosted providers.** Turning provider 429s into a fast \"busy, retry after N seconds\" at\n  your own front door, instead of letting each agent loop retry blindly.","mini_project":"**Shed load at the run level, not the call level (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show that admitting whole agent runs keeps completed runs high under overload, while\n  per-call admission wastes work on runs that fail midway.\n- **Steps:** build a FastAPI endpoint that runs a fake 8-step agent (each step sleeps to mimic a model\n  call); add a semaphore that admits runs, a queue-wait budget, and a 503 with `Retry-After` when the\n  budget is exceeded; load-test it at 2× capacity with and without run-level admission.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of completed runs, rejected runs and wasted steps for both modes, and\n  run-level admission shows near-zero wasted steps."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A bigger queue absorbs the spike.\"** By Little's law, while arrivals exceed service rate the wait grows every second; past the client timeout every queued request is wasted GPU work plus a retry. Bound queue time and reject the excess fast instead.","**\"High GPU utilisation means we are overloaded.\"** A busy engine can sit near 95% utilisation both with an empty queue (healthy) and with a queue growing every second (minutes from collapse), so the number cannot tell them apart. Queue wait time, the engine's waiting gauge and preemptions are the pressure signals, so alert and autoscale on those.","**\"Rejecting requests is the failure; serving everything is the goal.\"** Under overload, serving everything means serving everyone late, and goodput collapses. Fast 503s with `Retry-After` keep the admitted share at normal latency while autoscaling catches up.","**\"Rate limiting already gives us backpressure.\"** A rate limit caps one caller's share regardless of server health; backpressure signals that the server as a whole is full. Callers well within quota can still overload you together, so you need both, with 429 versus 503 telling them which happened.","**\"Gate every model call and the agent is protected.\"** Rejecting step 7 of a run throws away six paid steps. Admit the whole run before step 1, prioritise in-progress runs at the call gate, and cap per-run fan-out."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request arrives\"] --> S{\"Slot free within wait budget?\"}\n    S -->|\"no\"| X[\"503 + Retry-After\"]\n    S -->|\"yes\"| D{\"Client still connected?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| Q[\"499, no GPU work\"]\n    D -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Engine: prefill and decode\"]\n    E --> O[\"Stream response\"]\n    X -.->|\"backoff with jitter\"| C[\"Client slows down\"]\n    E -.->|\"queue wait, KV usage\"| M[\"Metrics and autoscaler\"]","caption":"Follow a request from arrival: it is shed at the edge if the wait budget runs out, and work starts only\nafter the disconnect check, so no GPU time goes to callers who left. For an agent, \"request\" is the\nwhole run: the gate sits before step 1."},"interview":[{"q":"What is backpressure, and why is an unbounded queue not a solution?","a":"- **Definition:** a stage that is full signals its upstream to slow down, by bounding admission and\n  rejecting the excess quickly.\n- **Why not queue everything:** by Little's law an unbounded queue lets wait time grow without limit, so\n  requests outlive client timeouts, clients retry, and the GPU works for callers who already left."},{"q":"p99 latency is climbing for minutes but the error rate is flat. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect an unbounded queue.** Check queue-wait time and the engine's waiting gauge, not GPU\n  utilisation, which looks the same whether the queue is empty or exploding.\n- **Check preemption and KV-cache usage** to see if the engine is over-admitting.\n- **Fix** with a wait budget and fast 503s at the edge, then lower the engine limit until its own queue\n  stays near zero."},{"q":"Your background research agents and your chat agent share one deployment. When do you queue and when do you reject?","a":"- **Queue when callers can wait:** batch pipelines, offline jobs and background agent runs, behind a\n  durable queue with a bounded consumer pool and a maximum depth or age.\n- **Reject for interactive traffic,** because once a reply takes longer than the user will wait, finishing\n  it is wasted GPU time.\n- **Give each class its own limit** and shed background runs first, monitoring rejections per class so\n  they are not starved forever."},{"q":"During a traffic spike your agent's token spend rises but completed runs fall. What is happening?","a":"- **Runs are shed halfway.** A call-level gate rejects step 7 of a run that already paid for six steps,\n  so tokens are spent with no answer delivered.\n- **Fix with run-level admission:** decide before step 1, give in-progress runs priority at the call gate,\n  and cap parallel tool calls per run so one planner cannot starve the rest.\n- **Check the loop's retry behaviour,** because tight retries on 429 inside the loop add load exactly when\n  the system is full; honour `Retry-After` within the run's deadline."},{"q":"Traffic is 6 req/s at 5 s mean latency, and users abandon after 8 s. How do you size the gate?","a":"- **Concurrency from Little's law:** 6 × 5 = 30 in flight, then lower it under a load test until the\n  engine's own waiting queue stays near zero.\n- **Wait budget from the latency budget:** 8 s minus 5 s of generation leaves at most 3 s; pick 1.5–2 s\n  to leave headroom for long requests, and shed the rest with 503 plus `Retry-After`.\n- **Autoscale on p95 queue wait** crossing part of that budget, because GPU utilisation reads the same\n  whether the queue is empty or exploding."},{"q":"Why can a single concurrency number not protect a mixed workload?","a":"- **Costs differ by orders of magnitude.** One 100k-token document displaces about fifty chat turns, so a\n  request count hides the real load.\n- **Use token-aware admission** (prompt tokens plus `max_tokens`) or an adaptive limit driven by the\n  latency gradient, which tracks shifts in prompt mix and model."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast 503s vs slow timeouts.** Shedding early turns some requests into fast 503s instead of all into slow timeouts. It flips when callers cannot retry (synchronous webhooks, batch jobs with no scheduler), where a longer queue with a strict age limit is kinder.","**Static limit vs adaptive.** A static concurrency limit is predictable and trivial to reason about, but is tuned for one prompt mix; when 50k-token documents arrive it is too high (OOM, preemption) or too low (idle GPU). Token-aware or adaptive limits cost that simplicity back.","**Delay control vs maintenance.** Queue-delay control bounds latency regardless of request size, but the wait budget is a product decision to revisit whenever generation length or the latency SLO changes.","**Durable queue vs explicit rejection.** A durable queue hides overload from producers, right for pipelines and wrong for chat: once a reply takes longer than a user will wait, finishing it is wasted GPU time, so interactive paths need explicit rejection.","**Admitting runs vs admitting calls.** Run-level admission protects finished-run throughput, but it reserves capacity for runs that may idle on slow tools. It flips when tool time dominates: then admit more runs than model-call slots and let the call gate do the real limiting."],"pitfalls":["**Unbounded in-memory queues,** which many frameworks give you by default. Symptom: p99 latency climbs for minutes with a flat error rate, then an out-of-memory crash with no 5xx warning.","**Watching queue length instead of queue time.** Symptom: a \"healthy\" depth of ten while p99 explodes, because those ten are long-document requests.","**Rejecting after expensive work** (after retrieval, prompt assembly or, worst, after prefill). Symptom: GPU at 100% while completed requests per second falls.","**Omitting `Retry-After`, or clients ignoring it.** Symptom: a sawtooth of load spikes at a fixed interval after each shedding episode, as every rejected client retries together.","**Wiring load shedding into the load balancer's health check.** Symptom: total outage during a partial overload, because every instance reports unhealthy at once and is removed. Keep liveness separate from load.","**Shedding agent runs halfway.** Symptom: token spend rises during overload while completed runs fall, because runs are rejected at step 6 or 8 by the call-level gate. Fix: admit at run start and give in-progress runs priority.","**Agent loops that retry 429s immediately.** Symptom: provider 429 rate climbs after each incident and one user's run consumes the whole quota. Fix: honour `Retry-After` in the loop and cap waits by the run's deadline."],"when":"- **Use at every boundary that fronts a GPU or a metered provider quota:** the gateway in front of your\n  API, the application in front of the engine, and the engine's own scheduler limits. For agents, add\n  the run-level gate and the per-run fan-out cap.\n- **Start simple.** Semaphore plus wait budget first; add token weighting when prompt lengths vary widely,\n  and priority tiers when batch and interactive traffic share a deployment.\n- **Measure** queue wait time, rejection rate, in-flight tokens and KV-cache usage. Alert on wait time\n  rather than errors, because it moves first.\n- **Day to day** it is the question \"what happens when traffic doubles for ten minutes\".\n- **Interviews:** walk from Little's law to bounded queues to 503-with-Retry-After, and distinguish it\n  from rate limiting, which protects against a single caller rather than aggregate load.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the admission gate in the API layer, sizes `max_num_seqs` and queue limits on the engine, exports queue-wait histograms, and tests the path by load-testing past capacity.","architect":"Decides where in the chain rejection happens, which traffic classes are shed first, and how the latency SLO translates into a queue wait budget and autoscaling triggers.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"the model got slow at 2 pm\" incident from queue depth and preemption metrics, and negotiates priority tiers between their batch pipeline and their chat product on shared GPUs."},"check":{"q":"Your vLLM-backed API serves 4 requests per second at 8 seconds mean latency. A campaign triples\ntraffic for ten minutes. Walk through what happens with no backpressure, then design the controls you\nwould add and the numbers you would set.","a":"By Little's law steady state needs about 32 concurrent requests; at 12 requests per second it\nneeds 96, so with the engine capped near 32 the queue grows by roughly 8 requests every second. After a\nminute some 480 requests are waiting and a new arrival faces roughly two minutes of queueing (the wait\ngrows by about two seconds every second, since 8 join per second and 4 leave); clients time out at 30 s\nand retry, raising the arrival rate further, while the GPU prefills prompts for abandoned connections.\nUseful throughput falls below the original 4 per second: that is the collapse. With backpressure: keep\nthe engine limit near its true capacity, put a semaphore with a 1–2 s wait budget in front of it, return\n503 with Retry-After for the rest, cancel generation on client disconnect, and shed batch traffic before\ninteractive. Roughly two thirds of requests get a fast rejection, but the third that is served sees\nnormal latency, and queue-wait time becomes the autoscaling trigger. A strong answer also mentions\ntoken-aware admission if prompt lengths vary, and notes that per-tenant rate limiting is a separate\ncontrol for a different problem."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should an admission controller for LLM requests bound queue *time* rather than queue *length*?","options":["Queue length cannot be measured accurately in an asynchronous server","Service times vary by orders of magnitude with prompt and output length, so the same queue length can mean one second or two minutes of waiting","HTTP clients can only communicate time-based limits, not length-based ones","Queue time is cheaper to compute than queue length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A queue of ten short chat turns drains in a second; ten 50k-token document requests take minutes. Length hides this, while measured wait time directly tracks what the caller experiences, which is why delay-based control is the right signal."},{"q":"A server at aggregate capacity must reject a request from a caller who is well within their own quota. Which response is correct?","options":["429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After","503 Service Unavailable with Retry-After","408 Request Timeout","200 with an empty completion and a warning header"],"answer":1,"explanation":"429 states that the caller exceeded their allowance, which is false here and would send them to the wrong fix. 503 says the server is the bottleneck, and Retry-After tells clients when to come back without a synchronized retry storm."},{"q":"Which is the primary reason an unbounded queue in front of a GPU server is harmful rather than merely wasteful?","options":["It makes the engine's continuous batching less efficient","Queued requests consume KV-cache memory before they are scheduled","The server keeps doing expensive work for requests whose clients have already timed out, while their retries add new load","HTTP keep-alive connections are limited to a fixed number per process"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once wait time exceeds client timeouts, every completed request is wasted and every timed-out client retries, so useful throughput falls as load rises. Waiting requests do not yet hold KV memory; the damage is the wasted GPU time and the feedback loop."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/fallbacks","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It! (circuit breaker and stability patterns)","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (Addressing Cascading Failures)","Official docs: Anthropic API errors and rate limits"],"why":"- **Hosted models fail in ways a retry cannot fix.** A provider returns 529 overloaded or 429 for minutes\n  during a demand spike, a region has an incident, a deployment pushes elevated 5xx, a model is\n  deprecated, a content filter refuses, latency triples under load.\n- **Self-hosted engines add their own.** A GPU node dies, a rollout ships a bad build, a long-context\n  burst preempts everything.\n- **Retries assume health a second later.** During an incident there is none: three attempts against a\n  30-second timeout is a 90-second path to the same error.\n- **The alternative is an error.** Sometimes right (a medical triage assistant should fail closed), but\n  for most product surfaces a slightly worse answer beats none.\n- **A naive `try/except` that calls model B fails in production,** for reasons you discover only when A\n  is already down (table below). Fallbacks are a designed subsystem, not an exception handler.\n\n| Naive assumption | What actually happens |\n|---|---|\n| B accepts the same request | B may not support your tool schema, JSON mode or context length |\n| B is good enough | your eval suite never ran on B, so fallback quality is unknown |\n| B has room | A fails for all traffic at once and B's unprovisioned rate limit is exceeded in seconds: the fallback storm |\n| A did nothing | A's timeout may fire after it executed a side effect inside an agent loop, so B duplicates it |\n| B can take over a stream | tokens already streamed from A cannot switch models mid-sentence |","how":"### The ladder\nOrder alternatives by **behavioural distance from the primary, not by cost**. Each rung costs quality, so\nexhaust the cheapest-in-quality options first:\n\n1. **Same model, different endpoint or region.** The major models are available through more than one\n   cloud, which keeps prompts and evals valid.\n2. **Sibling model** from the same family, one size down.\n3. **Different vendor's model** with a translated request.\n4. **Non-generative degraded answer:** cached response, retrieval results without synthesis, a template.\n5. **Explicit error** with a retry affordance.\n\n### Trigger classification\nOnly some failures justify a fallback:\n\n| Failure | Examples | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Transient capacity | 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529 (Anthropic's overloaded status), connection failures, timeouts before the first token | one retry on the same target, then fall back |\n| Request-specific | 400 malformed body, context-length overflow, unsupported parameter | do not fail over: it fails identically on any equivalent target |\n| Context overflow (exception) | prompt too long for this window | may legitimately route to a model with a larger window |\n| Auth and billing | invalid key, exhausted credit | configuration fault: page a human, do not fail over |\n| Latency | time to first token over budget | cancel and try the next rung |\n\n- **Latency is a trigger** because the user cannot tell the difference between slow and down.\n- **Hedging is a softer variant.** Once the primary has been silent past roughly its p95 time to first\n  token, start the fallback in parallel, keep whichever produces a first token first and cancel the other.\n- **Hedge cost.** Only the slowest few percent of calls spawn a hedge, so the extra load is small, but each\n  hedge is billed on both targets until the loser is cancelled.\n\n### Circuit breaker per target\nWithout a breaker, every request during an outage pays the primary's full timeout before falling back, so\nlatency doubles exactly when the system is under stress. The breaker converts \"try and wait\" into \"skip,\nbecause we already know\":\n\n1. **Track the failure *rate*** over a sliding window of recent calls per target.\n2. **Open when the rate crosses a threshold,** with a minimum call count, so three failures out of three at\n   2 a.m. do not trip it.\n3. **While open,** route straight to the fallback.\n4. **After a cooldown, go *half-open*:** let a single probe through; its result decides close or reopen.\n\nHow much a breaker buys depends on how failures arrive:\n- **Slow failures** (a 30-second timeout, a stalled stream) make \"try the primary first\" expensive, so\n  breakers pay off most there.\n- **Fast failures** (a 529 or 503 in tens of milliseconds) cost almost nothing to attempt. For a *partial*\n  outage it is often better to leave the breaker closed and let each request try the primary once,\n  keeping the healthy fraction of capacity in use.\n- **Consecutive-failure counters behave badly.** At a 20% independent error rate, five failures in a row\n  happen only once every few thousand calls, so the breaker almost never opens; at a 60% rate it flaps.\n\n### Compatibility layer\nThe fallback must receive an equivalent request:\n- **Normalise** message formats, translate tool schemas, map parameter names and ranges.\n- **Re-check the context window** using the target's tokenizer.\n- **Know** whether structured output is supported.\n- **Gateways own this translation:** self-built, or products such as LiteLLM, OpenRouter or Portkey.\n- **Evaluate prompts on every rung;** where a model needs a variant prompt, version it alongside the\n  primary's.\n\n### One deadline for the whole chain\n- **Give the request a single deadline,** for example 20 seconds, and hand each rung the remainder rather\n  than its own timeout.\n- **Otherwise chained timeouts add up** to a wait no user will tolerate.\n\n### Keeping the backup real\n- **Provision quota on the fallback deliberately.**\n- **Route a trickle of live traffic** (a few percent) to it, so credentials, quota, prompts and evals are\n  continuously verified. An unexercised fallback is an untested one.\n\n### Observability and streaming\n- **Tag every response** with the target that served it and the reason for any fallback.\n- **Alert when the fallback rate rises,** because quality regressions in fallback mode are invisible\n  otherwise.\n- **Streaming: decide before the first byte.** A failure after tokens were emitted either restarts the\n  response under a new message id (so the client can discard the partial one) or surfaces an error with a\n  retry button.\n\n### Fallbacks inside an agent loop\nAn agent changes three things about the ladder:\n1. **Fall back per step, not per run.** Step 9 failing does not mean restarting at step 1; the transcript\n   so far is the state, so the next rung receives the same history and continues.\n2. **Tool-calling parity is a hard requirement.** Every rung must accept your tool definitions and return\n   well-formed tool calls. A rung that answers in prose ends the run at the next parse, so evaluate tool\n   selection and argument accuracy per rung, not just answer quality.\n3. **Side effects pin the step.** If the primary's tool call may have executed (timeout after dispatch),\n   the fallback must not regenerate and re-run it. Check the tool's idempotency key or its result store\n   first.\n- **Mixed-model transcripts.** After a fallback the history contains another model's turns. Most chat\n  APIs accept that, but provider-specific content (thinking blocks, cached-prompt markers, vendor tool-call\n  ids) may need stripping or translating when the run switches vendor.","example":"### Fallback chain with a rate-based breaker\nThe chain tries each target in order, skips any whose breaker is open, gives each the remaining share of\none deadline, refuses to fail over on request-specific errors, and labels the response with what served\nit. Per-target retries live inside `call`, where the SDK already does them.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom collections import deque\n\nclass Breaker:\n    \"\"\"Opens when >= `threshold` of the last `window` calls failed; one half-open probe per cooldown.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, window=20, min_calls=10, threshold=0.5, cooldown_s=30):\n        self.results, self.min_calls, self.threshold = deque(maxlen=window), min_calls, threshold\n        self.cooldown_s, self.state, self.open_until = cooldown_s, \"closed\", 0.0\n    def available(self):\n        if self.state == \"open\" and time.monotonic() >= self.open_until:\n            self.state = \"half_open\"; return True      # exactly one probe gets through\n        return self.state == \"closed\"\n    def record(self, ok):\n        if self.state == \"half_open\":                  # the probe's result decides\n            self.state = \"closed\" if ok else \"open\"\n            self.results.clear(); self.open_until = time.monotonic() + self.cooldown_s\n            return\n        self.results.append(ok)\n        n, fails = len(self.results), self.results.count(False)\n        if n >= self.min_calls and fails / n >= self.threshold:\n            self.state, self.open_until = \"open\", time.monotonic() + self.cooldown_s\n\nTRANSIENT = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529, \"timeout\", \"connection\"}\nLADDER = [(\"anthropic\", \"primary-model\"), (\"bedrock\", \"primary-model\"), (\"anthropic\", \"smaller-model\")]\nbreakers = {t: Breaker() for t in LADDER}\n\ndef generate(req, deadline_s=20.0):\n    deadline, last_err = time.monotonic() + deadline_s, None\n    for target in LADDER:\n        remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()\n        if remaining <= 0 or not breakers[target].available():\n            continue\n        try:\n            resp = call(target, adapt(req, target), timeout=min(remaining, 10.0))\n        except ProviderError as e:\n            if e.code not in TRANSIENT:                # malformed request, policy, auth: no rung fixes this\n                breakers[target].record(True); raise   # the target was healthy; our request was not\n            breakers[target].record(False); last_err = e; continue\n        breakers[target].record(True)\n        resp.meta = {\"served_by\": target, \"fallback\": target != LADDER[0]}\n        return resp\n    return degraded_response(req, last_err)            # cached/template answer, or an explicit error\n```\n\n### Worked incident trace\nWith a 50% threshold over 20 calls (numbers are starting points to tune against your own error and\nlatency distributions):\n\n| Incident | Breaker | What each request pays |\n|---|---|---|\n| Primary returns 529 on 20% of requests | stays closed | failing requests pay one fast 529 and move to the second endpoint; the 80% that succeed keep using the primary |\n| Primary times out on most calls | window crosses 50% after at least ten calls, opens | for the next 30 seconds, requests go straight to the second endpoint without paying a 10-second timeout |\n| Cooldown ends | half-open | one probe tests recovery and decides close or reopen |\n\n### Why consecutive-failure breakers misbehave\nPure Python, runs as is: the chance that five calls in a row fail at independent error rate p is p⁵.\n\n```python\nfor p in (0.2, 0.6):\n    run5 = p ** 5\n    print(f\"error rate {p:.0%}: P(5 in a row) = {run5:.4f}, about 1 in {round(1 / run5):,} windows\")\n# error rate 20%: P(5 in a row) = 0.0003, about 1 in 3,125 windows  -> almost never opens\n# error rate 60%: P(5 in a row) = 0.0778, about 1 in 13 windows     -> opens and closes repeatedly\n```\n\n### Agent step with a tool-compatible fallback (LangChain)\nBind the tools to *each* model before chaining them, because the fallback wrapper does not carry the\nprimary's tool binding. Only transient errors fail over; a 400 from a bad tool schema still raises.\n\n```python\nimport anthropic\nfrom langchain_anthropic import ChatAnthropic\nfrom langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\n\ntools = [search_tickets, create_ticket]          # your @tool functions\n\nprimary = ChatAnthropic(model=\"primary-model\", timeout=10, max_retries=1).bind_tools(tools)\nsecond = ChatOpenAI(model=\"evaluated-fallback-model\", timeout=10, max_retries=1).bind_tools(tools)\n\n# Transient errors only. Newer SDK versions raise dedicated classes for 503 and 529 (overloaded)\n# that are not InternalServerError subclasses, so include them when the installed SDK defines them.\nTRANSIENT = (\"RateLimitError\", \"InternalServerError\", \"ServiceUnavailableError\",\n             \"OverloadedError\", \"APIConnectionError\")   # APITimeoutError subclasses APIConnectionError\nagent_llm = primary.with_fallbacks(\n    [second],\n    exceptions_to_handle=tuple(getattr(anthropic, n) for n in TRANSIENT if hasattr(anthropic, n)),\n)\n\nmsg = agent_llm.invoke(history)                  # same transcript, either model\nfor i, call in enumerate(msg.tool_calls):        # key is stable across models: run, step, position\n    run_tool_once(call, key=f\"{run_id}:{step}:{i}:{call['name']}\")\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a 15-step support agent during a 529 burst\nIllustrative: primary fails 20% of calls with fast 529s for 30 minutes; each run makes 15 model calls.\n\n| Design | Runs that hit at least one failure | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| No fallback, no retry | 1 − 0.8^15 ≈ 96% | almost every run errors out somewhere |\n| One retry on the same target | per call 0.2² = 4% fail, so 1 − 0.96^15 ≈ 46% | still about half the runs fail |\n| One retry, then a tool-compatible fallback | near zero, if the fallback is healthy and provisioned | runs finish; trace shows mixed served_by tags |\n| Fallback without tool parity | the step \"succeeds\" in prose | run fails at the next tool-call parse instead |","patterns":["**Per-step ladders with tool-call evals.** Agent teams give the planner and tool-calling steps a ladder whose every rung passed the tool-selection and argument evals, and may give summarisation steps a cheaper ladder. Tag each step's span in LangSmith or your tracer with served_by and fallback reason; monitor run success rate by served_by mix.","**Same model in a second region first.** When a provider or cloud offers the model in several regions or platforms, make that rung one, because prompts and evals carry over. Monitor per-target error rate and latency per rung.","**Gateway-owned fallback chains.** Put the ladder, request adaptation and served-by tagging in one AI gateway (self-built, or LiteLLM, OpenRouter or Portkey) instead of in each service. Monitor fallback rate per route and alert when it rises.","**Warm backups with a live trickle.** Send a few percent of real traffic to each fallback all the time and reserve quota sized for the share that would move. Monitor the backup's own 429 rate during primary incidents to catch a fallback storm early.","**Hedge at p95 time to first token.** For latency-sensitive chat, start the next rung in parallel once the primary is silent past its p95, then cancel the loser. Monitor hedge rate and double-billed tokens.","**Quality by served-by tag.** Run the eval suite against every rung and break production quality metrics (JSON parse errors, refusal rate, thumbs-down) down by serving target, because silent model swaps hide there."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A 15-step run touches the provider 15 times, so one bad minute hits far\n  more runs than chat. A fallback model must also speak your tool-calling format, or the run fails at the\n  next step instead of this one.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** LangChain's `.with_fallbacks()` on a chat model or chain, LiteLLM\n  router fallbacks, gateways such as OpenRouter or Portkey, and LangSmith traces where you tag which model\n  served each step.\n- **What you control.** The ladder per step type (planner, tool-argument writer, summariser), which\n  errors fail over, the run's shared deadline, and whether a step that already fired a tool may be\n  replayed on another model.\n- **What you can skip.** Circuit-breaker theory beyond \"rate-based, with a minimum call count, and a\n  half-open probe\". Gateways implement the rest.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Customer-support agents during a provider outage.** A ladder from the primary model to a second\n  provider to a \"we will follow up\" handoff keeps the channel answering instead of erroring.\n- **Cost-tiered agents.** Steps such as summarising tool output fall back to a smaller model under load,\n  while the planner keeps the strongest model.\n- **Agents with side-effecting tools.** Failing over only before a tool fires, and resuming from the\n  recorded tool result after, prevents a duplicate refund or email.\n- **RAG assistants.** Falling back to a cached or retrieval-only answer with sources when generation is\n  unavailable, and labelling it as degraded.","mini_project":"**A fallback ladder that survives tool calls (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** keep a 5-step tool-calling agent completing runs when its primary model fails, without\n  repeating any tool side effect.\n- **Steps:** wrap two model clients behind one function with an ordered ladder; inject failures (timeouts,\n  5xx, 429) on the primary at a set rate; record each executed tool call with an idempotency key so a\n  failed-over step resumes from the recorded result; tag every step with the model that served it.\n- **Done when:** under a 30% injected failure rate most runs complete, no tool runs twice for the same\n  key, and a trace or log shows which model served each step."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A fallback is a try/except that calls model B.\"** Model B may reject your tool schema, JSON mode or context length, may never have been evaluated, and may have no quota when the whole fleet moves at once. A fallback is a designed, evaluated and warm subsystem.","**\"Order the ladder from most to least expensive.\"** Order it by behavioural distance from the primary: the same model in another region keeps prompts and evals valid, so it comes before any cheaper or different model.","**\"Any error should fail over.\"** A 400 or context-length overflow fails identically on an equivalent target, and auth or billing faults need a human. Only transient capacity errors and latency over budget justify the next rung, plus a context overflow routed deliberately to a larger-window model.","**\"A circuit breaker should open after N failures in a row.\"** At a 20% independent error rate five in a row happen about once in 3,125 windows, and at 60% the breaker flaps. Use a failure rate over a window with a minimum call count.","**\"If the agent step failed, the fallback can simply redo it.\"** The primary may have timed out after dispatching a tool call. Check the step's idempotency key or result store first, then continue the same transcript instead of regenerating the side effect."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request with one deadline\"] --> B1{\"Primary breaker closed?\"}\n    B1 -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Call primary\"]\n    B1 -->|\"open\"| B2{\"Second endpoint breaker closed?\"}\n    P -->|\"ok\"| OK[\"Return, tag served_by\"]\n    P -->|\"400, auth, policy\"| ERR[\"Raise, no failover\"]\n    P -->|\"429, 5xx, 529, timeout\"| B2\n    B2 -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Same model, other region\"]\n    B2 -->|\"open\"| SM[\"Smaller evaluated model\"]\n    S -->|\"ok\"| OK\n    S -->|\"transient error\"| SM\n    SM -->|\"ok\"| OK\n    SM -->|\"fails or deadline spent\"| DG[\"Degraded answer or honest error\"]","caption":"Trace one agent step's model call down the ladder: each rung is skipped if its breaker is open,\nrequest-specific errors stop the chain, and every rung shares the run's remaining deadline."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a retry and a fallback, and why do you need both?","a":"- **A retry repeats the same call** on the same target, betting the fault clears in a second; a fallback\n  switches to a different, pre-decided path.\n- **During an incident retries only burn the deadline,** because the target stays unhealthy; the fallback\n  is what actually returns an answer. One quick retry on transient errors, then fall back."},{"q":"During an outage, p99 latency equals the sum of every timeout in your chain. What went wrong?","a":"- **Each rung has its own timeout** instead of sharing one deadline, so a request waits out each one in turn.\n- **No breaker is open,** so every request still pays the primary's full timeout first.\n- **Fix:** one deadline per request with each rung given the remainder, plus a rate-based breaker per target."},{"q":"How do you size and verify quota on a backup provider?","a":"- **Size for the share that will move:** if the primary fails on about 20% of requests, the backup needs\n  quota for about a fifth of peak; for a full outage, all of it.\n- **Verify continuously** by routing a few percent of live traffic to it, and watch its 429 rate during\n  primary incidents, because an untested backup fails in the first real outage."},{"q":"When would you deliberately not add a fallback?","a":"- **When a worse answer is worse than none,** such as medical triage, where failing closed is correct.\n- **When the backup changes compliance posture,** for example moving data to a vendor or region the\n  customer has not approved.\n- **When the fallback has not been evaluated** against the product's accuracy bar, or, for an agent,\n  against your tool-selection and argument evals, since an unevaluated rung fails the run one step later."},{"q":"Your agent's primary model times out at step 9, right after emitting a create_ticket call. How should the fallback behave?","a":"- **Find out whether the tool ran.** The timeout may have fired after dispatch, so look up the step's\n  idempotency key or the ticket system before doing anything else.\n- **If it ran, record the result and continue** on the fallback with that tool result appended; do not let\n  the fallback regenerate the call, which would create a second ticket.\n- **If it did not, the fallback gets the same transcript** and makes its own call, under the same key."},{"q":"How do you choose and validate a fallback model for a tool-calling agent?","a":"- **Tool-calling parity first:** it must accept your tool schemas and structured-output mode, and fit the\n  run's context length with its own tokenizer.\n- **Evaluate per step type** on recorded agent traces: tool selection accuracy, argument validity and\n  task success, not just answer quality.\n- **Keep it warm** with a few percent of live runs and alert on run success rate broken down by served_by."}],"tradeoffs":["**Availability vs known quality.** Availability rises, but fallback quality is lower and, unless you evaluate every rung, unknown. When the fallback cannot meet the product's accuracy bar, failing closed is the better design.","**Fast breakers vs stable breakers.** Breakers cut incident latency sharply but can trip on a noisy error burst and divert healthy traffic. A low threshold and short window react fast but flap; a high threshold and minimum call count are stable but leave users paying timeouts longer. The slower the target's failures, the more \"react fast\" wins.","**Second vendor vs second region.** A second vendor removes correlated outage risk but doubles compliance scope (data residency, DPAs), prompt maintenance and eval cost. A second region of the same provider is cheaper when the risk you fear is regional rather than vendor-wide.","**Degraded answers vs honesty.** Non-LLM answers keep the interface alive, but presenting a cached or templated response as fresh can be worse than an honest error for time-sensitive questions.","**Switching models mid-run vs restarting the run.** Continuing on the fallback keeps the work already done, but the run mixes two models' reasoning styles and tool habits. When the fallback is much weaker at planning, restarting the whole run on one model (or failing it cleanly) gives more predictable results."],"pitfalls":["**A fallback that has never served traffic.** Symptom: fallback rate and error rate spike together in the first real incident, because the key expired, the quota was zero or the schema never matched.","**Failing over on non-transient errors.** Symptom: every request costs two or three calls and still fails, with identical 400s from each target in the trace.","**Independent timeouts per rung.** Symptom: p99 latency during incidents equals the sum of all the timeouts. Use one shared deadline.","**A fallback storm.** Symptom: the secondary returns 429 within seconds of the primary's incident, because the whole fleet moved at once onto unprovisioned quota.","**Silent model swaps.** Symptom: JSON parse errors or refusal-rate changes that correlate with fallback rate, because output formatting quirks differ between models and the parser was tuned to one.","**Falling back after a side effect.** Symptom: duplicate tickets or emails whenever the primary times out mid-tool-call, an idempotency problem the fallback exposes.","**A fallback that never sees the tools.** Symptom: during an incident the agent stops calling tools and answers in prose, or the parser fails on every fallback step. Cause: tools were bound to the primary only, or the fallback model does not support the schema. Fix: bind tools per rung and eval each rung."],"when":"- **Use when** an LLM call sits on a user-facing path with an availability target one provider cannot\n  meet alone, and wherever a cheaper degraded answer is genuinely acceptable. Multi-step agents need it\n  sooner than chat, because each run makes many calls.\n- **Avoid when** a worse answer is worse than no answer, or when the \"fallback\" would silently change\n  compliance posture.\n- **Build order:** same model on a second endpoint first (preserves prompts and evals), a smaller model\n  only after evaluating it, a non-LLM degraded response as the last rung.\n- **Measure** fallback rate, per-target error rate, latency per rung and quality by served-by tag.\n- **Interviews:** classify the trigger, break the circuit, share one deadline, keep the fallback\n  compatible and warm, and explain why streaming and side effects make \"just try B\" unsafe.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the target ladder, breaker state and request adapters, writes evals that run against every rung, and tags traces with the serving target and the fallback reason.","architect":"Owns the policy: which failures fail over, which fail closed, which models are approved as fallbacks, and how much quota is reserved on each backup.","fde":"Negotiates with the customer which degraded behaviour is acceptable for their use case, and verifies that their data-residency rules still hold when traffic moves to a second region or vendor."},"check":{"q":"Your primary provider starts returning 529 overloaded errors for about 20% of requests for half an\nhour. Design the fallback behaviour for a customer-support chat product and explain what you would\ndeliberately avoid doing.","a":"First, classify: 529 is transient and, importantly, fast; it comes back in milliseconds, so\nattempting the primary costs little. At a 20% error rate the primary is still serving most traffic, so I\nwould *not* want the breaker to open and push all load onto the backup; a rate-based breaker with a\nthreshold well above 20% stays closed, and each failing request makes one quick attempt (or one short\njittered retry) and then moves to the next rung. The breaker exists for the case where the primary\ndegrades into timeouts or a majority error rate. Route to the\nsame model on a second endpoint or region first, because prompts and evals carry over; only if that is\nalso degraded, step down to a smaller model that has already been evaluated on support transcripts.\nConfirm the backup has quota for about a fifth of peak traffic, since that is what will move. Give\nthe whole chain one deadline of a few seconds to first token, since chat users abandon quickly. Decide\nbefore streaming starts; a mid-stream failure shows the partial answer with a retry button rather than\nsilently restarting. Tag every reply with served-by and watch quality metrics in fallback mode. Avoid:\nfailing over on 400s, letting the entire fleet shift onto a backup with no pre-provisioned quota, and\nfailing over tool-executing turns without idempotency keys, which would duplicate ticket creation."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why put a circuit breaker in front of the primary model instead of simply catching its errors and calling the fallback?","options":["Breakers are required to satisfy provider terms of service during outages","Without one, every request during an outage waits for the primary's full timeout before falling back, so latency doubles when the system is already under stress","Catching exceptions is unreliable for streaming responses","Breakers reduce the number of distinct fallback targets you need"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A try/except still issues the doomed primary call. A breaker remembers recent failures and skips the primary while open, so fallback requests are fast instead of adding a full timeout to each response; the gain is largest when failures are slow timeouts rather than instant error codes."},{"q":"Which failure should NOT trigger failover to a second provider?","options":["A 503 from the primary provider","A timeout before the first token arrives","A 400 caused by a malformed tool schema in your request","A 529 overloaded error"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A malformed request fails identically everywhere, so failing over doubles cost and latency for nothing; the fix is in your code. The other three are capacity or transient faults where another target can genuinely succeed."},{"q":"What is a \"fallback storm\"?","options":["A loop where the fallback model's errors trigger retries against the primary","The whole fleet shifting onto the backup at once when the primary fails, exceeding quota the backup never had provisioned","Repeated breaker trips caused by a noisy error threshold","Clients retrying with no jitter after a 503"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During a primary outage every request moves simultaneously to the fallback. If its rate limit was sized for a trickle, it fails within seconds, turning one outage into two. Pre-provisioned quota and load shedding prevent it."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/retry-strategies","sources":["Brooker, 2015 — Exponential Backoff and Jitter (AWS Architecture Blog)","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (Handling Overload)","Official docs: Anthropic SDK (retries and timeouts)","Dean & Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale"],"why":"- **Provider APIs fail a small fraction of the time by design:** 429 when shared capacity is tight, 529\n  or 503 when overloaded, 5xx during deployments, connection resets through proxies, timeouts on long\n  generations.\n- **One retry fixes most of it.** At a 1% independent failure rate and 100,000 calls a day that is 1,000\n  user-visible errors; a single retry cuts it to about 10, because two independent failures have\n  probability 0.01%. That is why every SDK retries by default.\n- **Correlated failures break the arithmetic,** and during an incident they always are. Each retry is new\n  load on a saturated system; three immediate retries quadruple offered load.\n- **Layers multiply.** An SDK retrying twice (3 attempts), a service retrying three times (4) and a\n  browser retrying three times (4) turn one click into up to 3 × 4 × 4 = 48 attempts. That retry storm\n  can turn a 30-second blip into an outage lasting many minutes.\n- **LLM calls raise the stakes.** Each attempt costs seconds and money, a timed-out call may already have\n  done its work (or run a tool with side effects), and a stream cannot be retried once tokens reach the\n  user. The question is which failures, how often, how spaced, under what limit, with what duplicate\n  guarantee.","how":"### Classify first\n| Retry | Do not retry |\n|---|---|\n| 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504 | 400 (malformed request, context-length overflow) |\n| 529 (Anthropic's overloaded status) | 401 and 403 (credentials) |\n| connection errors | 404 (wrong model id) |\n| timeouts, only when the operation is idempotent | 413, 422 and content-policy refusals |\n\n- **Non-retryable errors fail identically next time.**\n- **A 429 needs a second look:** a per-minute rate limit clears in seconds, a monthly quota exhaustion does\n  not, and `Retry-After` tells you which.\n\n### Exponential backoff with jitter\n1. **Grow the wait geometrically:** before attempt *n*, wait `min(cap, base × 2ⁿ)`, so a persistent fault\n   quickly spreads retries thin.\n2. **Add jitter,** because pure exponential backoff synchronizes: every client that failed at the same\n   instant retries at the same instant, in waves at 1 s, 2 s, 4 s.\n3. **Pick a jitter style:**\n   - *Full jitter:* uniform between 0 and the exponential ceiling.\n   - *Decorrelated jitter:* uniform between the base and three times the previous wait, capped.\n- **Evidence:** Brooker's simulations showed both complete the same work with far fewer total calls than\n  un-jittered backoff, which is why full jitter is a common library default.\n\n### Honour the server's schedule\n- **Use `Retry-After` instead of your own schedule** when present: the server knows when capacity\n  returns, and waiting less is wasted load that may count against you.\n- **Parse both forms:** a number of seconds or an HTTP date.\n- **Still cap it.** A `Retry-After` longer than the request's remaining deadline means \"give up or fall\n  back now\", not \"sleep\".\n- **Slow down before the first 429.** Providers also return remaining-quota and reset headers; an outbound\n  rate limiter can read them.\n\n### Caps and budgets\n| Control | Scope | Typical setting | What it bounds |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Max attempts | per request | two or three | worst case for one call |\n| Total deadline (includes backoff sleeps) | per request | from latency budget | a 30-second provider timeout cannot become a two-minute wait |\n| Retry budget | per client | retries below roughly 10% of recent requests (the SRE book's figure) | aggregate amplification to about 1.1× instead of 3× |\n\n- **Budget as a token bucket.** Every successful request deposits 0.1 tokens, every retry spends one, and\n  retries stop when the bucket is empty.\n- **Normal operation:** the bucket stays full and nothing changes.\n- **Outage:** nothing succeeds, so it drains after a few retries and the client degrades to one attempt\n  per request, exactly what you want from a fleet hammering a saturated provider.\n\nWorked numbers, a fleet at 200 requests per second when the provider goes fully down:\n\n| Policy | Load on the provider | During recovery |\n|---|---|---|\n| Three attempts, no budget | 600 requests per second | every rejected request comes back twice more, so partial recovery meets up to triple load and can stay pinned in overload |\n| 10% budget | about 220 | offered load is near real demand from the start; successes refill the budget only as the provider heals |\n\n### Retry at one layer\n- **Know what the SDK does.** The official Anthropic and OpenAI SDKs retry twice by default with\n  exponential backoff and honour `Retry-After` (check the current defaults for your version).\n- **Either disable it and own the policy, or let it be the only retrying layer;** two layers multiply.\n\n### LLM-specific rules\n- **Tight time-to-first-token timeout plus a retry** beats a long total timeout plus a retry, because\n  stalled requests rarely recover and the user is waiting.\n- **Never retry a stream after the first token was forwarded;** restart it as a new message id so the\n  client can discard the partial one.\n- **Give every retried operation an idempotency key,** so a retried tool call does not send a second email.\n- **Prompt caching makes retried prefill cheaper,** since the retry reads the prefix the first attempt\n  wrote, provided it got far enough (on Anthropic an entry becomes readable once the first response\n  begins).\n- **Hedged requests** start a second attempt once the first exceeds the p95 latency and keep whichever\n  finishes first. Only the slowest 5% of calls spawn a hedge, so tail latency drops for a few percent of\n  extra load; firing immediately would double it. It needs cancellation of the loser (Dean and Barroso's\n  \"tail at scale\" pattern).\n- **A wrong answer is not a transient failure.** Validation-driven regeneration is a separate loop with\n  its own bound, usually with a changed prompt or temperature.\n\n### Retry layers in an agent loop\n| Layer | What fails | Who should handle it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Model transport | 429, 5xx, 529, connection reset | the SDK, as the single retrying layer |\n| Framework node | an exception escaping a graph node | a node retry policy only if the SDK does not already retry; otherwise none |\n| Tool transport | a tool's HTTP API times out or returns 503 | the tool wrapper, with jittered backoff and an idempotency key |\n| Tool semantics | bad arguments, \"not found\", permission denied | return the error to the model as the tool result, so it can change the call |\n| Output validation | unparseable tool arguments or JSON | a bounded re-ask with the validation error, usually once or twice |\n\n- **Semantic errors are not retried, they are reported.** The model sees \"customer 4412 not found\" and\n  can search instead; a blind retry would fail identically.\n- **Count every layer against one run budget.** A cap on total model calls and tool calls per run is\n  the agent version of the retry budget, because it bounds what any loop of retries can cost.","example":"### Retry helper with full jitter, Retry-After and a budget\nThe helper decides by status code, prefers the server's `Retry-After`, otherwise sleeps with full jitter\nunder a cap, refuses to exceed the request's deadline, and consults a process-wide retry budget so an\nincident cannot multiply load. In an async service replace `time.sleep` with `await asyncio.sleep`.\n\n```python\nimport random, time\n\nRETRYABLE = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529}\nBASE_S, CAP_S, MAX_ATTEMPTS, DEADLINE_S = 0.5, 8.0, 3, 30.0\n\nclass RetryBudget:\n    \"\"\"Process-wide: retries may not exceed `ratio` of recent requests (use a sliding window in prod).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, ratio=0.1): self.ratio, self.requests, self.retries = ratio, 0, 0\n    def allow(self): return self.retries < self.ratio * max(self.requests, 10)\n\nbudget = RetryBudget()\n\ndef call_with_retry(send):\n    deadline = time.monotonic() + DEADLINE_S\n    budget.requests += 1\n    for attempt in range(MAX_ATTEMPTS):\n        try:\n            return send()                                   # raises ApiError(status, retry_after)\n        except ApiError as e:\n            last_try = attempt == MAX_ATTEMPTS - 1\n            if e.status not in RETRYABLE or last_try or not budget.allow():\n                raise\n            if e.retry_after is not None:                   # the server knows when capacity returns\n                delay = min(float(e.retry_after), CAP_S * 4)\n            else:\n                delay = random.uniform(0, min(CAP_S, BASE_S * 2 ** attempt))   # full jitter\n            if time.monotonic() + delay > deadline:\n                raise                                       # a retry that cannot finish in time is waste\n            budget.retries += 1\n            time.sleep(delay)\n```\n\n- **`retry_after` is assumed to be seconds already.** If the header carries the HTTP-date form, convert\n  it first with `email.utils.parsedate_to_datetime` and subtract the current time, or `float()` raises.\n\n### Token-bucket retry budget, simulated through an outage\nPure Python, runs as is. Successes deposit 0.1, retries spend 1; during a full outage the bucket drains\nand the fleet falls back to one attempt per request.\n\n```python\nclass TokenBucketBudget:\n    def __init__(self, capacity=10.0, deposit=0.1):\n        self.tokens, self.capacity, self.deposit = capacity, capacity, deposit\n    def on_success(self):\n        self.tokens = min(self.capacity, self.tokens + self.deposit)\n    def try_retry(self):\n        if self.tokens >= 1:\n            self.tokens -= 1\n            return True\n        return False\n\nb = TokenBucketBudget()\ncalls = 0\nfor _ in range(200):                 # 200 requests while the provider is fully down\n    calls += 1                       # first attempt always goes out\n    for _ in range(2):               # up to 2 retries per request\n        if not b.try_retry():\n            break\n        calls += 1\nprint(calls)   # 210: 10 retries drained the bucket, then one attempt per request (vs 600 with no budget)\n```\n\n### Worked amplification audit\nOne user click traced through a stack that retries at three layers, then after the fix:\n\n| Layer | Attempts before | Attempts after |\n|---|---|---|\n| Browser | 4 (retries three times) | 1 (no retries) |\n| Service | 4 (retries three times) | 1 per target (breaker and fallback instead) |\n| SDK | 3 (retries twice) | 3 (the only retrying layer) |\n| Worst case per click | 3 × 4 × 4 = 48 | 3 per target tried, so 6 if the fallback rung also runs |\n\n### Agent tool wrapper: retry transport, report semantics\nTransport failures are retried with jitter under the same key; semantic failures go back to the model as\nthe tool result. `ToolHTTPError` and `send_email_api` stand in for your tool client.\n\n```python\nimport random, time\n\nTRANSIENT = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504}\n\nclass RunBudget:\n    \"\"\"Caps every call a run makes, retries included.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, max_calls=40): self.left = max_calls\n    def spend(self):\n        self.left -= 1\n        if self.left < 0:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"run budget exhausted: stop and report\")\n\ndef call_tool(fn, args, key, budget, attempts=3, base=0.5, cap=4.0):\n    \"\"\"Returns a tool-result dict the agent appends to its transcript.\"\"\"\n    for attempt in range(attempts):\n        budget.spend()                                  # every attempt counts against the run\n        try:\n            return {\"ok\": True, \"result\": fn(**args, idempotency_key=key)}   # same key on every attempt\n        except ToolHTTPError as e:\n            if e.status in TRANSIENT and attempt < attempts - 1:\n                time.sleep(random.uniform(0, min(cap, base * 2 ** attempt)))  # full jitter\n                continue\n            # Semantic error, or retries exhausted: let the model decide what to do next.\n            return {\"ok\": False, \"error\": f\"{e.status}: {e.message}\", \"retryable\": e.status in TRANSIENT}\n\nrun_budget = RunBudget(max_calls=40)                  # shared by every model and tool call in this run\nresult = call_tool(send_email_api, {\"to\": \"a@example.com\", \"body\": \"...\"},\n                   key=f\"{run_id}:step4:email\", budget=run_budget)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: layered retries in a 10-step agent during a provider blip\nIllustrative: the provider fails every attempt for 20 seconds; the agent is at step 6.\n\n| Layers that retry | Attempts for step 6 alone | What the trace shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| SDK 3 attempts × LangGraph node 3 attempts × user regenerate 2 | 3 × 3 × 2 = 18 | 18 model spans for one step, then the whole run restarts |\n| SDK only (node retry off), then fallback | 3 then 1 on the fallback | 4 spans, run continues from step 6 |\n| Plus a per-run budget of 40 calls | never above 40 for the run | runaway loops stop with a clear \"budget exhausted\" result |","patterns":["**Errors as observations, plus a run budget.** Agent teams retry only transport failures in code, return semantic tool errors to the model as tool results, bound output re-asks to one or two, and cap total calls per run. Monitor calls per run, tool error rate by tool, and runs ended by the budget.","**SDK as the single retry layer.** Keep the official SDK's retries and set them explicitly, for example `max_retries` and `timeout` on the Anthropic or OpenAI Python client, and remove retries above it. Monitor the amplification factor (provider calls per user request).","**Process-wide retry budget.** Wrap outbound calls in a token bucket or sliding-window budget of about 10% of recent requests. Monitor budget exhaustion events; they should appear only during incidents.","**Tight TTFT timeout for chat.** Set a short time-to-first-token budget, retry once, then fall back, because a stalled stream rarely recovers. Monitor TTFT p95 and success-after-retry rate.","**Quota-aware pacing.** Read the provider's remaining-quota and reset headers into an outbound rate limiter so the client slows before the first 429. Monitor 429 rate and remaining-quota headroom.","**Batch jobs retry through the queue.** For pipelines, cap backoff in minutes and re-enqueue with a delay instead of sleeping in a worker, with an idempotency key per item. Monitor retries per job and dead-letter queue depth."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Retry layers multiply inside a loop: SDK retries × framework node\n  retries × the model re-calling a failed tool × the user pressing \"regenerate\". A run that should cost 10\n  calls can cost 100, and a retried tool call can send a second email.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `max_retries` and `timeout` on the Anthropic and OpenAI clients;\n  LangChain's `.with_retry()`; LangGraph's per-node `RetryPolicy`; tenacity around tool functions; and\n  LangSmith traces, where stacked retries are visible as repeated spans.\n- **What you control.** Which single layer retries transport errors, which tool errors go back to the\n  model as observations instead, a per-run retry and step budget, and idempotency keys on every\n  side-effecting tool.\n- **What you can skip.** Comparing jitter formulas in depth. Use full jitter, honour `Retry-After`, and\n  spend the attention on layering and budgets.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents on rate-limited provider APIs.** One retry layer that honours `Retry-After` keeps runs alive\n  through 429 bursts without multiplying load.\n- **Tool calls to flaky internal services.** Transient tool errors are retried in code, while validation\n  errors go back to the model as observations it can correct.\n- **Side-effecting agent actions.** Retries on payment, email or ticket tools carry an idempotency key, so\n  a timeout never becomes a duplicate action.\n- **Batch evaluation runs.** A retry budget stops an eval sweep of thousands of agent runs from turning a\n  provider hiccup into a self-inflicted outage.","mini_project":"**Find and collapse stacked retries (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure how many model calls one agent run really makes when every layer retries, then fix it.\n- **Steps:** build a small tool-calling loop with SDK retries, a tenacity wrapper on the tool and a\n  framework-level retry; inject a 20% transient failure rate; count attempts per run; then move transport\n  retries to one layer with full jitter and a per-run retry budget.\n- **Done when:** you have a before/after table of attempts per run and p95 latency, and the after column\n  shows attempts close to the step count with no duplicate tool side effects."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"More retries make the system more reliable.\"** That holds only for independent failures. During an incident failures are correlated, so three attempts per request triple load on a saturated provider; a retry budget of about 10% keeps amplification near 1.1×.","**\"Exponential backoff is enough on its own.\"** Every client that failed at the same instant computes the same schedule and returns in waves at 1, 2 and 4 seconds. Full jitter spreads those waves.","**\"Adding retries at my layer is harmless.\"** Layers multiply: 3 SDK attempts × 4 service attempts × 4 browser attempts is 48 calls for one click. Pick one retrying layer, usually the SDK, and use a breaker and fallback above it.","**\"A timeout means the call did nothing, so retrying is safe.\"** A timed-out model or tool call may already have run its side effect. Retry only under the same idempotency key, or reconcile first.","**\"Every failed tool call should be retried in code.\"** Retry transport faults (5xx, timeouts) in code, but return semantic errors such as \"customer 4412 not found\" to the model as the tool result, because a blind retry fails identically and the model can change the call."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"Call failed\"] --> C{\"Retryable status?\"}\n    C -->|\"no: 400, 401, 404, 422\"| E[\"Raise to caller\"]\n    C -->|\"yes\"| A{\"Attempts left?\"}\n    A -->|\"no\"| FB[\"Fallback or error\"]\n    A -->|\"yes\"| B{\"Retry budget allows?\"}\n    B -->|\"no\"| FB\n    B -->|\"yes\"| W[\"Wait: Retry-After or full jitter\"]\n    W --> D{\"Fits remaining deadline?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| FB\n    D -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Retry the call\"]\n    R -.->|\"fails again\"| F","caption":"Each failure passes through four gates in order; any \"no\" ends the loop with an error or a fallback\ninstead of another attempt."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is exponential backoff without jitter not enough?","a":"- **Backoff spreads one client's retries,** but every client that failed at the same instant computes the\n  same schedule and retries in synchronized waves at 1, 2 and 4 seconds.\n- **Jitter randomizes each wait** (full jitter: uniform from 0 to the ceiling), flattening the waves and,\n  in Brooker's simulations, completing the same work with far fewer calls."},{"q":"One user click produces 40 provider calls in a trace. How do you find and fix it?","a":"- **Look for stacked retries:** browser, service and SDK each retrying multiplies attempts (3 × 4 × 4 = 48\n  worst case).\n- **Fix by choosing one retrying layer,** usually the SDK, and replace retries above it with a breaker\n  and fallback.\n- **Add a retry budget** so even a misconfigured layer cannot amplify load during an incident."},{"q":"Your agent's send_email tool timed out. Should the loop retry it?","a":"- **Not unless the operation is idempotent,** because the timed-out call may already have sent the email or\n  created the ticket. Without an idempotency key, surface the timeout instead.\n- **With a key, retry under the same key,** so the email provider deduplicates; never let the model\n  regenerate the call with a fresh key.\n- **With no key, reconcile before acting:** look the effect up in the target system (sent-mail log, ticket\n  search by run id), then record the result or report the timeout to the model, rather than retrying blind."},{"q":"Design the retry policy for a LangGraph agent whose nodes call a hosted model and three HTTP tools.","a":"- **One layer for model transport:** keep the SDK's `max_retries` and leave node retry policies off for\n  model nodes (or the reverse), so attempts do not multiply.\n- **Tool wrappers retry 5xx and timeouts** with full jitter and idempotency keys; semantic errors return to\n  the model as tool results.\n- **A per-run budget** on model and tool calls, plus a fallback model after retries, bounds cost; LangSmith\n  traces confirm the amplification factor."},{"q":"The provider goes fully down and your fleet sends 200 requests per second. What load does it see?","a":"- **Three attempts, no budget:** about 600 requests per second, and up to triple load during partial\n  recovery, which can keep the provider pinned in overload.\n- **A 10% retry budget:** about 220, close to real demand, because the bucket drains after a few retries\n  and refills only as successes return."},{"q":"How do you handle a 429 differently from a 503?","a":"- **A 429 can mean two things:** a per-minute rate limit that clears in seconds, or quota exhaustion that\n  does not. `Retry-After` and the reset headers tell you which.\n- **A 503 is server-side capacity:** retry with jittered backoff, then fall back. In both cases cap the\n  wait at the remaining deadline."}],"tradeoffs":["**Success rate vs amplification.** Retries raise success rates when failures are independent but amplify load when correlated. The retry budget makes the trade safe; without it the first incident proves the point.","**Provider-friendly vs user-friendly caps.** A higher backoff cap is gentler on the provider but worse for the user. Interactive features: one or two short retries, then fall back. Batch pipelines: cap in minutes and let the queue absorb it.","**Hedging vs load.** Hedged requests cut tail latency but add load (a few percent when fired at the p95, up to double when fired immediately) and need cancellation. They pay off only when time-to-first-token variance is high and the call is cheap relative to the user's time.","**Retrying timeouts vs duplicates.** Retrying after timeouts recovers from stalls but risks duplicated effects; without idempotency keys the safe choice is to surface the timeout.","**Model-driven recovery vs code retries.** Handing a tool error to the model lets it fix arguments or pick another tool, but each recovery is a full model call and can loop. Code retries are cheaper for pure transport faults; the model is better only when the request itself must change."],"pitfalls":["**Retrying 400s and context-length errors.** Symptom: the identical error three times in a row in the logs and triple the latency on every such request.","**No jitter.** Symptom: load spikes at 1, 2 and 4 seconds after an incident starts, a sawtooth on the provider's error graph.","**Stacked retries across SDK, service and client.** Symptom: one user action producing 20 or more provider calls in a single trace.","**Ignoring `Retry-After`.** Symptom: long streaks of 429s whose remaining-quota headers never recover, because your retries keep spending the refill.","**`time.sleep` inside an async handler.** Symptom: every in-flight request stalls whenever one request backs off.","**Retrying inside an open stream.** Symptom: duplicated paragraphs appear in the user interface.","**Framework retries on top of SDK retries.** Symptom: a LangSmith trace shows nine model spans for one agent step during a blip. Fix: turn retries off in either the node policy or the client, not both.","**Retrying a semantic tool error in code.** Symptom: the same \"not found\" error three times, then the run fails without the model ever seeing it. Fix: return it as the tool result."],"when":"- **Always have an explicit policy** for every outbound LLM call, even if it is \"the SDK's two attempts\n  and nothing else\".\n- **Choose numbers from the latency budget:** interactive paths get one or two retries with a small cap,\n  then a fallback; background jobs get more attempts, a larger cap and a queue to wait in.\n- **Pair retries** with idempotency keys for side effects, a circuit breaker when the dependency can be\n  down for minutes, and a rate limiter so retries never push you over quota.\n- **Measure** retry rate, success-after-retry rate and the amplification factor (provider calls per user\n  request).\n- **Interviews:** name classification, exponential backoff with full jitter, `Retry-After`, per-request\n  caps plus a global budget, and explain why layering retries is dangerous.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures or replaces SDK retries, implements jittered backoff and a budget, and makes sure async code never blocks the event loop while waiting.","architect":"Sets the policy per traffic class, decides which layer owns retries, and defines the amplification factor the system may impose on providers during incidents.","fde":"Traces a customer's \"the API is flaky\" complaint to stacked retries in their integration and tunes their client against the provider's Retry-After and quota headers."},"check":{"q":"You are shipping a chat feature that calls a hosted LLM through an SDK that already retries twice.\nDescribe the retry policy you would deploy end to end and justify each number.","a":"I would make the SDK the only retrying layer for transport-level failures, keep its default of two\nattempts, and set its timeout from the product's latency budget rather than the long default, since a\nchat user leaves after a few seconds. Retries apply only to 408, 429, 5xx, 529 and connection errors; a\n400 or a context-length error goes straight to the caller. Backoff is exponential with full jitter and\nhonours Retry-After. Above the SDK I add a circuit breaker and a fallback rather than more retries, so an\noutage costs one short wait and then a different target, and a process-wide retry budget of about 10% so\nthat an incident cannot triple our load on the provider. Every turn carries an idempotency key, which\nmakes retrying a timed-out tool call safe, and streaming responses are never retried after the first\ntoken; a failure there becomes a new message. I would monitor the amplification factor to prove the\npolicy holds."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why add random jitter to exponential backoff?","options":["It shortens the average wait so users see lower latency","It desynchronizes clients that failed at the same moment, so their retries do not arrive in simultaneous waves","It is required for the server's Retry-After header to be respected","It prevents the exponential delay from exceeding the cap"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without jitter every client that failed together retries together at 1, 2 and 4 seconds, recreating the overload. Spreading each wait uniformly over the window flattens those waves; Brooker's simulations showed it also reduces total calls."},{"q":"Which of these should be retried against the same endpoint without changing the request?","options":["A 503 Service Unavailable","A 400 reporting that the prompt exceeds the context window","A 401 Unauthorized","A 422 reporting an invalid tool schema"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A 503 is a capacity signal that will likely clear. The other three describe defects in the request or credentials that will fail identically on every attempt, so retrying only adds latency and cost."},{"q":"What does a client-wide retry budget provide that a per-request maximum attempt count does not?","options":["A guarantee that no single request waits longer than the deadline","Protection against retrying non-idempotent operations","A bound on aggregate load amplification when every request is failing at once","Automatic detection of which errors are transient"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Three attempts per request still means 3× load when all requests fail together. A budget limits retries to a fraction of recent traffic, so during an incident the extra load stays near 10% instead of 200%."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/rate-limiting","sources":["Official docs: Anthropic API rate limits","Official docs: OpenAI API rate limits","IETF HTTPAPI working group — RateLimit header fields for HTTP (draft)"],"why":"- **The resource is shared, finite and expensive.** A provider gives your organisation a quota, typically\n  requests and tokens per minute per model, and when it is exceeded the 429s hit every caller in the\n  organisation. A self-hosted GPU has a fixed token budget per second.\n- **One bad caller hurts everyone.** Without an inbound limiter, one customer's retry loop or one agent\n  stuck in a tool cycle exhausts the shared allowance, every other customer sees errors, and the bill\n  arrives anyway.\n- **\"Let the provider's 429 be our limiter\" fails four ways.** The blast radius is the whole organisation;\n  it is reactive (requests already queued, latency already spent); you cannot prioritise, because the\n  provider does not know which tenants matter; and every rejected request comes back as a retry.\n- **\"Limit requests per minute like any web API\" fails too.** One request can carry 200 tokens or 200,000,\n  so a request limit is gamed (pack more work per call) and does not protect the resource that runs out:\n  tokens.\n- **So LLM rate limiting must** be cost-aware, estimate costs before they are known, and run in two\n  directions at once.","how":"### Units\n| Who | Units limited |\n|---|---|\n| Providers | requests per minute, tokens per minute (sometimes input and output separately, sometimes per day too), occasionally concurrency |\n| Your inbound limiter | per-tenant requests, per-tenant tokens and, for agents, steps or dollars per task |\n\nThe exact names and tiers vary by vendor and are published in their docs; mirror them inbound.\n\n### Algorithms, and why the token bucket wins here\n| Algorithm | How it works | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Token bucket** | capacity *B*, refills at *r* units per second; admit a request costing *c* if the bucket holds at least *c*, then remove it | bursts up to *B* may exceed an upstream's own windowing |\n| **Leaky bucket** | requests drain at a fixed rate, smoothing output; equivalent in enforcement (the GCRA formulation) | reshapes bursts into delay rather than admitting them |\n| **Fixed window** | a counter per minute | allows 2× the limit across a boundary: full allowance at 0:59 and again at 1:00 |\n| **Sliding window log** | store every request's timestamp; exact | memory grows with traffic |\n| **Sliding window counter** | weight the previous window's count by its overlap with the current one | approximate, but constant memory; good enough for most inbound limits |\n\n- **Why token bucket:** it permits bursts up to *B* while enforcing a long-run average of *r*, and its\n  state is two numbers (level, last refill time).\n- **It fits LLM traffic,** which is bursty and cost-weighted; the \"tokens\" in the name map directly onto\n  LLM tokens if you set the units that way.\n\n### Reserve, then settle\nCost = input tokens (countable before sending) + output tokens (known only at the end, but bounded by\n`max_tokens`).\n\n1. **Reserve** input plus an output estimate at admission.\n2. **Settle** when the response arrives: refund the difference, or *debit* when the actual exceeds the\n   estimate, letting the bucket go into debt that later refill pays off. Refunding in only one direction\n   turns every underestimate into silent overspend.\n\n| Reservation | Gain | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Full `max_tokens` | never overshoots | under-uses quota when clients set generous limits |\n| Expected output length | uses quota well | occasionally overshoots and takes a provider 429 |\n\n- **Pick by how expensive an overshoot is for you.**\n- **Generous `max_tokens` hurts everyone:** clients who set it far above their needs shrink everyone's\n  headroom.\n- **Check your provider's accounting** before sizing reservations: some count cached prompt tokens\n  differently toward input-token limits, and the exact accounting is vendor-specific.\n\nWorked numbers in a bucket with 80,000 tokens of capacity:\n\n| Event | Bucket effect |\n|---|---|\n| Request reserves a 20,000-token prompt plus `max_tokens` 4,000 | takes 30% of the burst at once |\n| It actually generates 600 tokens | settlement returns 3,400 |\n| Three such requests arrive together | they hold 72,000 of the 80,000; the fourth waits for refill (or for the first three to settle and refund): the limiter doing its job |\n| A single 100,000-token request | can never fit: reject with a non-retryable error, or size the bucket above the largest request you intend to accept |\n\n### Distributed enforcement\nSeveral replicas share one provider quota, so state must be shared or the quota partitioned:\n\n| Option | How | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shared state | Redis, with check-and-decrement done atomically in a Lua script | a round trip and a dependency |\n| Static partitioning | each of three replicas gets a third | wastes headroom whenever load is uneven |\n| Central gateway | one process enforces policy | the usual home for per-tenant policy |\n\n- **Atomicity matters** because two replicas that read, decide and then write will both admit the last\n  unit.\n\n### The response contract and the feedback loop\n- **Inbound rejections return 429 with `Retry-After`** and remaining-limit headers. A standard set of\n  header names is being defined by the IETF; providers currently use their own.\n- **Outbound, read the provider's remaining-quota and reset headers** and slow down before the first 429,\n  decreasing the local rate when headroom is thin.\n- **Keep 429 for \"you exceeded your allowance\" and 503 for \"we are out of capacity\";** clients fix them\n  differently.\n\n### Fairness and layering\n- **Per-tenant buckets** sized from the plan.\n- **A global bucket** from the provider quota minus a safety margin: running at around 80% leaves room\n  for retries and bursts.\n- **Priority borrowing:** high-priority traffic borrows from the global bucket before low-priority does.\n- **Refund across layers:** if the tenant bucket admits but the global bucket then rejects, return the\n  tenant's reservation, or the tenant is charged for work that never ran.\n- **More quota:** multiple keys, workspaces or regions multiply quota when a single bucket is not enough.\n\n### Budgets for agent runs\nA per-request bucket cannot see that twenty requests belong to one runaway run, so agent platforms add a\nthird layer keyed on the run id:\n\n| Budget | Why | What happens at the limit |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Steps per run** | stops tool cycles and planner loops | end the run with a clear \"step limit reached\" result |\n| **Tokens per run** | each step re-sends the growing transcript, so cost per step climbs | summarise and stop, or ask the user to continue |\n| **Dollars per run or per tenant per day** | ties the limit to the bill, across models with different prices | refuse new runs, keep running ones to a safe stop |\n| **Parallel tool calls or sub-agents per run** | fan-out multiplies calls inside one step | queue the extra branches inside the run |\n\n- **Reserve per step, settle per step.** Each model call in the loop reserves prompt tokens plus\n  `max_tokens` from the tenant and global buckets and settles on the usage the provider returns.\n- **A rejection mid-run is expensive,** because earlier steps are already paid for. Check the run budget\n  and tenant headroom at admission, before step 1, so a run that cannot finish is refused up front.","example":"### Cost-aware token bucket with reserve and settle\nCapacity is ten seconds of refill so short bursts are absorbed, the refill rate is 80% of an\nillustrative provider limit, and unused reservations are refunded once the real output length is known.\nIn production the bucket lives in Redis with `try_reserve` as an atomic script.\n\n```python\nimport time, threading\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    \"\"\"Cost-aware limiter: `capacity` units, refilled continuously at `rate` units/second.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, capacity: float, rate: float):\n        self.capacity, self.rate = capacity, rate\n        self.level, self.updated = capacity, time.monotonic()\n        self.lock = threading.Lock()\n    def _refill(self):\n        now = time.monotonic()\n        self.level = min(self.capacity, self.level + (now - self.updated) * self.rate)\n        self.updated = now\n    def try_reserve(self, cost: float) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Reserve `cost` now. Returns 0 if admitted, else seconds until it would fit.\"\"\"\n        if cost > self.capacity:              # would never fit: reject outright, do not say \"retry later\"\n            raise ValueError(\"request larger than bucket capacity\")\n        with self.lock:\n            self._refill()\n            if self.level >= cost:\n                self.level -= cost\n                return 0.0\n            return (cost - self.level) / self.rate\n    def settle(self, reserved: float, actual: float):\n        with self.lock:                       # refund unused quota, or debit an overshoot (level may go\n            self.level = min(self.capacity, self.level + reserved - actual)   # negative = a debt)\n\n# Illustrative provider limit of 400k tokens/minute; run at 80% to leave headroom for retries.\nTPM = 400_000 * 0.8\ntpm_bucket = TokenBucket(capacity=TPM / 6, rate=TPM / 60)     # 10 s of burst, ~5,333 tokens/s\n\ndef call_llm(prompt_tokens: int, max_tokens: int):\n    reserved = prompt_tokens + max_tokens                  # safe estimate; expected output is the alternative\n    wait = tpm_bucket.try_reserve(reserved)\n    if wait > 0:\n        raise RateLimited(retry_after=round(wait, 1))      # inbound: 429 + Retry-After; outbound: sleep\n    resp = provider.generate(max_tokens=max_tokens)\n    tpm_bucket.settle(reserved, prompt_tokens + resp.usage.output_tokens)\n    return resp\n```\n\n### The same bucket shared across replicas in Redis\nA production shape: the refill-and-reserve runs as one Lua script, so two replicas can never both admit\nthe last unit. Uses redis-py's `register_script`; calling `TIME` inside a script that then writes needs\nRedis 5 or later.\n\n```python\nimport redis\n\nRESERVE = \"\"\"\nlocal cap, rate, cost = tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3])\nlocal t = redis.call('TIME')\nlocal now = tonumber(t[1]) + tonumber(t[2]) / 1000000\nlocal s = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'level', 'updated')\nlocal level = math.min(cap, (tonumber(s[1]) or cap) + (now - (tonumber(s[2]) or now)) * rate)\nlocal wait = 0\nif level >= cost then level = level - cost else wait = (cost - level) / rate end\nredis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'level', level, 'updated', now)\nredis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], 3600)\nreturn tostring(wait)\n\"\"\"\n\nr = redis.Redis()\nreserve = r.register_script(RESERVE)\n\ndef try_reserve(key, capacity, rate, cost):\n    if cost > capacity:                    # can never fit: non-retryable error, not an endless Retry-After\n        raise ValueError(\"request larger than bucket capacity\")\n    return float(reserve(keys=[key], args=[capacity, rate, cost]))   # 0.0 means admitted\n\ndef settle(key, reserved, actual):\n    # Refund or debit; the next reserve clamps the level back to capacity.\n    r.hincrbyfloat(key, \"level\", reserved - actual)\n\nwait = try_reserve(\"rl:model:tpm\", 53_333, 5_333, 24_000)\n```\n\n### Agent loop with a run budget on top of the bucket\nEach step reserves from the shared bucket, and the run carries its own step and token budget so a tool\ncycle ends cleanly instead of draining the tenant. `llm.step` stands in for one model call of your loop.\n\n```python\nclass RunBudget:\n    def __init__(self, max_steps=20, max_tokens=200_000):\n        self.max_steps, self.max_tokens = max_steps, max_tokens\n        self.steps = self.tokens = 0\n    def charge(self, used: int):\n        self.steps += 1; self.tokens += used\n    def exhausted(self) -> bool:\n        return self.steps >= self.max_steps or self.tokens >= self.max_tokens\n\ndef run_agent(messages, budget: RunBudget, max_tokens=1_000):\n    while not budget.exhausted():\n        prompt_tokens = count_tokens(messages)              # same tokenizer as the model\n        reserved = prompt_tokens + max_tokens\n        wait = tpm_bucket.try_reserve(reserved)\n        if wait > 0:\n            return {\"status\": \"busy\", \"retry_after\": round(wait, 1)}   # surface it, do not spin\n        resp = llm.step(messages, max_tokens=max_tokens)\n        used = prompt_tokens + resp.usage.output_tokens\n        tpm_bucket.settle(reserved, used)\n        budget.charge(used)\n        if resp.final:\n            return {\"status\": \"done\", \"answer\": resp.text}\n        messages += resp.tool_messages()                    # transcript grows every step\n    return {\"status\": \"budget_exhausted\", \"steps\": budget.steps, \"tokens\": budget.tokens}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one runaway agent against a shared quota\nAn illustrative 400k tokens/minute key, limiter at 80% (320,000 tokens/minute). An agent stuck calling the\nsame failing tool starts with a 6k-token prefix and adds ~1.5k tokens per step.\n\n| Step | Prompt tokens | Cumulative tokens | Share of one minute's 320,000 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 6,000 | 6,000 | 2% |\n| 10 | 19,500 | ~128,000 | 40% |\n| 20 | 34,500 | ~405,000 | over the limit |\n\n- **Without a run budget,** this one agent pushes every other tenant into 429s within its first minute if\n  its steps are fast.\n- **With a 20-step and 200,000-token budget,** it stops after step 14 at about 220,000 tokens (the budget\n  is checked before each step, so the last step overshoots), and the tenant bucket confines the damage to\n  that tenant.\n- **Tokens, not steps, bind first** here, because each step re-sends the growing transcript.","patterns":["**Run budgets as the first check.** Give every agent run a step, token and dollar budget keyed on the run id, checked before each model call and before admission. Monitor runs ending on budget per agent version; a jump after a prompt change usually means a new tool loop.","**Surface 429s to the planner, not just the SDK.** When the bucket says wait, either pause the run and resume after `Retry-After`, or return a \"busy\" state the UI can show, instead of letting the loop retry instantly. Monitor time agents spend waiting on quota.","**Two-layer limiter at the gateway.** Per-tenant request and token buckets inbound, one global token bucket per provider model outbound, both checked before retrieval or prompt assembly. Monitor rejection rate per tenant and upstream 429 rate, which should be near zero.","**Atomic Redis bucket shared by replicas.** Run reserve as a Lua script (as above) keyed per tenant and per model, with a TTL so idle keys expire. Monitor Redis latency on the limiter path, because it now sits on every request.","**Header-driven outbound pacing.** Parse the provider's remaining-requests, remaining-tokens and reset headers after each call and lower the local refill rate when headroom is thin. Monitor quota utilisation against the provider's limit.","**Reservation accuracy tracking.** Log reserved versus actual tokens per route; if the ratio drifts far from 1, tighten `max_tokens` defaults or switch to expected-output reservations. Monitor the reservation-to-actual ratio.","**Separate keys for heavy workloads.** Give a nightly batch job or a dominant tenant its own provider key or workspace, so it cannot starve interactive traffic. Monitor per-key utilisation."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** One stuck agent in a tool cycle can burn a whole organisation's tokens\n  per minute in seconds, and every other agent then sees provider 429s. A request counter misses this,\n  because each agent step re-sends a growing transcript of thousands of tokens.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider 429s and remaining-quota headers in your SDK client, the\n  limiter in your agent gateway or FastAPI server, Redis buckets shared by replicas, and the step limit\n  your agent framework exposes (for example LangGraph's recursion limit).\n- **What you control.** Per-run budgets (steps, tokens, dollars), per-tenant buckets, one global bucket\n  per provider model at about 80% of quota, the `max_tokens` you reserve per step, and whether the loop\n  waits on `Retry-After` or stops the run.\n- **What you can skip.** Proving the GCRA equivalence or comparing sliding-window variants in depth. Use\n  a token bucket, make it atomic, and weight it in LLM tokens.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Multi-tenant agent platforms.** Per-tenant token buckets under one global bucket stop a single\n  customer's agents from exhausting the shared provider key.\n- **Runaway-agent containment.** Per-run step and token budgets end tool cycles and planner loops before\n  they cost real money.\n- **Batch agent jobs beside interactive agents.** Priority borrowing or a separate provider key keeps a\n  nightly eval or enrichment sweep from starving live user sessions.\n- **Self-hosted agent serving.** A token-per-second bucket in front of a vLLM fleet keeps admission inside\n  what the GPUs can actually generate.","mini_project":"**A token-weighted limiter for a multi-tenant agent API (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** protect a shared (fake or real) provider quota from one runaway agent while other tenants keep\n  working.\n- **Steps:** put a Redis token bucket per tenant and one global bucket in front of a small tool-calling\n  loop; reserve prompt plus `max_tokens` per step and settle on reported usage; add a per-run step and\n  token budget; simulate three tenants, one of which runs an agent stuck in a tool loop.\n- **Done when:** a table per tenant shows the runaway run ended on its budget, the other two tenants saw no\n  429s, and upstream 429s stayed at zero."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Requests per minute is the limit that matters.\"** One request can carry 200 tokens or 200,000, so a request counter is green while a few long prompts exhaust tokens per minute. Weight the bucket in LLM tokens.","**\"The provider's 429 is our rate limiter.\"** It is reactive, it hits every caller in the organisation at once, it cannot prioritise tenants, and every rejection comes back as a retry. Limit inbound per tenant and outbound at about 80% of quota.","**\"You cannot limit what you cannot count yet.\"** Output length is unknown, but `max_tokens` bounds it: reserve prompt tokens plus an estimate at admission, then settle on the reported usage, refunding or debiting the difference.","**\"Each replica can keep its own counter.\"** Independent counters each sized to the provider quota let N replicas spend up to N times it, and even a shared counter updated by read-decide-write lets two replicas both admit the last unit. Use one atomic check-and-reserve in Redis, or partition the quota explicitly (each replica gets 1/N).","**\"Per-request limits contain a runaway agent.\"** Every call in a tool cycle looks normal; the transcript just grows each step. Only a per-run step and token budget keyed on the run id sees that twenty calls are one runaway."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request: prompt + max_tokens\"] --> T{\"Tenant bucket has room?\"}\n    T -->|\"no\"| X[\"429 + Retry-After\"]\n    T -->|\"yes\"| G{\"Global bucket has room?\"}\n    G -->|\"no, global shortfall\"| Y[\"503 + Retry-After\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Call provider\"]\n    P --> S[\"Settle: refund or debit actual tokens\"]\n    S --> B[(\"Shared bucket state in Redis\")]\n    G -.->|\"atomic reserve\"| B\n    P -.->|\"remaining-quota headers\"| G","caption":"Every agent step's model call passes the tenant bucket, then the shared global bucket, before any\nprovider call; the settle step afterwards corrects the reservation using the real output length."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a token bucket, and why does it suit LLM traffic better than a fixed window?","a":"- **Definition:** a bucket of capacity *B* refilling at *r* units per second; a request costing *c* is\n  admitted if *c* units are available. It allows bursts up to *B* with a long-run average of *r*.\n- **Why it fits:** costs can be weighted in LLM tokens, and it has no window boundary, so a caller cannot\n  spend twice the limit across 0:59 and 1:00."},{"q":"Your request dashboard is green but the provider keeps returning 429s. What is happening?","a":"- **You are limiting requests, not tokens.** A few long prompts exhaust tokens per minute while the\n  request count looks healthy.\n- **Or replicas are racing:** a non-atomic check-then-decrement lets each replica admit the last unit, so\n  overshoot grows with replica count.\n- **Fix** with a token-weighted bucket shared atomically in Redis, at about 80% of the provider limit."},{"q":"Would you reserve the full `max_tokens` or an expected output length?","a":"- **Full `max_tokens`** never overshoots, but strands quota when clients set generous limits.\n- **Expected output** uses quota fully but occasionally overshoots into a provider 429, so settlement must\n  debit as well as refund.\n- **Choose by the cost of an upstream rejection:** for user-facing chat, overshoot hurts more than\n  stranded quota."},{"q":"A bucket holds 80,000 tokens. A request reserves a 20,000-token prompt plus 4,000 `max_tokens` and generates 600. Walk through it.","a":"- **Admission takes 24,000,** 30% of the burst; three such requests hold 72,000 and the fourth waits.\n- **Settlement refunds 3,400** once the 600-token output is known.\n- **A 100,000-token request never fits,** so it must get a non-retryable error rather than `Retry-After`."},{"q":"One agent in a tool loop is pushing your whole organisation into provider 429s. What do you change?","a":"- **Contain it now:** the run id identifies the culprit; stop that run and confirm the tenant bucket is\n  token-weighted, not request-counted.\n- **Prevent it:** a per-run step and token budget checked before each model call, plus per-tenant buckets\n  under a global bucket at about 80% of the provider limit.\n- **Detect it next time:** alert on steps per run and tokens per run outliers, and on repeated identical\n  tool calls inside a run."},{"q":"How should an agent loop react when the limiter says wait?","a":"- **Not by spinning:** an instant retry inside the loop adds load and burns the user's patience.\n- **Short waits:** sleep for `Retry-After` and continue the run, within the run's deadline.\n- **Long waits or a global 503:** checkpoint the run and return a \"busy, resuming later\" state, so earlier\n  steps are not wasted and the user knows what is happening.\n- **Read 429 vs 503 correctly:** 429 means this tenant or run is over its allowance, so slow this caller;\n  503 means global capacity is short, so every caller backs off."}],"tradeoffs":["**Bursts vs upstream windowing.** Token buckets admit bursts, which users like, but a burst that fits your bucket may still exceed the provider's own windowing; keep burst capacity below what the upstream tolerates.","**Safe vs full reservations.** Reserving `max_tokens` never overshoots but strands quota when clients over-specify; reserving an expected output uses quota fully but accepts occasional provider 429s. Pick by how costly an upstream rejection is.","**Shared vs partitioned state.** A shared Redis limiter is exact across replicas at the price of a round trip and a dependency; static partitioning is free and sufficient when replicas are few and load is even.","**Isolation vs utilisation.** Strict per-tenant limits guarantee fairness but block legitimate spikes; borrowing unused global headroom improves utilisation at the cost of weaker isolation.","**Tight vs loose run budgets.** A low step limit kills runaway agents early but also cuts off legitimate long research tasks; set budgets per agent type from the step-count distribution of successful runs."],"pitfalls":["**Limiting requests only.** Symptom: provider 429s while your requests-per-minute dashboard is green, because long prompts exhausted the token limit.","**Fixed windows.** Symptom: bursts of upstream 429s at minute boundaries as callers double-spend across the edge.","**Non-atomic check-then-decrement across replicas.** Symptom: overshoot that scales with replica count and vanishes in single-instance tests.","**Never refunding reservations.** Symptom: sustained throughput far below quota while the limiter reports itself full.","**Bucket capacity below the largest legitimate request.** Symptom: a few long-document requests get `Retry-After` indefinitely while everything else flows, because they can never fit.","**Limiting after expensive work** (retrieval, prompt assembly). Symptom: CPU and vector-store load that does not fall when requests are rejected.","**Counting tokens with the wrong tokenizer.** Symptom: systematic under- or over-reservation that appears the day a new model is added.","**Treating 429 and 503 as interchangeable.** Symptom: clients back off per-caller when the problem is global, or hammer a saturated server believing they are under quota.","**Rate limiting calls but not runs.** Symptom: one agent in a tool cycle consumes most of a tenant's tokens while each individual call looks normal. Fix: a per-run step and token budget."],"when":"- **Inbound:** in front of every LLM endpoint that serves more than one tenant or more than one\n  application.\n- **Outbound:** in front of every provider key that more than one process uses.\n- **Start with** per-tenant request and token buckets plus one global token bucket per provider model;\n  add priority borrowing and per-agent step budgets when agents appear.\n- **For agents, always:** a per-run step and token budget, because a loop multiplies calls in a way\n  per-request limits cannot see.\n- **Measure** rejection rate per tenant, quota utilisation against the provider's limit,\n  reservation-to-actual ratio and upstream 429 rate; the last should be near zero if the outbound\n  limiter works.\n- **Interviews:** expect why requests per minute is insufficient, how to limit when cost is unknown up\n  front, and how to make a limiter correct across replicas.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the bucket in Redis with an atomic script, wires reserve and settle around every provider call, and returns 429 with Retry-After from the API layer.","architect":"Allocates provider quota across tenants, priorities and regions, decides the safety margin, and defines what each plan tier is entitled to in tokens, not just requests.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their nightly batch job starved the chat assistant, and configures per-workload buckets or separate keys so the two no longer share one allowance."},"check":{"q":"You have a single provider key limited to one million tokens per minute, three application\nreplicas and forty tenants of very different sizes. Design the rate limiting and explain each choice.","a":"Two layers. Outbound: one global token bucket per provider model, held in Redis so all three\nreplicas share it, with capacity set to perhaps ten seconds of refill and the rate at about 80% of the\nlimit to leave headroom for retries. Each call reserves prompt tokens plus an output estimate and refunds\nthe difference on completion, because output length is unknown until the response ends. Inbound: a\nper-tenant token bucket sized from the tenant's plan, enforced before retrieval or prompt assembly so\nrejected requests cost nothing. Interactive traffic may borrow from global headroom before batch traffic\ndoes. Rejections return 429 with Retry-After and limit headers; a global shortfall returns 503 instead so\nclients back off correctly. I would read the provider's remaining-quota headers to slow down before the\nfirst upstream 429, and monitor upstream 429 rate, per-tenant rejection rate and reservation accuracy. If\none tenant genuinely needs most of the quota, I would give them a separate key rather than let them\ndominate the shared bucket."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a requests-per-minute limit insufficient for LLM traffic?","options":["Providers do not publish request limits, only token limits","Requests vary in cost by orders of magnitude, so the real constraint, tokens per minute, can be exhausted by a few large requests","Request counts cannot be shared across replicas","Token limits are easier to compute than request counts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A single 150k-token request costs as much as hundreds of short ones. Limiting requests alone leaves the token quota unprotected, which is why provider 429s appear while request dashboards look healthy."},{"q":"What is the main weakness of a fixed-window counter?","options":["It requires storing a timestamp for every request","A caller can spend a full allowance at the end of one window and again at the start of the next, producing a 2× burst","It cannot be implemented atomically in Redis","It does not allow any bursting at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fixed windows reset abruptly, so two full allowances can land within seconds across the boundary. Sliding windows and token buckets spread enforcement continuously and avoid the edge."},{"q":"Why does an LLM token limiter reserve at admission and settle at completion?","options":["Because provider APIs require clients to pre-declare token usage","To make the limiter work without a shared store","Because output token count is unknown until generation finishes, so the limiter must charge an estimate first and correct it afterwards","To allow retries to reuse the original reservation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Input tokens can be counted up front but output tokens cannot; `max_tokens` only bounds them. Reserving an estimate prevents overshoot while settling refunds unused quota so throughput is not stranded."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/idempotency","sources":["Official docs: Stripe API (idempotent requests)","IETF HTTPAPI working group — The Idempotency-Key HTTP Header Field (draft)"],"why":"- **A timeout is ambiguous.** The request may never have arrived, may still be running, or may have\n  completed after the client stopped listening. The client can only give up (poor availability) or retry.\n- **Exactly-once delivery is impossible** in general across a network. Systems achieve at-least-once\n  delivery plus idempotent processing, which gives effectively-once results; that is why durable queues,\n  webhooks and payment APIs are built around deduplication rather than preventing duplicates.\n- **LLM generation is expensive.** A retried 30-second, 10k-token call doubles cost and latency; if the\n  first call completed you paid twice for one answer.\n- **Generation is non-deterministic, and agents act.** A second attempt produces different text, so one\n  click can show two different replies. A retried step calling `create_ticket` or `send_email` does it\n  twice, turning a flaky network into a customer-visible incident.\n- **The naive alternatives fail.** \"Never retry\" sacrifices availability. \"Dedupe by content\" breaks when\n  content is legitimately repeated (\"send another reminder\"). \"Rely on a unique database constraint\"\n  needs a natural key, which an LLM call rarely has. Idempotency keys supply it explicitly.","how":"### The key\n- **One key per *logical operation*,** usually a UUID generated by the client and reused on every retry\n  of that operation.\n- **Scoped to the caller** (tenant, API key), so collisions between callers are impossible.\n- **Expires after a retention window;** Stripe, the canonical example, keeps keys for 24 hours.\n- **Operation vs attempt is the whole idea:** a new key means \"do it again\", the same key means \"make\n  sure it happened once\".\n\n### The server algorithm\n1. **Fingerprint** the request body (a hash of its canonical serialisation).\n2. **Claim the key:** atomically insert `(key, fingerprint, status=in_progress)`. On conflict:\n\n   | Existing row | Meaning | Response |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | different fingerprint | key reused for a different request, a client bug | 422 |\n   | `status=completed` | already done | replay the stored response, same status code and body, no work done |\n   | `status=in_progress` | a duplicate is racing the original | 409, or wait for the first to finish |\n\n   Returning 409 rather than running a second copy is why the claim must be the first write.\n3. **Execute** the operation.\n4. **Store the outcome** atomically with `status=completed`. Transient failures release the claim so a\n   retry can run; permanent failures (4xx) can be stored and replayed.\n\n### The lease and the honest limit\n- **The claim needs a lease.** If the process crashes between steps 3 and 4 the key stays `in_progress`\n  forever, so the row carries a short expiry after which a retry may take over.\n- **Lease length is a genuine tension.** Too short: a retry takes over while the first attempt (a long\n  generation or slow tool) still runs, and you get the duplicate you were preventing. Too long: every\n  second of lease is a second a crashed attempt blocks recovery.\n- **Long operations heartbeat:** renew the lease periodically, and the completing write checks it still\n  holds the lease, for example by matching an attempt id stored in the row, before recording the outcome.\n- **The pattern's limit:** unless the side effect and the key store share one transaction, there is a\n  window where the effect happened but was not recorded, and the retry repeats it. Outbox patterns and\n  reconciliation jobs close that window.\n\n### Idempotent by design\nOften you can skip the key store by making the operation naturally idempotent:\n- **Deterministic ids:** `job_id = hash(tenant, document_id, prompt_version)` inserted with\n  `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`.\n- **Set, not append:** \"the summary of document X is S\" rather than \"add a summary\" or an increment.\n- **Conditional writes** with version numbers, so a stale duplicate is rejected.\n\n### Where it lands in an LLM system\n| Surface | Key | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Chat turns** | message id generated by the client when the user presses send | server appends the user turn and reply under that id only if absent and stores the text under it; a double click or retry returns the same reply and the second generation is never billed |\n| **Agent tool calls** | run id plus a stable step identifier (not only the model's tool-call id), passed to the tool | the tool dedupes on it and forwards it to external APIs that accept an idempotency header |\n| **Queues and webhooks** | processed message or event ids (the inbox pattern) | needed because deliveries are at-least-once by contract |\n| **Provider calls** | your layer's key | most generation endpoints do not accept one themselves (check the specific provider), so coalesce concurrent duplicates onto one in-flight call and replay the stored completion to late retries |\n\n- **Agent caveat:** a regenerated model turn produces new tool-call ids, so the derivation must include\n  something stable about the step, not only the model's id.\n\n### Deriving a stable key inside an agent loop\n| Candidate key | Survives a transport retry | Survives a regenerated turn | Survives resume from checkpoint |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model's tool-call id | yes | no: new id each generation | only if the checkpoint stored it |\n| Random UUID made in the tool | no: new id every attempt | no | no |\n| `run_id` + step index + tool name + hash of key arguments | yes | yes, if the step index is the same | yes |\n\n- **Include the arguments that define the effect** (recipient, amount, ticket subject), so a corrected\n  call with different arguments is a new operation, not a replay.\n- **Record the tool result in the transcript or checkpoint** under the same key, so a resumed run feeds\n  the model the stored result instead of calling the tool again.\n\n### Streaming\n- **Partial output complicates replay.** Persist the final text under the key when the stream completes.\n- **A retry arriving mid-stream** either attaches to the in-flight generation or restarts under the same\n  message id so the client can discard the partial render.","example":"### Minimal idempotency layer\nClaim first, fingerprint check, replay on completion, 409 on a racing duplicate, release on transient\nfailure so the client can retry. In production the dictionary is a Redis `SET NX PX` or a table with a\nunique index, and `claim` is a single atomic operation.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time\n\nclass IdempotencyStore:\n    \"\"\"In production: Redis SET NX PX, or a table with a unique index on key.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, ttl_s=86_400, lease_s=120):\n        self.rows, self.ttl_s, self.lease_s = {}, ttl_s, lease_s\n    def claim(self, key, fingerprint):\n        row = self.rows.get(key)\n        if row and row[\"expires\"] > time.monotonic():\n            if row[\"fp\"] != fingerprint:\n                return \"mismatch\", None\n            return row[\"status\"], row.get(\"response\")      # \"in_progress\" or \"done\"\n        self.rows[key] = {\"fp\": fingerprint, \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n                          \"expires\": time.monotonic() + self.lease_s}   # short lease while running\n        return \"claimed\", None\n    def complete(self, key, response):\n        self.rows[key].update(status=\"done\", response=response, expires=time.monotonic() + self.ttl_s)\n    def release(self, key):\n        self.rows.pop(key, None)\n\nstore = IdempotencyStore()\n\ndef handle_generate(idem_key: str, tenant: str, body: dict):\n    key = f\"{tenant}:{idem_key}\"                              # scope keys per caller\n    fp = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(body, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()\n    status, saved = store.claim(key, fp)\n    if status == \"mismatch\":    return 422, {\"error\": \"idempotency key reused with a different body\"}\n    if status == \"done\":        return 200, saved             # replay: no second generation, no second bill\n    if status == \"in_progress\": return 409, {\"error\": \"already in flight; retry shortly\"}\n    try:\n        resp = llm.generate(**body)       # expensive, non-deterministic; must finish within lease_s\n    except TransientError:\n        store.release(key)                                    # let the client retry with the same key\n        raise                             # a permanent error could be stored and replayed instead\n    store.complete(key, resp)\n    return 200, resp\n```\n\n### Postgres claim with a lease and attempt id\nA production shape for the same algorithm. The claim inserts, or takes over only an expired lease with a\nmatching fingerprint; the completing write succeeds only for the attempt that holds the lease.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE idempotency_keys (\n  scope       text        NOT NULL,           -- tenant or API key\n  key         text        NOT NULL,\n  fingerprint text        NOT NULL,\n  status      text        NOT NULL DEFAULT 'in_progress',\n  attempt_id  uuid        NOT NULL,\n  lease_until timestamptz NOT NULL,\n  response    jsonb,\n  PRIMARY KEY (scope, key)\n);\n\n-- Claim: returns a row only if this attempt now owns the key.\nINSERT INTO idempotency_keys (scope, key, fingerprint, attempt_id, lease_until)\nVALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, now() + interval '120 seconds')\nON CONFLICT (scope, key) DO UPDATE\n  SET attempt_id = EXCLUDED.attempt_id, lease_until = EXCLUDED.lease_until\n  WHERE idempotency_keys.status = 'in_progress'\n    AND idempotency_keys.lease_until < now()\n    AND idempotency_keys.fingerprint = EXCLUDED.fingerprint\nRETURNING attempt_id;\n-- No row returned: SELECT the existing row and answer 422, replay, or 409.\n\n-- Heartbeat during long work, and completion: both only for the lease holder.\n-- (Each statement numbers its own parameters: $1 scope, $2 key, $3 this attempt's id.)\nUPDATE idempotency_keys SET lease_until = now() + interval '120 seconds'\nWHERE scope = $1 AND key = $2 AND attempt_id = $3 AND status = 'in_progress';\n\nUPDATE idempotency_keys SET status = 'completed', response = $4\nWHERE scope = $1 AND key = $2 AND attempt_id = $3 AND status = 'in_progress';\n-- 0 rows updated: the lease was lost, so another attempt owns the outcome.\n```\n\n### Worked timeline: why the lease must outlast the slowest call\nA tool call that takes 150 s against a 120 s lease, without a heartbeat (illustrative numbers):\n\n| Time | Attempt A | Attempt B (client retry, same key) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 s | claims key, lease until 120 s, starts `create_ticket` | |\n| 30 s | still running | claim conflicts, lease valid: 409 |\n| 130 s | still running | lease expired: takes over and creates a second ticket |\n| 150 s | finishes, completion write matches no row | |\n\nWith a heartbeat every 30 s, B keeps getting 409 until A completes, then gets the replay.\n\n### Agent tool wrapper keyed on run and step\nA decorator for side-effecting tools: the key comes from the run, the step and the effect-defining\narguments, so a retried, regenerated or resumed step returns the stored result. `store` is the\n`IdempotencyStore` above; `billing.create_invoice` stands in for your external API.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, functools\n\ndef idempotent_tool(effect_args):\n    def wrap(fn):\n        @functools.wraps(fn)\n        def tool(ctx, **kwargs):\n            effect = {k: kwargs[k] for k in effect_args}\n            digest = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(effect, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:16]\n            key = f\"{ctx.tenant}:{ctx.run_id}:{ctx.step}:{fn.__name__}:{digest}\"\n            status, saved = store.claim(key, digest)\n            if status == \"done\":\n                return saved                                   # replay: no second invoice\n            if status == \"in_progress\":\n                return {\"error\": \"already running, check back\"}   # observation for the model\n            try:\n                result = fn(ctx, idem_key=key, **kwargs)\n            except TransientError:\n                store.release(key)\n                raise\n            store.complete(key, result)\n            return result\n        return tool\n    return wrap\n\n@idempotent_tool(effect_args=[\"customer_id\", \"amount_cents\"])\ndef create_invoice(ctx, idem_key, customer_id, amount_cents, memo=\"\"):\n    return billing.create_invoice(customer_id, amount_cents, memo, idempotency_key=idem_key)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one timeout, three layers of duplicates\nA support agent's step calls `create_invoice` (invoice 1), then the model call that reads the result\ntimes out and the step fails.\n\n| Event | Without keys | With run-and-step keys |\n|---|---|---|\n| Framework node retry re-runs the whole step | tool executes again: second invoice | tool claim finds `completed`, replays invoice id |\n| User clicks \"regenerate\" | new turn, new tool-call id: third invoice | same run and step, same arguments: replay |\n| Worker crashes, run resumes from checkpoint | step re-executes: fourth invoice | stored result fed back to the model |\n| Invoices created | 4 | 1 |\n\n- **A plain SDK retry of the model call is not the danger here,** because it re-sends a transcript that\n  already holds the tool result; duplicates come from layers that re-run the step itself.\n\n- **Regenerate the text, not the effect.** \"Regenerate\" gets a new key for the model's reply (the user\n  wants a different answer), while tool effects inside the step keep their run-and-step keys.","patterns":["**Key every side-effecting tool by default.** Wrap tools that email, charge, create or delete with an idempotency decorator in the tool registry, so a new tool cannot ship without one. Monitor tools in the registry without a key as a release check.","**Persist tool results with the checkpoint.** When an agent framework resumes a run, it should replay recorded tool results to the model rather than re-executing tools. Monitor tool executions per resumed run; it should be zero for already-completed steps.","**Client-generated message ids for chat.** The UI creates the id when the user presses send and sends it as an `Idempotency-Key` header (the IETF draft's name) on every retry. Monitor replay rate per client version; a jump points at an over-eager retry loop.","**Atomic claim in Redis or Postgres.** Use `SET key value NX PX <lease_ms>` in Redis or the `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT` claim above, scoped per tenant. Monitor 409 rate and claim latency.","**Stable step keys for agent tools.** Derive the key from run id plus a stable step identifier and forward it to external APIs that accept idempotency keys (Stripe does). Monitor duplicate-effect counts from reconciliation.","**Inbox table for queues and webhooks.** Record processed event ids in the same transaction as the handler's writes, because deliveries are at-least-once. Monitor duplicate deliveries skipped.","**Reconciliation job for cross-system effects.** Periodically compare external records (invoices, tickets) against recorded steps, because the key store and the external system share no transaction. Monitor mismatches found per run."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A model call that times out after the tool already ran, a regenerated\n  turn, a resumed checkpoint: each replays a step. Without keys, `send_email`, `create_ticket` or a refund\n  happens twice, and that is a customer-visible incident, not a log line.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Your tool functions (the only place the effect actually happens),\n  the `Idempotency-Key` header on your agent API, external APIs that accept keys (Stripe does), durable\n  execution and checkpointing in agent frameworks, and the queues that deliver agent jobs.\n- **What you control.** How the key is derived (run id plus a stable step identifier, never only the\n  model's tool-call id), the atomic claim, the lease length versus your slowest tool, and which tools are\n  idempotent by design.\n- **What you can skip.** Formal exactly-once theory. Know that it is impossible across a network and that\n  at-least-once plus idempotent handlers is the substitute.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents that move money or create records.** Refund, invoice and order tools keyed on run and step\n  make retries and regenerations safe.\n- **Email and notification agents.** A stored outcome per key means a timed-out step never sends the same\n  message twice.\n- **Durable, resumable agent runs.** Checkpointed runs replay recorded tool results after a crash instead\n  of re-executing them.\n- **Chat front ends for agents.** Client-generated message ids stop a double click from starting two\n  agent runs and billing two generations.","mini_project":"**Make a side-effecting agent tool safe under chaos (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove that a tool-calling agent creates exactly one record per intended action, despite\n  timeouts, regenerations and crashes.\n- **Steps:** build a small agent with a `create_ticket` tool writing to SQLite; wrap it with a run-and-step\n  key and an atomic claim; inject a timeout after the tool runs, a \"regenerate\" of the turn, and a worker\n  kill mid-run; resume from a checkpoint each time.\n- **Done when:** across 50 chaos runs the ticket table holds exactly one row per intended ticket, and a log\n  shows which events were replays."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"With a reliable queue or network we can get exactly-once.\"** A timeout is ambiguous: the request may be lost, running or done. Across a network you get at-least-once delivery, and idempotent processing on a key turns that into effectively-once results.","**\"Check whether the key exists, then do the work.\"** Two concurrent duplicates both see \"absent\" and both act. The claim must be one atomic insert (`SET NX PX`, or `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT`) before any work, with 409 for the loser.","**\"The model's tool-call id is a fine idempotency key.\"** A regenerated turn or resumed run gets new tool-call ids, so the effect repeats. Derive the key from run id, a stable step identifier, the tool name and the effect-defining arguments.","**\"Temperature zero makes generation idempotent.\"** Output is not guaranteed identical across hardware and batch composition, and idempotency is about effects and cost, not text: a second call still bills and may run tools again.","**\"A key store closes the duplicate window completely.\"** Unless the side effect and the key store share a transaction, a crash after the effect but before the record lets a retry repeat it, and a lease shorter than the slowest tool does the same. Heartbeats and reconciliation jobs close the gap."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request with key and body hash\"] --> C{\"Atomic claim on key\"}\n    C -->|\"new key\"| W[\"Execute: generate or call tool\"]\n    C -->|\"different body hash\"| E1[\"422: key reused\"]\n    C -->|\"completed\"| RP[\"Replay stored response\"]\n    C -->|\"in progress, lease valid\"| E2[\"409: already in flight\"]\n    C -->|\"in progress, lease expired\"| W\n    W -->|\"success, still lease holder\"| S[(\"Store outcome, status completed\")]\n    W -->|\"transient error\"| RL[\"Release claim, client retries\"]\n    S --> OK[\"200 with result\"]","caption":"Every request with a key goes through the atomic claim first; only the \"claimed\" branch does any work,\nand only the lease holder may record the outcome."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is exactly-once delivery impossible, and what do systems do instead?","a":"- **A timeout is ambiguous:** the sender cannot know whether the request was lost, is running, or\n  finished, so it must either give up or resend.\n- **Systems use at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing,** deduplicating on a key so repeats\n  have no extra effect, which yields effectively-once results."},{"q":"Users report two different replies to one message. How do you debug and fix it?","a":"- **Likely cause:** a double click or client retry triggered a second, non-deterministic generation.\n- **Check** whether the client sends a stable message id and whether the server claims it atomically\n  before generating; a check-then-insert race shows up only under load.\n- **Fix:** client-generated id per send, atomic claim, replay the stored reply, 409 for in-flight duplicates."},{"q":"When would you not use an idempotency key store?","a":"- **When a natural deterministic id exists,** such as `hash(tenant, document_id, prompt_version)`; an\n  upsert with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` gives the same guarantee without an extra store.\n- **For pure reads,** which have no effect to duplicate.\n- **When set semantics fit,** because setting a value twice leaves one state."},{"q":"Your agent's slowest tool takes up to 150 s. How long should the idempotency lease be, and what happens if you get it wrong?","a":"- **It must outlast the slowest legitimate execution,** or a retry takes over a running attempt and\n  duplicates its effect.\n- **But a long lease delays recovery** after a crash, so long operations heartbeat and the completing\n  write checks the attempt id before recording."},{"q":"How do you derive idempotency keys for an agent's tool calls?","a":"- **Not from the model's tool-call id alone,** because a regenerated turn gets a new id and repeats the\n  effect.\n- **Use run id, a stable step identifier, the tool name and a hash of the effect-defining arguments,**\n  scoped per tenant, and forward the key to external APIs that accept one.\n- **Store the result under that key** and feed it back on resume, so a restarted run never re-executes a\n  finished step."},{"q":"Why does temperature zero not make generation idempotent?","a":"- **Determinism is not guaranteed** across hardware and batch composition, so replayed outputs can differ\n  subtly.\n- **And idempotency is about effects, not text:** a second generation still costs money and may run tools\n  again, so you still need the key and stored outcome."}],"tradeoffs":["**Key store vs natural id.** A key store adds a write and a read to every request and a dependency on the store; when a deterministic natural id exists, an upsert achieves the same guarantee for free.","**Consistency vs a better answer.** Replaying the stored response gives consistency but means a retry can never get a better answer; \"regenerate\" must therefore be a new key, not a retry.","**409 vs coalescing.** Returning 409 to a racing duplicate is simple and safe; coalescing onto the in-flight call is a better experience but holds connections and needs more state.","**Replay cache vs retention duty.** Stored responses double as a cache, but they are retained user content with privacy and retention obligations, so the TTL is a policy decision as well as a technical one.","**Coarse vs fine step keys.** Keying on run and step only blocks a legitimate second call in the same step (two different emails); adding effect arguments allows it, but a model that rewords a memo slightly then looks like a new operation. Pick the effect-defining arguments per tool."],"pitfalls":["**Generating the key inside the retry loop,** so each attempt gets a fresh one. Symptom: duplicates persist despite \"idempotency\" being implemented.","**Check-then-insert instead of an atomic claim.** Symptom: duplicates that appear only under concurrency or load tests and never in local runs.","**No fingerprint check.** Symptom: a client bug that reuses keys silently returns one request's answer to an unrelated request.","**Storing transient failures as the final outcome.** Symptom: a momentary 503 becomes permanent for that key until it expires.","**Agent tool calls keyed only on the model's tool-call id.** Symptom: duplicate tickets after a regenerated turn, because the regenerated call carries a new id.","**A lease shorter than the slowest generation or tool call.** Symptom: duplicates only on long requests, with two attempts for the same key overlapping in the trace.","**Assuming temperature zero makes generation idempotent.** Symptom: subtly different replayed outputs, since determinism is not guaranteed across hardware and batch composition.","**Resuming a checkpointed run by re-executing its last step.** Symptom: duplicate effects only after worker restarts or deploys. Fix: record each tool result under its key before advancing the checkpoint."],"when":"- **Require a key** on every endpoint that creates something, costs money or triggers a side effect: in\n  an LLM product, message creation, job submission and every tool with an external effect.\n- **Skip the key store** where a natural deterministic id makes the write an upsert, and for pure reads.\n- **Pair it with the retry policy:** retries are only safe because of this.\n- **Measure** replay rate (how often a key is reused) and 409 rate. A high replay rate points at an\n  overly aggressive client; a nonzero duplicate-effect rate points at a window in the\n  claim-execute-store sequence.\n- **Interviews:** define idempotency precisely, explain why exactly-once is impossible and at-least-once\n  plus idempotency is the substitute, and walk through claim, fingerprint, replay and lease.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds the key header to the API, implements the atomic claim and replay in Redis or Postgres, and threads run and step ids into every agent tool call.","architect":"Decides which operations must be idempotent, where the key store lives relative to the side effect, and how reconciliation closes the gap the store cannot.","fde":"Audits a customer's agent integration for tools that email, charge or create records without keys, and proves the fix with a chaos test that injects timeouts mid-step."},"check":{"q":"An agent step calls a `create_invoice` tool and the LLM request times out after the tool has\nalready run. How do you make the whole system safe to retry end to end?","a":"Treat each layer separately. The user's turn carries a client-generated message id, so the retried\nturn replays or attaches to the in-flight run instead of starting a second one. Inside the run, each\ntool call gets an idempotency key derived from the run id and a stable step identifier, not only the\nmodel's tool-call id, which changes if the turn is regenerated. The `create_invoice` implementation\nclaims that key atomically before acting, forwards it as the idempotency header to the billing API if\nthat API supports one, and stores the invoice id under the key; a retried step then returns the existing\ninvoice instead of creating another. The LLM call itself is wrapped so a retry after timeout coalesces\nonto the in-flight generation or replays the stored completion. Finally, because the key store and the\nbilling system are not one transaction, a reconciliation job compares invoices against recorded steps to\ncatch the rare window where the effect happened but the record did not."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which operation is idempotent by construction, with no key store needed?","options":["Append the model's reply to the conversation","Set the summary of document 42 to the generated text","Increment the tenant's token counter by the response usage","Send the generated digest email to the user"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Setting a value to the same thing twice leaves one state; appending, incrementing and sending accumulate with each repeat. Designing writes as sets instead of appends removes the need for deduplication. If the text is regenerated on the retry the final value may differ, but there is still exactly one summary."},{"q":"A client sends a request with idempotency key K, times out, and retries with K while the first attempt is still generating. What should the server do?","options":["Start a second generation, since the first may have failed","Detect the in-progress claim on K and return 409 (or attach the retry to the in-flight result) instead of generating again","Return the stored response for K, which is empty","Reject the retry with 422 because the key was reused"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The atomic claim exists precisely to catch this race: the key is in progress, so a second execution would duplicate cost and effects. 422 is for a key reused with a different body, which this is not."},{"q":"Why should the server store a fingerprint of the request body alongside the idempotency key?","options":["To allow the key to be shorter than a UUID","To let the server compress the stored response","To detect a key being reused for a different request, which is a client bug that would otherwise return the wrong stored response","To make the key unique across tenants"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Keys are generated by clients, and clients have bugs. Without the fingerprint a reused key silently replays a response to an unrelated request; with it the server can reject the mismatch explicitly."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/caching","sources":["Official docs: Anthropic prompt caching","Official docs: OpenAI prompt caching","Zheng et al., 2024 — SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs (RadixAttention)","Bang, 2023 — GPTCache: An Open-Source Semantic Cache for LLM Applications"],"why":"- **Prefill cost scales with prompt length, and most of the prompt repeats.** In agent and RAG systems the\n  system prompt, tool schemas, few-shot examples and long documents are identical call to call. A 40-turn\n  agent session with a 12k-token prefix re-processes roughly half a million tokens the model has already\n  seen, and at long contexts prefill dominates time to first token.\n- **Some products repeat whole questions** (\"what is your refund policy\"), where the entire generation\n  repeats.\n- **\"Cache responses like any HTTP response\" fails three ways.** Exact hits are rare in conversation,\n  because prompts carry user- and time-specific content, so hit rate rounds to zero. A hit may be wrong\n  for the new context: a cached answer about *this* customer's order is a data leak for the next one. And\n  response caches do nothing for agent loops, where every turn is new but the prefix is identical.\n- **Each layer targets one problem and adds one failure mode.** Prompt caching attacks prefill compute\n  without touching the answer (failure: no speedup). Semantic caching trades exactness for hit rate\n  (failure: wrong answers). Understand them separately before combining them.","how":"### The three layers at a glance\n| Layer | Skips | Key | Failure mode |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Exact-match response cache | the whole generation | hash of every input that affects output | near-zero hit rate on chat |\n| Semantic cache | the whole generation | embedding of the normalised query, within a namespace | confident wrong answers |\n| Prompt caching (prefix KV reuse) | prefill of the shared prefix only | exact token sequence, in order | silent misses, no speedup |\n\n### Layer 1: exact-match response cache\n- **Key:** a hash of model id and version, system prompt, messages, tool definitions, sampling parameters\n  and any retrieved context.\n- **Hit:** return the stored response.\n- **Most valuable in deterministic pipelines** (classification, extraction, embedding of the same chunk),\n  where identical inputs really recur.\n- **Invalidate by versioning:** the key includes the prompt template, so bump a template version on change.\n- **Temperature above zero:** product semantics decide whether a cached answer is acceptable; a\n  \"regenerate\" action must bypass it.\n\n### Layer 2: semantic cache\nGPTCache is a well-known open-source implementation; the data flow is the same in any of them:\n1. **Scope.** Build a namespace from tenant, corpus or knowledge-base version, prompt version and model,\n   *because* a similar question against a different tenant's data or an older policy must never hit.\n   Lookups only search inside the namespace.\n2. **Embed** the normalised query, usually the latest user message with any context that changes the\n   answer folded in. Embedding only the last message is what makes \"and for business accounts?\" match an\n   unrelated earlier question.\n3. **Search.** Nearest-neighbour lookup over stored queries in the namespace; take the best match and its\n   similarity.\n4. **Decide.** Hit if similarity ≥ τ. The threshold is a precision/recall knob: \"how do I cancel my order\"\n   and \"how do I cancel my subscription\" embed close together and have different answers, so a threshold\n   tuned only for hit rate ships wrong answers. A cheap verifier call (\"does this stored answer answer\n   this question?\") can raise precision at some latency cost.\n5. **Fill and expire.** On a miss, generate, then store query, embedding and answer with a TTL. Delete the\n   namespace (or bump its version) when the underlying knowledge changes, because the cache does not know\n   the policy page was edited.\n\n- **Measure hit *precision*** (the fraction of sampled hits a human or judge rates correct) alongside hit\n  rate, because the failure is a confident wrong answer that no error metric sees.\n- **Fit:** FAQ-like, high-repeat, low-personalisation traffic; poor for chat with history or\n  user-specific context.\n\n### Layer 3: prompt caching (prefix KV reuse)\n- **Mechanism.** The engine keeps the key and value tensors for a prompt prefix; when a new request's\n  token sequence begins with the same prefix, it skips prefill for those tokens and processes only the\n  suffix.\n- **Where it shows up.** Providers expose it as a billing and latency feature; self-hosted engines as a\n  scheduler feature (vLLM's automatic prefix caching by block hash, SGLang's RadixAttention tree with LRU\n  eviction).\n\nRules that follow from the mechanism:\n1. **Exact token sequence, in order.** The request renders as tools, then system, then messages; any change\n   at position *i* invalidates everything after it.\n2. **Static first, volatile last:** timestamps, user names and per-request retrieved documents go at the\n   end.\n3. **Isolation:** the cache is per model and isolated per organisation or workspace.\n4. **Entries have a TTL and hits are not guaranteed under load,** so the usage report is the only ground\n   truth. On Anthropic: `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` alongside the uncached\n   `input_tokens`. OpenAI reports cached tokens inside its usage details.\n5. **Parallel cold requests all pay:** an entry becomes readable only once the writing request has begun\n   responding, so N parallel identical requests all pay full price.\n\nVendor specifics at the time of writing, which change:\n\n| | Anthropic | OpenAI |\n|---|---|---|\n| Activation | explicit `cache_control` breakpoints, up to four per request | automatic above a minimum prompt length |\n| TTL | default five minutes, refreshed on every read; optional one hour | set by the provider; check current docs |\n| Write price | more than ordinary input: 1.25× (five minutes), 2× (one hour) | no separate write charge |\n| Read price | a fraction of input, around 0.1× | discounted cached input tokens |\n| Minimum prefix | model-dependent, on the order of one to a few thousand tokens; shorter prefixes silently do not cache | a minimum length applies |\n\n- **On both,** caching reduces input cost and time to first token; it does nothing for output tokens or\n  decode speed.\n\n### Which layer for which traffic\n| Traffic | Layers |\n|---|---|\n| FAQ bot | semantic plus exact |\n| Extraction or classification pipeline | exact |\n| Agent loops, multi-turn chat, RAG with a large static context | prompt caching |\n| Embedding generation | exact |\n\nMost real systems run prompt caching always and add a response-level cache only where repeats are\nmeasured.\n\n### Caching inside an agent loop\n| What repeats in an agent | Cache it with | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tool schemas and system prompt | prompt caching, breakpoint after them | byte-identical and in a fixed order on every step |\n| Earlier turns of the transcript | prompt caching, incremental | append only; editing or trimming an old turn misses from that point |\n| Read-only tool results (search, lookup) | exact cache keyed on tool, arguments, tenant | short TTL; never for tools with side effects |\n| Embeddings of retrieved chunks | exact cache keyed on chunk hash and embedding model | invalidate on model change |\n| The agent's final answer | usually nothing | depends on run state, so semantic hits are wrong |\n\n- **Compaction costs one miss.** Summarising old turns into a new block rewrites the transcript, so the\n  next call writes a fresh cache entry; compact rarely, at a boundary, not a little every step.\n- **Sub-agents share prefixes only if they share bytes.** Give parallel sub-agents the same tool list and\n  system prompt in the same order if you want them to read one cached prefix.","example":"### Explicit prompt caching on the Anthropic API\nThe static manual sits before the breakpoint and the volatile question after it; the usage fields\nconfirm whether the second call read the prefix.\n\n```python\nimport anthropic\n\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()\nMANUAL = open(\"policy_manual.md\").read()          # ~20k tokens, identical on every request\n\ndef ask(question: str) -> str:\n    resp = client.messages.create(\n        model=\"claude-opus-5-5\",\n        max_tokens=1024,\n        system=[\n            {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Answer questions using only the policy manual below.\"},\n            {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": MANUAL,\n             \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},   # breakpoint: everything up to here is cacheable\n        ],\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}],   # volatile content after the breakpoint\n    )\n    u = resp.usage\n    print(f\"uncached={u.input_tokens} cache_write={u.cache_creation_input_tokens} \"\n          f\"cache_read={u.cache_read_input_tokens} output={u.output_tokens}\")\n    return next(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == \"text\")\n\nask(\"What is the refund window?\")      # first call: cache_write ≈ 20k, cache_read = 0\nask(\"Who can approve an exception?\")   # within the TTL: cache_read ≈ 20k, uncached ≈ the question\n```\n\n### Worked cost scenario\n- **Traffic:** a 20,000-token manual, a 200-token question, a 400-token answer, and 1,000 questions a day\n  arriving more often than every five minutes, so the entry stays warm.\n- **Illustrative prices:** 3 per million input tokens, 3.75 per million for cache writes (1.25×), 0.30 per\n  million for cache reads (0.1×) and 15 per million output tokens.\n\n| Cost line | Without caching | With caching |\n|---|---|---|\n| Manual prefix | 1,000 × 20,200 × 3 / 10⁶ ≈ 60.6 (input incl. question) | one write: 20,000 × 3.75 / 10⁶ ≈ 0.08 |\n| Cache reads | none | 1,000 × 20,000 × 0.30 / 10⁶ = 6.0 |\n| Uncached questions | included above | 1,000 × 200 × 3 / 10⁶ = 0.6 |\n| **Input total** | **≈ 60.6** | **≈ 6.7, a nine-fold reduction** |\n| Output | 1,000 × 400 × 15 / 10⁶ = 6.0 | 6.0, unchanged |\n| **Daily total** | **≈ 66.6** | **≈ 12.7** |\n\n- **Latency:** time to first token drops by roughly the time it took to prefill 20,000 tokens.\n- **Break-even:** at 1.25× write and 0.1× read, a prefix reused even once within the TTL is already\n  cheaper than not caching (1.35× versus 2×).\n\n### A scoped semantic cache, and why the threshold matters\nPure Python, runs as is. A bag-of-words vector stands in for a real embedding model; the order/subscription\npair shows how a threshold tuned for hit rate returns a wrong answer.\n\n```python\nimport math, re, time\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef embed(text):                                   # stand-in for a real embedding model\n    return Counter(re.findall(r\"[a-z]+\", text.lower()))\n\ndef cosine(a, b):\n    dot = sum(a[t] * b[t] for t in a)\n    na, nb = math.sqrt(sum(v * v for v in a.values())), math.sqrt(sum(v * v for v in b.values()))\n    return dot / (na * nb) if na and nb else 0.0\n\nclass SemanticCache:\n    def __init__(self, threshold, ttl_s=3600):\n        self.threshold, self.ttl_s, self.entries = threshold, ttl_s, {}\n    def get(self, ns, query):\n        q, best, best_sim = embed(query), None, 0.0\n        for e in self.entries.get(ns, []):         # search only inside the namespace\n            sim = cosine(q, e[\"vec\"])\n            if e[\"expires\"] > time.monotonic() and sim > best_sim:\n                best, best_sim = e, sim\n        hit = best is not None and best_sim >= self.threshold\n        return (best[\"answer\"] if hit else None), round(best_sim, 3)\n    def put(self, ns, query, answer):\n        self.entries.setdefault(ns, []).append(\n            {\"vec\": embed(query), \"answer\": answer, \"expires\": time.monotonic() + self.ttl_s})\n    def invalidate(self, ns):                      # call when the knowledge base changes\n        self.entries.pop(ns, None)\n\nns = (\"acme\", \"kb-v7\", \"prompt-v3\", \"model-x\")     # tenant, corpus version, prompt version, model\nfor tau in (0.9, 0.8):\n    c = SemanticCache(threshold=tau)\n    c.put(ns, \"How do I cancel my order?\", \"Orders can be cancelled within 1 hour.\")\n    print(tau, c.get(ns, \"how do i cancel my order\"))           # identical wording: hit\n    print(tau, c.get(ns, \"How do I cancel my subscription?\"))   # 0.833: miss at 0.9, WRONG hit at 0.8\n    print(tau, c.get((\"globex\",) + ns[1:], \"How do I cancel my order?\"))  # other tenant: never hits\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: prompt caching across a 40-step agent session\nOnly the 12k-token tools-and-system prefix is counted, at the same illustrative prices (3 per million\ninput, 1.25× write, 0.1× read), with steps arriving inside the five-minute TTL.\n\n| Cost line | Without caching | With caching |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prefix tokens sent | 40 × 12,000 = 480,000 | same tokens, billed differently |\n| First step | 12,000 × 3 / 10⁶ ≈ 0.036 | one write: 12,000 × 3.75 / 10⁶ ≈ 0.045 |\n| Steps 2-40 | 39 × 12,000 × 3 / 10⁶ ≈ 1.40 | 39 reads: 39 × 12,000 × 0.30 / 10⁶ ≈ 0.14 |\n| **Prefix cost per session** | **≈ 1.44** | **≈ 0.19, about 7-8× less** |\n\n- **The growing transcript adds more:** with incremental breakpoints each step writes only its own delta\n  and reads the rest, so the saving grows with session length.\n- **One dynamic line at the top** (a timestamp) turns every step back into the left column, plus the write\n  premium.\n\n### Memoising read-only tool results within an agent run\nAgents often repeat the same lookup across steps. Cache only tools marked read-only, keyed on tenant, tool\nname and canonical arguments, with a short TTL.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time\n\nREAD_ONLY = {\"search_docs\", \"get_order_status\"}      # never cache tools with side effects\n_cache = {}\n\ndef call_tool(ctx, name, args, tools, ttl_s=60):\n    if name not in READ_ONLY:\n        return tools[name](**args)\n    key = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps([ctx.tenant, name, args], sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()\n    hit = _cache.get(key)\n    if hit and hit[\"expires\"] > time.monotonic():\n        ctx.metrics[\"tool_cache_hits\"] += 1\n        return hit[\"value\"]\n    value = tools[name](**args)\n    _cache[key] = {\"value\": value, \"expires\": time.monotonic() + ttl_s}\n    return value\n```","patterns":["**Track cached-input share per agent step.** Log uncached, cache-write and cache-read input tokens from the usage fields on every step of every run, and alert when the cache-read share for step 2 onward falls. Monitor it per agent version, because prompt edits are the usual cause.","**Freeze the tool list per run.** Load and sort tool schemas once at run start, rather than toggling tools per step by feature flag, so the cached prefix survives all steps. Monitor prefix-cache misses that coincide with tool-list changes.","**Static-first prompt with a CI assertion.** Render tools, then system, then messages; put the breakpoint after the last stable block; add an integration test that sends the same prefix twice and asserts `cache_read_input_tokens` > 0. Monitor cache-read tokens as a fraction of total input.","**Longer TTL for sessions with gaps.** When users pause longer than five minutes between turns, use Anthropic's one-hour option (`\"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\", \"ttl\": \"1h\"}`) only if reuse per hour justifies the 2× write. Monitor write-to-read token ratio.","**Prefix caching on self-hosted engines.** Enable vLLM's automatic prefix caching (the `--enable-prefix-caching` engine argument; check your version's default) or use SGLang's RadixAttention, and route a session's requests to the same replica so its prefix stays resident. Monitor prefix-cache hit rate, TTFT and KV memory pressure.","**Versioned exact cache for pipelines.** Key on a hash of canonical JSON of model, prompt version, parameters and inputs, stored in Redis with a TTL; bump the prompt version to invalidate. Monitor hit rate and spend avoided.","**Semantic cache with sampled precision review.** Namespace per tenant and knowledge-base version, start with a strict threshold, and have a human or judge grade a daily sample of hits. Monitor hit precision, not just hit rate, and invalidate on every knowledge-base publish."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A 40-step session over a 12k-token prefix re-sends roughly half a million\n  prefix tokens. Prompt caching turns most of that into cheap cache reads and cuts time to first token on\n  every step, which is what makes long tool-calling loops affordable.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `cache_control` breakpoints and the usage fields on the Anthropic\n  API, automatic caching and cached-token counts on OpenAI, vLLM prefix caching or SGLang RadixAttention\n  when self-hosting, and Redis or a vector store for response and tool-result caches.\n- **What you control.** Prompt order (tools, then system, then an append-only transcript), where\n  breakpoints sit, keeping tool schemas byte-identical, which read-only tool results you memoise, and\n  whether any user-facing answer may come from a semantic cache at all.\n- **What you can skip.** Semantic-cache tuning for the main agent loop: answers depend on conversation\n  state, so near-miss hits are wrong. Spend the effort on prefix stability instead.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Long-running coding and research agents.** Prompt caching keeps per-step input cost and TTFT flat as\n  the transcript grows, as long as it stays append-only.\n- **RAG agents over a large static manual.** A cached document prefix answers many questions for a\n  fraction of the input price.\n- **Support FAQ front door.** A tenant-scoped semantic cache answers repeated policy questions before the\n  agent loop starts, with sampled precision review.\n- **Agents that repeat lookups.** Memoised read-only tool results cut repeated search or status calls\n  within one run.","mini_project":"**Measure and protect prompt-cache hits in an agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show the cost and TTFT effect of a stable prefix across a 10-step tool-calling loop, and catch\n  regressions automatically.\n- **Steps:** build a small agent with two tools and a long system prompt above the model's minimum; add a\n  `cache_control` breakpoint after it; log uncached, cache-write and cache-read tokens per step; then\n  inject a timestamp at the top and rerun; write a test asserting `cache_read_input_tokens` > 0 on step 2.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-step table for both runs, a cost estimate per session for each, and a test\n  that fails on the timestamped prompt."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Prompt caching stores the model's answer.\"** It reuses the KV tensors of a shared prefix, so only prefill is skipped: input cost and time to first token fall, while the answer is generated fresh and output cost and decode speed are unchanged.","**\"Similar prompts share the cache.\"** Matching is on the exact token sequence in order. One timestamp, user name or reordered tool list near the top misses everything after it, on every agent step.","**\"Turning caching on can only save money.\"** Anthropic cache writes cost about 1.25× input (2× for the one-hour TTL), so a prefix nobody reads within the TTL costs more than no caching. Verify reads in the usage fields.","**\"A semantic cache is a smarter response cache for the agent's answers.\"** Embedding similarity is not answer equivalence: \"cancel my order\" and \"cancel my subscription\" sit close together, and agent answers depend on run state. Keep semantic caches to scoped, FAQ-like traffic with measured precision.","**\"A high hit rate means the cache works.\"** Hits on short prefixes barely move the bill, and semantic hits can be confidently wrong. Track cost and TTFT deltas, cache-read share of input, and sampled hit precision."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request\"] --> E{\"Exact key hit?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes\"| A1[\"Return stored response\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| N[\"Scope to namespace: tenant, KB, prompt, model\"]\n    N --> S{\"Semantic similarity at least tau?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes\"| A2[\"Return cached answer\"]\n    S -->|\"no\"| P{\"Prefix matches cached KV?\"}\n    P -->|\"yes\"| PF[\"Prefill only the suffix\"]\n    P -->|\"no\"| FULL[\"Full prefill, write prefix\"]\n    PF --> G[\"Decode answer fresh\"]\n    FULL --> G\n    G -.->|\"store with TTL\"| N","caption":"Read the request top to bottom: the cheapest full skip is tried first, and whatever falls through still\nbenefits from the prefix cache during prefill."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between prompt caching and a response cache?","a":"- **A response cache skips the whole generation** and returns stored text, so it saves output cost too\n  but only helps when requests repeat.\n- **Prompt caching reuses the KV cache of a shared prefix,** skipping prefill but generating the answer\n  fresh, so it helps every call with a stable prefix and never returns stale text."},{"q":"After a release, your agent's `cache_read_input_tokens` dropped to zero and the bill rose. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Diff the rendered prompt** between two requests and find the first differing token; a timestamp,\n  request id, user name or reordered tool list early in the prompt breaks every later position.\n- **Check the prefix length** is still above the model's minimum and the breakpoint is after the stable\n  block.\n- **Fix** by moving volatile content after the breakpoint, then add a CI test asserting cache reads."},{"q":"When would you refuse to add a semantic cache?","a":"- **When answers are personalised or depend on conversation state,** because a near-miss hit returns a\n  confident wrong answer.\n- **When the corpus changes often** and invalidation cannot keep up.\n- **When a wrong answer is costly,** for example policy, billing or medical questions, since no error\n  metric catches the failure."},{"q":"A 20,000-token manual serves 1,000 questions a day. What does prompt caching save?","a":"- **Input cost falls about nine-fold** with illustrative prices: from about 60.6 to about 6.7 a day, since\n  reads cost around 0.1× input.\n- **Output cost (6.0) is unchanged,** so the total goes from about 66.6 to about 12.7.\n- **TTFT drops** by about the prefill time of 20,000 tokens."},{"q":"How do you choose and validate a semantic-cache threshold?","a":"- **Start strict and loosen with evidence,** because similar queries (\"cancel my order\" vs \"cancel my\n  subscription\") can need different answers.\n- **Validate on sampled hits** graded by a human or judge, tracking hit precision next to hit rate, and\n  consider a cheap verifier call before returning a hit."},{"q":"Which parts of a tool-calling agent would you cache, and with which layer?","a":"- **Prompt caching for the loop:** tools and system prompt first and byte-stable, an append-only\n  transcript, breakpoints after the stable block, verified with the usage fields per step.\n- **Exact caches for deterministic sub-calls:** read-only tool results within a run and embeddings of\n  retrieved chunks, keyed on tenant and arguments with short TTLs.\n- **No semantic cache on the agent's answers,** because they depend on run state, and no caching of\n  side-effecting tools at all."}],"tradeoffs":["**Exact cache: never wrong for its key, rarely hit.** Hit rate on free-form conversation is close to zero; it earns its keep in pipelines and embedding jobs, not chat.","**Semantic cache: hit rate vs correctness.** High hit rates on FAQ traffic, but every hit bets that similarity implies same answer. It flips to a liability when answers are personalised, the corpus changes often, or a wrong answer is costly.","**Prompt caching: cheap reads vs costly writes.** A prefix used once per TTL costs more than no caching. With a 1.25× write and 0.1× read the break-even is a single reuse; a one-hour TTL at a 2× write needs more.","**Prefix ordering vs prompt habits.** Putting the date or user's name at the top fights caching; every byte of volatile content moved earlier shortens the cacheable prefix.","**Resident prefixes vs batch size.** On self-hosted engines a prefix cache occupies KV memory that could hold running sequences, so retention competes with batch size under load.","**Tool-result caching: fewer calls vs stale observations.** Memoising a lookup saves latency and API cost inside a run, but the agent may act on data that changed seconds ago; keep TTLs short and skip caching for anything the agent will act on irreversibly."],"pitfalls":["**A timestamp, request id or user name early in the system prompt.** Symptom: `cache_read_input_tokens` stuck at zero while the bill is unchanged or slightly higher, because every request pays the write premium for an entry nothing reads.","**A prefix shorter than the model's minimum.** Symptom: no cache-creation tokens ever reported despite the breakpoint.","**Tool definitions in nondeterministic order, or a per-request tool list.** Symptom: intermittent hits that correlate with which feature flags were on.","**A semantic cache without tenant scoping.** Symptom: answers referencing another customer's documents, a security incident, not a quality bug.","**A similarity threshold tuned for hit rate only.** Symptom: support tickets saying the assistant answered a different question.","**Caching responses at temperature above zero without a \"regenerate\" bypass.** Symptom: regenerate returns identical text.","**Reporting hit rate instead of cost and latency deltas.** Symptom: a dashboard at 90% hits while the bill barely moves, because the hits were on short prefixes.","**Expecting decode to speed up.** Symptom: unchanged tokens per second after enabling caching, which is correct, since only prefill is skipped.","**Rewriting the agent transcript every step** (trimming, re-summarising, re-inserting memory at the top). Symptom: cache reads cover only the tools-and-system block while step cost keeps climbing. Fix: append-only transcript, memory after the stable prefix, compaction at rare boundaries."],"when":"- **Prompt caching:** wherever a prompt has a stable prefix above the model's minimum: agent loops, long\n  system prompts, document question answering, few-shot classifiers.\n- **Exact-match cache:** deterministic pipelines and embeddings.\n- **Semantic cache:** only for repeat-heavy, low-personalisation traffic, with tenant scoping, explicit\n  invalidation and measured hit precision.\n- **Measure** cache-read tokens as a fraction of total input, TTFT before and after, cost per request,\n  and for semantic caches both hit rate and hit precision.\n- **Day to day:** \"why did the bill double after we added the date to the system prompt\".\n- **Interviews:** distinguish the three layers, explain why prompt caching needs ordered, byte-identical\n  prefixes, and name the semantic cache's wrong-answer risk.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Orders the prompt static-first, places breakpoints, asserts in integration tests that a second identical request reports cache reads, and keys response caches on every input that affects output.","architect":"Decides which layers each product surface gets, sets tenant isolation and retention rules for cached content, and models the cost and latency impact against provider pricing.","fde":"Finds the one dynamic line a customer put at the top of their system prompt, restores their cache hits, and explains why their semantic cache must be partitioned per business unit."},"check":{"q":"An agent product makes about 40 model calls per session over a 12k-token system-and-tools prefix\nfollowed by a growing conversation. Design its caching and state what each layer buys and cannot buy.","a":"Prompt caching is the main lever. Render tools and system prompt first and keep them byte-stable;\nput a breakpoint at the end of that 12k prefix so every call reads it instead of prefilling it, and let\nthe conversation tail be cached incrementally so each turn writes only its own delta. That removes most\ninput cost and most time to first token, but it does nothing for output tokens or decode speed, and it\nneeds the session to stay inside the TTL or to be re-warmed. An exact-match response cache buys little\nhere, since every turn is new, except for deterministic sub-calls such as embeddings or classification\ninside tools. A semantic cache is inappropriate for the main loop because answers depend on conversation\nstate and would be wrong on a near-miss hit. I would verify with the usage fields, assert cache reads in\nan integration test, and watch the bill for regressions when someone adds a dynamic line to the system\nprompt."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does provider-side prompt caching reduce?","options":["Output token cost and decode latency","Input token cost and time to first token for the cached prefix","The number of model calls needed per conversation","The size of the context window needed for the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provider reuses the KV cache for the prefix so prefill is skipped and cached input is billed at a discount. Generation of the answer still happens in full, so output cost and decode speed are unchanged."},{"q":"Why does changing one word at the start of the system prompt eliminate prompt-cache hits?","options":["Because the provider hashes the whole request and any change invalidates the hash","Because the cache matches the exact token sequence in order, so a change at position i invalidates every cached position after it","Because system prompts are never cached, only messages","Because changing the prompt resets the five-minute TTL"],"answer":1,"explanation":"KV entries for position i depend on all earlier tokens, so matching is a prefix match on the token sequence. An early change means no later position can be reused, which is why volatile content belongs at the end."},{"q":"What is the primary risk of a semantic cache?","options":["It increases time to first token for cache misses","It cannot be scoped per tenant","A query similar in embedding space but different in meaning receives a confident, wrong cached answer","It requires the model to support prompt caching"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embedding similarity is not answer equivalence. \"Cancel my order\" and \"cancel my subscription\" sit close together and need different answers, so hit precision must be measured and the threshold tuned for correctness, not hit rate."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/engines","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Zheng et al., 2024 — SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs","Official docs: TensorRT-LLM","Official docs: llama.cpp and Ollama"],"why":"- **A plain `model.generate()` loop wastes the GPU.** It serves one request at a time, allocates KV cache\n  contiguously at maximum length, and runs generic attention kernels: single-digit utilisation and\n  throughput tens of times below the hardware, because decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and only\n  batching amortises the weight reads.\n- **Serving well is a long list of systems work:** continuous batching, paged KV cache, fused attention\n  kernels, quantized weight kernels, prefix caching, speculative decoding, constrained decoding, tensor\n  parallelism, a streaming HTTP API, metrics and graceful draining. Each is a research-paper-sized\n  problem; engines package them so an application team does not rebuild them.\n- **The local end has a different problem.** A 7B model on a laptop or edge box needs memory-mapped\n  quantized weights, CPU vector instructions, Apple Metal or a consumer GPU, and a binary with no Python\n  dependency tree. That is llama.cpp's reason to exist; Ollama makes it a one-line install with a model\n  registry.\n- **The naive alternatives fail.** \"Write our own server on Transformers\" loses on throughput and memory.\n  \"Only use a hosted API\" fails when data cannot leave the premises, when per-token prices exceed owning\n  GPUs at volume, or when you need a fine-tuned or open-weight model the provider does not host. Engines\n  make self-hosting viable.","how":"### Shared machinery\nAll the datacenter engines implement the same core ideas:\n- **A token-granularity scheduler** (continuous or in-flight batching).\n- **A block-based KV cache with a page table** (PagedAttention or an equivalent).\n- **Prefix sharing** across requests.\n- **Quantized weight formats** (AWQ, GPTQ, FP8 and relatives).\n- **An OpenAI-compatible HTTP server** with streaming, and grammar-constrained decoding.\n\nThey differ in emphasis, hardware reach, model format and how much work it takes to run them.\n\n### A request's path through a datacenter engine\n1. **Startup sizing.** Load weights, run a profiling pass to measure peak activation memory, and turn the\n   rest of the memory allowance into a fixed pool of KV blocks. In vLLM the allowance is\n   `--gpu-memory-utilization` × GPU memory, so the KV pool is that minus weights minus activations; the\n   startup log reports the capacity in tokens. Everything later is bounded by this pool, *because* KV\n   memory, not compute, limits concurrency.\n2. **Admission.** The HTTP layer applies the chat template, tokenizes, and queues the request. Templating\n   inside the engine is why a wrong template corrupts output silently: the model sees a format it was\n   never trained on.\n3. **Scheduling each step.** The scheduler picks which waiting requests to prefill and which running ones\n   to decode, subject to the sequence cap and free blocks. Prefill is often chunked so a 30k-token prompt\n   does not stall every running decode for a whole step.\n4. **Prefix lookup.** Before prefilling, reuse the prompt's leading blocks if cached, so only the new\n   suffix is computed.\n5. **Decode and stream.** Each step emits one token per running sequence (more with speculative\n   decoding), applies any grammar mask for structured output, detokenizes incrementally and streams.\n6. **Finish or preempt.** On a stop condition blocks return to the pool. If a running sequence needs a\n   block and none is free, the engine preempts a sequence and recomputes or swaps its cache later, which\n   shows up as latency spikes rather than errors.\n\n- **Engines differ mainly in steps 3 to 5:** scheduler policy, prefix matching and kernel speed, which is\n  why they must be compared on your traffic rather than on a single prompt.\n\n### vLLM\n- **What it is:** the engine that introduced PagedAttention. Python-first, loads Hugging Face checkpoints\n  directly, widest model range.\n- **Hardware:** several backends, NVIDIA first, AMD and others following.\n- **Features:** tensor and pipeline parallelism, automatic prefix caching, speculative decoding,\n  multi-LoRA serving and guided decoding.\n- **Run it:** `vllm serve <model>` gives an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The flags that matter most are\n  `--max-model-len`, `--gpu-memory-utilization`, `--max-num-seqs` and `--tensor-parallel-size`.\n- **Why default:** the default for production serving on NVIDIA hardware because of breadth and community\n  pace.\n\n### SGLang\n- **From the LMSYS group.** Its distinctive piece is RadixAttention: the KV cache is a radix tree over\n  token sequences, so any request sharing a prefix with any cached sequence reuses it automatically, with\n  LRU eviction.\n- **Strong for** multi-turn chat, agents and few-shot workloads where prefixes repeat heavily.\n- **Also ships** a frontend language for structured programs (parallel calls, constrained generation) and\n  fast constrained decoding.\n- **API and deployment** are similar to vLLM; teams usually benchmark both on their own traffic.\n\n### TGI (Text Generation Inference)\n- **Hugging Face's server:** a Rust router in front of Python model shards, continuous batching, paged\n  attention, several quantization backends, token streaming, and an OpenAI-style Messages API.\n- **Its draw** is integration with the Hub and Inference Endpoints; if your fleet already lives in the\n  Hugging Face ecosystem it is the shortest path.\n- **Caveat:** its pace on the newest serving tricks has generally trailed vLLM and SGLang, so check the\n  project's current maintenance status and roadmap before choosing it for a new deployment.\n\n### TensorRT-LLM\n- **NVIDIA's engine.** It compiles models ahead of time into TensorRT engines specialised for a GPU\n  architecture and a configuration (maximum batch size, sequence length, parallelism).\n- **That buys** aggressive kernel fusion and FP8 on Hopper-class parts. It implements in-flight batching\n  and paged KV cache.\n- **Newer option:** recent releases add a PyTorch-based backend that loads checkpoints directly,\n  narrowing the operational gap, but the compiled path remains the reason to choose it.\n- **Serving:** typically behind Triton Inference Server (NVIDIA's NIM containers build on the same stack).\n- **Ceiling vs cost:** the highest performance ceiling on NVIDIA hardware and the highest operational\n  cost: a build step per model and GPU type, NVIDIA-only, and rebuilds when limits change.\n\n### llama.cpp\n- **A C/C++ runtime** built around the GGUF weight format and its k-quant family (Q4_K_M and relatives).\n- **Runs on** CPUs with SIMD, Apple Silicon through Metal, and GPUs through CUDA, Vulkan and others.\n- **Partial offload:** a chosen number of layers goes to the GPU (`-ngl`), the rest stays in system RAM.\n- **API:** `llama-server` exposes an OpenAI-compatible API.\n- **Scope:** designed for one or a few users on commodity hardware; it has batching, but not the many-user\n  throughput machinery of the datacenter engines.\n\n### Ollama\n- **A packaging layer over llama.cpp and its ggml library.** Newer releases run some model families on\n  Ollama's own ggml-based engine, but the design and limits are the same.\n- **Adds** a model registry (`ollama pull`), a Modelfile for prompts and parameters, a REST API on port\n  11434 with an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and automatic GPU detection.\n- **The standard developer-laptop and prototyping runtime.**\n- **Defaults are single-user:** environment variables such as `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` and the per-model\n  context length need changing before it serves a team, and it inherits llama.cpp's throughput ceiling.\n\n### Side by side\n| Engine | Target | Concurrency machinery | Hardware | Weight format |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| vLLM | datacenter | continuous batching, paged KV, prefix cache | NVIDIA, AMD, others | HF checkpoints; AWQ/GPTQ/FP8 |\n| SGLang | datacenter | as vLLM, plus RadixAttention | NVIDIA, AMD | HF checkpoints; quantized variants |\n| TGI | datacenter, HF ecosystem | continuous batching, paged attention | NVIDIA, AMD, others | HF checkpoints; several quant backends |\n| TensorRT-LLM | datacenter, peak performance | in-flight batching, paged KV | NVIDIA only | compiled TensorRT engines |\n| llama.cpp | laptop, edge, CPU | small-scale batching | CPU, Apple Silicon, consumer GPUs | GGUF |\n| Ollama | developer machine | single-user defaults | as llama.cpp | GGUF via registry |\n\n### What agent traffic asks of an engine\n| Agent need | Engine feature | What to check |\n|---|---|---|\n| Steps re-send a long, stable prefix | prefix caching (vLLM automatic prefix caching, SGLang RadixAttention) | prefix-cache hit rate and TTFT on step 2 onward |\n| Many concurrent runs, each with a growing transcript | KV pool size, `--max-num-seqs`, `--max-model-len` | preemptions as runs get long |\n| Tool calls in the model's output | a tool-call parser and the right chat template per model | parse-error rate on a tool-calling contract suite |\n| Structured arguments and plans | grammar-constrained or guided decoding | invalid-JSON rate with and without it |\n| Fan-out to parallel sub-agents | continuous batching across the parallel calls | step latency at peak fan-out |\n\n- **Tool calling is engine-specific.** In vLLM, OpenAI-format tool calls need `--enable-auto-tool-choice`\n  plus a `--tool-call-parser` matching the model family (for example `llama3_json` for Llama 3.1) at\n  startup; other engines have their own switches. Without them the request is rejected or the call arrives\n  as plain text the agent cannot run.","example":"### One client, two engines\nThe same client code runs against a datacenter engine and a laptop runtime because both speak the OpenAI\nchat API; only the base URL and model name change. The vLLM flags set the context ceiling, the share of\nGPU memory handed to weights plus KV cache, the batch cap and prefix caching.\n\n```bash\n# Datacenter: vLLM serving an 8B instruct model, OpenAI-compatible API on :8000\nvllm serve meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct --max-model-len 8192 \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 --max-num-seqs 128 --enable-prefix-caching\n# Laptop: Ollama (llama.cpp underneath), OpenAI-compatible API on :11434\nollama pull llama3.1:8b && ollama serve\n```\n\n- **Order matters for Ollama:** `ollama pull` talks to a running server, so on a headless Linux box start\n  `ollama serve` first (in another shell or as a service); the desktop app on macOS and Windows already\n  runs it.\n\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nENGINES = {\"vllm\":   (\"http://localhost:8000/v1\",  \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\"),\n           \"ollama\": (\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", \"llama3.1:8b\")}\n\ndef stream(engine: str, prompt: str):\n    base_url, model = ENGINES[engine]\n    client = OpenAI(base_url=base_url, api_key=\"not-needed\")   # local engines ignore the key\n    resp = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=model, max_tokens=128, stream=True,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n    )\n    for chunk in resp:\n        delta = chunk.choices[0].delta.content\n        if delta:\n            print(delta, end=\"\", flush=True)\n    print()\n\nstream(\"vllm\", \"Explain continuous batching in two sentences.\")\nstream(\"ollama\", \"Explain continuous batching in two sentences.\")\n# Same code, two engines: what differs is throughput under concurrency, not the API.\n```\n\n### What the vLLM flags buy on one 80 GB GPU\nLlama-3.1-8B has 32 layers, 8 KV heads and head dimension 128.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| KV cache per token (bf16) | 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KiB |\n| Memory allowance | 0.90 × 80 = 72 GB |\n| Weights | about 16 GB |\n| Activations and CUDA graphs | a few GB (illustrative) |\n| KV pool | roughly 52 GB, or about 400,000 tokens |\n| Sequences at the full 8,192-token context | only about 48 |\n\n- **`--max-num-seqs 128` is reachable** only if the average live sequence is around 3,000 tokens or less;\n  beyond that, the scheduler holds requests in the waiting queue or preempts.\n- **Raising `--max-model-len` to 32k does not change the pool,** it only lets fewer, longer sequences use\n  it.\n\n### Capacity calculator to run before choosing flags\nPure Python, runs as is; plug in your model config and GPU.\n\n```python\ndef kv_pool_tokens(gpu_gb, util, weights_gb, activations_gb, n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim, bytes_el=2):\n    per_token = 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_el      # K and V, every layer\n    pool_bytes = (gpu_gb * util - weights_gb - activations_gb) * 1e9\n    return per_token, int(pool_bytes // per_token)\n\nper_tok, pool = kv_pool_tokens(80, 0.90, 16, 4, n_layers=32, n_kv_heads=8, head_dim=128)\nprint(per_tok // 1024, \"KiB per token;\", f\"{pool:,} tokens in the pool\")   # 128 KiB; 396,728 tokens\nfor ctx in (8_192, 3_000, 1_000):\n    print(f\"avg live sequence {ctx:>5,} tokens -> {pool // ctx} concurrent sequences\")\n# 8,192 -> 48   3,000 -> 132   1,000 -> 396\n```\n\n### Tool-calling contract test against any engine\nRun the same agent-shaped request against the hosted API, vLLM and Ollama before switching an agent's\n`base_url`. It asserts the engine returns a structured tool call with parseable arguments, not text.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nTOOLS = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_order_status\",\n    \"description\": \"Look up an order by id\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                   \"required\": [\"order_id\"]}}}]\n\ndef check_tool_call(base_url, model):\n    client = OpenAI(base_url=base_url, api_key=\"not-needed\")\n    resp = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=model, max_tokens=256, tools=TOOLS,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is order A-1042?\"}])\n    calls = resp.choices[0].message.tool_calls or []\n    assert calls, f\"{base_url}: no structured tool call, got text: {resp.choices[0].message.content!r}\"\n    args = json.loads(calls[0].function.arguments)          # raises if arguments are not valid JSON\n    assert calls[0].function.name == \"get_order_status\" and args.get(\"order_id\") == \"A-1042\"\n    return \"ok\"\n\nfor name, (url, model) in ENGINES.items():                  # ENGINES from the example above\n    print(name, check_tool_call(url, model))\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing one GPU for concurrent agent runs\nSame 80 GB GPU and Llama-3.1-8B (128 KiB per token, about 400,000 tokens of KV pool). Agents start at a\n6k-token prefix and grow ~1.5k tokens per step (illustrative).\n\n| Agent run length | Live tokens at the last step | Concurrent runs at that length |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 steps | ~12,000 | ~33 |\n| 10 steps | ~19,500 | ~20 |\n| 20 steps | ~34,500 | ~11 |\n\n- **Long runs, not users, set capacity:** the same GPU holds three times fewer 20-step runs than 5-step\n  ones, and needs `--max-model-len` above 32k.\n- **Prefix sharing recovers some of it:** with prefix caching, runs sharing the 6k prefix can share its\n  blocks instead of storing it once per run.","patterns":["**Develop agents locally, serve them on a GPU engine.** Point the agent framework's OpenAI-compatible client at Ollama during development and at vLLM or SGLang in staging and production, changing only `base_url` and the model name. Monitor tool-call parse errors when you switch.","**Benchmark on recorded agent traces.** Replay real multi-step runs, including parallel tool calls, at target concurrency, rather than single prompts, because prefix reuse and long transcripts dominate agent traffic. Monitor step latency p95 and preemptions as runs lengthen.","**Size flags from KV arithmetic.** Compute the pool as above, then set `--max-model-len` and `--max-num-seqs` so the expected traffic mix fits; confirm against the capacity the vLLM startup log reports. Monitor KV-cache usage and preemption counts on the engine's Prometheus `/metrics` endpoint.","**Pin and re-benchmark on upgrade.** Pin the engine version in the container image and replay recorded production traffic at target concurrency before every upgrade, because vLLM and SGLang move fast. Monitor tokens per second at target concurrency and p95 time to first token.","**Contract tests per engine and model.** Keep an OpenAI-compatible contract, and run tool-calling and JSON-mode tests against every engine and model pair, because each implements function calling with its own parser and chat template. Monitor parse-error rate in production.","**Configure Ollama before sharing it.** For a small team, raise `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` and set the context length explicitly (for example `PARAMETER num_ctx 8192` in a Modelfile), so long prompts are not silently truncated. Monitor latency as users are added; move to a GPU engine when it climbs.","**Build TensorRT-LLM engines per GPU type in CI.** Treat the compiled engine as an artifact tagged by model, GPU architecture and limits, and fail the deploy if the node type does not match. Monitor load failures after hardware refreshes."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent traffic is many calls per run over long, repeated prefixes with\n  tool calls in the output. The engine's prefix reuse, KV pool and tool-call parser decide step latency,\n  concurrent runs per GPU and whether your loop sees valid tool calls at all.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The `base_url` your agent framework's OpenAI-compatible client\n  points at; `vllm serve` flags; Ollama on a laptop for local agent development; the engine's `/metrics`\n  endpoint behind your agent dashboards.\n- **What you control.** Engine choice per deployment, context ceiling and batch cap from KV arithmetic,\n  prefix caching, the chat template and tool-call parser for the model, and a contract test suite for tool\n  calling and JSON on every engine and model pair.\n- **What you can skip.** Kernel internals and the TensorRT build details unless you run a fixed,\n  high-volume model fleet. Most agent teams use a hosted API or vLLM or SGLang.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Local agent development.** Ollama or llama.cpp on a laptop runs the agent loop offline, with the same\n  OpenAI-compatible client code used in production.\n- **Agents on private data.** vLLM or SGLang inside a customer's VPC keeps prompts and tool results on\n  premises.\n- **Serving a fine-tuned agent model.** An engine hosts the model the provider does not, with prefix\n  caching for the agent's stable prompt.\n- **High-volume agent fleets.** A fixed model on a homogeneous NVIDIA fleet can justify TensorRT-LLM's\n  build pipeline for peak throughput.","mini_project":"**Run one agent on two engines and compare (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** move a small tool-calling agent from Ollama to vLLM or SGLang without code changes beyond\n  `base_url` and model name, and measure the difference.\n- **Steps:** build a 5-step agent with two tools using an OpenAI-compatible client; run the tool-calling\n  contract test on both engines; replay 20 concurrent runs on each; record step latency, prefix-cache hit\n  rate where reported, and tool-call parse failures.\n- **Done when:** you have a table per engine of p50 and p95 step latency, parse failures and completed\n  runs at 20-way concurrency, plus the flags you had to set to make tool calls parse."},"typed":{"type":"lab","task":"Your support agent runs on a hosted API and the team wants to move it to a self-hosted engine. You build\nthe deploy gate for that move.\n- **Contract check:** classify each engine's recorded tool-call response; the agent may switch\n  `base_url` only to engines that pass.\n- **KV plan:** set `--max-model-len` and the concurrent-run cap for 20-step runs on one 80 GB GPU.\n- **Time box:** 45-60 minutes.\n- **Prerequisites:** KV cache memory estimation, and this lesson's capacity calculator and tool-calling\n  contract test.\n- **Runs offline:** the five responses were recorded in OpenAI chat-completions shape, so no GPU, engine\n  or API key is needed. Standard library plus `pytest`; checked on Python 3.12 with pytest 9.1.\n- **Done means** the tests pass and the script prints the expected report.","starter":"```python\n# engine_gate.py: decide whether your agent may switch base_url to a self-hosted engine\n# Offline: the responses below were recorded in OpenAI chat-completions shape; no GPU or server needed.\nimport json\n\nTOOLS = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_order_status\",\n    \"description\": \"Look up an order by id\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                   \"required\": [\"order_id\"]}}}]\n\ndef _resp(content=None, tool_calls=None, finish=\"tool_calls\"):\n    return {\"choices\": [{\"finish_reason\": finish,\n                         \"message\": {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": content, \"tool_calls\": tool_calls}}]}\n\ndef _call(name, arguments):\n    return [{\"id\": \"call_1\", \"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": name, \"arguments\": arguments}}]\n\n# Recorded answers to \"Where is order A-1042?\" with TOOLS attached, one per engine setup.\nRECORDED = {\n    \"hosted\":         _resp(tool_calls=_call(\"get_order_status\", '{\"order_id\": \"A-1042\"}')),\n    \"vllm-no-parser\": _resp(content='{\"name\": \"get_order_status\", \"parameters\": {\"order_id\": \"A-1042\"}}',\n                            finish=\"stop\"),\n    \"vllm-parser\":    _resp(tool_calls=_call(\"get_order_status\", '{\"order_id\": \"A-1042\"}')),\n    \"ollama-short\":   _resp(tool_calls=_call(\"get_order_status\", '{\"order_id\": \"A-10'), finish=\"length\"),\n    \"local-gguf\":     _resp(tool_calls=_call(\"get_order_status\", \"{}\")),\n}\n\ndef contract_check(resp: dict, tools: list) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return one verdict for the first tool call in a recorded response:\n    \"ok\"             structured call to a known tool, JSON arguments, every required argument present\n    \"text_tool_call\" no structured call, but the content is JSON naming a known tool (parser not enabled)\n    \"no_tool_call\"   no structured call and the content is not a tool call\n    \"unknown_tool\"   structured call to a tool that is not in `tools`\n    \"bad_json\"       arguments are not valid JSON\n    \"missing_args\"   valid JSON, but a required argument is absent\n    \"\"\"\n    # YOUR CODE: classify the response; check the cases in the order listed above.\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"contract_check\")\n\ndef kv_plan(gpu_gb, util, weights_gb, activations_gb, n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim,\n            prefix_tokens, tokens_per_step, steps, bytes_el=2) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Size vLLM flags for agent runs that start at `prefix_tokens` and grow `tokens_per_step` per step.\n    Return a dict with these int keys:\n      per_token_bytes  K and V for every layer, for one token\n      pool_tokens      (gpu_gb * util - weights_gb - activations_gb) * 1e9 // per_token_bytes\n      peak_tokens      live tokens at the last step: prefix + (steps - 1) * tokens_per_step\n      max_model_len    peak_tokens rounded UP to a multiple of 1024\n      concurrent_runs  how many runs at peak length fit in the pool: pool_tokens // peak_tokens\n    \"\"\"\n    # YOUR CODE\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"kv_plan\")\n\ndef ready_engines(recorded: dict, tools: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Names of engines whose recorded response passes the contract, in insertion order.\"\"\"\n    # YOUR CODE: reuse contract_check\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"ready_engines\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    try:\n        for name, resp in RECORDED.items():\n            print(f\"{name:<15}{contract_check(resp, TOOLS)}\")\n        p = kv_plan(80, 0.90, 16, 4, n_layers=32, n_kv_heads=8, head_dim=128,   # Llama-3.1-8B, 80 GB GPU\n                    prefix_tokens=6_000, tokens_per_step=1_500, steps=20)\n        print(f\"KV per token: {p['per_token_bytes'] // 1024} KiB\")\n        print(f\"KV pool: {p['pool_tokens']:,} tokens\")\n        print(f\"20-step run peaks at {p['peak_tokens']:,} tokens -> --max-model-len {p['max_model_len']}, \"\n              f\"{p['concurrent_runs']} concurrent runs\")\n        print(\"switch allowed to:\", \", \".join(ready_engines(RECORDED, TOOLS)))\n    except NotImplementedError as e:\n        print(\"not implemented yet:\", e)\n```\n\nAs given, it prints `not implemented yet: contract_check`. The recorded cases mirror real failures:\nthe parser switch left off, a `max_tokens` too small for the arguments, and a local model that calls\nthe tool without its required argument.","expected":"```\nhosted         ok\nvllm-no-parser text_tool_call\nvllm-parser    ok\nollama-short   bad_json\nlocal-gguf     missing_args\nKV per token: 128 KiB\nKV pool: 396,728 tokens\n20-step run peaks at 34,500 tokens -> --max-model-len 34816, 11 concurrent runs\nswitch allowed to: hosted, vllm-parser\n```","tests":"```python\n# test_engine_gate.py: run with `pytest -q`\nimport pytest\nfrom engine_gate import RECORDED, TOOLS, _call, _resp, contract_check, kv_plan, ready_engines\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"engine, verdict\", [\n    (\"hosted\", \"ok\"), (\"vllm-no-parser\", \"text_tool_call\"), (\"vllm-parser\", \"ok\"),\n    (\"ollama-short\", \"bad_json\"), (\"local-gguf\", \"missing_args\")])\ndef test_recorded_verdicts(engine, verdict):\n    assert contract_check(RECORDED[engine], TOOLS) == verdict\n\ndef test_plain_prose_is_not_a_tool_call():\n    assert contract_check(_resp(content=\"Your order is on its way.\", finish=\"stop\"), TOOLS) == \"no_tool_call\"\n\ndef test_unknown_tool_is_caught_before_arguments():\n    assert contract_check(_resp(tool_calls=_call(\"delete_order\", \"not json\")), TOOLS) == \"unknown_tool\"\n\ndef test_text_naming_an_unknown_tool_is_just_text():\n    assert contract_check(_resp(content='{\"name\": \"refund\", \"parameters\": {}}'), TOOLS) == \"no_tool_call\"\n\ndef test_kv_plan_for_20_step_runs():\n    p = kv_plan(80, 0.90, 16, 4, n_layers=32, n_kv_heads=8, head_dim=128,\n                prefix_tokens=6_000, tokens_per_step=1_500, steps=20)\n    assert p == {\"per_token_bytes\": 131_072, \"pool_tokens\": 396_728, \"peak_tokens\": 34_500,\n                 \"max_model_len\": 34_816, \"concurrent_runs\": 11}\n\ndef test_kv_plan_short_runs_fit_three_times_more():\n    p = kv_plan(80, 0.90, 16, 4, n_layers=32, n_kv_heads=8, head_dim=128,\n                prefix_tokens=6_000, tokens_per_step=1_500, steps=5)\n    assert (p[\"peak_tokens\"], p[\"max_model_len\"], p[\"concurrent_runs\"]) == (12_000, 12_288, 33)\n\ndef test_only_passing_engines_are_ready():\n    assert ready_engines(RECORDED, TOOLS) == [\"hosted\", \"vllm-parser\"]\n```\n\nAll 11 fail on the starter with `NotImplementedError`; a correct solution passes all 11.","failures":"- **Inject: skip the required-argument check.** `local-gguf` turns `ok` and `ready_engines` admits it,\n  so in production the agent calls `get_order_status` with no id. Debug by printing the verdict per\n  recorded case and diffing against the table; any extra `ok` means a check is missing.\n- **Inject: parse arguments before checking the tool name.** `test_unknown_tool_is_caught_before_arguments`\n  gets `bad_json`, which sends you to fix JSON when the model actually picked a tool you never offered.\n  Debug by checking the order of the cases against the docstring.\n- **Inject: round `max_model_len` down (`peak // 1024 * 1024`).** You get 33792, below the 34,500-token\n  peak, so the engine would refuse the run's last steps for exceeding the context. Debug by asserting\n  `max_model_len >= peak_tokens` before printing flags.\n- **Inject: use 1024³ bytes per GB.** The pool becomes 425,984 tokens and the run cap rises from 11 to\n  12 (33 to 35 for 5-step runs), so both `kv_plan` tests fail and you would over-admit. Debug by matching\n  the unit to the one your engine's startup log reports.","submission":"- **`engine_gate.py`** with the three functions implemented and no edits to `RECORDED` or `TOOLS`.\n- **A `pytest -q` run** showing 11 passed, plus the script output matching the expected block exactly.\n- **One extra recorded case** of your own (a response from a real engine if you have one, or a\n  hand-written prose answer) with its verdict and a test for it.\n- **A short note (3-5 sentences)** naming the vLLM flags the `vllm-no-parser` setup is missing, and the\n  `--max-model-len` and run cap you would deploy, tied to the plan's numbers.\n- **Evaluated on:** tests green, verdicts in the documented order, and a note that a reviewer can turn\n  into a `vllm serve` command."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q1{\"Where does it run?\"} -->|\"laptop or edge\"| L{\"Want one-line install?\"}\n    L -->|\"yes\"| OL[\"Ollama\"]\n    L -->|\"no, embed or tune\"| LC[\"llama.cpp\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"shared GPU service\"| Q2{\"Traffic shape?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"heavy prefix reuse, agents\"| SG[\"SGLang\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"unique prompts, broad models\"| VL[\"vLLM\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"few fixed models, NVIDIA fleet\"| TR[\"TensorRT-LLM\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"Hugging Face platform\"| TG[\"TGI\"]\n    SG --> B[\"Benchmark at target concurrency\"]\n    VL --> B\n    TR --> B\n    TG --> B","caption":"Start at the top with where the agent's model runs, then let traffic shape and operational budget pick\nthe engine; the leaves are starting points to benchmark on your agent traces, not verdicts."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is an inference engine much faster than a `model.generate()` loop on the same GPU?","a":"- **Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound,** so serving one request at a time leaves the GPU at single-digit\n  utilisation; continuous batching reuses each weight read across many sequences.\n- **Paged KV cache** avoids allocating every request at maximum length, so far more sequences fit, and\n  fused and quantized kernels cut time per step."},{"q":"vLLM runs far fewer sequences than `--max-num-seqs` 128 and keeps preempting. What do you check?","a":"- **The KV pool size in the startup log:** `--gpu-memory-utilization` × GPU memory minus weights and\n  activations, divided by bytes per token.\n- **The live context lengths:** with long sequences the pool fills well before 128; on an 80 GB GPU with\n  an 8B GQA model only about 48 fit at 8,192 tokens.\n- **Fix** with a lower `--max-model-len` (sending long-context traffic to a separate deployment), an FP8\n  KV cache (`--kv-cache-dtype fp8`) or a quantized model, or more GPUs."},{"q":"When would you not pick TensorRT-LLM despite its higher ceiling?","a":"- **When the model list is long or changing,** because every model, GPU type and limit change needs a\n  rebuild.\n- **On mixed or non-NVIDIA hardware,** since it is NVIDIA-only.\n- **When the team cannot own a build pipeline;** vLLM or SGLang get most of the performance with far less\n  operational cost."},{"q":"Are llama.cpp and vLLM competitors?","a":"- **No: they target different deployments.** llama.cpp runs GGUF weights on CPUs, Apple Silicon and\n  consumer GPUs for one or a few users; vLLM serves many concurrent users on datacenter GPUs.\n- **Formats differ too:** GGUF for llama.cpp, Hugging Face checkpoints (optionally AWQ, GPTQ or FP8) for\n  vLLM, so moving between them means switching weight files."},{"q":"Your tool calls work on the hosted API but break on a self-hosted engine. Why?","a":"- **\"OpenAI-compatible\" covers the request shape, not behaviour.** Each engine implements function calling\n  with its own parser and chat template, so outputs parse differently by engine and model.\n- **Check the chat template** matches the model (base vs instruct), and run contract tests for tool\n  calling and JSON mode on every engine and model pair."},{"q":"You are moving a 20-step agent from a hosted API to one GPU. Which engine and settings?","a":"- **Engine:** SGLang or vLLM with prefix caching on, because every step re-sends the same tools-and-system\n  prefix; benchmark both on recorded agent traces.\n- **Settings from KV arithmetic:** a 20-step run reaches ~34.5k tokens, so `--max-model-len` must exceed\n  that and only about 11 such runs fit on 80 GB with an 8B GQA model; cap concurrent runs to match.\n- **Gate on behaviour:** tool-calling parser and template enabled (in vLLM, `--enable-auto-tool-choice`\n  and `--tool-call-parser`) and a contract suite passing before any traffic moves."}],"tradeoffs":["**vLLM breadth vs churn.** Breadth (models, hardware, features) comes with a fast-moving codebase; pinning and re-benchmarking on upgrade is part of owning it, whereas a compiled TensorRT-LLM engine is frozen and predictable until you need to change it.","**TensorRT-LLM peak vs rigidity.** Peak throughput is bought with an ahead-of-time build per model, GPU type and configuration; it pays off for a few high-volume models on a homogeneous NVIDIA fleet, not for a long tail of models or mixed hardware.","**SGLang prefix sharing vs unique prompts.** Radix-tree sharing wins with heavy prefix reuse (agents, multi-turn, shared system prompts); on single-shot traffic with unique prompts the advantage over vLLM shrinks to whatever the current kernels give.","**Local portability vs concurrency.** llama.cpp and Ollama run anywhere with no Python stack, but their throughput under many concurrent users is far below a GPU engine: right for one user per box, wrong for a shared service.","**Self-hosted engine vs hosted API for agents.** Self-hosting controls data and per-token cost at volume and lets you serve a fine-tuned agent model, but you take on tool-call parsing, template correctness and capacity; a hosted API wins until volume or data rules force the move.","**TGI ecosystem fit vs cadence.** Hub and Inference Endpoints integration saves work, but adopting it means accepting Hugging Face's cadence for serving optimisations."],"pitfalls":["**Benchmarking a single request and choosing llama.cpp or Ollama for a multi-user service.** Symptom: latency climbs steeply as users are added, because batching is limited to a handful of parallel slots rather than the hundreds of sequences a datacenter scheduler juggles.","**Setting `--max-model-len` and `--gpu-memory-utilization` without the KV-cache arithmetic.** Symptom: an OOM at startup or a scheduler that admits only a handful of sequences.","**Mixing weight formats.** Symptom: a GGUF file the datacenter engines load only experimentally or not at all, or an AWQ checkpoint llama.cpp cannot read, discovered on deploy day.","**Assuming \"OpenAI-compatible\" means identical behaviour.** Symptom: tool calls and JSON mode parse differently across engines and models, because each implements function calling with its own parser and chat template.","**Running Ollama with its default context length.** Symptom: long prompts silently truncated, since the runtime applies its own context window unless the model or request overrides it.","**Serving a base model with an instruct chat template, or the reverse.** Symptom: rambling or never-terminating output.","**A TensorRT engine built on one GPU type deployed to another.** Symptom: a load failure on the new nodes after a hardware refresh.","**Serving an agent model without tool-call parsing enabled.** Symptom: the agent loop receives the tool call as plain text in `content` and either stops or answers without acting. Fix: enable the engine's tool-calling support with the right parser and template, and gate the deploy on the contract test."],"when":"- **Where does it run?** Developer laptop or edge: llama.cpp or Ollama. A shared GPU service: one of the\n  datacenter engines.\n- **What concurrency and traffic shape?** Unique single-shot prompts favour vLLM; heavy prefix reuse and\n  structured programs favour SGLang; a few very high-volume models on a homogeneous NVIDIA fleet justify\n  TensorRT-LLM; a Hugging Face-centric platform points at TGI.\n- **How much operational effort?** Compiled engines need a build pipeline, vLLM and SGLang need version\n  discipline, Ollama needs almost nothing.\n- **Measure** tokens per second at your target concurrency, p95 time to first token and GPU memory\n  headroom; never choose on a single-request benchmark.\n- **Interviews:** explain what continuous batching and paged KV cache do, why llama.cpp and vLLM are not\n  competitors, and what the TensorRT-LLM build step buys and costs.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the engine, tunes context length, memory utilisation and batch caps, enables prefix caching and quantization, and benchmarks candidates on recorded production traffic.","architect":"Chooses the engine family per deployment target, decides whether the TensorRT-LLM build pipeline is worth its rigidity, and standardises on an OpenAI-compatible contract so engines stay swappable.","fde":"Stands up vLLM or SGLang inside a customer's VPC, or Ollama on an analyst's workstation, and translates their \"it is slow\" into the batching and memory numbers that explain it."},"check":{"q":"A customer prototyped a support assistant on Ollama on a workstation and wants to roll it out to\n300 agents on two NVIDIA GPUs. What do you change, and what do you keep?","a":"Keep the model family, the prompts and the OpenAI-compatible client code, since those transfer.\nChange the runtime: Ollama's llama.cpp core serves one or a few users well but lacks the large-batch\nscheduler and paged KV-cache machinery that 300 concurrent users need, so latency would grow with every\nadded user.\nMove to vLLM or SGLang on the GPUs; SGLang if the support prompts share a large system prefix, because\nRadixAttention reuses it automatically, otherwise vLLM for breadth. Switch from the GGUF file to the\nHugging Face checkpoint, quantized with AWQ or FP8 if memory demands it, and do the KV-cache arithmetic\nto set the context ceiling and memory utilisation so the scheduler can hold enough sequences. Put the two\nGPUs behind one server with tensor parallelism or run two replicas behind a load balancer, depending on\nmodel size. Benchmark on recorded traffic at target concurrency, verify tool calling still parses, and\nadd backpressure and metrics in front. TensorRT-LLM is a later optimisation if the model and fleet stay\nfixed."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which engine's distinctive workflow compiles the model ahead of time for a specific GPU architecture and configuration?","options":["vLLM","SGLang","TensorRT-LLM","TGI"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TensorRT-LLM builds a TensorRT engine specialised to the GPU type, batch and sequence limits, which enables aggressive kernel fusion but means a rebuild when hardware or limits change. The others load checkpoints at startup and need no per-GPU build step."},{"q":"What is the relationship between Ollama and llama.cpp?","options":["They are independent implementations of the same GGUF specification","Ollama packages llama.cpp with a model registry, a REST API and defaults for local use","llama.cpp is a GPU-only fork of Ollama","Ollama is the datacenter version of llama.cpp with continuous batching"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ollama is built on llama.cpp and its ggml library; it adds pulling models, a Modelfile, an HTTP API and device detection. It inherits llama.cpp's strengths on commodity hardware and its limits under many concurrent users."},{"q":"Which workload benefits most from SGLang's RadixAttention relative to a standard paged KV cache?","options":["Single-shot requests with unique prompts","Multi-turn chat and agent loops where many requests share long prefixes","Offline batch embedding of documents","CPU-only inference on edge devices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"RadixAttention stores the KV cache as a radix tree over token sequences, so any request whose prefix matches a cached one reuses it without recomputation. vLLM's block-hash prefix caching also reuses shared prefixes, so the gap is one of degree; it is largest in multi-turn and agent traffic, where branching conversations share long, irregular prefixes."}]}]});
